export const PACK = { id: "core-v4", json: "[{\"skill_code\":\"area_volume\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A rectangular rug has a perimeter of 46 feet. The length of the rug is 15 feet. What is the area, in square feet, of the rug?\",\"option_a\":\"23\",\"option_b\":\"120\",\"option_c\":\"240\",\"option_d\":\"345\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The perimeter is $2(\\\\ell + w) = 46$, so $\\\\ell + w = 23$ and $w = 23 - 15 = 8$ feet. The area is $15 \\\\cdot 8 = 120$ square feet. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A (23) is the sum of the length and the width, not the area. Choice C (240) is twice the area. Choice D (345) is $15 \\\\cdot 23$; it uses half the perimeter as the width.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Perimetr $2(\\\\ell + w) = 46$, demak $\\\\ell + w = 23$ va $w = 23 - 15 = 8$ fut. Yuza $15 \\\\cdot 8 = 120$ kvadrat fut. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA (23) — uzunlik va enning yig‘indisi, yuza emas. C (240) — yuzaning ikki baravari. D (345) — bu $15 \\\\cdot 23$; unda perimetrning yarmi en sifatida olingan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"rectangle\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"area_volume\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A storage box is shaped like a rectangular prism. The base of the box is 9 inches long and 8 inches wide, and the volume of the box is 360 cubic inches. What is the height, in inches, of the box?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"5\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The volume of a rectangular prism is $V = \\\\ell w h$. So $360 = 9 \\\\cdot 8 \\\\cdot h = 72h$, and $h = \\\\frac{360}{72} = 5$ inches. If you got 40, you divided 360 by only one dimension of the base.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"To‘g‘ri burchakli parallelepiped hajmi $V = \\\\ell w h$. Demak $360 = 9 \\\\cdot 8 \\\\cdot h = 72h$ va $h = \\\\frac{360}{72} = 5$ dyuym. Agar 40 chiqqan bo‘lsa, siz 360 ni asosning faqat bitta o‘lchamiga bo‘lgansiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"prism_volume\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"area_volume\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A right circular cone has a base radius of 3 centimeters and a height of 8 centimeters. What is the volume, in cubic centimeters, of the cone?\",\"option_a\":\"$8\\\\pi$\",\"option_b\":\"$24\\\\pi$\",\"option_c\":\"$72\\\\pi$\",\"option_d\":\"$96\\\\pi$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The volume of a cone is $V = \\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\pi r^2 h = \\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\pi (3)^2(8) = 24\\\\pi$ cubic centimeters. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A uses $r$ instead of $r^2$: $\\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\pi(3)(8) = 8\\\\pi$. Choice C, $\\\\pi(3)^2(8) = 72\\\\pi$, is the volume of a cylinder; it leaves out the factor $\\\\frac{1}{3}$. Choice D uses the diameter 6 as the radius: $\\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\pi(6)^2(8) = 96\\\\pi$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Konus hajmi $V = \\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\pi r^2 h = \\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\pi (3)^2(8) = 24\\\\pi$ kub santimetr. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA javobda $r^2$ o‘rniga $r$ olingan: $\\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\pi(3)(8) = 8\\\\pi$. C, ya’ni $\\\\pi(3)^2(8) = 72\\\\pi$, silindr hajmi; unda $\\\\frac{1}{3}$ ko‘paytuvchi tushib qolgan. D javobda radius o‘rniga diametr 6 olingan: $\\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\pi(6)^2(8) = 96\\\\pi$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"cone_volume\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"area_volume\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A solid metal ball is shaped like a sphere with a radius of 3 inches. What is the volume, in cubic inches, of the ball?\",\"option_a\":\"$12\\\\pi$\",\"option_b\":\"$27\\\\pi$\",\"option_c\":\"$36\\\\pi$\",\"option_d\":\"$108\\\\pi$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The volume of a sphere is $V = \\\\frac{4}{3}\\\\pi r^3 = \\\\frac{4}{3}\\\\pi(27) = 36\\\\pi$ cubic inches. Choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A is $4\\\\pi r = 12\\\\pi$, which is not a volume formula. Choice B is $\\\\pi r^3 = 27\\\\pi$; it leaves out the factor $\\\\frac{4}{3}$. Choice D is $4\\\\pi r^3 = 108\\\\pi$; it forgets to divide by 3.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Shar hajmi $V = \\\\frac{4}{3}\\\\pi r^3 = \\\\frac{4}{3}\\\\pi(27) = 36\\\\pi$ kub dyuym. To‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA — bu $4\\\\pi r = 12\\\\pi$, hajm formulasi emas. B — bu $\\\\pi r^3 = 27\\\\pi$; unda $\\\\frac{4}{3}$ ko‘paytuvchi tushib qolgan. D — bu $4\\\\pi r^3 = 108\\\\pi$; 3 ga bo‘lish unutilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"sphere_volume\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"area_volume\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A cube has a volume of 64 cubic centimeters. What is the total surface area, in square centimeters, of the cube?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"96\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The edge length $s$ satisfies $s^3 = 64$, so $s = 4$ centimeters. A cube has 6 square faces, each with area $4^2 = 16$, so the total surface area is $6 \\\\cdot 16 = 96$ square centimeters. If you got 16, you found the area of only one face.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qirra uzunligi $s$ uchun $s^3 = 64$, demak $s = 4$ santimetr. Kubning 6 ta kvadrat yog‘i bor, har birining yuzi $4^2 = 16$, shuning uchun to‘la sirt yuzi $6 \\\\cdot 16 = 96$ kvadrat santimetr. Agar 16 chiqqan bo‘lsa, siz faqat bitta yoqning yuzini topgansiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"surface_area\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"area_volume\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The length of a rectangle is doubled, and the width of the rectangle is tripled. The area of the new rectangle is how many times the area of the original rectangle?\",\"option_a\":\"5\",\"option_b\":\"6\",\"option_c\":\"9\",\"option_d\":\"36\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"If the original rectangle has length $\\\\ell$ and width $w$, its area is $\\\\ell w$. The new area is $(2\\\\ell)(3w) = 6\\\\ell w$, which is 6 times the original area. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A adds the two factors instead of multiplying them. Choice C is $3^2$, and choice D is $(2 \\\\cdot 3)^2$; neither length is squared when only the length and width change.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Agar dastlabki to‘g‘ri to‘rtburchakning uzunligi $\\\\ell$, eni $w$ bo‘lsa, yuzi $\\\\ell w$. Yangi yuza $(2\\\\ell)(3w) = 6\\\\ell w$, ya’ni dastlabkisidan 6 marta katta. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA javobda ikki ko‘paytuvchi ko‘paytirilish o‘rniga qo‘shilgan. C — bu $3^2$, D esa $(2 \\\\cdot 3)^2$; faqat uzunlik va en o‘zgarganda hech narsa kvadratga oshirilmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"scaling\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"area_volume\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Cone $A$ and cone $B$ are similar right circular cones. The height of cone $A$ is 4 centimeters, and the height of cone $B$ is 10 centimeters. The volume of cone $A$ is $16\\\\pi$ cubic centimeters. What is the volume, in cubic centimeters, of cone $B$?\",\"option_a\":\"$40\\\\pi$\",\"option_b\":\"$100\\\\pi$\",\"option_c\":\"$250\\\\pi$\",\"option_d\":\"$400\\\\pi$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The scale factor from cone $A$ to cone $B$ is $\\\\frac{10}{4} = \\\\frac{5}{2}$. Volumes of similar solids scale by the cube of the scale factor, so the volume of cone $B$ is $16\\\\pi \\\\cdot \\\\left(\\\\frac{5}{2}\\\\right)^3 = 16\\\\pi \\\\cdot \\\\frac{125}{8} = 250\\\\pi$. Choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A multiplies by the linear factor $\\\\frac{5}{2}$, and choice B multiplies by its square, $\\\\frac{25}{4}$, which is the factor for areas. Choice D multiplies by $5^2 = 25$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$A$ konusdan $B$ konusga o‘xshashlik koeffitsiyenti $\\\\frac{10}{4} = \\\\frac{5}{2}$. O‘xshash jismlarning hajmlari koeffitsiyentning kubiga proporsional, shuning uchun $B$ konus hajmi $16\\\\pi \\\\cdot \\\\left(\\\\frac{5}{2}\\\\right)^3 = 16\\\\pi \\\\cdot \\\\frac{125}{8} = 250\\\\pi$. To‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA javobda chiziqli koeffitsiyent $\\\\frac{5}{2}$ ga, B javobda esa uning kvadrati $\\\\frac{25}{4}$ ga ko‘paytirilgan — bu yuzalar uchun koeffitsiyent. D javobda $5^2 = 25$ ga ko‘paytirilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"similar_solids\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"area_volume\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A right circular cylinder has a volume of $180\\\\pi$ cubic meters and a height of 5 meters. What is the radius, in meters, of the cylinder?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"6\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The volume of a cylinder is $V = \\\\pi r^2 h$. So $180\\\\pi = \\\\pi r^2 (5)$, which gives $r^2 = 36$ and $r = 6$ meters. If you got 36, you stopped at $r^2$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Silindr hajmi $V = \\\\pi r^2 h$. Demak $180\\\\pi = \\\\pi r^2 (5)$, bundan $r^2 = 36$ va $r = 6$ metr. Agar 36 chiqqan bo‘lsa, siz $r^2$ da to‘xtab qolgansiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"cylinder_volume\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"area_volume\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A closed wooden crate is shaped like a rectangular prism with edges of length 3 feet, 4 feet, and 10 feet. What is the total surface area, in square feet, of the outside of the crate?\",\"option_a\":\"82\",\"option_b\":\"120\",\"option_c\":\"140\",\"option_d\":\"164\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The prism has three pairs of congruent faces with areas $3 \\\\cdot 4 = 12$, $3 \\\\cdot 10 = 30$, and $4 \\\\cdot 10 = 40$. The total surface area is $2(12 + 30 + 40) = 164$ square feet. Choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoice A counts each pair of faces only once. Choice B is the volume, $3 \\\\cdot 4 \\\\cdot 10$. Choice C, $2(3 + 4) \\\\cdot 10 = 140$, is only the four side faces; it leaves out the two $3$-by-$4$ ends.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Parallelepipedning yuzalari $3 \\\\cdot 4 = 12$, $3 \\\\cdot 10 = 30$ va $4 \\\\cdot 10 = 40$ bo‘lgan uch juft teng yog‘i bor. To‘la sirt yuzi $2(12 + 30 + 40) = 164$ kvadrat fut. To‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\nA javobda har bir juft yoq faqat bir marta hisoblangan. B — hajm, $3 \\\\cdot 4 \\\\cdot 10$. C, ya’ni $2(3 + 4) \\\\cdot 10 = 140$ — faqat to‘rtta yon yoq; ikkita $3 \\\\times 4$ o‘lchamli asos tushib qolgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"surface_area\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"area_volume\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A rectangular sheet of metal is 20 inches long and 12 inches wide. A square with a side length of 5 inches is cut out of one corner of the sheet. What is the perimeter, in inches, of the remaining piece of metal?\",\"option_a\":\"54\",\"option_b\":\"64\",\"option_c\":\"74\",\"option_d\":\"215\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The original perimeter is $2(20 + 12) = 64$ inches. Cutting out the corner square removes two 5-inch pieces of the boundary but adds two new 5-inch edges along the cut, so the perimeter stays $64$ inches. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A subtracts the two removed pieces but forgets the two new edges, and choice C adds the new edges without removing the old ones. Choice D is the area of the remaining piece, $240 - 25 = 215$ square inches.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Dastlabki perimetr $2(20 + 12) = 64$ dyuym. Burchakdagi kvadrat kesib olinganda chegaradan ikkita 5 dyuymli bo‘lak olib tashlanadi, lekin kesim bo‘ylab ikkita yangi 5 dyuymli tomon paydo bo‘ladi, shuning uchun perimetr $64$ dyuymligicha qoladi. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA javobda olib tashlangan ikki bo‘lak ayirilgan, ammo ikkita yangi tomon unutilgan; C javobda esa yangi tomonlar qo‘shilgan, eskilari ayirilmagan. D — qolgan bo‘lakning yuzi: $240 - 25 = 215$ kvadrat dyuym.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"composite_figure\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"area_volume\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A right circular cylinder and a right circular cone have the same base radius and the same volume. The height of the cylinder is 7 inches. What is the height, in inches, of the cone?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{7}{3}$\",\"option_b\":\"7\",\"option_c\":\"14\",\"option_d\":\"21\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Let $r$ be the common radius and $h$ the height of the cone. Equal volumes give $\\\\pi r^2 (7) = \\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\pi r^2 h$, so $7 = \\\\frac{h}{3}$ and $h = 21$ inches. Choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoice A divides by 3 instead of multiplying: a cone with the same base and height is one third of the cylinder, so the cone must be taller, not shorter. Choice B ignores the factor $\\\\frac{1}{3}$, and choice C uses a factor of 2.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Umumiy radius $r$, konus balandligi $h$ bo‘lsin. Hajmlar tengligidan $\\\\pi r^2 (7) = \\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\pi r^2 h$, demak $7 = \\\\frac{h}{3}$ va $h = 21$ dyuym. To‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\nA javobda 3 ga ko‘paytirish o‘rniga bo‘lingan: asosi va balandligi bir xil konus silindrning uchdan biriga teng, shuning uchun konus pastroq emas, balandroq bo‘lishi kerak. B javobda $\\\\frac{1}{3}$ ko‘paytuvchi e’tiborga olinmagan, C javobda esa 2 ko‘paytuvchi ishlatilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"cone_cylinder\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"area_volume\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A rectangular aquarium has an inside base that is 30 centimeters long and 20 centimeters wide, and it is partly filled with water. A metal block is placed in the aquarium, and the block is completely under the water. As a result, the water level rises by 1.5 centimeters, and no water overflows. What is the volume, in cubic centimeters, of the block?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"900\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The volume of the block equals the volume of the water it pushes up: a layer with base $30 \\\\times 20$ and height 1.5. So the volume is $30 \\\\cdot 20 \\\\cdot 1.5 = 900$ cubic centimeters. If you got 600, you found only the area of the base.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Blokning hajmi u ko‘targan suv hajmiga teng: asosi $30 \\\\times 20$, balandligi 1.5 bo‘lgan qatlam. Demak hajm $30 \\\\cdot 20 \\\\cdot 1.5 = 900$ kub santimetr. Agar 600 chiqqan bo‘lsa, siz faqat asos yuzini topgansiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"displacement\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"area_volume\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The radius of a right circular cylinder is increased by 20%, and the height of the cylinder is decreased by 20%. Which of the following describes the change in the volume of the cylinder?\",\"option_a\":\"The volume does not change.\",\"option_b\":\"The volume decreases by 4%.\",\"option_c\":\"The volume increases by 15.2%.\",\"option_d\":\"The volume increases by 44%.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The volume is $V = \\\\pi r^2 h$. The new volume is $\\\\pi (1.2r)^2 (0.8h) = 1.44 \\\\cdot 0.8 \\\\cdot \\\\pi r^2 h = 1.152V$, an increase of 15.2%. Choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A assumes the two 20% changes cancel. Choice B uses $1.2 \\\\cdot 0.8 = 0.96$; it forgets that the radius is squared. Choice D uses only $1.2^2 = 1.44$ and ignores the change in height.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Hajm $V = \\\\pi r^2 h$. Yangi hajm $\\\\pi (1.2r)^2 (0.8h) = 1.44 \\\\cdot 0.8 \\\\cdot \\\\pi r^2 h = 1.152V$, ya’ni 15.2% ga oshadi. To‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA javob ikkala 20% lik o‘zgarish bir-birini yo‘qqa chiqaradi deb hisoblaydi. B javobda $1.2 \\\\cdot 0.8 = 0.96$ olingan; radius kvadratga oshirilishi unutilgan. D javobda faqat $1.2^2 = 1.44$ olingan, balandlik o‘zgarishi hisobga olinmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"percent_change_dimensions\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"area_volume\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A closed box shaped like a rectangular prism has a square base. The height of the box is twice the side length of the base, and the total surface area of the box is 250 square inches. What is the volume, in cubic inches, of the box?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"250\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Let the base have side $s$; then the height is $2s$. The box has 2 square faces with area $s^2$ and 4 side faces with area $s \\\\cdot 2s = 2s^2$, so the surface area is $2s^2 + 8s^2 = 10s^2 = 250$. Then $s^2 = 25$ and $s = 5$ inches, the height is 10 inches, and the volume is $5 \\\\cdot 5 \\\\cdot 10 = 250$ cubic inches. (A common error is to count only one square face, which gives $9s^2 = 250$ and no whole-number side.)\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Asos tomoni $s$ bo‘lsin; u holda balandlik $2s$. Qutining yuzi $s^2$ bo‘lgan 2 ta kvadrat yog‘i va yuzi $s \\\\cdot 2s = 2s^2$ bo‘lgan 4 ta yon yog‘i bor, shuning uchun sirt yuzi $2s^2 + 8s^2 = 10s^2 = 250$. U holda $s^2 = 25$, $s = 5$ dyuym, balandlik 10 dyuym va hajm $5 \\\\cdot 5 \\\\cdot 10 = 250$ kub dyuym. (Ko‘p uchraydigan xato — faqat bitta kvadrat yoqni hisoblash; bu $9s^2 = 250$ ni beradi va butun son chiqmaydi.)\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"surface_area_volume\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"area_volume\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A sphere is placed inside a cube so that the sphere is tangent to each of the six faces of the cube. What fraction of the volume of the cube lies outside the sphere?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{6}$\",\"option_b\":\"$1 - \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{6}$\",\"option_c\":\"$1 - \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{1}{3}$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"If the sphere has radius $r$, the edge of the cube is $2r$. The cube's volume is $(2r)^3 = 8r^3$, and the sphere's volume is $\\\\frac{4}{3}\\\\pi r^3$. The sphere fills $\\\\frac{\\\\frac{4}{3}\\\\pi r^3}{8r^3} = \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{6}$ of the cube, so the fraction outside is $1 - \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{6}$. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A is the fraction inside the sphere. Choice C uses the area ratio of a circle inscribed in a square, $\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4}$. Choice D uses $\\\\frac{2}{3}$, the ratio of a sphere to the cylinder that surrounds it, not to a cube.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Shar radiusi $r$ bo‘lsa, kub qirrasi $2r$. Kub hajmi $(2r)^3 = 8r^3$, shar hajmi $\\\\frac{4}{3}\\\\pi r^3$. Shar kubning $\\\\frac{\\\\frac{4}{3}\\\\pi r^3}{8r^3} = \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{6}$ qismini egallaydi, demak tashqarida qolgan qism $1 - \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{6}$. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA — shar ichidagi qism. C javobda kvadratga ichki chizilgan doira yuzalari nisbati $\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4}$ ishlatilgan. D javobda $\\\\frac{2}{3}$ ishlatilgan — bu shar hajmining kubga emas, uni o‘rab turgan silindrga nisbati.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"inscribed_solids\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"area_volume\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"Two rectangular prisms are similar. The total surface area of the smaller prism is 88 square centimeters, and the total surface area of the larger prism is 198 square centimeters. The volume of the smaller prism is 48 cubic centimeters. What is the volume, in cubic centimeters, of the larger prism?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"162\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The ratio of the surface areas is $\\\\frac{198}{88} = \\\\frac{9}{4}$, which is the square of the scale factor, so the scale factor is $\\\\frac{3}{2}$. Volumes scale by the cube of the scale factor: $48 \\\\cdot \\\\left(\\\\frac{3}{2}\\\\right)^3 = 48 \\\\cdot \\\\frac{27}{8} = 162$ cubic centimeters. If you got 108, you multiplied the volume by the area ratio $\\\\frac{9}{4}$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Sirt yuzalari nisbati $\\\\frac{198}{88} = \\\\frac{9}{4}$ — bu o‘xshashlik koeffitsiyentining kvadrati, demak koeffitsiyent $\\\\frac{3}{2}$. Hajmlar koeffitsiyentning kubiga proporsional: $48 \\\\cdot \\\\left(\\\\frac{3}{2}\\\\right)^3 = 48 \\\\cdot \\\\frac{27}{8} = 162$ kub santimetr. Agar 108 chiqqan bo‘lsa, siz hajmni yuzalar nisbati $\\\\frac{9}{4}$ ga ko‘paytirgansiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"similar_solids\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Engineer Malika Rustamova first tested her clay water filters in a laboratory, using muddy river water. She then gave the filters to families in three rural villages, who used them every day for a ______ villagers reported which filters clogged fastest and which were easiest to clean.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"month, the\",\"option_b\":\"month the\",\"option_c\":\"month. The\",\"option_d\":\"month, then the\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text before the blank (“She then gave … every day for a month”) and the text after it (“the villagers reported …”) are both independent clauses. Choice C correctly separates them with a period.\\n\\nChoice A joins two independent clauses with only a comma, creating a comma splice. Choice B joins them with no punctuation at all, creating a run-on sentence. Choice D is also a comma splice: “then” is an adverb, not a coordinating conjunction such as “and” or “so,” so it cannot join two independent clauses after a comma.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bo‘sh joydan oldingi qism (“She then gave … every day for a month”) ham, keyingi qism (“the villagers reported …”) ham mustaqil gap. C ularni nuqta bilan to‘g‘ri ajratadi.\\n\\nA ikki mustaqil gapni faqat vergul bilan bog‘laydi — bu “comma splice” xatosi. B ularni hech qanday tinish belgisisiz qo‘shib yuboradi (run-on). D ham “comma splice”: “then” — ravish, “and” yoki “so” kabi bog‘lovchi emas, shuning uchun verguldan keyin ikki mustaqil gapni bog‘lay olmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"independent-clauses\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"To learn how white storks choose their migration routes, researchers fitted forty young storks with lightweight solar-powered tags. The tags recorded each ______ every fifteen minutes, allowing the team to map the birds' journeys from Europe to Africa in detail.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"bird's position,\",\"option_b\":\"bird's position\",\"option_c\":\"bird's, position\",\"option_d\":\"bird's: position\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"In the clause “The tags recorded each bird's position every fifteen minutes,” “recorded” is the verb, “each bird's position” is its object, and “every fifteen minutes” tells when. No punctuation is needed anywhere in this phrase, so choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A puts a comma between the object and the essential time phrase “every fifteen minutes.” Choice C splits the possessive “bird's” from the noun it modifies. Choice D's colon interrupts the object of the verb; a colon must follow a complete independent clause.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“The tags recorded each bird's position every fifteen minutes” gapida “recorded” — kesim, “each bird's position” — to‘ldiruvchi, “every fifteen minutes” esa vaqtni bildiradi. Bu qismda hech qanday tinish belgisi kerak emas, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA to‘ldiruvchi bilan zarur vaqt birikmasi (“every fifteen minutes”) orasiga vergul qo‘yadi. C qaratqich shakldagi “bird's” ni o‘zi aniqlayotgan otdan ajratib qo‘yadi. D dagi ikki nuqta to‘ldiruvchini bo‘lib yuboradi; ikki nuqta faqat to‘liq mustaqil gapdan keyin keladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"no-punct-verb-object\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"A traditional Central Asian yurt can be taken down and loaded onto pack animals in a few hours. Its frame consists of just a few main ______ a folding wooden lattice for the walls, long curved roof poles, and a heavy wooden ring at the top.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"parts,\",\"option_b\":\"parts;\",\"option_c\":\"parts\",\"option_d\":\"parts:\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first part of the sentence (“Its frame consists of just a few main parts”) is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a list of those parts. A colon is the correct way to introduce a list after an independent clause, so choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoice A's comma makes it seem that “a folding wooden lattice” is just one more item in a series rather than the start of the list being introduced. Choice B's semicolon should join two independent clauses, but the list is not an independent clause. Choice C runs “parts” directly into the list with no punctuation, which is ungrammatical.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapning birinchi qismi (“Its frame consists of just a few main parts”) to‘liq mustaqil gap, undan keyin esa shu qismlarning ro‘yxati keladi. Mustaqil gapdan keyin ro‘yxatni ikki nuqta bilan boshlash to‘g‘ri, shuning uchun javob D.\\n\\nA dagi vergul “a folding wooden lattice” ni ro‘yxatning boshi emas, qandaydir qatorning yana bir bo‘lagi kabi ko‘rsatadi. B dagi nuqtali vergul ikki mustaqil gapni bog‘lashi kerak, ro‘yxat esa mustaqil gap emas. C da “parts” so‘zi hech qanday belgisiz ro‘yxatga qo‘shilib ketadi, bu grammatik jihatdan noto‘g‘ri.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"colon-list\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Gas bubbles have been rising more and more quickly from the crater lake on top of the volcano. According to ______ this change suggests that fresh magma is moving closer to the surface.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"volcanologist, Tomás Ferreira,\",\"option_b\":\"volcanologist, Tomás Ferreira\",\"option_c\":\"volcanologist Tomás Ferreira,\",\"option_d\":\"volcanologist: Tomás Ferreira,\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"“Volcanologist” is a title that directly precedes the name “Tomás Ferreira” and identifies him, so no punctuation should separate the title from the name. A comma is then needed after the introductory phrase “According to volcanologist Tomás Ferreira.” Choice C does both.\\n\\nChoices A and B wrongly place a comma between the title and the name; choice B also leaves out the comma that ends the introductory phrase. Choice D's colon must follow an independent clause, and “According to volcanologist” is not one.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“Volcanologist” — ismdan bevosita oldin keladigan va shaxsni aniqlaydigan unvon, shuning uchun unvon bilan ism (“Tomás Ferreira”) orasiga tinish belgisi qo‘yilmaydi. Shundan so‘ng kirish birikmasi “According to volcanologist Tomás Ferreira” dan keyin vergul kerak. C ikkalasini ham bajaradi.\\n\\nA va B unvon bilan ism orasiga noto‘g‘ri vergul qo‘yadi; B bundan tashqari kirish birikmasini yopuvchi vergulni ham tushirib qoldiradi. D dagi ikki nuqta mustaqil gapdan keyin kelishi kerak, “According to volcanologist” esa mustaqil gap emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"title-before-name\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"When the town's first public library opened in 1894, it occupied a single rented room above a bakery. Even so, word of the new library spread ______ more than three hundred residents to its reading room during its first week.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"quickly; drawing\",\"option_b\":\"quickly. Drawing\",\"option_c\":\"quickly, it drew\",\"option_d\":\"quickly, drawing\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"“Drawing more than three hundred residents to its reading room during its first week” is a participial phrase, not an independent clause. It describes the result of the news spreading, so it should be attached to the main clause with a comma, as in choice D.\\n\\nChoice B places a period before the phrase, leaving “Drawing … week” as a sentence fragment. Choice A uses a semicolon, which must join two independent clauses, before a phrase that is not one. Choice C creates a comma splice by joining two independent clauses (“word … spread quickly” and “it drew …”) with only a comma.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“Drawing more than three hundred residents to its reading room during its first week” — sifatdoshli (participial) birikma, mustaqil gap emas. U xabar tarqalishining natijasini tasvirlaydi, shuning uchun bosh gapga vergul bilan qo‘shilishi kerak — D.\\n\\nB birikmadan oldin nuqta qo‘yadi, natijada “Drawing … week” to‘liq bo‘lmagan gap (fragment) bo‘lib qoladi. A nuqtali vergul ishlatadi, u esa ikki mustaqil gapni bog‘lashi kerak, keyingi qism esa mustaqil gap emas. C ikki mustaqil gapni (“word … spread quickly” va “it drew …”) faqat vergul bilan bog‘lab, “comma splice” xatosini keltirib chiqaradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"fragment-participle\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Many people picture deserts as places of constant, scorching ______ temperatures in the Gobi Desert of Mongolia and northern China regularly fall below −30°C during the winter months.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"heat, however,\",\"option_b\":\"heat; however,\",\"option_c\":\"heat; however\",\"option_d\":\"heat however,\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The sentence contains two independent clauses: “Many people picture deserts as places of constant, scorching heat” and “temperatures in the Gobi Desert … fall below −30°C during the winter months.” When a conjunctive adverb such as “however” begins the second clause, it needs a semicolon (or period) before it and a comma after it, as in choice B.\\n\\nChoice A joins the two clauses with only commas, creating a comma splice. Choice C omits the comma that should follow “however.” Choice D has no punctuation before “however,” producing a run-on sentence.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapda ikki mustaqil gap bor: “Many people picture deserts as places of constant, scorching heat” va “temperatures in the Gobi Desert … fall below −30°C during the winter months.” “However” kabi bog‘lovchi ravish ikkinchi gapni boshlaganda undan oldin nuqtali vergul (yoki nuqta), keyin esa vergul kerak — B.\\n\\nA ikki gapni faqat vergullar bilan bog‘laydi (“comma splice”). C “however” dan keyingi vergulni tushirib qoldiradi. D da “however” dan oldin hech qanday belgi yo‘q, natijada run-on gap hosil bo‘ladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"conjunctive-adverb\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The botanical garden's new desert greenhouse displays plants collected from three very different deserts: the Atacama, in northern Chile; the Namib, along the coast of southwestern ______ and the Sonoran, in northwestern Mexico and the southwestern United States.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"Africa,\",\"option_b\":\"Africa:\",\"option_c\":\"Africa;\",\"option_d\":\"Africa—\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The list after the colon has three items, and each item already contains a comma (“the Atacama, in northern Chile”). To keep the items clearly separate, a semicolon must divide them, just as one already follows “Chile.” Choice C places a semicolon after the second item.\\n\\nChoice A's comma makes it hard to tell where the second item ends, since commas are already used inside the items. Choice B uses a second colon, which is not used to separate list items. Choice D's dash is not used to separate items in a series and does not match the semicolon that ends the first item.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikki nuqtadan keyingi ro‘yxatda uchta element bor va har birining ichida allaqachon vergul bor (“the Atacama, in northern Chile”). Elementlarni aniq ajratish uchun ular orasiga nuqtali vergul qo‘yilishi kerak — “Chile” dan keyin ham shunday. C ikkinchi elementdan keyin nuqtali vergul qo‘yadi.\\n\\nA dagi vergul ikkinchi element qayerda tugashini aniqlashni qiyinlashtiradi, chunki vergullar elementlar ichida ishlatilgan. B ikkinchi marta ikki nuqta qo‘yadi, ro‘yxat elementlari esa ikki nuqta bilan ajratilmaydi. D dagi tire ro‘yxat elementlarini ajratish uchun ishlatilmaydi va birinchi elementdan keyingi nuqtali vergulga mos kelmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"semicolon-complex-list\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The Silk Road—a name first used in 1877 by the German geographer Ferdinand von ______ was never a single road. It was a shifting network of caravan routes linking China with Central Asia, Persia, and the Mediterranean world.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"Richthofen\",\"option_b\":\"Richthofen—\",\"option_c\":\"Richthofen;\",\"option_d\":\"Richthofen,\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The phrase “a name first used in 1877 by the German geographer Ferdinand von Richthofen” is a nonessential element that interrupts the subject “The Silk Road” and its verb “was.” It opens with a dash, so it must close with a matching dash, as in choice B.\\n\\nChoice D closes the element with a comma, which does not match the opening dash. Choice C's semicolon would separate the subject from its verb, and the text before it is not an independent clause. Choice A leaves the element unclosed, so it seems to run straight into “was never a single road.”\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“a name first used in 1877 by the German geographer Ferdinand von Richthofen” — ega (“The Silk Road”) va kesim (“was”) orasiga kirgan qo‘shimcha (nonessential) qism. U tire bilan ochilgan, demak, xuddi shunday tire bilan yopilishi kerak — B.\\n\\nD qismni vergul bilan yopadi, bu ochuvchi tirega mos emas. C dagi nuqtali vergul egani kesimdan ajratib qo‘yadi, undan oldingi qism esa mustaqil gap emas. A qismni yopmay qoldiradi, natijada u “was never a single road” ga qo‘shilib ketadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"paired-dashes\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Tardigrades, microscopic animals often called water ______ can survive conditions that would kill most other life forms. When their surroundings dry out, they curl into a compact, nearly lifeless state and can remain that way for years until water returns.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"bears\",\"option_b\":\"bears;\",\"option_c\":\"bears—\",\"option_d\":\"bears,\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The appositive “microscopic animals often called water bears” is a nonessential element placed between the subject “Tardigrades” and the verb “can survive.” Because it opens with a comma, it must close with a comma, as in choice D.\\n\\nChoice A leaves the appositive unclosed. Choice C closes it with a dash, which does not match the opening comma. Choice B's semicolon would separate the subject from its verb, and “Tardigrades, microscopic animals often called water bears” is not an independent clause.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“microscopic animals often called water bears” — ega (“Tardigrades”) va kesim (“can survive”) orasida turgan izohlovchi (appositive) qo‘shimcha qism. U vergul bilan ochilgani uchun vergul bilan yopilishi kerak — D.\\n\\nA izohlovchini yopmay qoldiradi. C uni tire bilan yopadi, bu ochuvchi vergulga mos emas. B dagi nuqtali vergul egani kesimdan ajratadi, “Tardigrades, microscopic animals often called water bears” esa mustaqil gap emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"paired-commas-appositive\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Do bus riders care more about how fast a trip is or about whether the bus arrives when expected? A survey that economist Rafael Duarte conducted among 3,000 bus riders in five ______ found that most riders would accept a slightly slower trip in exchange for more reliable arrival times.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"cities\",\"option_b\":\"cities,\",\"option_c\":\"cities;\",\"option_d\":\"cities—\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The subject of the sentence is the long phrase “A survey that economist Rafael Duarte conducted among 3,000 bus riders in five cities,” and the verb is “found.” The “that” clause is essential because it tells which survey is meant. No punctuation belongs between a subject and its verb, so choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoice B places a comma between the subject and the verb. Choice C's semicolon would separate the subject from the verb, and the subject is not an independent clause. Choice D's single dash also separates the subject from its verb without any reason.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapning egasi — uzun birikma “A survey that economist Rafael Duarte conducted among 3,000 bus riders in five cities”, kesimi — “found.” “that” bilan boshlangan qism zarur (essential), chunki qaysi so‘rovnoma haqida gap ketayotganini ko‘rsatadi. Ega va kesim orasiga tinish belgisi qo‘yilmaydi, shuning uchun javob A.\\n\\nB ega va kesim orasiga vergul qo‘yadi. C dagi nuqtali vergul egani kesimdan ajratadi, ega esa mustaqil gap emas. D dagi yakka tire ham hech qanday sababsiz egani kesimdan ajratib qo‘yadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"no-punct-subject-verb\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Restorers cleaning a sixteenth-century ceiling mural expected the sky to be painted with ground lapis lazuli, the costly blue stone favored by many painters of the period. To their surprise, the blue ______ identified in the sky turned out to be a much cheaper copper-based pigment.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"pigment, that they\",\"option_b\":\"pigment that they\",\"option_c\":\"pigment that, they\",\"option_d\":\"pigment; that they\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"“that they identified in the sky” is an essential clause: it tells which pigment the sentence is about, so it must not be set off with commas. It also sits inside the subject (“the blue pigment that they identified in the sky”), which should not be separated from its verb, “turned out.” Choice B uses no punctuation and is correct.\\n\\nChoice A places a comma before an essential “that” clause. Choice C puts a comma inside the clause, between “that” and its subject “they.” Choice D's semicolon cuts the subject in half; neither part is an independent clause.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“that they identified in the sky” — zarur (essential) ergash gap: u qaysi pigment haqida gap ketayotganini aniqlaydi, shuning uchun vergul bilan ajratilmaydi. U, shuningdek, ega (“the blue pigment that they identified in the sky”) ichida joylashgan va egani kesim (“turned out”) dan ajratib bo‘lmaydi. B hech qanday belgi qo‘ymaydi va to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA zarur “that” ergash gapidan oldin vergul qo‘yadi. C ergash gap ichiga — “that” bilan uning egasi “they” orasiga — vergul qo‘yadi. D dagi nuqtali vergul egani ikkiga bo‘ladi; ikkala qism ham mustaqil gap emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"essential-that-clause\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In an experiment on indoor farming, lettuce seedlings grown under red light alone looked healthy at first but soon became tall and ______ without any blue light, the plants could not properly control how fast their stems lengthened.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"spindly\",\"option_b\":\"spindly,\",\"option_c\":\"spindly; because\",\"option_d\":\"spindly:\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Both “lettuce seedlings … soon became tall and spindly” and “without any blue light, the plants could not properly control how fast their stems lengthened” are independent clauses, and the second explains the first. A colon can join two independent clauses when the second explains the first, so choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoice B joins two independent clauses with only a comma, creating a comma splice. Choice A uses no punctuation, creating a run-on sentence. Choice C places a semicolon before “because …,” which turns the second part into a dependent clause; a semicolon cannot join an independent clause to a dependent one.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“lettuce seedlings … soon became tall and spindly” ham, “without any blue light, the plants could not properly control how fast their stems lengthened” ham mustaqil gap, ikkinchisi esa birinchisini izohlaydi. Ikkinchi gap birinchisini tushuntirganda ularni ikki nuqta bilan bog‘lash mumkin — D.\\n\\nB ikki mustaqil gapni faqat vergul bilan bog‘laydi (“comma splice”). A hech qanday belgi qo‘ymaydi (run-on). C “because …” dan oldin nuqtali vergul qo‘yadi; “because” ikkinchi qismni ergash gapga aylantiradi, nuqtali vergul esa mustaqil gapni ergash gap bilan bog‘lay olmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"colon-explanation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Naira Ospanova's essay “Notes from a Night ______ written over a single winter in Tbilisi and first printed in a small student magazine—was later translated into twelve languages and is now taught in many university writing courses.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"Train”—\",\"option_b\":\"Train,”\",\"option_c\":\"Train”,\",\"option_d\":\"Train”\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The phrase “written over a single winter in Tbilisi and first printed in a small student magazine” is a nonessential element between the subject (“Naira Ospanova's essay …”) and the verb “was.” It closes with a dash, so it must open with a matching dash. The title's closing quotation mark comes first, because it is part of the title, and the dash follows it, as in choice A.\\n\\nChoices B and C open the element with a comma (inside or outside the quotation mark), which does not match the closing dash. Choice D uses no opening punctuation at all, leaving the dash after “magazine” without a partner.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“written over a single winter in Tbilisi and first printed in a small student magazine” — ega (“Naira Ospanova's essay …”) va kesim (“was”) orasidagi qo‘shimcha qism. U tire bilan yopilgan, demak, unga mos tire bilan ochilishi kerak. Avval sarlavhaga tegishli yopuvchi qo‘shtirnoq keladi, keyin tire — A.\\n\\nB va C qismni vergul bilan ochadi (qo‘shtirnoq ichida yoki tashqarisida), bu yopuvchi tirega mos emas. D hech qanday ochuvchi belgi qo‘ymaydi, natijada “magazine” dan keyingi tire juftsiz qoladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"dash-quotation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"When astronomers first spotted the comet in January, early models predicted that it would grow steadily brighter until it passed closest to the Sun in May and might even become visible without a telescope. The ______ began to fade in late March, most likely because its icy nucleus had started to break apart.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"comet, however,\",\"option_b\":\"comet however,\",\"option_c\":\"comet, however\",\"option_d\":\"comet; however,\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Here “however” does not join two clauses; it interrupts a single clause, “The comet … began to fade in late March.” An interrupting word like this must be set off by a pair of commas, as in choice A.\\n\\nChoice B lacks the opening comma, and choice C lacks the closing comma, so neither sets off “however” on both sides. Choice D places a semicolon between the subject “The comet” and its verb “began”; a semicolon must join two independent clauses, and “The comet” is not one.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bu yerda “however” ikki gapni bog‘lamaydi; u bitta gap — “The comet … began to fade in late March” — ichiga kirib keladi. Bunday kiritma so‘z ikki tomondan vergul bilan ajratilishi kerak — A.\\n\\nB da ochuvchi vergul, C da esa yopuvchi vergul yo‘q, shuning uchun ikkalasi ham “however” ni ikki tomondan ajratmaydi. D ega (“The comet”) va kesim (“began”) orasiga nuqtali vergul qo‘yadi; nuqtali vergul ikki mustaqil gapni bog‘lashi kerak, “The comet” esa mustaqil gap emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"interrupting-conjunctive-adverb\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Dendrochronologists date old wooden objects by matching the pattern of wide and narrow growth rings in the wood to a master ______ built from overlapping samples of living trees, old roof beams, and logs preserved in bogs that together can stretch back thousands of years.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"chronology. A record\",\"option_b\":\"chronology; a record\",\"option_c\":\"chronology, a record\",\"option_d\":\"chronology, it is a record\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text after the blank, “a record built from overlapping samples … that together can stretch back thousands of years,” is a long noun phrase, not an independent clause: “built” is a participle and “can stretch” belongs to the “that” clause, so the phrase has no main verb of its own. It renames “a master chronology,” so it should be attached with a comma as an appositive, as in choice C.\\n\\nChoice A turns the noun phrase into a separate sentence, creating a fragment. Choice B uses a semicolon, which must join two independent clauses, before a phrase that is not one. Choice D adds “it is,” turning the phrase into an independent clause joined to the first with only a comma, a comma splice.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bo‘sh joydan keyingi “a record built from overlapping samples … that together can stretch back thousands of years” — uzun ot birikmasi, mustaqil gap emas: “built” — sifatdosh, “can stretch” esa “that” ergash gapiga tegishli, shuning uchun birikmaning o‘z bosh kesimi yo‘q. U “a master chronology” ni izohlaydi, demak, izohlovchi (appositive) sifatida vergul bilan qo‘shilishi kerak — C.\\n\\nA ot birikmasini alohida gapga aylantiradi va to‘liq bo‘lmagan gap (fragment) hosil qiladi. B nuqtali vergul ishlatadi, u esa faqat ikki mustaqil gapni bog‘laydi. D “it is” qo‘shib, birikmani mustaqil gapga aylantiradi va uni birinchi gapga faqat vergul bilan bog‘laydi — bu “comma splice”.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"appositive-vs-fragment\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Archaeologists excavating a rock shelter in the foothills found evidence of human activity in several layers of sediment. The bone needles, shell beads, and lumps of red ochre recovered from the deepest layer of the ______ that people were already making clothing and ornaments there more than 30,000 years ago.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"shelter suggest\",\"option_b\":\"shelter, suggest\",\"option_c\":\"shelter—suggest\",\"option_d\":\"shelter; suggest\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The subject of the second sentence is the long phrase “The bone needles, shell beads, and lumps of red ochre recovered from the deepest layer of the shelter,” and its verb is “suggest.” Although the subject begins with a list, the list is finished before “recovered,” so no comma is needed after “shelter.” No punctuation should separate a subject from its verb, so choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoice B adds a comma between the subject and the verb, perhaps because the subject begins with a series. Choice C's single dash also separates the subject from its verb. Choice D's semicolon requires independent clauses on both sides, but neither the subject nor “suggest that …” is one.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkinchi gapning egasi — uzun birikma “The bone needles, shell beads, and lumps of red ochre recovered from the deepest layer of the shelter”, kesimi — “suggest.” Ega ro‘yxat bilan boshlansa ham, ro‘yxat “recovered” dan oldin tugaydi, shuning uchun “shelter” dan keyin vergul kerak emas. Ega va kesim orasiga tinish belgisi qo‘yilmaydi — A.\\n\\nB ega bilan kesim orasiga vergul qo‘yadi (ehtimol, ega ro‘yxat bilan boshlangani uchun). C dagi yakka tire ham egani kesimdan ajratadi. D dagi nuqtali vergul ikki tomonda ham mustaqil gap talab qiladi, lekin na ega, na “suggest that …” mustaqil gap.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"no-punct-compound-subject\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"As the Aral Sea shrank during the twentieth century, it left behind a vast plain of sand and salt that strong winds can lift into choking dust storms. To hold this ground in place, foresters in Uzbekistan have planted large areas of the dry former seabed with saxaul, a hardy shrub that tolerates both drought and salty soil. Saxaul's long roots bind loose sand, and its branches slow the wind near the ground. Foresters hope that as the plantings spread, they will reduce the amount of dust blown from the old seabed onto nearby towns and farms.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"The Aral Sea has begun to refill because saxaul shrubs keep its water from evaporating.\",\"option_b\":\"Foresters are planting a hardy shrub on the former seabed of the Aral Sea to hold the sand in place and reduce blowing dust.\",\"option_c\":\"Saxaul is the only plant that can grow anywhere in Central Asia.\",\"option_d\":\"Dust storms from the old seabed no longer reach nearby towns and farms.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text explains that the dry former seabed produces dust storms and that foresters are planting saxaul there because its roots \\\"bind loose sand\\\" and its branches \\\"slow the wind,\\\" in the hope of reducing dust. Choice B states this main idea.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the text never says the sea is refilling; saxaul holds sand, not water. Choice C is wrong because the text says only that saxaul tolerates drought and salty soil, not that it is the only plant that can grow anywhere. Choice D overstates the text: foresters only \\\"hope\\\" the plantings will reduce dust.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda qurigan sobiq dengiz tubidan chang bo‘ronlari ko‘tarilishi va o‘rmonchilar u yerga saksovul ekayotgani aytiladi: saksovulning ildizlari \\\"bo‘sh qumni bog‘lab turadi\\\", shoxlari esa \\\"shamolni sekinlashtiradi\\\". Ular shu orqali changni kamaytirishga umid qilishadi. B javob aynan shu asosiy g‘oyani ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matnda dengiz yana to‘layotgani haqida gap yo‘q; saksovul suvni emas, qumni ushlab turadi. C noto‘g‘ri: matnda faqat saksovul qurg‘oqchilik va sho‘r tuproqqa chidamli ekani aytilgan, uning har joyda o‘sadigan yagona o‘simlik ekani emas. D matndagi fikrni oshirib yuboradi: o‘rmonchilar changni kamaytirishga faqat \\\"umid qilishadi\\\".\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"main_idea\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Beginning in the eighth century, the city of Samarkand became a celebrated center of papermaking. Craftspeople there soaked linen rags and other plant fibers, beat them into a pulp, spread the pulp in thin sheets on screens, and left the sheets to dry. Over the following centuries, paper gradually replaced parchment, a writing material made from animal skin, across much of the Islamic world. Because paper was far less expensive to produce than parchment, scribes could copy books more cheaply, and libraries throughout the region grew larger as a result.\",\"stem\":\"According to the text, why did paper allow libraries to grow larger?\",\"option_a\":\"Paper lasted much longer than parchment, so fewer books had to be replaced.\",\"option_b\":\"Craftspeople in Samarkand copied books for libraries throughout the region.\",\"option_c\":\"Paper was made from animal skins, which were easier to obtain than plant fibers.\",\"option_d\":\"Paper cost far less to produce than parchment, so copying books became cheaper.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The last sentence explains the cause: \\\"Because paper was far less expensive to produce than parchment, scribes could copy books more cheaply, and libraries throughout the region grew larger as a result.\\\" Choice D paraphrases this.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the text says nothing about how long paper lasted. Choice B is wrong because the craftspeople made paper; the text says scribes copied books, not that Samarkand's papermakers did. Choice C reverses the text: parchment, not paper, was made from animal skin.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Oxirgi gapda sabab aniq ko‘rsatilgan: qog‘oz ishlab chiqarish pergamentga qaraganda ancha arzon bo‘lgani uchun kotiblar kitoblarni arzonroq ko‘chira olgan va natijada kutubxonalar kattalashgan. D javob shuni o‘z so‘zlari bilan ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matnda qog‘ozning qancha vaqt saqlanishi haqida hech narsa aytilmagan. B noto‘g‘ri: Samarqand hunarmandlari qog‘oz yasagan; kitoblarni esa kotiblar ko‘chirgan. C matnga teskari: hayvon terisidan qog‘oz emas, pergament tayyorlangan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"detail\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is adapted from a short story. Nodira is spending the day helping her grandfather bake bread.\\n\\nNodira had expected baking with her grandfather to be quick work. Instead, he made her wait: wait for the dough to rise under its cloth, wait for the clay oven to grow hot enough, wait while he pressed a pattern into each round loaf with a ring of pins. By the third hour she was drumming her fingers on the table. Then he drew the first loaf from the oven, broke it open, and handed her half. Steam rose from the soft center, and the crust cracked like thin ice. Chewing slowly, she understood that every one of those slow steps was in the bread.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Although Nodira grows impatient with her grandfather's slow baking, the finished bread makes her see the value of each step.\",\"option_b\":\"Nodira's grandfather teaches her a faster way to bake bread so that she will not become bored.\",\"option_c\":\"Nodira's grandfather becomes annoyed when she drums her fingers on the table.\",\"option_d\":\"Nodira decides that she would rather buy bread than spend hours making it.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Nodira expects quick work, grows restless (\\\"drumming her fingers\\\"), but after tasting the bread she understands \\\"that every one of those slow steps was in the bread.\\\" Choice A captures this change.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because the grandfather does not speed up; he makes her wait at every stage. Choice C is wrong because the text never describes the grandfather's reaction to her impatience. Choice D contradicts the ending, in which Nodira comes to value the slow process.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Nodira ishni tez tugaydi deb o‘ylaydi, kutishdan zerikib \\\"barmoqlari bilan stolni chertadi\\\", lekin nonni tatib ko‘rgach, o‘sha sekin bosqichlarning har biri nonda borligini tushunadi. A javob shu o‘zgarishni ifodalaydi.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: bobosi ishni tezlashtirmaydi, aksincha, har bosqichda kutishga majbur qiladi. C noto‘g‘ri: matnda bobosining uning sabrsizligiga qanday munosabatda bo‘lgani tasvirlanmagan. D yakunga zid: oxirida Nodira sekin jarayonni qadrlay boshlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"main_idea_literary\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In a time bank, neighbors trade services using hours instead of money: an hour spent repairing one member's bicycle earns an hour of, say, language tutoring from another member. Organizers often promote time banks as a way for people to save money. But when sociologist Dilfuza Rahimova interviewed members of several time banks, most said that saving money was not their main reason for staying. Instead, they valued getting to know neighbors they would otherwise never have met.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Time banks save their members more money than organizers expected.\",\"option_b\":\"Most time bank members prefer language tutoring to other services.\",\"option_c\":\"Although time banks are promoted as a way to save money, many members value them mainly for the social connections they create.\",\"option_d\":\"Sociologists have found that time banks work better in large cities than in small towns.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text sets up a contrast: organizers promote time banks as a way to save money, \\\"But\\\" Rahimova found that members mainly valued \\\"getting to know neighbors.\\\" Choice C states this main idea.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the text never compares actual savings with organizers' expectations. Choice B treats an example (language tutoring) as a finding. Choice D is wrong because the text never compares cities and towns.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda qarama-qarshilik bor: tashkilotchilar vaqt banklarini pul tejash usuli sifatida targ‘ib qilishadi, \\\"lekin\\\" Rahimova a’zolar asosan \\\"qo‘shnilar bilan tanishish\\\"ni qadrlashini aniqlagan. C javob shu asosiy g‘oyani ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matnda haqiqiy tejash tashkilotchilarning kutganlari bilan solishtirilmagan. B noto‘g‘ri: til o‘rgatish faqat misol sifatida keltirilgan, tadqiqot natijasi emas. D noto‘g‘ri: matnda katta shaharlar va kichik shaharchalar taqqoslanmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"main_idea\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Glass frogs, which live in the forests of Central and South America, have skin on their undersides so clear that some of their internal organs can be seen through it. Their backs are partly see-through as well, which helps them blend in with the leaves they rest on during the day. In 2022, a team of researchers discovered how the frogs become even more transparent while they sleep: they remove most of their red blood cells from circulation and pack them into the liver, an organ wrapped in a reflective, mirror-like coating. When the frogs wake and become active, the cells return to the bloodstream.\",\"stem\":\"According to the text, how do glass frogs become more transparent while they sleep?\",\"option_a\":\"They change the color of their skin to match the leaves they rest on.\",\"option_b\":\"They move most of their red blood cells out of circulation and store them in the liver.\",\"option_c\":\"They stop their hearts until they wake up and become active.\",\"option_d\":\"They grow a reflective coating over their internal organs each night.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text states that while sleeping, glass frogs \\\"remove most of their red blood cells from circulation and pack them into the liver.\\\" Choice B paraphrases this detail.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the text says the frogs blend in by being see-through, not by changing color. Choice C is wrong because the text never mentions the heart stopping. Choice D is wrong because the reflective coating already wraps the liver; the text does not say the frogs grow it each night or that it covers all their organs.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda shisha qurbaqalar uxlayotganda \\\"qizil qon hujayralarining ko‘p qismini qon aylanishidan chiqarib, jigarga joylashtirishi\\\" aytilgan. B javob shu tafsilotni o‘z so‘zlari bilan beradi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matnga ko‘ra qurbaqalar rangini o‘zgartirib emas, shaffofligi tufayli barglarga qo‘shilib ketadi. C noto‘g‘ri: matnda yurak to‘xtashi haqida gap yo‘q. D noto‘g‘ri: aks ettiruvchi qoplama jigarni doim o‘rab turadi; uning har kecha paydo bo‘lishi yoki barcha a’zolarni qoplashi aytilmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"detail\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Between 2017 and 2020, folklorist Gulshan Ergasheva recorded more than 200 lullabies sung by grandmothers in mountain villages of southern Uzbekistan. Ergasheva was mainly interested in the melodies, but she soon noticed something else: the lullabies were full of words for tools, plants, and household tasks that younger villagers no longer used in everyday speech. Because lullabies are memorized and repeated nearly word for word, Ergasheva concludes, they have preserved parts of the local vocabulary that ordinary conversation has gradually lost.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Ergasheva found that the melodies of the lullabies had changed greatly over time.\",\"option_b\":\"Younger villagers in southern Uzbekistan no longer learn lullabies from their grandmothers.\",\"option_c\":\"Lullabies from mountain villages describe tools and plants more accurately than scientific books do.\",\"option_d\":\"Ergasheva's recordings suggest that because lullabies are repeated word for word, they preserve local words that have dropped out of everyday speech.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text builds to Ergasheva's conclusion: because lullabies are \\\"repeated nearly word for word,\\\" they \\\"have preserved parts of the local vocabulary that ordinary conversation has gradually lost.\\\" Choice D states this main idea.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the text does not say the melodies changed. Choice B is wrong because the text says younger villagers no longer use certain words, not that they no longer learn lullabies. Choice C makes a comparison with scientific books that the text never makes.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn Ergashevaning xulosasiga olib boradi: allalar \\\"deyarli so‘zma-so‘z takrorlangani\\\" uchun ular kundalik nutqda yo‘qolib borayotgan mahalliy so‘zlarni saqlab qolgan. D javob shu asosiy g‘oyani ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matnda kuylar o‘zgargani aytilmagan. B noto‘g‘ri: matnga ko‘ra yoshlar ayrim so‘zlarni ishlatmay qo‘ygan, allalarni o‘rganmay qo‘ygani emas. C noto‘g‘ri: matnda ilmiy kitoblar bilan hech qanday solishtirish yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"main_idea\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Many desert shrubs produce bitter chemicals in their leaves, and botanists have generally explained these chemicals as a defense against grazing animals such as goats and gazelles. Plant ecologist Ravshan Tojiyev noticed, however, that one such shrub kept producing large amounts of the chemicals even on fenced plots that no grazing animals could reach. In laboratory tests, Tojiyev found that the chemicals also slowed the loss of water through the leaves during hot afternoons. His results suggest that, at least for this shrub, the chemicals may help the plant survive drought as well as discourage grazers.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Tojiyev showed that grazing animals do not actually avoid the bitter leaves of desert shrubs.\",\"option_b\":\"Desert shrubs produce more bitter chemicals when fences protect them from grazing animals.\",\"option_c\":\"Tojiyev's findings suggest that the bitter chemicals in one desert shrub may do more than protect it from grazing animals.\",\"option_d\":\"Bitter chemicals are found only in plants that grow in hot, dry places.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text moves from the usual explanation (the chemicals defend against grazers) to Tojiyev's findings that the chemicals also slow water loss, so they \\\"may help the plant survive drought as well as discourage grazers.\\\" Choice C states this main idea.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Tojiyev does not reject the defense explanation; his results add a second function (\\\"as well as\\\"). Choice B distorts a detail: the shrub \\\"kept producing\\\" the chemicals on fenced plots, which does not mean it produced more. Choice D overgeneralizes; the text discusses desert shrubs but never claims that only desert plants make such chemicals.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn odatiy tushuntirishdan (kimyoviy moddalar o‘txo‘r hayvonlardan himoya qiladi) Tojiyevning yangi natijalariga o‘tadi: bu moddalar barglardan suv yo‘qolishini ham sekinlashtiradi, ya’ni o‘simlikka qurg‘oqchilikda omon qolishga \\\"ham\\\" yordam berishi mumkin. C javob shu asosiy g‘oyani ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Tojiyev himoya haqidagi tushuntirishni rad etmaydi, balki ikkinchi vazifani qo‘shadi. B tafsilotni buzib ko‘rsatadi: o‘simlik o‘ralgan maydonlarda ham moddalarni \\\"ishlab chiqarishda davom etgan\\\", bu esa ko‘proq ishlab chiqargan degani emas. D haddan tashqari umumlashtiradi: matnda bunday moddalar faqat cho‘l o‘simliklarida bo‘ladi deyilmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"main_idea\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Caravanserais, walled compounds built along trade routes such as the Silk Road, are usually described as inns where merchants and their animals could rest for the night. But at a caravanserai excavated in southern Uzbekistan, archaeologist Akmal Qodirov and his team uncovered more than sleeping rooms. Near the main gate, they found dozens of bronze weights made to standard sizes, fragments of balance scales, and clay seals of the kind used to close and mark bundles of goods. Qodirov argues that these finds show the caravanserai also served as a place where goods were weighed, sealed, and sold.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the text, what evidence does Qodirov use to support his argument about the caravanserai?\",\"option_a\":\"Objects used for weighing goods and for sealing bundles were found near the caravanserai's gate.\",\"option_b\":\"The caravanserai was larger than other inns along the Silk Road.\",\"option_c\":\"Records show that merchants paid to stay in the caravanserai's sleeping rooms.\",\"option_d\":\"The caravanserai was built along a major trade route where many merchants traveled.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Qodirov's argument is that the caravanserai was also a place where goods were weighed, sealed, and sold. His evidence is the set of finds near the main gate: standard bronze weights, fragments of scales, and clay seals for bundles of goods. Choice A describes this evidence.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because the text never compares the size of the caravanserai with other inns. Choice C is wrong because no payment records are mentioned, and paying for rooms would support the usual \\\"inn\\\" view, not Qodirov's argument. Choice D is true of caravanserais in general, but it is background information, not the evidence Qodirov cites for his specific claim.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qodirovning fikricha, karvonsaroy tovarlar tortiladigan, muhrlanadigan va sotiladigan joy ham bo‘lgan. Uning dalili asosiy darvoza yonidan topilgan buyumlar: standart bronza toshlar (tarozi toshlari), tarozi bo‘laklari va yuk bog‘lamlari uchun loy muhrlar. A javob shu dalilni tasvirlaydi.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: matnda karvonsaroy hajmi boshqa karvonsaroylar bilan solishtirilmagan. C noto‘g‘ri: to‘lov yozuvlari tilga olinmagan, qolaversa, xonalar uchun to‘lov odatiy \\\"mehmonxona\\\" qarashini tasdiqlaydi, Qodirovning fikrini emas. D umuman karvonsaroylar haqida to‘g‘ri ma’lumot, lekin bu fon ma’lumoti, Qodirov keltirgan dalil emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"detail\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"When a regional water utility redesigned its sign-up form, it made paperless billing the automatic choice: new customers would receive bills by email unless they checked a box requesting paper bills. Economist Lola Abdullayeva compared customers who signed up in the year before the change with those who signed up in the year after it. Before the change, 31 percent of new customers chose paperless billing; afterward, 78 percent kept it. Abdullayeva also found that customers who had paperless billing only because of the new default paid their bills on time just as often as customers who had actively chosen it.\",\"stem\":\"According to the text, what did Abdullayeva find about customers who received paperless billing by default?\",\"option_a\":\"They were more likely to pay their bills late than customers who received paper bills.\",\"option_b\":\"They paid their bills on time about as often as customers who had actively chosen paperless billing.\",\"option_c\":\"Most of them eventually switched back to paper bills.\",\"option_d\":\"They saved more money than customers who had requested paper bills.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The last sentence states that customers with paperless billing \\\"only because of the new default paid their bills on time just as often as customers who had actively chosen it.\\\" Choice B paraphrases this finding.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the text compares default customers with customers who chose paperless billing, not with paper-bill customers, and it reports no difference. Choice C contradicts the text: 78 percent of new customers kept paperless billing. Choice D is wrong because the text says nothing about how much money customers saved.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Oxirgi gapda aytilishicha, elektron hisobni faqat yangi standart tanlov tufayli olgan mijozlar uni o‘zi tanlagan mijozlar kabi hisoblarni o‘z vaqtida to‘lagan. B javob shu natijani o‘z so‘zlari bilan beradi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matnda bu mijozlar qog‘oz hisob oluvchilar bilan emas, elektron hisobni o‘zi tanlaganlar bilan solishtirilgan va farq topilmagan. C matnga zid: yangi mijozlarning 78 foizi elektron hisobni saqlab qolgan. D noto‘g‘ri: matnda mijozlar qancha pul tejagani haqida hech narsa yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"detail\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is adapted from a novel. Madame Orlova has taught the violin for forty years.\\n\\nThe boy's bowing was clumsy, and twice he missed the high note entirely. Madame Orlova, who had once dismissed students for less, found that she had stopped counting his errors halfway through the piece. Something in the way he leaned into the slow passage, as though he were listening for the melody rather than reciting it, had made her forget the pencil in her hand. When he finished, he looked up, already apologizing. She raised a hand to stop him. “Again,” she said, “and this time, do not apologize for the part that matters.”\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Madame Orlova decides that the boy is not talented enough to remain her student.\",\"option_b\":\"The boy is embarrassed because Madame Orlova points out his mistakes as he plays.\",\"option_c\":\"Madame Orlova regrets that she once dismissed students for making small errors.\",\"option_d\":\"Although the boy makes technical mistakes, Madame Orlova is so moved by the feeling in his playing that she wants him to keep it.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Orlova notices the boy's errors but stops counting them because of the way he \\\"leaned into the slow passage,\\\" listening for the melody. Her instruction not to \\\"apologize for the part that matters\\\" shows she values his expressiveness. Choice D states this main idea.\\n\\nChoice A is the opposite of what happens: she asks him to play again. Choice B is wrong because she does not point out mistakes; she even forgets her pencil. Choice C is a trap: the text says she \\\"had once dismissed students for less,\\\" but it never says she regrets doing so.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Orlova bolaning xatolarini sezadi, ammo uning sekin qismni \\\"kuyni tinglayotgandek\\\" chalishi tufayli xatolarni sanashni to‘xtatadi. \\\"Muhim qism uchun uzr so‘rama\\\" degan so‘zlari uning bolaning ifodaliligini qadrlashini ko‘rsatadi. D javob shu asosiy g‘oyani ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA voqealarga zid: Orlova undan yana chalishni so‘raydi. B noto‘g‘ri: u xatolarni ko‘rsatmaydi, hatto qo‘lidagi qalamni ham unutadi. C tuzoq: matnda u ilgari bundan kichikroq xatolar uchun ham o‘quvchilarni haydagani aytilgan, lekin bundan afsuslangani aytilmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"main_idea_literary\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Junipers in the mountains of Central Asia can live for many centuries, and each year they add a growth ring to their trunks. Researchers led by climatologist Sanjar Ismoilov collected wood samples from junipers growing at the very top of the forest, just below the elevation above which trees cannot survive. They chose these trees deliberately: at such heights, the short, cold summer is what mainly limits growth, so a tree's ring for a given year is wide when the summer was warm and narrow when it was cool. The team used the rings to reconstruct summer temperatures over the past eight centuries.\",\"stem\":\"According to the text, why did Ismoilov's team collect samples from junipers growing near the upper limit of the forest?\",\"option_a\":\"At that elevation, summer temperature is the main factor that controls how much the trees grow each year.\",\"option_b\":\"The junipers growing there are older than any other trees in the mountains.\",\"option_c\":\"Junipers growing at lower elevations do not add a new growth ring every year.\",\"option_d\":\"At that elevation, the trees grow wider rings in years with heavy rainfall.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text says the researchers \\\"chose these trees deliberately\\\" because at such heights \\\"the short, cold summer is what mainly limits growth,\\\" so ring width reflects summer temperature. Choice A states this reason.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because, although junipers can live for centuries, the text does not say the trees near the tree line are the oldest or give age as the reason. Choice C is wrong because the text says junipers add a ring each year and makes no exception for lower elevations. Choice D names the wrong factor: ring width at that elevation depends on temperature, not rainfall.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda tadqiqotchilar bu daraxtlarni \\\"ataylab tanlagani\\\" aytiladi, chunki bunday balandlikda o‘sishni asosan \\\"qisqa va sovuq yoz\\\" cheklaydi, ya’ni halqa qalinligi yozgi haroratni aks ettiradi. A javob shu sababni ifodalaydi.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: archalar asrlar davomida yashashi mumkin bo‘lsa-da, matnda o‘rmon chegarasidagi daraxtlar eng qari ekani ham, sabab yosh ekani ham aytilmagan. C noto‘g‘ri: matnga ko‘ra archalar har yili halqa qo‘shadi, pastroq joylar uchun istisno aytilmagan. D noto‘g‘ri omilni ko‘rsatadi: u balandlikda halqa qalinligi yog‘ingarchilikka emas, haroratga bog‘liq.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"detail\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Many economic historians have portrayed the craft guilds of medieval Europe as monopolies that mainly served their own members, limiting competition so that prices stayed high. Historian Ilse Brandt does not dispute that guilds restricted competition. But in the surviving records of a bakers' guild in a small German town, she found that guild officers regularly weighed loaves sold in the market and fined bakers whose bread was underweight or made with spoiled flour. Brandt argues that, at least in this case, the guild also protected buyers by enforcing standards of quality.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Brandt's research shows that medieval guilds did not actually limit competition among their members.\",\"option_b\":\"Medieval bakers in Germany were often fined for selling bread at high prices.\",\"option_c\":\"Brandt's study suggests that a medieval guild, besides limiting competition, may also have protected buyers by enforcing quality standards.\",\"option_d\":\"Most economic historians now agree that medieval guilds benefited buyers more than their own members.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text presents the common view of guilds as self-serving monopolies, notes that Brandt \\\"does not dispute\\\" that they restricted competition, and then gives her finding that one guild \\\"also protected buyers by enforcing standards of quality.\\\" Choice C states this main idea.\\n\\nChoice A contradicts the text, since Brandt accepts that guilds restricted competition. Choice B misreads the detail: bakers were fined for underweight bread or spoiled flour, not for high prices. Choice D overstates the text, which reports one historian's argument about one guild, not a new consensus.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda gildiyalar haqidagi keng tarqalgan qarash (ular faqat o‘z a’zolariga xizmat qiluvchi monopoliyalar bo‘lgan) keltiriladi, Brandt ular raqobatni cheklaganini \\\"inkor etmasligi\\\" aytiladi, so‘ng uning bitta gildiya sifat me’yorlarini nazorat qilib, xaridorlarni \\\"ham\\\" himoya qilgani haqidagi xulosasi beriladi. C javob shu asosiy g‘oyani ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA matnga zid: Brandt gildiyalar raqobatni cheklaganini tan oladi. B tafsilotni noto‘g‘ri o‘qiydi: novvoylar qimmat narx uchun emas, vazni kam non yoki buzilgan un uchun jarimaga tortilgan. D matndagi fikrni oshirib yuboradi: unda bitta tarixchining bitta gildiya haqidagi fikri keltirilgan, yangi umumiy fikr emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"main_idea\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In greenhouse experiments, plants supplied with extra carbon dioxide (CO₂) often grow much faster, since CO₂ is the raw material for photosynthesis. Such results led some scientists to predict that rising levels of CO₂ in the atmosphere would substantially speed the growth of forests worldwide. Ecologist Laylo Hamroyeva suspected that this prediction overlooked an important condition. She grew oak seedlings in open-air plots where extra CO₂ was piped in; some plots had soil rich in nitrogen, a nutrient plants need to build new tissue, and others had nitrogen-poor soil. Extra CO₂ increased growth by nearly a third in the nitrogen-rich soil but produced almost no gain in the nitrogen-poor soil.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Hamroyeva found that extra CO₂ slows the growth of oak seedlings planted in nitrogen-poor soil.\",\"option_b\":\"Hamroyeva's study shows that greenhouse experiments on CO₂ are unreliable and should no longer be conducted.\",\"option_c\":\"Hamroyeva found that nitrogen is more important than CO₂ for the growth of all plants.\",\"option_d\":\"Hamroyeva's results suggest that the growth benefit of extra CO₂ depends on how much nitrogen the soil supplies, a condition the prediction about forests did not account for.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text presents a prediction based on greenhouse results and then Hamroyeva's test of an \\\"overlooked\\\" condition: extra CO₂ boosted growth in nitrogen-rich soil but gave \\\"almost no gain\\\" in nitrogen-poor soil. Choice D states this main idea.\\n\\nChoice A overstates the result: \\\"almost no gain\\\" means growth did not increase much, not that it slowed. Choice B is too extreme; the text questions a prediction drawn from greenhouse results but never says such experiments should stop. Choice C overgeneralizes from oak seedlings to \\\"all plants\\\" and makes a ranking the study did not test.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda issiqxona tajribalariga asoslangan bashorat keltiriladi, so‘ng Hamroyeva \\\"e’tibordan chetda qolgan\\\" shartni tekshiradi: qo‘shimcha CO₂ azotga boy tuproqda o‘sishni oshirgan, azoti kam tuproqda esa \\\"deyarli hech qanday o‘sish bermagan\\\". D javob shu asosiy g‘oyani ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA natijani oshirib yuboradi: \\\"deyarli o‘sish yo‘q\\\" o‘sish sekinlashdi degani emas. B haddan tashqari keskin: matn issiqxona natijalaridan chiqarilgan bashoratni shubha ostiga oladi, lekin bunday tajribalarni to‘xtatish kerak demaydi. C eman ko‘chatlaridan \\\"barcha o‘simliklar\\\"ga umumlashtiradi va tadqiqotda tekshirilmagan taqqoslashni qiladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"main_idea\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is adapted from a novel. Shahnoza is translating a collection of poems from Uzbek into English.\\n\\nFor three days the word sat on her desk like a stone she could not lift. In the poem it meant the particular hush of a courtyard after guests have gone: not silence exactly, but the warmth that silence keeps for a while. English offered her “quiet,” “stillness,” “calm,” each of them honest and each of them wrong. On the fourth morning she wrote “stillness” and drew a line beneath it, not in triumph but in something closer to apology. The poem in English would be a smaller house than the poem in Uzbek, she decided; her task was to make sure that whoever entered it still felt the guests had only just left.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Shahnoza finally finds an English word that captures the full meaning of the Uzbek word.\",\"option_b\":\"Shahnoza accepts that her English version will lose some of the original's meaning, but she aims to preserve the feeling at its core.\",\"option_c\":\"Shahnoza concludes that English is a less expressive language than Uzbek.\",\"option_d\":\"Shahnoza regrets agreeing to translate poetry and wishes she were translating prose instead.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Shahnoza judges every English option \\\"wrong,\\\" chooses \\\"stillness\\\" with \\\"something closer to apology,\\\" and decides that although the English poem \\\"would be a smaller house,\\\" her task is to keep the feeling that \\\"the guests had only just left.\\\" Choice B states this main idea.\\n\\nChoice A contradicts the text: she settles on a word she knows is imperfect. Choice C overgeneralizes from one word and one poem to the whole language; the \\\"smaller house\\\" image refers to this translation, not to English itself. Choice D is wrong because the text never mentions prose or any regret about taking on the work.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Shahnoza inglizcha variantlarning har birini \\\"noto‘g‘ri\\\" deb biladi, \\\"stillness\\\" so‘zini g‘alaba bilan emas, \\\"uzr so‘rashga yaqinroq\\\" his bilan tanlaydi va inglizcha she’r \\\"kichikroq uy\\\" bo‘lishini tan olgan holda, o‘quvchi \\\"mehmonlar endigina ketgan\\\"dek his qilishini saqlashni o‘z vazifasi deb biladi. B javob shu asosiy g‘oyani ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA matnga zid: u nomukammal ekanini bilgan so‘zni tanlaydi. C bitta so‘z va bitta she’rdan butun til haqida umumiy xulosa chiqaradi; \\\"kichikroq uy\\\" obrazi ingliz tiliga emas, aynan shu tarjimaga tegishli. D noto‘g‘ri: matnda nasr ham, bu ishni olganidan afsuslanish ham tilga olinmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"main_idea_literary\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Development economists have often attributed farmers' slow adoption of new, higher-yielding crop varieties to risk aversion: a family that depends on a single harvest may avoid an unfamiliar seed even if it promises larger average yields. Economist Bahrom Nazarov tested this explanation in 40 villages. Before a new drought-tolerant millet was introduced, he measured each farmer's willingness to take financial risks using a series of small betting games. Two years later, risk tolerance barely predicted which farmers had planted the new millet. What predicted adoption strongly was whether a farmer's close neighbors had grown it in the first year, a pattern that held even among the farmers Nazarov had classified as the most risk-averse.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Nazarov's findings suggest that seeing neighbors grow the new millet, more than farmers' attitudes toward risk, shaped whether farmers planted it.\",\"option_b\":\"Nazarov found that the farmers who were most risk-averse were the most likely to plant the new millet.\",\"option_c\":\"Nazarov found that the new millet produced lower yields than expected, so most farmers decided not to plant it.\",\"option_d\":\"Nazarov's betting games proved to be accurate predictors of which farmers would plant the new millet.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text presents the risk-aversion explanation and then Nazarov's test of it: risk tolerance \\\"barely predicted\\\" adoption, while neighbors' first-year planting predicted it \\\"strongly,\\\" even for the most risk-averse farmers. Choice A states this main idea.\\n\\nChoice B distorts the last detail: the neighbor pattern held among risk-averse farmers, but the text never says they adopted more than other farmers. Choice C is wrong because the text reports nothing about the millet's actual yields. Choice D contradicts the finding that risk tolerance, measured by the betting games, \\\"barely predicted\\\" adoption.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda avval xavfdan qochish haqidagi tushuntirish beriladi, so‘ng Nazarovning uni tekshirgani aytiladi: xavfga moyillik ekishni \\\"deyarli bashorat qilmagan\\\", qo‘shnilarning birinchi yili ekkani esa uni \\\"kuchli\\\" bashorat qilgan, hatto eng ehtiyotkor fermerlar orasida ham. A javob shu asosiy g‘oyani ifodalaydi.\\n\\nB oxirgi tafsilotni buzadi: qo‘shnilar bilan bog‘liq qonuniyat ehtiyotkor fermerlarda ham kuzatilgan, lekin ular boshqalardan ko‘proq ekkani aytilmagan. C noto‘g‘ri: matnda tariqning haqiqiy hosildorligi haqida ma’lumot yo‘q. D natijaga zid: tikish o‘yinlari orqali o‘lchangan xavfga moyillik ekishni \\\"deyarli bashorat qilmagan\\\".\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"main_idea\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Modern viewers often treat the large world maps of medieval Europe, which place Jerusalem at the center and fill distant lands with marvelous creatures, as failed attempts at geography. Historian Corinne Mallet argues that this judgment asks the maps to do a job they were never meant to do. Such maps, she notes, were usually displayed in cathedrals or bound into books of history rather than carried by travelers; they gathered biblical events, ancient legends, and the known world into a single picture of human history. Mallet adds that the same era produced practical sailing charts that traced coastlines with impressive accuracy, which shows that precise mapping was possible when precision was the goal.\",\"stem\":\"According to the text, why does Mallet believe it is a mistake to judge medieval world maps as failed attempts at geography?\",\"option_a\":\"Medieval mapmakers had no reliable information about the shapes of distant lands.\",\"option_b\":\"The maps were more accurate than modern viewers realize, especially in their placement of Jerusalem.\",\"option_c\":\"The maps were made to present a combined picture of history and belief rather than to guide travelers.\",\"option_d\":\"The maps were copied from practical sailing charts, which traced coastlines accurately.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Mallet argues that the maps were never meant to be practical geography: they were displayed in cathedrals or bound into history books, not carried by travelers, and they combined \\\"biblical events, ancient legends, and the known world into a single picture of human history.\\\" Choice C states her reason.\\n\\nChoice A sounds plausible but runs against Mallet's point: she cites accurate sailing charts to show that precise mapping was possible, so the maps' style was a choice, not a lack of information. Choice B is wrong because Mallet does not claim the maps were accurate; she argues accuracy was not their purpose. Choice D distorts the text: the sailing charts are mentioned as a contrast, not as the source of the world maps.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Mallet fikricha, bu xaritalar amaliy geografiya uchun mo‘ljallanmagan: ular sayohatchilar olib yuradigan narsa emas, soborlarda osilgan yoki tarix kitoblariga tikilgan bo‘lib, \\\"Injil voqealari, qadimiy afsonalar va ma’lum dunyoni\\\" insoniyat tarixining yagona manzarasiga birlashtirgan. C javob uning sababini ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA ishonarli tuyuladi, lekin Mallet fikriga zid: u aniq dengiz xaritalarini aniq xaritachilik mumkin bo‘lganini ko‘rsatish uchun keltiradi, demak xaritalar uslubi bilim yetishmasligi emas, tanlov natijasi. B noto‘g‘ri: Mallet xaritalar aniq bo‘lgan demaydi, aniqlik ularning maqsadi bo‘lmagan deydi. D matnni buzadi: dengiz xaritalari dunyo xaritalarining manbai sifatida emas, qarama-qarshi misol sifatida tilga olinadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"detail\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"circles\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In the $xy$-plane, the graph of $(x + 3)^2 + (y - 7)^2 = 25$ is a circle. What are the coordinates of the center of the circle, and what is its radius?\",\"option_a\":\"Center $(-3, 7)$, radius 5\",\"option_b\":\"Center $(3, -7)$, radius 5\",\"option_c\":\"Center $(-3, 7)$, radius 25\",\"option_d\":\"Center $(3, -7)$, radius 25\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"A circle with center $(h, k)$ and radius $r$ has the equation $(x - h)^2 + (y - k)^2 = r^2$. Here $x + 3 = x - (-3)$ and $y - 7$, so the center is $(-3, 7)$, and $r^2 = 25$ gives $r = 5$. Choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoices B and D take the signs inside the parentheses as the coordinates of the center. Choices C and D give $r^2 = 25$ as the radius.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Markazi $(h, k)$ va radiusi $r$ bo‘lgan aylana tenglamasi $(x - h)^2 + (y - k)^2 = r^2$. Bu yerda $x + 3 = x - (-3)$ va $y - 7$, demak markaz $(-3, 7)$, $r^2 = 25$ dan esa $r = 5$. To‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\nB va D javoblarda qavs ichidagi ishoralar markaz koordinatalari sifatida olingan. C va D javoblarda radius sifatida $r^2 = 25$ berilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"equation_center_radius\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"circles\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A circle has a circumference of $18\\\\pi$ centimeters. The area of the circle is $k\\\\pi$ square centimeters. What is the value of $k$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"81\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The circumference is $2\\\\pi r = 18\\\\pi$, so $r = 9$. The area is $\\\\pi r^2 = 81\\\\pi$, so $k = 81$. If you got 324, you used the diameter 18 as the radius.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Aylana uzunligi $2\\\\pi r = 18\\\\pi$, demak $r = 9$. Doira yuzi $\\\\pi r^2 = 81\\\\pi$, shuning uchun $k = 81$. Agar 324 chiqqan bo‘lsa, siz radius o‘rniga diametr 18 ni olgansiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"circumference_area\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"circles\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"An angle measures $150^\\\\circ$. What is the measure of the angle in radians?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{5\\\\pi}{12}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{5\\\\pi}{6}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{5\\\\pi}{3}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{6\\\\pi}{5}$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Since $180^\\\\circ = \\\\pi$ radians, multiply by $\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{180}$: $150 \\\\cdot \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{180} = \\\\frac{5\\\\pi}{6}$. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A divides by 360 instead of 180, and choice C is twice the correct value. Choice D inverts the fraction $\\\\frac{150}{180}$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$180^\\\\circ = \\\\pi$ radian bo‘lgani uchun $\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{180}$ ga ko‘paytiramiz: $150 \\\\cdot \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{180} = \\\\frac{5\\\\pi}{6}$. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA javobda 180 o‘rniga 360 ga bo‘lingan, C esa to‘g‘ri qiymatning ikki baravari. D javobda $\\\\frac{150}{180}$ kasr teskari olingan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"radians\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"circles\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Point $P$ lies on a circle with center $O$. Line $\\\\ell$ is tangent to the circle at point $P$, and point $Q$ lies on line $\\\\ell$. The measure of angle $POQ$ is $58^\\\\circ$. What is the measure of angle $OQP$?\",\"option_a\":\"$32^\\\\circ$\",\"option_b\":\"$58^\\\\circ$\",\"option_c\":\"$90^\\\\circ$\",\"option_d\":\"$122^\\\\circ$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"A tangent line is perpendicular to the radius drawn to the point of tangency, so angle $OPQ$ is $90^\\\\circ$. In triangle $OPQ$, angle $OQP = 180^\\\\circ - 90^\\\\circ - 58^\\\\circ = 32^\\\\circ$. Choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoice B repeats angle $POQ$. Choice C is angle $OPQ$, not angle $OQP$. Choice D is $180^\\\\circ - 58^\\\\circ$, which ignores the right angle.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Urinma urinish nuqtasiga o‘tkazilgan radiusga perpendikulyar, shuning uchun $OPQ$ burchak $90^\\\\circ$. $OPQ$ uchburchakda $OQP$ burchak $= 180^\\\\circ - 90^\\\\circ - 58^\\\\circ = 32^\\\\circ$. To‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\nB javob $POQ$ burchakni takrorlaydi. C — bu $OQP$ emas, $OPQ$ burchak. D — bu $180^\\\\circ - 58^\\\\circ$; to‘g‘ri burchak hisobga olinmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"tangent\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"circles\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A circle has a radius of 9 inches. A sector of the circle has a central angle of $40^\\\\circ$. The area of the sector is $k\\\\pi$ square inches. What is the value of $k$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"9\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The sector is $\\\\frac{40}{360} = \\\\frac{1}{9}$ of the circle. The area of the whole circle is $\\\\pi(9)^2 = 81\\\\pi$, so the sector's area is $\\\\frac{1}{9} \\\\cdot 81\\\\pi = 9\\\\pi$, and $k = 9$. If you got 2, you found the arc length, $\\\\frac{1}{9} \\\\cdot 18\\\\pi = 2\\\\pi$, instead of the area.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Sektor doiraning $\\\\frac{40}{360} = \\\\frac{1}{9}$ qismi. Butun doira yuzi $\\\\pi(9)^2 = 81\\\\pi$, demak sektor yuzi $\\\\frac{1}{9} \\\\cdot 81\\\\pi = 9\\\\pi$ va $k = 9$. Agar 2 chiqqan bo‘lsa, siz yuza o‘rniga yoy uzunligini, $\\\\frac{1}{9} \\\\cdot 18\\\\pi = 2\\\\pi$ ni topgansiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"sector_area\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"circles\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In the $xy$-plane, a circle has its center at $(2, -1)$ and passes through the point $(5, 3)$. Which of the following is an equation of the circle?\",\"option_a\":\"$(x - 2)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 5$\",\"option_b\":\"$(x - 2)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 25$\",\"option_c\":\"$(x + 2)^2 + (y - 1)^2 = 25$\",\"option_d\":\"$(x - 5)^2 + (y - 3)^2 = 25$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The radius is the distance from the center to $(5, 3)$: $r^2 = (5 - 2)^2 + (3 - (-1))^2 = 9 + 16 = 25$. With center $(2, -1)$, the equation is $(x - 2)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 25$. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A puts the radius 5 where $r^2$ belongs. Choice C has the signs of the center reversed. Choice D uses the point on the circle as the center.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Radius — markazdan $(5, 3)$ nuqtagacha masofa: $r^2 = (5 - 2)^2 + (3 - (-1))^2 = 9 + 16 = 25$. Markaz $(2, -1)$ bo‘lgani uchun tenglama $(x - 2)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 25$. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA javobda $r^2$ o‘rniga radius 5 yozilgan. C javobda markaz ishoralari teskari. D javobda aylanadagi nuqta markaz deb olingan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"equation_from_point\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"circles\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In the $xy$-plane, the graph of $x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 14y + 42 = 0$ is a circle. What is the radius of the circle?\",\"option_a\":\"4\",\"option_b\":\"8\",\"option_c\":\"16\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\sqrt{42}$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Complete the square: $(x^2 + 6x + 9) + (y^2 - 14y + 49) = -42 + 9 + 49$, so $(x + 3)^2 + (y - 7)^2 = 16$. Then $r^2 = 16$ and $r = 4$. Choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoice B is the diameter. Choice C is $r^2$. Choice D uses the constant term 42 as $r^2$ without completing the square.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"To‘la kvadratga keltiramiz: $(x^2 + 6x + 9) + (y^2 - 14y + 49) = -42 + 9 + 49$, demak $(x + 3)^2 + (y - 7)^2 = 16$. U holda $r^2 = 16$ va $r = 4$. To‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\nB — diametr. C — bu $r^2$. D javobda to‘la kvadratga keltirmasdan ozod had 42 $r^2$ deb olingan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"complete_square\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"circles\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A circle has a radius of 8 meters. An arc of the circle is intercepted by a central angle that measures $\\\\frac{3\\\\pi}{4}$ radians. The length of the arc is $k\\\\pi$ meters. What is the value of $k$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"6\"],\"explanation_en\":\"For a central angle $\\\\theta$ in radians, the arc length is $s = r\\\\theta = 8 \\\\cdot \\\\frac{3\\\\pi}{4} = 6\\\\pi$ meters, so $k = 6$. If you got 24, you found the sector area, $\\\\frac{1}{2}r^2\\\\theta = 24\\\\pi$, instead of the arc length.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Radianda berilgan $\\\\theta$ markaziy burchak uchun yoy uzunligi $s = r\\\\theta = 8 \\\\cdot \\\\frac{3\\\\pi}{4} = 6\\\\pi$ metr, demak $k = 6$. Agar 24 chiqqan bo‘lsa, siz yoy uzunligi o‘rniga sektor yuzini, $\\\\frac{1}{2}r^2\\\\theta = 24\\\\pi$ ni topgansiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"arc_length_radians\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"circles\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Points $A$, $B$, and $C$ lie on a circle with center $O$. Point $C$ lies on the major arc $AB$ (the longer of the two arcs from $A$ to $B$). The measure of central angle $AOB$ is $130^\\\\circ$. What is the measure of angle $ACB$?\",\"option_a\":\"$50^\\\\circ$\",\"option_b\":\"$65^\\\\circ$\",\"option_c\":\"$115^\\\\circ$\",\"option_d\":\"$130^\\\\circ$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Inscribed angle $ACB$ intercepts minor arc $AB$, which has the same measure as central angle $AOB$, $130^\\\\circ$. An inscribed angle is half of the arc it intercepts, so angle $ACB = \\\\frac{130^\\\\circ}{2} = 65^\\\\circ$. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A is $180^\\\\circ - 130^\\\\circ$. Choice C, $115^\\\\circ$, would be the inscribed angle if $C$ were on the minor arc. Choice D treats the inscribed angle as equal to the central angle.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$ACB$ ichki chizilgan burchak kichik $AB$ yoyga tiraladi; bu yoy $AOB$ markaziy burchak bilan bir xil, ya’ni $130^\\\\circ$. Ichki chizilgan burchak o‘zi tiralgan yoyning yarmiga teng, shuning uchun $ACB = \\\\frac{130^\\\\circ}{2} = 65^\\\\circ$. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA — bu $180^\\\\circ - 130^\\\\circ$. C ($115^\\\\circ$) — agar $C$ kichik yoyda yotganda hosil bo‘ladigan burchak. D javobda ichki chizilgan burchak markaziy burchakka teng deb olingan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"inscribed_angle\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"circles\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A circle with center $O$ has a radius of 17 centimeters. Chord $\\\\overline{AB}$ of the circle has a length of 30 centimeters. What is the distance, in centimeters, from $O$ to $\\\\overline{AB}$?\",\"option_a\":\"2\",\"option_b\":\"8\",\"option_c\":\"15\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\sqrt{514}$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The perpendicular segment from $O$ to the chord bisects the chord, forming a right triangle with hypotenuse $OA = 17$ and one leg $\\\\frac{30}{2} = 15$. The distance $d$ satisfies $d^2 + 15^2 = 17^2$, so $d^2 = 289 - 225 = 64$ and $d = 8$. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A subtracts the lengths, $17 - 15$, instead of using the Pythagorean theorem. Choice C is half of the chord. Choice D adds the squares instead of subtracting them.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$O$ dan vatarga tushirilgan perpendikulyar vatarni teng ikkiga bo‘ladi va gipotenuzasi $OA = 17$, bir kateti $\\\\frac{30}{2} = 15$ bo‘lgan to‘g‘ri burchakli uchburchak hosil bo‘ladi. Masofa $d$ uchun $d^2 + 15^2 = 17^2$, demak $d^2 = 289 - 225 = 64$ va $d = 8$. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA javobda Pifagor teoremasi o‘rniga uzunliklar ayirilgan: $17 - 15$. C — vatarning yarmi. D javobda kvadratlar ayirilish o‘rniga qo‘shilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"chord_distance\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"circles\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"In the $xy$-plane, the point $(a, 5)$ lies on the circle $(x - 1)^2 + (y - 2)^2 = 25$, where $a$ is a positive constant greater than 1. What is the value of $a$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"5\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Substitute $y = 5$: $(a - 1)^2 + (5 - 2)^2 = 25$, so $(a - 1)^2 = 16$ and $a - 1 = 4$ or $a - 1 = -4$. That gives $a = 5$ or $a = -3$. Since $a > 1$, $a = 5$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$y = 5$ ni qo‘yamiz: $(a - 1)^2 + (5 - 2)^2 = 25$, demak $(a - 1)^2 = 16$, ya’ni $a - 1 = 4$ yoki $a - 1 = -4$. Bundan $a = 5$ yoki $a = -3$. $a > 1$ bo‘lgani uchun $a = 5$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"point_on_circle\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"circles\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Segment $\\\\overline{AC}$ is a diameter of a circle with center $O$, and point $B$ lies on the circle but is not $A$ or $C$. Central angle $AOB$ measures $\\\\frac{2\\\\pi}{5}$ radians. What is the measure, in radians, of angle $BOC$?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{10}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{2\\\\pi}{5}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{3\\\\pi}{5}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{8\\\\pi}{5}$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Since $\\\\overline{AC}$ is a diameter, $A$, $O$, and $C$ lie on a line, so angles $AOB$ and $BOC$ together form a straight angle of $\\\\pi$ radians. Angle $BOC = \\\\pi - \\\\frac{2\\\\pi}{5} = \\\\frac{3\\\\pi}{5}$. Choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A is $\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2} - \\\\frac{2\\\\pi}{5}$; it treats the angles as complementary. Choice B assumes the two angles are equal. Choice D is $2\\\\pi - \\\\frac{2\\\\pi}{5}$, the rest of a full turn, not of a straight angle.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$\\\\overline{AC}$ diametr bo‘lgani uchun $A$, $O$ va $C$ bir to‘g‘ri chiziqda yotadi, demak $AOB$ va $BOC$ burchaklar birgalikda $\\\\pi$ radianlik yoyiq burchak hosil qiladi. $BOC = \\\\pi - \\\\frac{2\\\\pi}{5} = \\\\frac{3\\\\pi}{5}$. To‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA — bu $\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2} - \\\\frac{2\\\\pi}{5}$; burchaklar yig‘indisi to‘g‘ri burchak deb olingan. B javob ikki burchakni teng deb hisoblaydi. D — bu $2\\\\pi - \\\\frac{2\\\\pi}{5}$, ya’ni yoyiq burchakning emas, to‘liq aylanishning qolgan qismi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"radians_supplementary\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"circles\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Point $P$ lies outside a circle with center $O$ and radius 9 centimeters. Lines $PA$ and $PB$ are tangent to the circle at points $A$ and $B$, respectively, and the measure of angle $APB$ is $50^\\\\circ$. What is the length, in centimeters, of minor arc $AB$?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{5\\\\pi}{2}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{13\\\\pi}{4}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{13\\\\pi}{2}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{23\\\\pi}{2}$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Each radius meets its tangent at a right angle, so in quadrilateral $OAPB$ the angles at $A$ and $B$ are $90^\\\\circ$. The angle sum is $360^\\\\circ$, so angle $AOB = 360^\\\\circ - 90^\\\\circ - 90^\\\\circ - 50^\\\\circ = 130^\\\\circ$. The minor arc is $\\\\frac{130}{360} \\\\cdot 2\\\\pi(9) = \\\\frac{13\\\\pi}{2}$ centimeters. Choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A uses $50^\\\\circ$ as the central angle. Choice B uses $\\\\pi r$ instead of $2\\\\pi r$ for the circumference. Choice D is the major arc, which corresponds to $230^\\\\circ$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Har bir radius o‘z urinmasi bilan to‘g‘ri burchak hosil qiladi, shuning uchun $OAPB$ to‘rtburchakda $A$ va $B$ dagi burchaklar $90^\\\\circ$. Burchaklar yig‘indisi $360^\\\\circ$, demak $AOB = 360^\\\\circ - 90^\\\\circ - 90^\\\\circ - 50^\\\\circ = 130^\\\\circ$. Kichik yoy $\\\\frac{130}{360} \\\\cdot 2\\\\pi(9) = \\\\frac{13\\\\pi}{2}$ santimetr. To‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA javobda markaziy burchak sifatida $50^\\\\circ$ olingan. B javobda aylana uzunligi uchun $2\\\\pi r$ o‘rniga $\\\\pi r$ ishlatilgan. D — katta yoy, u $230^\\\\circ$ ga mos keladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"tangents_arc\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"circles\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"In the $xy$-plane, a circle has its center at $(h, h)$, where $h$ is a constant, and the circle passes through the points $(0, 4)$ and $(6, 0)$. If the radius of the circle is $r$, what is the value of $r^2$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"26\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Both points are the same distance $r$ from the center: $(0 - h)^2 + (4 - h)^2 = (6 - h)^2 + (0 - h)^2$. The $h^2$ terms cancel, leaving $(4 - h)^2 = (6 - h)^2$, so $16 - 8h = 36 - 12h$ and $h = 5$. Then $r^2 = (0 - 5)^2 + (4 - 5)^2 = 25 + 1 = 26$. Check: $(6 - 5)^2 + (0 - 5)^2 = 26$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkala nuqta markazdan bir xil $r$ masofada: $(0 - h)^2 + (4 - h)^2 = (6 - h)^2 + (0 - h)^2$. $h^2$ hadlar qisqarib, $(4 - h)^2 = (6 - h)^2$ qoladi, demak $16 - 8h = 36 - 12h$ va $h = 5$. U holda $r^2 = (0 - 5)^2 + (4 - 5)^2 = 25 + 1 = 26$. Tekshiruv: $(6 - 5)^2 + (0 - 5)^2 = 26$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"center_from_points\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"circles\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A sector of a circle has an arc length of $4\\\\pi$ centimeters and an area of $10\\\\pi$ square centimeters. What is the measure, in radians, of the central angle of the sector?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{4\\\\pi}{25}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{2\\\\pi}{5}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{4\\\\pi}{5}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{8\\\\pi}{5}$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"For radius $r$ and central angle $\\\\theta$ in radians, the arc length is $s = r\\\\theta$ and the area is $A = \\\\frac{1}{2}r^2\\\\theta = \\\\frac{1}{2}rs$. So $10\\\\pi = \\\\frac{1}{2}r(4\\\\pi)$, which gives $r = 5$. Then $\\\\theta = \\\\frac{s}{r} = \\\\frac{4\\\\pi}{5}$. Choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A divides the arc length by $r^2$ instead of $r$. Choice B divides by the diameter 10. Choice D comes from using $A = rs$ without the $\\\\frac{1}{2}$, which gives $r = 2.5$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Radius $r$ va radiandagi markaziy burchak $\\\\theta$ uchun yoy uzunligi $s = r\\\\theta$, yuza esa $A = \\\\frac{1}{2}r^2\\\\theta = \\\\frac{1}{2}rs$. Demak $10\\\\pi = \\\\frac{1}{2}r(4\\\\pi)$, bundan $r = 5$. U holda $\\\\theta = \\\\frac{s}{r} = \\\\frac{4\\\\pi}{5}$. To‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA javobda yoy uzunligi $r$ ga emas, $r^2$ ga bo‘lingan. B javobda diametr 10 ga bo‘lingan. D javob $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ siz $A = rs$ formulasidan kelib chiqadi, u $r = 2.5$ ni beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"sector_arc_radians\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"circles\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"In the $xy$-plane, the graph of $x^2 + y^2 - 8x + 4y + c = 0$, where $c$ is a constant, is a circle that is tangent to the $x$-axis. What is the value of $c$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"16\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Complete the square: $(x - 4)^2 + (y + 2)^2 = 16 + 4 - c = 20 - c$. The center is $(4, -2)$. A circle tangent to the $x$-axis has a radius equal to the distance from its center to the $x$-axis, which is $|-2| = 2$. So $20 - c = 2^2 = 4$, and $c = 16$. If you got 18, you set $20 - c$ equal to the radius instead of $r^2$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"To‘la kvadratga keltiramiz: $(x - 4)^2 + (y + 2)^2 = 16 + 4 - c = 20 - c$. Markaz $(4, -2)$. $x$ o‘qiga urinadigan aylananing radiusi markazdan $x$ o‘qigacha bo‘lgan masofaga teng, ya’ni $|-2| = 2$. Demak $20 - c = 2^2 = 4$ va $c = 16$. Agar 18 chiqqan bo‘lsa, siz $20 - c$ ni $r^2$ ga emas, radiusga tenglagansiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"tangent_axis_parameter\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"For a science fair project, Sabina Rahimova placed identical ice cubes on plates made of four different materials and timed how long each cube took to melt completely at room temperature. She found that the ice melted fastest on the ______\\n\\n**Time for an ice cube to melt on plates of different materials**\\n\\n| Plate material | Melting time (minutes) |\\n| --- | ---: |\\n| Aluminum | 6 |\\n| Glass | 14 |\\n| Wood | 23 |\\n| Plastic | 19 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"wood plate, where the cube took 23 minutes to melt.\",\"option_b\":\"plastic plate, where the cube took 19 minutes to melt.\",\"option_c\":\"aluminum plate, where the cube took only 6 minutes to melt.\",\"option_d\":\"glass plate, where the cube took 14 minutes to melt.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The statement asks for the plate on which the ice melted fastest, that is, in the shortest time. The aluminum plate had the shortest melting time, 6 minutes, so choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A names the wood plate, which had the longest time (23 minutes), so the ice melted slowest there. Choices B and D give accurate times for the plastic and glass plates, but both times are longer than 6 minutes.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapda muz eng tez, ya’ni eng qisqa vaqtda erigan likop haqida so‘raladi. Eng qisqa vaqt — 6 daqiqa — alyuminiy likopga tegishli, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA yog‘och likopni ko‘rsatadi, unda vaqt eng uzun (23 daqiqa), ya’ni muz u yerda eng sekin erigan. B va D plastik va shisha likoplar uchun to‘g‘ri vaqtni beradi, lekin ikkalasi ham 6 daqiqadan uzun.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"complete_statement\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Librarian Shoira Qosimova asked members of a city library which book format they prefer. She found that among members aged 60 and older, the most popular format was ______\\n\\n**Preferred book format of library members, by age group (%)**\\n\\n| Age group | Printed books | E-books | Audiobooks |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| 18–29 | 38 | 27 | 35 |\\n| 30–44 | 45 | 30 | 25 |\\n| 45–59 | 52 | 21 | 27 |\\n| 60 and older | 61 | 9 | 30 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"audiobooks, which 30 percent of members in that age group preferred.\",\"option_b\":\"printed books, which 61 percent of members in that age group preferred.\",\"option_c\":\"e-books, which 9 percent of members in that age group preferred.\",\"option_d\":\"printed books, which 38 percent of members in that age group preferred.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The statement is about members aged 60 and older. In that row, printed books have the highest percentage, 61 percent, so choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A gives the correct figure for audiobooks (30 percent), but that is less than 61 percent. Choice C gives the figure for e-books, the least popular format in that group. Choice D names the right format but takes the figure from the wrong row: 38 percent is the share for members aged 18–29.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gap 60 va undan katta yoshdagi a’zolar haqida. Shu qatorda eng yuqori ko‘rsatkich bosma kitoblarga tegishli — 61 foiz, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA audiokitoblar uchun to‘g‘ri raqamni (30 foiz) beradi, lekin u 61 foizdan kam. C shu guruhdagi eng kam mashhur format — elektron kitoblar ko‘rsatkichini beradi. D formatni to‘g‘ri aytadi, lekin raqamni noto‘g‘ri qatordan oladi: 38 foiz 18–29 yoshdagilarga tegishli.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"complete_statement\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Museum educator Dilnoza Karimova recorded the number of visitors to a history museum in Bukhara on each day of one week in October. She noted that weekend attendance was much higher than attendance early in the week; for example, ______\\n\\n**Visitors to a history museum during one week**\\n\\n| Day | Number of visitors |\\n| --- | ---: |\\n| Monday | 410 |\\n| Tuesday | 455 |\\n| Wednesday | 520 |\\n| Thursday | 480 |\\n| Friday | 690 |\\n| Saturday | 1,130 |\\n| Sunday | 1,050 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"1,130 people visited the museum on Saturday, more than twice the 410 people who visited on Monday.\",\"option_b\":\"1,050 people visited the museum on Sunday, compared with 690 on Friday.\",\"option_c\":\"410 people visited the museum on Monday, the fewest of any day that week.\",\"option_d\":\"520 people visited the museum on Wednesday, compared with 455 on Tuesday.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The statement contrasts the weekend with the early part of the week. Choice A compares a weekend day (Saturday, 1,130 visitors) with an early weekday (Monday, 410 visitors) and accurately notes that Saturday's attendance was more than twice Monday's (2 × 410 = 820).\\n\\nChoice B compares Sunday with Friday, which is the end of the week, not the early part. Choice C gives only Monday's figure, with no weekend comparison. Choice D compares two weekdays and does not involve the weekend.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapda dam olish kunlari hafta boshidagi kunlar bilan solishtiriladi. A javob dam olish kunini (shanba, 1,130 tashrifchi) hafta boshidagi kun (dushanba, 410 tashrifchi) bilan solishtiradi va shanbadagi tashrifchilar dushanbadagidan ikki baravardan ko‘p ekanini to‘g‘ri aytadi (2 × 410 = 820).\\n\\nB yakshanbani jumaga solishtiradi, juma esa hafta boshi emas, oxiri. C faqat dushanba raqamini beradi, dam olish kuni bilan solishtirmaydi. D ikki ish kunini solishtiradi, dam olish kuni yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"complete_statement\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Chemistry student Ozoda Yusupova dissolved different amounts of table salt in samples of water and measured the temperature at which each sample began to freeze. She claims that the more salt is dissolved in water, the lower the temperature at which the water freezes.\\n\\n**Freezing temperature of salt water**\\n\\n| Salt in water (% by mass) | Freezing temperature (°C) |\\n| ---: | ---: |\\n| 0 | 0.0 |\\n| 5 | −3.0 |\\n| 10 | −6.6 |\\n| 15 | −10.9 |\\n| 20 | −16.5 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support Yusupova's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"Water with no salt froze at 0.0°C.\",\"option_b\":\"Water with 10% salt froze at −6.6°C, a higher temperature than water with 5% salt.\",\"option_c\":\"Water with 15% salt froze at −10.9°C.\",\"option_d\":\"As the amount of salt rose from 0% to 20%, the freezing temperature fell at every step, from 0.0°C to −16.5°C.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim describes a trend: more salt means a lower freezing temperature. Choice D describes that trend across all the samples: each increase in salt came with a lower freezing temperature, from 0.0°C with no salt to −16.5°C with 20% salt.\\n\\nChoices A and C each give a single, accurate data point, but one sample alone cannot show how freezing temperature changes as salt increases. Choice B misreads the table: −6.6°C is lower, not higher, than −3.0°C, the freezing temperature of the 5% sample.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’vo tendensiyani tasvirlaydi: tuz qancha ko‘p bo‘lsa, muzlash harorati shuncha past. D javob bu tendensiyani barcha namunalar bo‘yicha ko‘rsatadi: tuz har safar ko‘paytirilganda muzlash harorati pasaygan — tuzsiz suvda 0.0°C dan 20% tuzli suvda −16.5°C gacha.\\n\\nA va C har biri bitta to‘g‘ri ma’lumot beradi, lekin bitta namuna tuz ko‘payganda muzlash harorati qanday o‘zgarishini ko‘rsata olmaydi. B jadvalni noto‘g‘ri o‘qiydi: −6.6°C 5% li namunaning muzlash haroratidan (−3.0°C) yuqori emas, past.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"support_claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"A basketball coach claims that the players on his team who sleep more each night score more points per game. His assistant, Shahzod Ergashev, recorded each starting player's average nightly sleep and average points per game over one season.\\n\\n**Sleep and scoring of four basketball players**\\n\\n| Player | Average sleep (hours per night) | Average points per game |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Aziz | 7.0 | 12 |\\n| Behruz | 8.5 | 9 |\\n| Doniyor | 6.5 | 14 |\\n| Elyor | 9.0 | 7 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to weaken the coach's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"Aziz slept an average of 7.0 hours per night and scored an average of 12 points per game.\",\"option_b\":\"Doniyor slept the least, 6.5 hours per night, yet scored the most, 14 points per game, while Elyor slept the most, 9.0 hours, and scored the fewest, 7 points.\",\"option_c\":\"Elyor slept an average of 9.0 hours per night, more than any other player.\",\"option_d\":\"Behruz scored an average of 9 points per game, more than Elyor but fewer than Aziz.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The coach claims that more sleep goes with more points. Choice B shows the opposite pattern at both extremes: the player who slept least (Doniyor) scored most, and the player who slept most (Elyor) scored least. This weakens the claim.\\n\\nChoice A gives data for one player only, which cannot show a relationship between sleep and points. Choice C gives only sleep data, and choice D gives only scoring data, so neither connects the two quantities in the claim.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Murabbiy ko‘proq uxlash ko‘proq ochko bilan bog‘liq deb da’vo qiladi. B javob ikkala chekkada ham teskari holatni ko‘rsatadi: eng kam uxlagan o‘yinchi (Doniyor) eng ko‘p ochko olgan, eng ko‘p uxlagan o‘yinchi (Elyor) esa eng kam. Bu da’voni zaiflashtiradi.\\n\\nA faqat bitta o‘yinchi haqida ma’lumot beradi, bu uyqu va ochko o‘rtasidagi bog‘liqlikni ko‘rsata olmaydi. C faqat uyqu, D esa faqat ochko haqida ma’lumot beradi, shuning uchun ikkalasi ham da’vodagi ikki kattalikni bog‘lamaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"weaken_claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Biology student Gulnoza Aliyeva surveyed two ponds near Samarkand and counted how many species of four kinds of animals lived in each pond. She found that the two ponds had the same number of species in only one of these groups: ______\\n\\n**Number of species found in two ponds**\\n\\n| Animal group | Pond A | Pond B |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Dragonflies | 9 | 5 |\\n| Water beetles | 14 | 17 |\\n| Snails | 6 | 6 |\\n| Frogs | 3 | 2 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"water beetles, with 14 species in Pond A and 17 species in Pond B.\",\"option_b\":\"frogs, with 3 species in Pond A and 2 species in Pond B.\",\"option_c\":\"dragonflies, with 9 species in Pond A and 5 species in Pond B.\",\"option_d\":\"snails, with 6 species in each pond.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The statement asks for the one group with the same number of species in both ponds. Only snails have equal counts, 6 species in each pond, so choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoices A, B, and C report the table accurately, but in each of those groups the two ponds have different numbers of species, so none of them completes the statement.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapda ikkala ko‘lmakda tur soni bir xil bo‘lgan yagona guruh so‘raladi. Faqat shilliqqurtlarda son teng — har bir ko‘lmakda 6 tur, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\nA, B va C jadvaldagi raqamlarni to‘g‘ri beradi, lekin bu guruhlarning har birida ikki ko‘lmakdagi tur soni har xil, shuning uchun ular gapni to‘ldirmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"complete_statement\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Engineer Jamshid Turg'unov tested wall panels made of three insulating materials. Straw panels are known to be inexpensive, while foam panels are known to keep heat from escaping especially well. Turg'unov claims that panels made of wool felt combine these two advantages.\\n\\n**Cost and heat loss of three types of wall panels**\\n\\n| Material | Cost per panel (dollars) | Heat loss (watts per square meter) |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Straw | 12 | 38 |\\n| Foam | 41 | 22 |\\n| Wool felt | 14 | 24 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support Turg'unov's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"Wool felt panels cost 14 dollars each, close to the 12-dollar cost of straw panels, and lost 24 watts per square meter, close to the 22 watts lost by foam panels.\",\"option_b\":\"Foam panels lost only 22 watts per square meter, less than either of the other two materials.\",\"option_c\":\"Wool felt panels cost 14 dollars each, only slightly more than straw panels.\",\"option_d\":\"Wool felt panels lost 24 watts per square meter, far less than the 38 watts lost by foam panels.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim has two parts: wool felt is nearly as cheap as straw and nearly as good at preventing heat loss as foam (lower heat loss is better). Choice A supports both parts with accurate data: 14 dollars versus 12 for straw, and 24 watts versus 22 for foam.\\n\\nChoice B is accurate but describes only foam and says nothing about wool felt. Choice C supports only the cost part of the claim. Choice D misreads the table: 38 watts is the heat loss of straw panels, not foam panels.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’vo ikki qismdan iborat: jun kigiz deyarli somon kabi arzon va issiqlik yo‘qotilishining oldini olishda deyarli ko‘pik kabi yaxshi (issiqlik yo‘qotilishi qancha kam bo‘lsa, shuncha yaxshi). A javob ikkala qismni ham to‘g‘ri ma’lumot bilan qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi: somon uchun 12 dollarga nisbatan 14 dollar va ko‘pik uchun 22 vattga nisbatan 24 vatt.\\n\\nB to‘g‘ri, lekin faqat ko‘pikni tasvirlaydi, jun kigiz haqida hech narsa demaydi. C da’voning faqat narx qismini qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi. D jadvalni noto‘g‘ri o‘qiydi: 38 vatt ko‘pik emas, somon panellarining issiqlik yo‘qotilishi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"support_claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In 2018, conservationists released 40 goitered gazelles, a species of small antelope, into a fenced steppe reserve in Uzbekistan. Ecologist Nargiza Sodiqova, who counts the herd every autumn, reports that the population has grown every year since the release but that its growth has slowed in recent years as the herd has begun to fill the available habitat.\\n\\n**Number of goitered gazelles in the reserve**\\n\\n| Year | Number of gazelles |\\n| --- | ---: |\\n| 2018 | 40 |\\n| 2019 | 58 |\\n| 2020 | 81 |\\n| 2021 | 109 |\\n| 2022 | 124 |\\n| 2023 | 133 |\\n| 2024 | 138 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support Sodiqova's report?\",\"option_a\":\"The number of gazelles rose from 40 in 2018 to 138 in 2024.\",\"option_b\":\"The number of gazelles grew by 18 from 2018 to 2019 and by 28 from 2020 to 2021.\",\"option_c\":\"The number of gazelles grew by 28 from 2020 to 2021 but by only 5 from 2023 to 2024.\",\"option_d\":\"The number of gazelles fell from 133 in 2023 to 124 in 2024.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Sodiqova reports that the herd kept growing but that the growth has slowed recently. Choice C shows both: the herd was still growing from 2023 to 2024, but by only 5 animals, compared with a gain of 28 from 2020 to 2021.\\n\\nChoice A shows overall growth but not the slowdown. Choice B compares two early periods in which growth sped up (from 18 to 28), the opposite of a slowdown. Choice D misreads the table: the population rose from 133 to 138 in that period; 124 is the 2022 figure.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Sodiqova poda o‘sishda davom etgani, lekin so‘nggi yillarda o‘sish sekinlashganini aytadi. C javob ikkalasini ham ko‘rsatadi: 2023 yildan 2024 yilgacha poda hali ham o‘sgan, lekin bor-yo‘g‘i 5 taga, 2020 yildan 2021 yilgacha esa 28 taga o‘sgan edi.\\n\\nA umumiy o‘sishni ko‘rsatadi, lekin sekinlashuvni emas. B o‘sish tezlashgan (18 dan 28 ga) ikki erta davrni solishtiradi, bu sekinlashuvning aksi. D jadvalni noto‘g‘ri o‘qiydi: bu davrda son 133 dan 138 ga oshgan; 124 — 2022 yil ko‘rsatkichi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"support_claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Reeds provide cover in which many water birds hide their nests. Ornithologist Kamron Mahmudov claims that, among the wetlands he surveyed in the Amu Darya delta, the wetlands with a greater percentage of reed cover had more bird nests.\\n\\n**Reed cover and bird nests in four wetlands**\\n\\n| Wetland | Reed cover (%) | Bird nests counted |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Wetland 1 | 20 | 34 |\\n| Wetland 2 | 45 | 61 |\\n| Wetland 3 | 70 | 88 |\\n| Wetland 4 | 85 | 52 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to weaken Mahmudov's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"Wetland 3 had 70% reed cover and 88 nests, more nests than any other wetland.\",\"option_b\":\"Wetland 1 had the least reed cover, 20%, and the fewest nests, 34.\",\"option_c\":\"Wetland 2 had 45% reed cover and 61 nests.\",\"option_d\":\"Wetland 4 had the most reed cover, 85%, but only 52 nests, fewer than either Wetland 2 or Wetland 3.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"If more reed cover went with more nests, the wetland with the most reeds should have the most nests. Choice D shows that Wetland 4 had the most reed cover (85%) but fewer nests (52) than two wetlands with less reed cover, which weakens the claim.\\n\\nChoices A and B describe wetlands that fit the claim (high cover with many nests, low cover with few nests), so they support it. Choice C gives data for a single wetland, which cannot show whether nests increase with reed cover.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Agar qamish ko‘p bo‘lgan joyda uyalar ko‘p bo‘lsa, eng ko‘p qamishli botqoqlikda eng ko‘p uya bo‘lishi kerak. D javob 4-botqoqlikda qamish eng ko‘p (85%) bo‘lsa ham, unda qamishi kamroq bo‘lgan ikki botqoqlikdan kamroq uya (52) borligini ko‘rsatadi. Bu da’voni zaiflashtiradi.\\n\\nA va B da’voga mos botqoqliklarni (qamish ko‘p — uya ko‘p, qamish kam — uya kam) tasvirlaydi, ya’ni da’voni qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi. C faqat bitta botqoqlik haqida ma’lumot beradi, bu uyalar soni qamish bilan birga ortishini ko‘rsata olmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"weaken_claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Urban planner Jasur Ortiqov surveyed residents of four districts of a large city about how they usually travel to work. He found that the metro was the most common way of commuting in only one district: ______\\n\\n**Usual way of commuting to work, by district (% of residents)**\\n\\n| District | Car | Bus | Metro | Walking or cycling |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| North | 35 | 30 | 20 | 15 |\\n| East | 25 | 22 | 41 | 12 |\\n| South | 44 | 29 | 10 | 17 |\\n| West | 31 | 36 | 18 | 15 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"the East district, where 41 percent of residents commuted by metro, compared with 25 percent by car and 22 percent by bus.\",\"option_b\":\"the North district, where 20 percent of residents commuted by metro.\",\"option_c\":\"the West district, where 36 percent of residents commuted by bus, more than by any other way.\",\"option_d\":\"the South district, where only 10 percent of residents commuted by metro.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The statement asks for the one district where the metro was the most common way of commuting. In the East district, 41 percent of residents used the metro, more than used any other way, so choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because in the North district the car (35 percent) and the bus (30 percent) were both more common than the metro (20 percent). Choice C names a district where the bus, not the metro, was most common. Choice D names the district with the lowest metro use.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapda metro eng keng tarqalgan qatnov usuli bo‘lgan yagona tuman so‘raladi. Sharqiy tumanda aholining 41 foizi metrodan foydalangan, bu boshqa har qanday usuldan ko‘p, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: Shimoliy tumanda avtomobil (35 foiz) ham, avtobus (30 foiz) ham metrodan (20 foiz) ko‘proq ishlatilgan. C metro emas, avtobus eng keng tarqalgan tumanni ko‘rsatadi. D metrodan eng kam foydalanilgan tumanni ko‘rsatadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"complete_statement\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Researchers at a language-learning app tested whether daily reminder messages help users keep studying. For one month, some new users received a reminder each day and others received none. The researchers claim that the reminders helped users of every age group keep studying.\\n\\n**New users still studying after 30 days (%)**\\n\\n| Age group | Without reminders | With reminders |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| 18–24 | 22 | 31 |\\n| 25–39 | 18 | 29 |\\n| 40–59 | 15 | 33 |\\n| 60 and older | 12 | 27 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the researchers' claim?\",\"option_a\":\"Among users aged 18–24, 31 percent of those who received reminders were still studying after 30 days.\",\"option_b\":\"In every age group, a greater percentage of users were still studying after 30 days with reminders (27 to 33 percent) than without them (12 to 22 percent).\",\"option_c\":\"Users aged 60 and older who received no reminders had the lowest rate of continued study, 12 percent.\",\"option_d\":\"Users aged 40–59 who received reminders had the highest rate of continued study, 33 percent.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim is about every age group, so the evidence must compare users with and without reminders in all four groups. Choice B does this: in each group, the rate with reminders (27 to 33 percent) is higher than the rate without (12 to 22 percent).\\n\\nChoice A gives only one figure for one age group, with no comparison to users without reminders. Choices C and D each describe a single cell of the table, which cannot show that reminders helped in every group.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’vo barcha yosh guruhlari haqida, shuning uchun dalil to‘rtta guruhning har birida eslatma olgan va olmagan foydalanuvchilarni solishtirishi kerak. B javob shuni qiladi: har bir guruhda eslatma bilan ko‘rsatkich (27–33 foiz) eslatmasiz ko‘rsatkichdan (12–22 foiz) yuqori.\\n\\nA bitta guruh uchun bitta raqam beradi va eslatma olmaganlar bilan solishtirmaydi. C va D jadvalning bittadan katagini tasvirlaydi, bu eslatmalar har bir guruhda yordam berganini ko‘rsata olmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"support_claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Agricultural economist Sherzod Normatov tracked the area planted with pistachio orchards in four farming regions. He observed that pistachio planting expanded very unevenly: while the orchard area in the Hill region more than tripled from 2015 to 2023, ______\\n\\n**Area of pistachio orchards (hectares)**\\n\\n| Region | 2015 | 2019 | 2023 |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Hill | 1,200 | 2,300 | 3,900 |\\n| Valley | 2,500 | 2,700 | 2,900 |\\n| Plain | 800 | 1,400 | 2,100 |\\n| River | 1,600 | 1,500 | 1,700 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"the orchard area in the Plain region grew from 800 hectares to 2,100 hectares over the same period.\",\"option_b\":\"the River region had 1,500 hectares of orchards in 2019.\",\"option_c\":\"the orchard area in the Valley region grew by only 400 hectares, from 2,500 hectares to 2,900 hectares.\",\"option_d\":\"the orchard area in the Valley region fell from 2,700 hectares in 2019 to 2,500 hectares in 2023.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The word \\\"while\\\" signals a contrast with the Hill region's rapid growth, so the blank needs a region whose orchard area grew little over the same period. Choice C accurately shows that the Valley region grew by only 400 hectares from 2015 to 2023.\\n\\nChoice A is accurate, but the Plain region also grew rapidly (more than doubling), so it does not contrast with the Hill region. Choice B gives a single figure from one year, which shows no change at all. Choice D misreads the table: the Valley region had 2,500 hectares in 2015 and 2,900 in 2023; its area never fell.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"\\\"While\\\" (\\\"holbuki\\\") so‘zi Tepalik mintaqasidagi tez o‘sish bilan qarama-qarshilikni bildiradi, shuning uchun bo‘sh joyga o‘sha davrda kam o‘sgan mintaqa kerak. C javob Vodiy mintaqasi 2015–2023 yillarda bor-yo‘g‘i 400 gektarga o‘sganini to‘g‘ri ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nA to‘g‘ri, lekin Tekislik mintaqasi ham tez o‘sgan (ikki baravardan ko‘p), shuning uchun u Tepalik mintaqasiga qarama-qarshi emas. B bitta yilning bitta raqamini beradi, hech qanday o‘zgarishni ko‘rsatmaydi. D jadvalni noto‘g‘ri o‘qiydi: Vodiy mintaqasida 2015 yilda 2,500, 2023 yilda 2,900 gektar bo‘lgan; maydon hech qachon kamaymagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"complete_statement\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Horticulturist Sevara Tojiyeva hypothesized that covering plants with shade cloth during the summer would increase the yield of lettuce, which wilts in strong heat, by a greater amount than it would increase the yield of tomatoes, which tolerate heat better. She grew both crops with and without shade cloth over two summers.\\n\\n**Average yield per plot (kilograms)**\\n\\n| Crop | 2023, no shade | 2023, shade cloth | 2024, no shade | 2024, shade cloth |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Lettuce | 18 | 27 | 15 | 25 |\\n| Tomatoes | 40 | 43 | 36 | 41 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support Tojiyeva's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"In both years, tomato plots produced more than lettuce plots, whether or not they were covered with shade cloth.\",\"option_b\":\"Shade cloth raised lettuce yields by 9 kilograms per plot in 2023 and by 10 kilograms in 2024, but raised tomato yields by only 3 and 5 kilograms.\",\"option_c\":\"In 2023, shade cloth raised the lettuce yield from 18 to 27 kilograms per plot, an increase of 9 kilograms.\",\"option_d\":\"In 2024, shade cloth raised the tomato yield from 36 to 41 kilograms per plot, a larger increase than in 2023.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The hypothesis compares the increase that shade produced for lettuce with the increase it produced for tomatoes. Choice B computes both increases for both years (lettuce: 27 − 18 = 9 and 25 − 15 = 10; tomatoes: 43 − 40 = 3 and 41 − 36 = 5) and shows that lettuce gained more each time.\\n\\nChoice A is accurate but compares total yields, not the effect of shade cloth. Choice C gives lettuce's increase for one year without comparing it with tomatoes. Choice D compares tomatoes' gains in two different years, which says nothing about lettuce.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gipoteza soya to‘rining salatga bergan o‘sishini pomidorga bergan o‘sish bilan solishtiradi. B javob ikkala yil uchun ikkala o‘sishni hisoblaydi (salat: 27 − 18 = 9 va 25 − 15 = 10; pomidor: 43 − 40 = 3 va 41 − 36 = 5) va har safar salat ko‘proq o‘sganini ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nA to‘g‘ri, lekin soya to‘rining ta’sirini emas, umumiy hosilni solishtiradi. C bitta yil uchun salatning o‘sishini beradi, pomidor bilan solishtirmaydi. D pomidorning ikki xil yildagi o‘sishini solishtiradi, salat haqida hech narsa demaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"support_claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Energy analyst Bobur Xolmatov compared the shares of electricity generated from wind and from solar power in four regional power grids. He notes that solar power grew faster than wind power in all four grids, so that by 2024 only one grid still generated a larger share of its electricity from wind than from solar: ______\\n\\n**Share of electricity from wind and solar power (%)**\\n\\n| Grid | Wind, 2015 | Solar, 2015 | Wind, 2024 | Solar, 2024 |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Grid 1 | 8 | 3 | 12 | 15 |\\n| Grid 2 | 5 | 6 | 9 | 14 |\\n| Grid 3 | 11 | 4 | 18 | 13 |\\n| Grid 4 | 2 | 1 | 4 | 9 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"Grid 1, which generated 8 percent of its electricity from wind and 3 percent from solar in 2015.\",\"option_b\":\"Grid 4, where wind's share of electricity doubled from 2 percent in 2015 to 4 percent in 2024.\",\"option_c\":\"Grid 2, which generated 9 percent of its electricity from wind in 2024.\",\"option_d\":\"Grid 3, which generated 18 percent of its electricity from wind in 2024, compared with 13 percent from solar.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The statement requires the one grid in which wind's share was larger than solar's in 2024. Comparing the two 2024 columns, only Grid 3 qualifies (18 percent from wind versus 13 percent from solar), so choice D is correct. (Grid 3 also led with wind in 2015, which fits the word \\\"still.\\\")\\n\\nChoice A uses the wrong year: Grid 1 favored wind in 2015, but by 2024 solar (15 percent) had passed wind (12 percent). Choice B is accurate but describes wind's growth in Grid 4, where solar (9 percent) exceeded wind (4 percent) in 2024. Choice C gives Grid 2's wind share without noting that its solar share was larger (14 percent).\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gap 2024 yilda shamol ulushi quyosh ulushidan katta bo‘lgan yagona tarmoqni talab qiladi. 2024 yilning ikki ustunini solishtirsak, faqat 3-tarmoq mos keladi (shamoldan 18 foiz, quyoshdan 13 foiz), shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob D. (3-tarmoqda 2015 yilda ham shamol ustun bo‘lgan, bu \\\"still\\\" — \\\"hali ham\\\" so‘ziga mos.)\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri yilni oladi: 1-tarmoqda 2015 yilda shamol ustun bo‘lgan, lekin 2024 yilga kelib quyosh (15 foiz) shamoldan (12 foiz) o‘tib ketgan. B to‘g‘ri, lekin 4-tarmoqdagi shamol o‘sishini tasvirlaydi, u yerda 2024 yilda quyosh (9 foiz) shamoldan (4 foiz) ko‘p. C 2-tarmoqning shamol ulushini beradi, lekin uning quyosh ulushi (14 foiz) kattaroq ekanini hisobga olmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"complete_statement\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Linguist Madina Rajabova studied how often speakers in four villages use a traditional verb ending that is gradually being replaced by a standard form. She interviewed speakers from three generations in each village. Rajabova claims that in every village she studied, each younger generation uses the traditional ending less often than the generation before it.\\n\\n**Use of the traditional verb ending (% of verbs where the ending could appear)**\\n\\n| Village | Speakers interviewed | Ages 60 and older | Ages 35–59 | Ages 15–34 |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Village 1 | 48 | 82 | 64 | 41 |\\n| Village 2 | 52 | 76 | 70 | 52 |\\n| Village 3 | 45 | 88 | 59 | 63 |\\n| Village 4 | 50 | 71 | 55 | 30 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to weaken Rajabova's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"In Village 3, speakers aged 15–34 used the traditional ending 63 percent of the time, more often than the 59 percent among speakers aged 35–59.\",\"option_b\":\"In Village 4, use of the traditional ending fell from 71 percent among speakers aged 60 and older to 30 percent among speakers aged 15–34.\",\"option_c\":\"In Village 3, speakers aged 60 and older used the traditional ending 88 percent of the time, more often than the oldest speakers in any other village.\",\"option_d\":\"In Village 2, speakers aged 15–34 used the traditional ending 52 percent of the time, less often than the 70 percent among speakers aged 35–59.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim says that in every village, each younger generation uses the traditional ending less than the one before. A single village where a younger generation uses it more would weaken the claim. Choice A identifies that case: in Village 3, the youngest speakers (63 percent) used the ending more than the middle generation (59 percent).\\n\\nChoices B and D describe declines from older to younger speakers, which fit the claim. Choice C is accurate but compares the oldest speakers across villages; the claim is about differences between generations within each village.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’voga ko‘ra, har bir qishloqda har bir yoshroq avlod an’anaviy qo‘shimchani oldingi avloddan kamroq ishlatadi. Yoshroq avlod uni ko‘proq ishlatadigan bitta qishloq ham da’voni zaiflashtiradi. A javob aynan shunday holatni ko‘rsatadi: 3-qishloqda eng yosh so‘zlovchilar (63 foiz) qo‘shimchani o‘rta avloddan (59 foiz) ko‘proq ishlatgan.\\n\\nB va D kattalardan yoshlarga qarab kamayishni tasvirlaydi, bu da’voga mos. C to‘g‘ri, lekin turli qishloqlarning eng keksa so‘zlovchilarini solishtiradi; da’vo esa har bir qishloq ichidagi avlodlar farqi haqida.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"weaken_claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Ornithologist Aziza Hamidova fitted four shorebirds of the same long-distance migratory species with small tracking devices and followed their spring migrations. She notes that among these birds, traveling a greater distance did not necessarily mean spending more days migrating: ______\\n\\n**Spring migrations of four tagged shorebirds**\\n\\n| Bird | Body mass (grams) | Distance traveled (kilometers) | Days spent migrating | Number of stopovers |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| R12 | 410 | 9,200 | 31 | 2 |\\n| K07 | 385 | 6,100 | 47 | 6 |\\n| T33 | 430 | 7,800 | 38 | 3 |\\n| M21 | 395 | 5,400 | 29 | 4 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"Bird K07 spent 47 days migrating and made 6 stopovers, more stopovers than any other bird.\",\"option_b\":\"Bird M21 traveled the shortest distance, 5,400 kilometers, and also spent the fewest days migrating, 29.\",\"option_c\":\"Bird R12 traveled 9,200 kilometers, the farthest of the four birds, yet spent only 31 days migrating, fewer than Birds K07 and T33.\",\"option_d\":\"Bird R12 traveled 9,200 kilometers, the farthest of the four birds, and spent more days migrating than any other bird.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The statement needs an example of a bird that traveled farther but did not spend more days migrating. Choice C accurately shows that R12 traveled the farthest (9,200 kilometers) yet spent fewer days migrating (31) than K07 (47 days, 6,100 kilometers) and T33 (38 days, 7,800 kilometers), both of which traveled shorter distances.\\n\\nChoice A is accurate but focuses on stopovers, which the statement is not about. Choice B is accurate, but a bird with both the shortest distance and the fewest days fits the idea that distance and time go together, so it does not illustrate the statement. Choice D misreads the table: K07 spent the most days migrating (47), not R12.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapga uzoqroq uchgan, lekin ko‘chishga ko‘proq kun sarflamagan qush misoli kerak. C javob R12 eng uzoq masofani (9,200 kilometr) bosib o‘tgani, ammo qisqaroq masofani bosgan K07 (47 kun, 6,100 kilometr) va T33 (38 kun, 7,800 kilometr) dan kamroq kun (31) sarflaganini to‘g‘ri ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nA to‘g‘ri, lekin gapga aloqasi yo‘q bo‘lgan to‘xtashlar soniga e’tibor qaratadi. B to‘g‘ri, lekin masofasi ham, kunlari ham eng kam bo‘lgan qush masofa va vaqt birga o‘zgaradi degan fikrga mos keladi, shuning uchun gapni misol bilan tasdiqlamaydi. D jadvalni noto‘g‘ri o‘qiydi: ko‘chishga eng ko‘p kun (47) R12 emas, K07 sarflagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"complete_statement\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"*The House by the Canal* is a novel. In the novel, the narrator describes her grandmother as tireless, working from early in the morning until late at night.\",\"stem\":\"Which quotation from *The House by the Canal* most effectively illustrates the claim?\",\"option_a\":\"“Grandmother's plov was famous in three neighborhoods, though she never once wrote down the recipe.”\",\"option_b\":\"“Our house had a blue gate that creaked whenever the wind came down from the mountains.”\",\"option_c\":\"“Before the roosters stirred, Grandmother was already sweeping the courtyard, and long after the lamps were out, I could hear her loom clicking in the dark.”\",\"option_d\":\"“On winter evenings, Grandmother told us stories about the caravans her own grandfather had led.”\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim is that the grandmother worked from early morning until late at night. Choice C shows both ends of her day: she is sweeping \\\"before the roosters stirred\\\" and still weaving \\\"long after the lamps were out.\\\"\\n\\nChoice A praises her cooking but says nothing about how long she worked. Choice B describes the house, not the grandmother. Choice D shows her telling stories in the evening, which is not evidence of nonstop work.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’voga ko‘ra, buvi ertalabdan kechgacha tinmay ishlagan. C javob uning kunining ikkala chegarasini ko‘rsatadi: u \\\"xo‘rozlar uyg‘onmasdan\\\" hovli supurayotgan va \\\"chiroqlar o‘chganidan ancha keyin\\\" ham to‘quv dastgohida ishlayotgan.\\n\\nA uning taomini maqtaydi, lekin qancha ishlagani haqida hech narsa demaydi. B buvini emas, uyni tasvirlaydi. D uning kechqurun ertak aytishini ko‘rsatadi, bu tinimsiz mehnatga dalil emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"literary_quote\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"“Orchard in January” is a poem. In the poem, the speaker walks through an orchard in the middle of winter and expresses confidence that the bare, frozen trees will bloom again in spring.\",\"stem\":\"Which quotation from “Orchard in January” most effectively illustrates the claim?\",\"option_a\":\"“Beneath the frost the patient roots keep count / of every day that leads them back to green.”\",\"option_b\":\"“The branches scratch the sky like rusted wire, / and crows sit heavy where the apples hung.”\",\"option_c\":\"“I walked here once in summer with my father, / who named each tree the way one names a friend.”\",\"option_d\":\"“The wind comes down the valley, sharp and thin, / and finds each gap in my old woolen coat.”\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim has two elements: the trees are bare and frozen now, and the speaker is confident they will bloom again. Choice A places the roots \\\"beneath the frost\\\" and describes them counting the days \\\"that lead them back to green,\\\" expressing that confidence.\\n\\nChoice B describes the bare winter orchard but offers no hope of spring. Choice C recalls a past summer rather than looking ahead. Choice D describes the cold wind and says nothing about the orchard's future.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’vo ikki qismdan iborat: bog‘ hozir yalang‘och va muzlagan, so‘zlovchi esa daraxtlar bahorda yana gullashiga ishonadi. A javobda ildizlar \\\"ayoz ostida\\\" sabr bilan ularni \\\"yana yashillikka qaytaradigan\\\" kunlarni sanaydi, ya’ni ishonch ifodalangan.\\n\\nB qishki yalang‘och bog‘ni tasvirlaydi, lekin bahorga umid bildirmaydi. C kelajakka emas, o‘tgan yozga qaraydi. D sovuq shamolni tasvirlaydi va bog‘ning kelajagi haqida hech narsa demaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"literary_quote\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"*The River Road* is a novel. In the novel, the narrator's father, Rustam, is shown to be both proud that his son has won a place at a university abroad and saddened that his son will soon be living so far away.\",\"stem\":\"Which quotation from *The River Road* most effectively illustrates the claim?\",\"option_a\":\"“Rustam told everyone at the teahouse about the letter from the university, reading it aloud so many times that the paper went soft at the folds.”\",\"option_b\":\"“Rustam had never traveled farther than the regional capital, and he distrusted airplanes on principle.”\",\"option_c\":\"“At night Rustam sat alone in the courtyard, looking at the empty chair where his son used to do his homework.”\",\"option_d\":\"“‘The whole village will hear of this,’ Rustam said, holding the letter up to the light; and then, more quietly, ‘Seven hours by plane. Seven hours.’”\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim has two parts: Rustam is proud of his son's achievement and sad about the distance. In choice D, he announces that \\\"the whole village will hear of this\\\" (pride) and then quietly repeats \\\"Seven hours by plane\\\" (sadness about how far away his son will be).\\n\\nChoice A shows only pride. Choice C shows only sadness. Choice B describes Rustam's own limited travel and distrust of airplanes but shows neither pride in his son nor sadness about the move.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’vo ikki qismdan iborat: Rustam o‘g‘lining yutug‘idan faxrlanadi va uning uzoqqa ketishidan qayg‘uradi. D javobda u \\\"butun qishloq bundan xabar topadi\\\" deydi (faxr), so‘ng sekin ovozda \\\"Samolyotda yetti soat\\\" deb takrorlaydi (masofadan qayg‘u).\\n\\nA faqat faxrni ko‘rsatadi. C faqat qayg‘uni ko‘rsatadi. B Rustamning o‘zi uzoqqa bormagani va samolyotlarga ishonmasligini tasvirlaydi, lekin unda na faxr, na qayg‘u bor.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"literary_quote\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"*The Silk Merchant's Niece* is a novel. In the novel, the narrator suggests that her aunt Saodat, who was stern with the children in front of others, showed her affection for them only when she believed no one was watching.\",\"stem\":\"Which quotation from *The Silk Merchant's Niece* most effectively illustrates the claim?\",\"option_a\":\"“Aunt Saodat corrected our posture, our handwriting, and our manners, and she did so in front of any guest who happened to be present.”\",\"option_b\":\"“Once, waking early, I saw her through the half-open door, smoothing my brother's blanket and humming the song she claimed not to know; when she noticed me, her face closed like a shutter.”\",\"option_c\":\"“At the wedding, in front of all our relatives, she lifted my little sister onto her lap and fed her sweets until the child fell asleep.”\",\"option_d\":\"“She kept the household accounts in a green ledger, and no expense, however small, escaped her notice.”\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim requires three things: Saodat shows affection, she does so in private, and she hides it once she is observed. In choice B, she tenderly smooths a blanket and hums while unaware of the narrator, and when she notices she is being watched, \\\"her face closed like a shutter.\\\"\\n\\nChoice A shows only her sternness in public. Choice C shows affection, but \\\"in front of all our relatives,\\\" which contradicts the claim that she showed it only when unobserved. Choice D shows her strictness with money, which has nothing to do with affection for the children.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’vo uch narsani talab qiladi: Saodat mehr ko‘rsatadi, buni yolg‘izlikda qiladi va kimdir ko‘rganini sezsa, yashiradi. B javobda u hech kim ko‘rmayapti deb o‘ylab, adyolni mehr bilan to‘g‘rilaydi va xirgoyi qiladi, hikoyachini sezgach esa \\\"yuzi darcha qopqog‘idek yopiladi\\\".\\n\\nA faqat uning boshqalar oldidagi qattiqqo‘lligini ko‘rsatadi. C mehrni ko‘rsatadi, lekin \\\"barcha qarindoshlar oldida\\\" — bu mehr faqat hech kim ko‘rmaganda ko‘rsatilgan degan da’voga zid. D uning pul masalasidagi sinchkovligini ko‘rsatadi, bu bolalarga mehr bilan bog‘liq emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"literary_quote\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Ecologist Feruza Nazarova hypothesizes that magpies nesting in a city park can recognize individual people who have previously disturbed their nests. To test this idea, she had one volunteer briefly climb toward several nests while the birds watched. A few weeks later, that volunteer and several other volunteers, all wearing identical clothing, walked separately through the park.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Nazarova's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"The magpies gave alarm calls at all of the volunteers equally often.\",\"option_b\":\"The magpies gave alarm calls far more often at the volunteer who had climbed toward the nests than at the other volunteers.\",\"option_c\":\"Magpies in the park built their nests higher in trees than magpies outside the city did.\",\"option_d\":\"The magpies ignored all of the volunteers, including the one who had climbed toward the nests.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"If magpies recognize the individual who disturbed them, they should react to that person differently from others dressed the same way. Choice B describes exactly that: far more alarm calls at the volunteer who had climbed toward the nests.\\n\\nChoice A suggests the birds reacted to people in general, not to one individual, which would weaken the hypothesis. Choice C concerns nest height, which is unrelated to recognizing people. Choice D suggests the magpies did not remember the disturbance at all.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Agar zag‘izg‘onlar o‘zini bezovta qilgan odamni taniy olsa, ular xuddi shunday kiyingan boshqalarga qaraganda unga boshqacha munosabatda bo‘lishi kerak. B javob aynan shuni tasvirlaydi: uyalarga chiqqan ko‘ngilliga ko‘proq xavf ovozi berilgan.\\n\\nA qushlar alohida odamga emas, umuman odamlarga munosabat bildirganini ko‘rsatadi, bu gipotezani zaiflashtiradi. C uya balandligi haqida, odamlarni tanishga aloqasi yo‘q. D zag‘izg‘onlar bezovtalikni umuman eslamaganini ko‘rsatadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"finding_support\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Education researcher Javlon Tursunov hypothesizes that students remember new material better when they explain it aloud to a classmate than when they simply reread it. He gave 120 students the same short reading about how volcanoes form. Half of the students then spent ten minutes explaining the reading to a partner, while the other half spent ten minutes rereading it on their own.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Tursunov's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"Students in both groups said that they enjoyed the reading about volcanoes.\",\"option_b\":\"On a quiz a week later, the two groups of students earned nearly identical average scores.\",\"option_c\":\"Students who explained the material reported feeling more nervous than students who reread it.\",\"option_d\":\"On a quiz a week later, students who had explained the material to a partner scored higher, on average, than students who had reread it.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The hypothesis predicts that explaining leads to better memory than rereading. Choice D shows that the explaining group remembered more a week later, as measured by higher quiz scores.\\n\\nChoice A concerns enjoyment, not memory, and does not distinguish the groups. Choice B shows no difference in memory, which would weaken the hypothesis. Choice C concerns nervousness, which says nothing about how well students remembered the material.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gipotezaga ko‘ra, tushuntirish qayta o‘qishdan ko‘ra yaxshiroq eslab qolishga olib keladi. D javob tushuntirgan guruh bir haftadan keyin testda yuqoriroq ball olganini, ya’ni ko‘proq eslab qolganini ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nA eslab qolish haqida emas, zavq haqida va guruhlarni farqlamaydi. B xotirada farq yo‘qligini ko‘rsatadi, bu gipotezani zaiflashtiradi. C hayajon haqida, o‘quvchilar materialni qanchalik eslab qolgani haqida hech narsa demaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"finding_support\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"At an ancient settlement in Central Asia, archaeologists found several large, flat stones with smooth, worn hollows in their upper surfaces. Archaeologist Nodir Usmonov hypothesizes that people at the settlement used the stones to grind wild grain into flour.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken Usmonov's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"Stones with similar hollows have been found at other ancient sites where people are known to have ground grain.\",\"option_b\":\"The hollows in the stones are deeper at the center than at the edges.\",\"option_c\":\"Chemical tests of the hollows detected traces of red mineral pigment but no traces of starch from grain.\",\"option_d\":\"Seeds of wild grasses were found in the soil near the stones.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"If the stones had been used to grind grain, traces of grain starch would be expected in the hollows. Choice C reports the opposite: pigment traces and no starch, which suggests the stones were used for something else, such as grinding pigment. This weakens the hypothesis.\\n\\nChoice A compares the stones with grain-grinding stones elsewhere, which supports the hypothesis. Choice B describes the shape of the hollows, which is consistent with grinding of any kind. Choice D shows that grain was present nearby, which also supports the hypothesis.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Agar toshlarda don tuyilgan bo‘lsa, chuqurchalarda don kraxmali izlari bo‘lishi kutiladi. C javob buning aksini aytadi: qizil mineral bo‘yoq izlari bor, kraxmal yo‘q. Bu toshlar boshqa maqsadda, masalan, bo‘yoq ezish uchun ishlatilganini ko‘rsatadi va gipotezani zaiflashtiradi.\\n\\nA toshlarni boshqa joylardagi don tuyiladigan toshlarga o‘xshatadi, bu gipotezani qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi. B chuqurchalar shaklini tasvirlaydi, bu har qanday ezish ishiga mos keladi. D yaqin atrofda don borligini ko‘rsatadi, bu ham gipotezani qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"finding_weaken\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In the nineteenth century, several water mills ground grain for the families of a mountain village in the Fergana Valley. Historian Malika Yoqubova claims that the mills were built and maintained by the villagers together rather than by a single wealthy landowner.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Yoqubova's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"Village records list dozens of households that contributed labor or money each year to repair the mills.\",\"option_b\":\"The mills were powered by a stream that flowed down from the surrounding mountains.\",\"option_c\":\"A wealthy landowner kept a detailed list of repairs to the mills and charged villagers a fee to use them.\",\"option_d\":\"Similar water mills were used in many other villages in the Fergana Valley.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Yoqubova claims that the villagers built and maintained the mills collectively. Choice A shows many households contributing labor or money for repairs every year, which is direct evidence of shared upkeep.\\n\\nChoice B explains how the mills were powered, not who maintained them. Choice C points to a single landowner controlling the mills, which would weaken the claim. Choice D says the mills were common in the region but reveals nothing about who built or maintained them.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Yoqubovaning da’vosiga ko‘ra, tegirmonlarni qishloq ahli birgalikda qurgan va saqlagan. A javob har yili ko‘plab xonadonlar ta’mirlash uchun mehnat yoki pul qo‘shganini ko‘rsatadi — bu birgalikdagi parvarishga bevosita dalil.\\n\\nB tegirmonlar qanday kuch bilan ishlaganini tushuntiradi, kim saqlaganini emas. C bitta boy yer egasi tegirmonlarni boshqarganini ko‘rsatadi, bu da’voni zaiflashtiradi. D bunday tegirmonlar mintaqada keng tarqalganini aytadi, lekin ularni kim qurgan yoki saqlaganini ochmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"finding_support\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Botanist Shohruh Aminov studies a wild mint that grows along streams in the foothills of the Chatkal Mountains. He has observed that the mint often grows tallest in shady spots, and he hypothesizes that shade increases the plant's height only when the soil is also moist. To test this hypothesis, he grew mint plants under four conditions: sun with moist soil, sun with dry soil, shade with moist soil, and shade with dry soil.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Aminov's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"Plants in shade with moist soil grew taller than plants in sun with dry soil.\",\"option_b\":\"Plants in shade with moist soil grew much taller than plants in sun with moist soil, but plants in shade with dry soil grew no taller than plants in sun with dry soil.\",\"option_c\":\"Plants in shade grew taller than plants in sun, whether the soil was moist or dry.\",\"option_d\":\"Plants with moist soil grew taller than plants with dry soil, in both sun and shade.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The hypothesis has two parts: shade increases height when the soil is moist, and shade does not increase height when the soil is dry. Choice B confirms both: shade made a large difference with moist soil and no difference with dry soil.\\n\\nChoice A changes both shade and moisture at once, so it cannot show what shade alone does under either condition. Choice C shows shade helping even in dry soil, which contradicts \\\"only when the soil is also moist.\\\" Choice D is about the effect of moisture, not about whether shade's effect depends on moisture.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gipoteza ikki qismdan iborat: tuproq nam bo‘lsa, soya bo‘yni oshiradi; tuproq quruq bo‘lsa, soya bo‘yni oshirmaydi. B javob ikkalasini ham tasdiqlaydi: nam tuproqda soya katta farq bergan, quruq tuproqda esa farq bermagan.\\n\\nA bir vaqtning o‘zida ham soyani, ham namlikni o‘zgartiradi, shuning uchun soyaning o‘zi nima qilishini ko‘rsata olmaydi. C soya quruq tuproqda ham yordam berganini ko‘rsatadi, bu \\\"faqat tuproq nam bo‘lganda\\\" degan shartga zid. D namlikning ta’siri haqida, soyaning ta’siri namlikka bog‘liqligi haqida emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"finding_support\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Many desert beetles have shiny, almost mirror-like shells. Entomologist Kamola Rashidova hypothesizes that this shine helps the beetles stay cool by reflecting sunlight. To test the idea, she placed two groups of beetles of the same species in direct sunlight: beetles with their natural shiny shells, and beetles whose shells she had covered with a thin, clear coating that removes the shine without changing the shell's color. She then measured the beetles' body temperatures.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken Rashidova's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"Shiny beetles of this species are found more often than dull-shelled beetles in the hottest parts of the desert.\",\"option_b\":\"Beetles of other species that have dull shells are active mostly at night.\",\"option_c\":\"The shiny beetles had lower body temperatures than the coated beetles did.\",\"option_d\":\"After an hour in the sun, the shiny beetles and the coated beetles had reached the same body temperatures.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"If shine keeps the beetles cool, the shiny beetles should stay cooler than the coated ones, which differ only in lacking shine. Choice D reports no difference in temperature, which suggests the shine does not help cooling, so it weakens the hypothesis.\\n\\nChoice A suggests shiny beetles do well in hot places, which is consistent with the hypothesis. Choice B suggests dull-shelled beetles avoid the daytime heat, which also fits the hypothesis. Choice C is the result the hypothesis predicts, so it supports rather than weakens it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Agar yaltiroqlik qo‘ng‘izlarni salqin saqlasa, yaltiroq qo‘ng‘izlar faqat yaltiroqligi yo‘qligi bilan farq qiladigan qoplangan qo‘ng‘izlardan salqinroq bo‘lishi kerak. D javob harorat farqi yo‘qligini aytadi, ya’ni yaltiroqlik sovishga yordam bermaydi — bu gipotezani zaiflashtiradi.\\n\\nA yaltiroq qo‘ng‘izlar issiq joylarda ko‘proq uchrashini aytadi, bu gipotezaga mos. B xira qobiqli qo‘ng‘izlar kunduzgi issiqdan qochishini ko‘rsatadi, bu ham gipotezaga mos. C gipoteza bashorat qilgan natija, shuning uchun uni zaiflashtirmaydi, aksincha qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"finding_weaken\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Psychologist Bekzod Alimov claims that the words used to describe a dish can change how much people enjoy eating it, even when the food itself is identical. In a university cafeteria, he served the same lentil soup on two different days. On one day, the menu listed it simply as “lentil soup”; on the other, it was listed as “slow-simmered mountain lentil soup.” Diners who ate the soup were asked to rate its taste.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Alimov's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"Diners gave the soup higher taste ratings on the day it was listed as “slow-simmered mountain lentil soup” than on the day it was listed as “lentil soup.”\",\"option_b\":\"More diners chose the soup on the day it was listed with the longer description than on the other day.\",\"option_c\":\"Diners gave the soup about the same taste ratings on both days.\",\"option_d\":\"Diners who ate the soup on both days said that they generally prefer soups with fewer ingredients.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Alimov's claim is about enjoyment: a more vivid description makes the same food taste better to people. Choice A shows higher taste ratings for the identical soup when it had the more vivid name.\\n\\nChoice B is a trap: it shows that the description affected how many people chose the soup, not how much they enjoyed eating it. Choice C shows no difference in enjoyment, which would weaken the claim. Choice D concerns a general preference that has nothing to do with the menu descriptions.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Alimovning da’vosi zavq haqida: ta’sirliroq tavsif bir xil taomni mazaliroq qilib ko‘rsatadi. A javob bir xil sho‘rva ta’sirliroq nom bilan berilganda yuqoriroq baholanganini ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nB tuzoq: u tavsif sho‘rvani nechta odam tanlaganiga ta’sir qilganini ko‘rsatadi, uni yeyishdan qanchalik zavq olganiga emas. C zavqda farq yo‘qligini ko‘rsatadi, bu da’voni zaiflashtiradi. D menyudagi tavsiflarga aloqasi yo‘q umumiy afzallik haqida.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"finding_support\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Linguist Anvar Saidov studies a group of words for weaving tools used in several villages along an old trade route. He argues that these words were not formed in the local language but were borrowed from Persian during a period in which Persian-speaking weavers settled in the region.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Saidov's argument?\",\"option_a\":\"The words appear in local written records from centuries before the Persian-speaking weavers arrived.\",\"option_b\":\"The Persian-speaking settlers also introduced new methods of dyeing cloth to the region.\",\"option_c\":\"The words first appear in local written records shortly after the settlers arrived, and they closely resemble Persian terms for the same tools.\",\"option_d\":\"Weavers in the villages today use several tools that are not found anywhere else along the trade route.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Saidov's argument has two parts: the words came from Persian, and they arrived when Persian-speaking weavers settled. Choice C supports both: the words resemble Persian terms for the same tools, and they first appear in records right after the settlers came.\\n\\nChoice A would weaken the argument, since words already in use before the settlers arrived could not have been borrowed from them. Choice B shows the settlers' influence on dyeing, but says nothing about the weaving words. Choice D concerns present-day tools, not the origin of the words.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Saidovning fikri ikki qismdan iborat: so‘zlar fors tilidan olingan va ular forsiyzabon to‘quvchilar ko‘chib kelgan davrda kirib kelgan. C javob ikkalasini ham qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi: so‘zlar xuddi shu asboblarning forscha nomlariga o‘xshaydi va yozma manbalarda to‘quvchilar ko‘chib kelgandan keyin paydo bo‘ladi.\\n\\nA fikrni zaiflashtiradi: ko‘chib kelganlardan oldin ishlatilgan so‘zlar ulardan o‘zlashtirilgan bo‘lishi mumkin emas. B ko‘chib kelganlarning bo‘yash usullariga ta’sirini ko‘rsatadi, lekin to‘quv so‘zlari haqida hech narsa demaydi. D so‘zlarning kelib chiqishi haqida emas, hozirgi asboblar haqida.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"finding_support\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"At the bottom of some lakes, sediment settles in thin, visible layers. Geologist Diyora Karimova hypothesizes that in one mountain lake in Kyrgyzstan, each pair of layers, one light and one dark, represents exactly one year: the light layer forms in summer and the dark layer in winter. If she is right, counting the pairs of layers would give an accurate record of the passage of time.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Karimova's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"Between two layers of volcanic ash from eruptions known to have occurred 214 years apart, Karimova counted 214 pairs of light and dark layers.\",\"option_b\":\"In the lake's sediment, the light layers are generally thicker than the dark layers.\",\"option_c\":\"Between two layers of volcanic ash from eruptions known to have occurred 214 years apart, Karimova counted 428 pairs of light and dark layers.\",\"option_d\":\"Thin layers of sediment are also found at the bottom of many other lakes in Central Asia.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"If each light-dark pair represents one year, then the number of pairs between two events should equal the number of years between them. Choice A gives exactly that match: 214 pairs between eruptions 214 years apart.\\n\\nChoice C reports twice as many pairs as years, which would mean that each pair represents about half a year, weakening the hypothesis. Choice B compares the thickness of the layers, which does not test whether a pair equals one year. Choice D notes that layers occur elsewhere but does not test what the layers in this lake represent.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Agar har bir och va to‘q qatlam jufti bir yilni bildirsa, ikki voqea orasidagi juftlar soni ular orasidagi yillar soniga teng bo‘lishi kerak. A javob aynan shu moslikni beradi: 214 yil farq bilan sodir bo‘lgan otilishlar orasida 214 juft.\\n\\nC yillardan ikki baravar ko‘p juft borligini aytadi, ya’ni har bir juft taxminan yarim yilni bildiradi — bu gipotezani zaiflashtiradi. B qatlamlar qalinligini solishtiradi, bu bir juft bir yilga tengligini tekshirmaydi. D boshqa ko‘llarda ham qatlamlar borligini aytadi, lekin bu ko‘ldagi qatlamlar nimani bildirishini tekshirmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"finding_support\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Animal behaviorist Ulugbek Sobirov studies how young goats learn to solve problems. He hypothesizes that a young goat learns to open a latched food box more quickly after watching its own mother open the box than after watching an unfamiliar adult goat open it. To test this, he divided young goats into three groups: those that watched their mother open the box, those that watched an unfamiliar adult open it, and those that saw no demonstration at all.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken Sobirov's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"The young goats that watched their mother opened the box much more quickly than the young goats that saw no demonstration.\",\"option_b\":\"Young goats in both demonstration groups opened the box more quickly than young goats that saw no demonstration.\",\"option_c\":\"The young goats that watched an unfamiliar adult opened the box just as quickly as those that watched their mother, and both groups were faster than those that saw no demonstration.\",\"option_d\":\"Adult goats that had raised young opened the box more quickly than adult goats that had not.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The hypothesis compares two specific groups: goats that watched their mother and goats that watched an unfamiliar adult, predicting that the first group will be faster. Choice C says these two groups were equally fast, so the mother's demonstration gave no special advantage. This directly weakens the hypothesis.\\n\\nChoice A compares the mother group with the no-demonstration group, the wrong comparison; it shows that watching helps, not that watching the mother helps more. Choice B also compares demonstration with no demonstration and says nothing about mother versus unfamiliar adult. Choice D concerns adult goats' own skill, not how young goats learn.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gipoteza ikki aniq guruhni solishtiradi: onasini kuzatgan uloqlar va notanish katta echkini kuzatgan uloqlar; birinchisi tezroq bo‘ladi deb taxmin qilinadi. C javob bu ikki guruh bir xil tezlikda bo‘lganini aytadi, ya’ni onaning ko‘rsatib berishi alohida ustunlik bermagan. Bu gipotezani bevosita zaiflashtiradi.\\n\\nA ona guruhini hech narsa ko‘rmagan guruh bilan solishtiradi — bu noto‘g‘ri taqqoslash; u kuzatish foyda berishini ko‘rsatadi, onani kuzatish ko‘proq foyda berishini emas. B ham ko‘rsatib berilgan va berilmagan guruhlarni solishtiradi, ona va notanish echka haqida hech narsa demaydi. D uloqlar qanday o‘rganishi haqida emas, katta echkilarning o‘z mahorati haqida.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"finding_weaken\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Historian Petra Lindqvist claims that in sixteenth-century northern Europe, having a local printing press did more to raise a town's literacy than simply having access to printed books did. She notes that towns with presses could produce cheap pamphlets and schoolbooks in the local language, not just the expensive volumes that traveling merchants carried from distant cities.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Lindqvist's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"Literacy rose faster in towns that had printing presses than in towns that had no access to printed books at all.\",\"option_b\":\"Towns that had printing presses also tended to have more merchants than towns without presses.\",\"option_c\":\"Printed books sold by traveling merchants were usually more expensive than pamphlets printed locally.\",\"option_d\":\"Among towns that regularly bought printed books from traveling merchants, literacy rose faster in towns that also had their own printing press than in towns that did not.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Lindqvist's claim is a comparison: a local press raised literacy more than access to printed books alone. To test it, one must compare towns that had books and a press with towns that had books but no press. Choice D makes exactly this comparison and finds faster literacy growth where there was a press.\\n\\nChoice A compares press towns with towns that had no printed books at all, so the difference could come from access to books in general rather than from the press. Choice B points to a difference (more merchants) that could explain higher literacy in press towns, which would weaken the claim. Choice C repeats Lindqvist's background point about cost but gives no evidence about literacy.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Lindqvistning da’vosi taqqoslashdan iborat: mahalliy bosmaxona savodxonlikni bosma kitoblardan foydalanish imkoniyatining o‘zidan ko‘ra ko‘proq oshirgan. Buni tekshirish uchun kitobi ham, bosmaxonasi ham bor shaharlarni kitobi bor, lekin bosmaxonasi yo‘q shaharlar bilan solishtirish kerak. D javob aynan shunday taqqoslaydi va bosmaxona bor joyda savodxonlik tezroq o‘sganini ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nA bosmaxonali shaharlarni umuman bosma kitob bo‘lmagan shaharlar bilan solishtiradi, shuning uchun farq bosmaxonadan emas, umuman kitob borligidan bo‘lishi mumkin. B bosmaxonali shaharlarda savodxonlik yuqoriligini boshqacha tushuntirishi mumkin bo‘lgan farqni (savdogarlar ko‘pligi) ko‘rsatadi, bu da’voni zaiflashtiradi. C Lindqvistning narx haqidagi fon fikrini takrorlaydi, lekin savodxonlik haqida dalil bermaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"finding_support\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In 2023, a midsize city added a dedicated bus lane along Route 7, its busiest bus route. Over the following year, ridership on Route 7 rose by 18 percent. Transportation analyst Diyor Hasanov concludes that the new lane caused the increase by making the buses faster and more reliable. Critics point out, however, that fuel prices in the city rose sharply in 2023, which may have pushed many drivers to switch to buses.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken Hasanov's conclusion?\",\"option_a\":\"After the lane opened, average travel times on Route 7 fell by several minutes.\",\"option_b\":\"Over the same year, ridership rose by about 18 percent on the city's other bus routes, none of which received a dedicated lane.\",\"option_c\":\"Surveys showed that most new riders on Route 7 had previously traveled by car.\",\"option_d\":\"Fuel prices in the city fell slightly in the year after the lane opened.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"If the lane caused the increase, Route 7 should have gained more riders than routes without a lane. Choice B shows that routes without a lane gained riders at the same rate, which suggests a citywide cause, such as fuel prices, rather than the lane. This weakens Hasanov's conclusion.\\n\\nChoice A shows that the lane made buses faster, which is consistent with Hasanov's explanation. Choice C fits both explanations, since drivers could have switched because of either the lane or fuel prices, so it does not weaken his conclusion. Choice D would actually undercut the critics' alternative explanation, making Hasanov's conclusion more plausible, not less.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Agar yo‘lak o‘sishga sabab bo‘lgan bo‘lsa, 7-yo‘nalish yo‘lagi bo‘lmagan yo‘nalishlarga qaraganda ko‘proq yo‘lovchi qo‘shib olishi kerak edi. B javob yo‘lagi bo‘lmagan yo‘nalishlarda ham yo‘lovchilar xuddi shunday sur’atda ko‘payganini ko‘rsatadi, bu yo‘lak emas, butun shaharga ta’sir qilgan sabab (masalan, yoqilg‘i narxi) borligini bildiradi. Bu Hasanovning xulosasini zaiflashtiradi.\\n\\nA yo‘lak avtobuslarni tezlashtirganini ko‘rsatadi, bu Hasanovning tushuntirishiga mos. C ikkala tushuntirishga ham mos keladi: haydovchilar yo‘lak tufayli ham, yoqilg‘i narxi tufayli ham o‘tgan bo‘lishi mumkin, shuning uchun xulosani zaiflashtirmaydi. D tanqidchilarning muqobil tushuntirishini zaiflashtiradi va Hasanovning xulosasini ishonarliroq qiladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"finding_weaken\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nMany people say they sleep badly around the time of a full moon. In a 2019 laboratory study of 40 volunteers, sleep researcher Saida Nurmatova found some support for this belief: on nights near the full moon, participants took an average of five minutes longer to fall asleep, even though they could not see the sky from the windowless lab.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nSleep researcher Bekzod Aliyev analyzed records from more than 5,000 nights of sleep collected from about 900 people in several sleep clinics. When he grouped the nights by the phase of the moon, he found no meaningful difference in how long people took to fall asleep or how long they slept.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Aliyev (Text 2) most likely respond to Nurmatova's finding (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"He would argue that it shows the moon affects sleep only when people can see it.\",\"option_b\":\"He would question whether it holds generally, since his much larger set of records showed no such effect.\",\"option_c\":\"He would agree with it, since his records also showed that people slept less near the full moon.\",\"option_d\":\"He would argue that people fall asleep faster, not slower, near the full moon.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Nurmatova found that, in a small study of 40 people, falling asleep took longer near the full moon. Aliyev studied more than 5,000 nights from about 900 people and found “no meaningful difference.” He would therefore doubt that her result applies generally, as choice B says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Nurmatova's participants could not see the sky, and Aliyev's data say nothing about seeing the moon. Choice C is wrong because Aliyev found no difference. Choice D is wrong because Aliyev found no effect in either direction.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Nurmatova 40 kishilik kichik tadqiqotda to‘lin oy paytida uxlab qolish uzoqroq vaqt olishini aniqlagan. Aliyev esa taxminan 900 kishining 5 000 dan ortiq tunini o‘rganib, “sezilarli farq yo‘q” degan xulosaga kelgan. Demak, u Nurmatovaning natijasi umumiy holatda to‘g‘ri ekaniga shubha qiladi — B javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Nurmatovaning ishtirokchilari osmonni ko‘ra olmagan, Aliyevning ma’lumotlari esa oyni ko‘rish haqida hech narsa demaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: Aliyev hech qanday farq topmagan. D noto‘g‘ri: Aliyev hech qaysi yo‘nalishda ta’sir topmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science\",\"respond-to-claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nThe medieval trading city of Sarbazar, which flourished on a caravan route between China and Persia, was largely abandoned by about 1450. Many historians have explained its decline as a result of the rise of sea trade: once merchants began shipping goods by sea, they argue, the overland caravans that had brought Sarbazar its wealth stopped coming.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nExcavations at Sarbazar led by archaeologist Feruza Hasanova show that the city's population had already fallen sharply by about 1390, after an epidemic that left mass graves in several districts. Large-scale sea trade between China and Persia, Hasanova notes, did not overtake overland trade until several decades after that.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Hasanova (Text 2) most likely respond to the explanation described in Text 1?\",\"option_a\":\"She would agree that sea trade began before the epidemic struck Sarbazar.\",\"option_b\":\"She would argue that Sarbazar was never an important stop on the caravan route.\",\"option_c\":\"She would argue that the epidemic had little effect on Sarbazar's population.\",\"option_d\":\"She would argue that Sarbazar's decline was already well underway before sea trade could have caused it.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Text 1 says sea trade caused Sarbazar's decline. Hasanova shows that the population fell sharply around 1390 after an epidemic, and that sea trade did not overtake overland trade until decades later. She would argue that the decline began before sea trade could have caused it, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Hasanova says the reverse: large-scale sea trade came decades after the epidemic. Choice B is wrong because Text 2 does not challenge Sarbazar's importance on the caravan route. Choice C contradicts Text 2, which links the epidemic to a sharp population drop and mass graves.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Text 1 ga ko‘ra, Sarbazarning tanazzuliga dengiz savdosi sabab bo‘lgan. Hasanova esa aholi taxminan 1390-yilda epidemiyadan keyin keskin kamayganini va dengiz savdosi quruqlikdagi savdodan faqat bir necha o‘n yil o‘tib o‘zib ketganini ko‘rsatadi. Demak, u tanazzul dengiz savdosi unga sabab bo‘lishidan oldinroq boshlangan deb ta’kidlaydi — D javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Hasanova buning aksini aytadi — keng ko‘lamli dengiz savdosi epidemiyadan o‘n yillar keyin boshlangan. B noto‘g‘ri: Text 2 Sarbazarning karvon yo‘lidagi ahamiyatini inkor etmaydi. C Text 2 ga zid: unda epidemiya aholining keskin kamayishi va ommaviy qabrlar bilan bog‘langan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"history\",\"respond-to-claim\",\"timeline\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nMany parents and teachers believe that more homework leads to better results in school. If an hour of homework each evening helps students learn, they reason, two or three hours should help even more.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nEducation researcher Madina Sharipova compared the nightly homework time of 6,000 secondary school students with their scores on national exams. Students who did about an hour of homework a night scored noticeably higher than those who did none. But students who did two or three hours scored no higher, on average, than those who did one hour, and they reported much higher levels of stress.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Sharipova (Text 2) most likely respond to the reasoning described in Text 1?\",\"option_a\":\"She would agree that some homework is helpful but argue that the benefits stop increasing beyond about an hour a night.\",\"option_b\":\"She would argue that homework has no effect on students' exam scores.\",\"option_c\":\"She would agree that two or three hours of homework produce the highest exam scores.\",\"option_d\":\"She would argue that students who do no homework score as high as those who do an hour.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Text 1 reasons that if one hour helps, more hours help more. Sharipova found that an hour of homework was linked to higher scores than none, but two or three hours brought no further gain. She would agree that some homework helps but reject the idea that more is always better, as choice A says.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because Sharipova found that an hour of homework was linked to higher scores. Choice C is wrong because students doing two or three hours scored no higher than those doing one. Choice D is wrong because students doing about an hour scored “noticeably higher” than those doing none.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Text 1 dagi mulohaza: agar bir soat yordam bersa, ko‘proq soat ko‘proq yordam beradi. Sharipova esa bir soatlik uy vazifasi umuman qilmaslikka nisbatan yuqoriroq ball bilan bog‘liqligini, lekin ikki-uch soat qo‘shimcha foyda bermasligini aniqlagan. Demak, u bir oz uy vazifasi foydali ekaniga qo‘shiladi, ammo “qancha ko‘p bo‘lsa, shuncha yaxshi” degan fikrni rad etadi — A javob.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: Sharipovaning ma’lumotlariga ko‘ra, bir soatlik uy vazifasi yuqoriroq ball bilan bog‘liq. C noto‘g‘ri: ikki-uch soat shug‘ullanganlar bir soat shug‘ullanganlardan yuqori ball olmagan. D noto‘g‘ri: taxminan bir soat shug‘ullanganlar umuman qilmaganlardan “sezilarli darajada yuqori” ball olgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"social-science\",\"respond-to-claim\",\"qualify\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nComposer Yusuf Akbarov (1898–1961) is often remembered for his symphonies, but music historian Lola Karimova argues that his most lasting achievement was his collection of folk songs. Between 1925 and 1940, Akbarov traveled to more than two hundred villages, writing down melodies that had never been recorded and that many younger villagers no longer knew.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nEthnomusicologist Sardor Nazarov notes that several of the village songs Akbarov wrote down can no longer be found anywhere else. Nazarov criticizes Akbarov for sometimes “correcting” irregular rhythms to fit European notation, but he acknowledges that without Akbarov's notebooks, these melodies would have been lost entirely.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, Karimova (Text 1) and Nazarov (Text 2) would most likely agree with which statement?\",\"option_a\":\"Akbarov's symphonies are more important than his folk song collection.\",\"option_b\":\"Akbarov recorded the folk melodies exactly as villagers performed them.\",\"option_c\":\"Akbarov's collecting work preserved melodies that might otherwise have disappeared.\",\"option_d\":\"Akbarov should have recorded only the songs that younger villagers still knew.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Karimova says Akbarov wrote down melodies that “had never been recorded” and that younger villagers no longer knew. Nazarov says some of these songs exist nowhere else and would have been “lost entirely” without Akbarov's notebooks. Both would agree with choice C.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Karimova argues the opposite, and Nazarov does not discuss the symphonies. Choice B is wrong because Nazarov says Akbarov sometimes changed irregular rhythms. Choice D is wrong because neither author suggests this; both value the songs that were being forgotten.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Karimovaning aytishicha, Akbarov “hech qachon yozib olinmagan” va yosh qishloqdoshlar endi bilmaydigan kuylarni yozib olgan. Nazarovning fikricha, bu qo‘shiqlarning ba’zilari boshqa hech qayerda uchramaydi va Akbarovning daftarlarisiz ular “butunlay yo‘qolib ketgan” bo‘lardi. Ikkalasi ham C ga qo‘shiladi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Karimova buning aksini ta’kidlaydi, Nazarov esa simfoniyalarni muhokama qilmaydi. B noto‘g‘ri: Nazarovning aytishicha, Akbarov ba’zan notekis ritmlarni o‘zgartirgan. D noto‘g‘ri: hech bir muallif bunday demaydi; ikkalasi ham unutilib borayotgan qo‘shiqlarni qadrlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"humanities\",\"both-agree\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nFor much of the twentieth century, many scientists assumed that feathers first evolved in the ancestors of birds as an adaptation for flight. On this view, feathers and flying developed together: the earliest feathers would have appeared because they helped animals glide or fly.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nFossils discovered in China since the 1990s show that many dinosaurs that could not fly, such as the small predator *Sinosauropteryx*, were covered with simple, hair-like feathers. Feathers this simple could not have supported flight. Many paleontologists therefore conclude that feathers first served other purposes, such as keeping the body warm or attracting mates, and were only later adapted for flight.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would many paleontologists described in Text 2 most likely respond to the assumption described in Text 1?\",\"option_a\":\"They would agree with it, since *Sinosauropteryx* used its feathers to glide.\",\"option_b\":\"They would argue that feathers have never been used for flight.\",\"option_c\":\"They would reject it, since feathers appear to have existed in animals that could not fly before they were used for flight.\",\"option_d\":\"They would argue that the dinosaurs found in China were actually early birds.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Text 1 assumes that feathers first appeared as an adaptation for flight. Text 2 describes nonflying dinosaurs with simple feathers that could not support flight and concludes that feathers first served other purposes and were “only later adapted for flight.” The paleontologists would reject the assumption, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Text 2 says *Sinosauropteryx* could not fly and its feathers could not support flight. Choice B is too extreme: Text 2 says feathers were later adapted for flight. Choice D is wrong because Text 2 describes these animals as dinosaurs that could not fly, not as birds.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Text 1 patlar dastlab uchishga moslashuv sifatida paydo bo‘lgan deb taxmin qiladi. Text 2 esa uchishni qo‘llab-quvvatlay olmaydigan oddiy patlarga ega, ucha olmaydigan dinozavrlarni tasvirlaydi va patlar avval boshqa vazifalarni bajargan, uchish uchun esa “faqat keyinroq moslashgan” degan xulosaga keladi. Demak, paleontologlar bu taxminni rad etadi — C javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Text 2 ga ko‘ra, *Sinosauropteryx* ucha olmagan va uning patlari uchishni ta’minlay olmas edi. B juda keskin: Text 2 da patlar keyinroq uchish uchun moslashgani aytiladi. D noto‘g‘ri: Text 2 bu hayvonlarni qushlar emas, ucha olmaydigan dinozavrlar deb ta’riflaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science\",\"respond-to-claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nIn a review of the English translation of Uzbek novelist Hamid Ortiq's comic novel *The Borrowed Donkey*, critic Daniel Hale writes that the book's humor did not survive the journey into English. Much of the original's comedy, Hale notes, depends on puns that have no English equivalents, and the translation, he complains, “reads like a joke being explained.”\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nTranslator Aziza Rahmonova says that when she translates comic fiction, she does not try to reproduce each pun word for word, which is usually impossible. Instead, she invents new wordplay in the target language that produces a similar effect at roughly the same point in the story, even if the joke itself is different.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Rahmonova (Text 2) most likely respond to Hale's complaint (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"She would agree that humor based on puns can never be translated.\",\"option_b\":\"She would suggest that the problem Hale describes could be addressed by replacing untranslatable puns with new wordplay in English.\",\"option_c\":\"She would argue that translators should add footnotes explaining each pun.\",\"option_d\":\"She would argue that *The Borrowed Donkey* is not really a comic novel.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Hale complains that the puns could not be carried into English, so the humor was lost. Rahmonova describes a solution to exactly this problem: invent new wordplay in the target language that has a similar effect. She would suggest that the problem could be addressed this way, as choice B says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Rahmonova believes humor can be carried over through new wordplay. Choice C is wrong because she does not mention footnotes; explaining jokes is what Hale already criticizes. Choice D is wrong because neither text questions that the novel is comic.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Hale so‘z o‘yinlarini ingliz tiliga o‘tkazib bo‘lmagani uchun hazil yo‘qolganidan shikoyat qiladi. Rahmonova aynan shu muammoning yechimini tasvirlaydi: tarjima tilida o‘xshash ta’sir beradigan yangi so‘z o‘yinini o‘ylab topish. Demak, u muammoni shu yo‘l bilan hal qilish mumkin deb aytadi — B javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Rahmonova hazilni yangi so‘z o‘yinlari orqali o‘tkazish mumkin deb hisoblaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: u izohlar haqida gapirmaydi; hazillarni tushuntirishni Hale allaqachon tanqid qilgan. D noto‘g‘ri: hech bir matn romanning kulgili ekaniga shubha qilmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"humanities\",\"respond-to-claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nIn cities, many trees produce leaves in spring several days earlier than trees of the same species in the surrounding countryside. Ecologist Gulshan Yo‘ldosheva attributes this difference to the urban heat island effect: buildings and pavement absorb heat during the day and release it at night, so cities stay warmer than rural areas, and warmth triggers early budding.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nPlant biologist Ravshan Ismoilov studied trees along streets in a single city. He found that trees standing within a few meters of a streetlight budded about four days earlier than nearby trees of the same species that received little artificial light at night, even though air temperatures at the two sets of trees were nearly identical.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Ismoilov (Text 2) most likely respond to Yo‘ldosheva's explanation (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"He would argue that her explanation may be incomplete, since artificial light at night also appears to cause earlier budding.\",\"option_b\":\"He would argue that cities are not actually warmer than surrounding rural areas.\",\"option_c\":\"He would agree that temperature is the only factor that determines when trees produce leaves.\",\"option_d\":\"He would argue that trees in cities produce leaves later than trees in the countryside.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Yo‘ldosheva explains early urban budding by warmth alone. Ismoilov found that trees near streetlights budded earlier even when temperatures were nearly the same, so light also seems to play a role. He would argue that her explanation leaves out a factor, as choice A says.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because Ismoilov's study does not measure differences between city and countryside temperatures. Choice C is wrong because his findings point to a factor other than temperature. Choice D contradicts both texts, which describe earlier budding in cities.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Yo‘ldosheva shaharlarda daraxtlarning erta kurtak chiqarishini faqat issiqlik bilan izohlaydi. Ismoilov esa harorat deyarli bir xil bo‘lsa ham, ko‘cha chiroqlari yonidagi daraxtlar ertaroq kurtak chiqarganini aniqlagan — demak, yorug‘lik ham rol o‘ynaydi. U Yo‘ldoshevaning izohida bir omil e’tibordan chetda qolgan deydi — A javob.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: Ismoilovning tadqiqoti shahar va qishloq haroratini solishtirmaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: uning natijalari haroratdan boshqa omilga ishora qiladi. D ikkala matnga ham zid: ikkalasida ham shaharlarda kurtak ertaroq chiqishi aytilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science\",\"respond-to-claim\",\"alternative-factor\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nWhen grocery stores in the city of Termiz were required to post their prices on a public website, economist Akmal Saidov found that the average price of a basket of basic foods across the city fell by 6 percent within a year. Saidov argues that making prices easy to compare forces stores to compete and benefits shoppers everywhere.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nEconomist Zilola Murodova examined Saidov's data store by store. She found that nearly all of the price decline occurred in neighborhoods with three or more grocery stores within walking distance. In neighborhoods served by a single store, prices barely changed, and in a few they rose slightly.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Murodova (Text 2) most likely respond to Saidov's argument (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"She would argue that the city's average price did not actually fall.\",\"option_b\":\"She would agree that shoppers in every neighborhood benefited equally from the policy.\",\"option_c\":\"She would argue that publishing prices online caused prices to rise across the city.\",\"option_d\":\"She would argue that the benefits of price transparency depend on whether shoppers have nearby stores to choose among.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Saidov claims that publishing prices benefits “shoppers everywhere.” Murodova found that prices fell mainly where several stores were close together and barely changed where only one store served the area. She would argue that the benefit depends on local competition, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Murodova does not dispute the citywide average; she shows where the decline happened. Choice B is wrong because her data show unequal effects. Choice C is wrong because prices rose only slightly in a few single-store neighborhoods, not across the city.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Saidov narxlarni e’lon qilish “hamma joydagi xaridorlar”ga foyda keltiradi deb da’vo qiladi. Murodova esa narxlar asosan bir nechta do‘kon yaqin joylashgan hududlarda tushganini, faqat bitta do‘kon bor joylarda esa deyarli o‘zgarmaganini aniqlagan. U foyda mahalliy raqobatga bog‘liq deb ta’kidlaydi — D javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Murodova shahar bo‘yicha o‘rtacha ko‘rsatkichga e’tiroz bildirmaydi, faqat pasayish qayerda bo‘lganini ko‘rsatadi. B noto‘g‘ri: uning ma’lumotlari ta’sir notekis ekanini ko‘rsatadi. C noto‘g‘ri: narxlar butun shahar bo‘ylab emas, faqat bir nechta bitta do‘konli hududda biroz oshgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"social-science\",\"respond-to-claim\",\"qualify\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nPrinted almanacs, small yearly books containing calendars, weather predictions, and farming advice, became common in the region of Zarafshan in the late 1800s. Historian Timur Bakirov argues that they were read mainly by wealthy townspeople. Because most rural families could not read, he reasons, almanacs would have been of little use to them.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nHistorian Shahlo Nurova studied over 300 inventories listing the belongings of rural households in Zarafshan between 1880 and 1910. She found almanacs listed in nearly a third of them, often in homes where no one was recorded as literate. Local accounts describe village schoolteachers and imams reading almanacs aloud to neighbors at planting time.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Nurova (Text 2) most likely respond to Bakirov's reasoning (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"She would agree that almanacs were found mostly in towns rather than villages.\",\"option_b\":\"She would argue that his reasoning overlooks the possibility that people who could not read still used almanacs by hearing them read aloud.\",\"option_c\":\"She would argue that most rural families in Zarafshan could, in fact, read.\",\"option_d\":\"She would argue that almanacs contained little information useful to farmers.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Bakirov reasons that because most rural people could not read, almanacs were of little use to them. Nurova found almanacs in many rural homes where no one was literate and describes teachers and imams reading them aloud. She would argue that illiterate villagers could still use almanacs by listening, as choice B says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Nurova found almanacs in nearly a third of rural households. Choice C is wrong because she does not claim most rural families could read; her evidence concerns homes where no one was literate. Choice D is wrong because both texts say almanacs contained farming advice, and Nurova shows they were read at planting time.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bakirovning mulohazasi: qishloq aholisining ko‘pchiligi o‘qiy olmagani uchun taqvimlar ularga deyarli foyda bermagan. Nurova esa hech kim savodli bo‘lmagan ko‘plab qishloq xonadonlarida taqvimlar borligini aniqlagan va o‘qituvchilar hamda imomlar ularni ovoz chiqarib o‘qib berganini tasvirlaydi. U o‘qiy olmaydigan qishloqdoshlar ham taqvimlardan tinglash orqali foydalanishi mumkin bo‘lgan deb ta’kidlaydi — B javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Nurova qishloq xonadonlarining qariyb uchdan birida taqvim topgan. C noto‘g‘ri: u qishloq oilalarining ko‘pchiligi o‘qiy olgan deb da’vo qilmaydi; uning dalillari hech kim savodli bo‘lmagan uylarga tegishli. D noto‘g‘ri: ikkala matnda ham taqvimlarda dehqonchilik maslahatlari bo‘lgani aytiladi, Nurova esa ular ekish paytida o‘qilganini ko‘rsatadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"history\",\"respond-to-reasoning\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nSome flowering plants add small amounts of caffeine to their nectar. In laboratory experiments, biologist Nigora Rasulova found that honeybees fed a sugar solution containing caffeine were about three times as likely to remember a flower's scent a day later as bees fed sugar alone. She suggests that caffeine helps plants by making bees more likely to return to them.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nEcologist Jamshid Usmonov studied honeybees foraging outdoors. Bees offered caffeinated sugar solution returned to it more often and recruited more nestmates to it than bees offered the same solution without caffeine, even when the caffeinated solution was less sweet. Usmonov argues that caffeine may lead bees to overrate a food source, which benefits the plant but could reduce the amount of food the colony collects.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, Rasulova (Text 1) and Usmonov (Text 2) would most likely agree with which statement?\",\"option_a\":\"Caffeine in nectar makes bees more likely to come back to the plants that produce it.\",\"option_b\":\"Caffeine in nectar helps bee colonies collect more food.\",\"option_c\":\"Bees cannot detect caffeine in nectar.\",\"option_d\":\"Caffeine in nectar has a greater effect on bees in the laboratory than outdoors.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Rasulova found that caffeine improves bees' memory of a flower's scent and suggests it makes them “more likely to return.” Usmonov found that bees returned more often to caffeinated solution. Both texts therefore support choice A.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because Usmonov suggests caffeine could reduce the food a colony collects, and Rasulova does not discuss colony food. Choice C is wrong because both studies show bees respond to caffeine, and neither claims they cannot detect it. Choice D is wrong because neither author compares the size of the effect in the two settings.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Rasulovaning aniqlashicha, kofein asalarilarning gul hidini eslab qolishini yaxshilaydi va u ularni “qaytib kelishga ko‘proq moyil” qiladi deb taxmin qiladi. Usmonov esa asalarilar kofeinli eritmaga tez-tez qaytganini aniqlagan. Demak, ikkala matn ham A ni qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: Usmonov kofein koloniya yig‘adigan oziqni kamaytirishi mumkin deydi, Rasulova esa bu masalani muhokama qilmaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: ikkala tadqiqot ham asalarilar kofeinga javob berishini ko‘rsatadi, hech kim ular uni sezmaydi demaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: hech bir muallif ikki sharoitdagi ta’sir kuchini taqqoslamaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science\",\"both-agree\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nThe poems of Kamila Oripova, written in the 1920s and 1930s, are filled with quiet orchards, clear streams, and birdsong. Critic Erkin Haydarov reads them as a retreat from the world: at a time of rapid change, he argues, Oripova turned away from public life and wrote about an untouched countryside that offered refuge from the problems of her day.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nLiterary scholar Dilfuza Mamatova points out that in the years Oripova was writing, new factories had begun pouring waste into the very rivers near her home village. Read against this background, Mamatova argues, Oripova's insistently clear streams are not an escape from her era's problems but a quiet reminder of what was being lost, a form of protest that did not need to name its target.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Mamatova (Text 2) most likely respond to Haydarov's interpretation (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"She would agree that Oripova avoided writing about the countryside.\",\"option_b\":\"She would argue that Oripova's poems openly criticized the factory owners by name.\",\"option_c\":\"She would agree that Oripova's poems have nothing to do with the events of her time.\",\"option_d\":\"She would argue that the images Haydarov sees as an escape may instead be an indirect response to the problems of Oripova's time.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Haydarov reads Oripova's clear streams and orchards as a retreat from the problems of her time. Mamatova argues that, given the pollution of nearby rivers, these images are “a quiet reminder of what was being lost,” a protest that does not name its target. She would see the same images as an indirect response to the era's problems, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because both critics agree Oripova wrote about the countryside. Choice B is wrong because Mamatova says the protest “did not need to name its target.” Choice C is the view Mamatova argues against.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Haydarov Oripovaning tiniq soylari va bog‘larini o‘z davri muammolaridan qochish deb talqin qiladi. Mamatova esa yaqin daryolarning ifloslanishini hisobga olib, bu obrazlar “yo‘qolib borayotgan narsaning sokin eslatmasi”, nishonini nomlamaydigan norozilik ekanini ta’kidlaydi. U aynan shu obrazlarni davr muammolariga bilvosita javob deb biladi — D javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: ikkala tanqidchi ham Oripova qishloq tabiati haqida yozganiga qo‘shiladi. B noto‘g‘ri: Mamatovaning aytishicha, norozilik “nishonini nomlashga muhtoj bo‘lmagan”. C — Mamatova aynan shu fikrga qarshi chiqadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"literature\",\"respond-to-interpretation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nWhen a large employer in Samarkand began enrolling new workers in its retirement savings plan automatically, unless they chose to opt out, participation among new hires jumped from 35 percent to 90 percent. Economist Botir Xolmatov argues that such automatic enrollment is a simple and powerful way to increase how much workers save.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nEconomist Nargiza Jo‘rayeva tracked the same workers for four years. She confirmed that automatic enrollment greatly increased participation. However, many of the automatically enrolled workers stayed at the plan's low default contribution of 2 percent of pay, whereas workers who had signed up on their own in earlier years usually chose to contribute 5 percent or more. As a result, total savings rose by much less than the jump in participation suggested.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Jo‘rayeva (Text 2) most likely respond to Xolmatov's argument (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"She would reject it, since automatic enrollment did not increase participation.\",\"option_b\":\"She would argue that workers should be required to contribute at least 5 percent of their pay.\",\"option_c\":\"She would agree that automatic enrollment raised participation but caution that its effect on the amount workers save may be smaller than he suggests.\",\"option_d\":\"She would argue that workers who sign up on their own save less than those who are enrolled automatically.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Xolmatov calls automatic enrollment a powerful way to increase saving. Jo‘rayeva confirms that participation rose but finds that many enrolled workers contributed only 2 percent, so total savings grew “by much less” than participation. She would accept part of his claim but qualify how much it raises saving, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Jo‘rayeva confirms the jump in participation. Choice B is wrong because she makes no recommendation about required contributions. Choice D reverses her finding: workers who signed up on their own usually contributed more.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Xolmatov avtomatik ro‘yxatga olishni jamg‘armani oshirishning kuchli usuli deb ataydi. Jo‘rayeva ishtirok oshganini tasdiqlaydi, lekin ko‘plab xodimlar faqat 2 foiz ajratgani sababli umumiy jamg‘arma ishtirokka qaraganda “ancha kam” oshganini aniqlaydi. U da’voning bir qismini qabul qiladi, ammo jamg‘arma qanchalik oshishiga aniqlik kiritadi — C javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Jo‘rayeva ishtirokning keskin oshganini tasdiqlaydi. B noto‘g‘ri: u majburiy badal haqida tavsiya bermaydi. D uning natijasini teskari qiladi: o‘zi ro‘yxatdan o‘tgan xodimlar odatda ko‘proq ajratgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"social-science\",\"respond-to-claim\",\"qualify\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nAbout 13,000 years ago, many large mammals in the Americas, including mammoths and giant ground sloths, disappeared. Paleontologist Odil Rahmatov argues that human hunters were responsible. He notes that in several regions the extinctions followed closely after the first clear evidence of human arrival, a pattern he considers too consistent to be coincidence.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nPaleoecologist Saodat Komilova accepts that the timing links humans to the extinctions. But her analysis of ancient pollen shows that the same period brought rapid climate swings that shrank the open grasslands many large grazers depended on. Populations already crowded into shrinking habitats, she argues, would have been far more vulnerable to hunting than populations in stable conditions.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Komilova (Text 2) most likely respond to Rahmatov's argument (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"She would agree that hunting played a role but argue that his account overlooks how climate change may have made the animals more vulnerable to it.\",\"option_b\":\"She would reject it entirely, arguing that climate change alone caused the extinctions.\",\"option_c\":\"She would argue that humans arrived in the Americas long after the large mammals had disappeared.\",\"option_d\":\"She would agree that hunting was the only important cause of the extinctions.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Rahmatov attributes the extinctions to human hunters. Komilova “accepts that the timing links humans to the extinctions” but adds that climate swings shrank grasslands, leaving populations “far more vulnerable to hunting.” She would accept a role for hunting while arguing that Rahmatov leaves out climate, as choice A says.\\n\\nChoice B is too extreme: Komilova does not reject a human role and says populations were vulnerable “to hunting.” Choice C contradicts both texts, which link the extinctions to the time of human arrival. Choice D ignores her point that climate change also mattered.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Rahmatov qirilib ketishni odam ovchilariga bog‘laydi. Komilova “vaqt jihatidan odamlar qirilib ketish bilan bog‘liqligini qabul qiladi”, ammo iqlim tebranishlari yaylovlarni qisqartirib, populyatsiyalarni “ovga nisbatan ancha zaif” qilib qo‘yganini qo‘shimcha qiladi. U ovning rolini tan oladi, lekin Rahmatov iqlimni e’tibordan chetda qoldirgan deydi — A javob.\\n\\nB juda keskin: Komilova odamlarning rolini rad etmaydi va populyatsiyalar aynan “ovga nisbatan” zaif bo‘lgan deydi. C ikkala matnga ham zid: ikkalasida ham qirilish odamlar kelgan davr bilan bog‘lanadi. D uning iqlim o‘zgarishi ham muhim bo‘lgan degan fikrini e’tiborsiz qoldiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science\",\"respond-to-argument\",\"too-extreme-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nHistorian Ulug‘bek Fayziyev argues that the nineteenth-century botanist Zuhra Inoyatova worked in near-complete isolation. In letters to the wealthy patrons who funded her expeditions, Inoyatova repeatedly described herself as “a solitary gatherer of weeds” who “knows nothing of what the learned men are doing,” which Fayziyev takes as evidence that she had little contact with other scientists.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nHistorian Malohat Sodiqova notes that letters to patrons in this period followed strict conventions: writers were expected to belittle their own work and knowledge while praising the patron's generosity. Sodiqova has also found Inoyatova's name in the visitor books of two botanical societies and in the acknowledgments of several other botanists' publications.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Sodiqova (Text 2) most likely respond to Fayziyev's argument (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"She would agree that Inoyatova's letters accurately describe her relationships with other scientists.\",\"option_b\":\"She would argue that his argument takes literally statements that may reflect the conventions of letters to patrons, and that other evidence suggests Inoyatova did have scientific contacts.\",\"option_c\":\"She would argue that Inoyatova's patrons prevented her from communicating with other botanists.\",\"option_d\":\"She would argue that Inoyatova's work was more highly regarded by patrons than by other scientists.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Fayziyev treats Inoyatova's self-descriptions in letters to patrons as evidence of isolation. Sodiqova explains that such letters were expected to belittle the writer's own knowledge, so those phrases may be convention rather than fact. She also cites visitor books and acknowledgments showing contact with other botanists. Choice B combines both points.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Sodiqova's evidence suggests the letters do not accurately describe Inoyatova's contacts. Choice C is wrong because nothing in either text suggests patrons limited her communication. Choice D is wrong because neither text compares how patrons and scientists regarded her work; the acknowledgments in fact suggest other botanists valued it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Fayziyev Inoyatovaning homiylarga yozgan xatlaridagi o‘zi haqidagi ta’riflarni uning yakkalanib qolganiga dalil deb biladi. Sodiqova esa bunday xatlarda yozuvchi o‘z bilimini kamsitishi kutilganini tushuntiradi, ya’ni bu iboralar haqiqatni emas, odatni aks ettirishi mumkin. U yana tashrif kitoblari va boshqa botaniklar asarlaridagi minnatdorchiliklarni keltirib, Inoyatova olimlar bilan aloqada bo‘lganini ko‘rsatadi. B javob ikkala fikrni birlashtiradi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Sodiqovaning dalillari xatlar Inoyatovaning aloqalarini to‘g‘ri tasvirlamasligini ko‘rsatadi. C noto‘g‘ri: hech bir matnda homiylar uning muloqotini cheklagani aytilmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: hech bir matnda homiylar va olimlarning baholari taqqoslanmaydi; minnatdorchiliklar esa boshqa botaniklar uning ishini qadrlaganini ko‘rsatadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":105,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"history\",\"respond-to-argument\",\"source-reliability\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nIn a 2015 study, psychologist Javohir Abdurahmonov found that children who spoke two languages outperformed children who spoke one on tasks requiring them to switch quickly between rules, such as sorting cards first by color and then by shape. He concluded that managing two languages trains a general ability to control attention.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nPsychologist Iroda Musayeva reviewed dozens of similar studies. The advantage appeared mainly in small studies; in large studies, especially those that compared children whose families had similar incomes and levels of education, differences between bilingual and monolingual children usually shrank or disappeared. Musayeva does not rule out a bilingual advantage, but she argues that it is far less certain than early studies suggested.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Musayeva (Text 2) most likely respond to Abdurahmonov's conclusion (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"She would argue that bilingual children perform worse than monolingual children on rule-switching tasks.\",\"option_b\":\"She would agree with it, since most large studies have confirmed a bilingual advantage.\",\"option_c\":\"She would caution that the difference he observed might be explained by other differences between the groups of children, so his conclusion is not firmly established.\",\"option_d\":\"She would argue that rule-switching tasks cannot measure children's ability to control attention.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Abdurahmonov concludes that speaking two languages trains attention control. Musayeva finds that the advantage tends to shrink or disappear in large studies that match families' income and education, which suggests such factors could explain the differences. She “does not rule out” an advantage but calls it “far less certain.” Choice C captures this cautious response.\\n\\nChoice A is too extreme: Musayeva says differences shrink or disappear, not that bilingual children do worse. Choice B misstates her review: the large studies usually did not confirm the advantage. Choice D is wrong because she questions what explains the results, not whether the tasks measure attention.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Abdurahmonov ikki tilda gapirish diqqatni boshqarish qobiliyatini mashq qildiradi degan xulosaga keladi. Musayeva esa oilalarning daromadi va ma’lumoti bir xil bo‘lgan katta tadqiqotlarda ustunlik qisqarishi yoki yo‘qolishini aniqlaydi — demak, farqlarni shu omillar tushuntirishi mumkin. U ustunlikni “istisno qilmaydi”, ammo uni “ancha noaniq” deb hisoblaydi. C javob aynan shu ehtiyotkor munosabatni ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA juda keskin: Musayeva farqlar qisqaradi yoki yo‘qoladi deydi, ikki tilli bolalar yomonroq natija ko‘rsatadi demaydi. B uning sharhini noto‘g‘ri beradi: katta tadqiqotlar odatda ustunlikni tasdiqlamagan. D noto‘g‘ri: u vazifalar diqqatni o‘lchay olishiga emas, natijalarni nima tushuntirishiga shubha qiladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":105,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"social-science\",\"respond-to-conclusion\",\"confound\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nInstruments on a space probe have detected small amounts of methane escaping from cracks on an icy moon of a distant planet. Planetary scientist Kamol Hakimov argues that the methane is probably produced by living microbes beneath the ice. No geological process known to operate on the moon, he notes, could produce methane at the observed rate.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nIn laboratory experiments, geochemist Yulduz Abdullayeva heated crushed rock similar to that thought to exist beneath the moon's ice in salty water at moderate temperatures and pressures. The reactions produced methane at rates comparable to those measured on the moon. Abdullayeva cautions that her results do not show how the moon's methane actually forms; they show only that rock and water alone could, in principle, generate it.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Abdullayeva (Text 2) most likely respond to Hakimov's reasoning (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"She would argue that her experiments prove that the moon's methane is produced by rock and water rather than by microbes.\",\"option_b\":\"She would agree that no non-living process could produce methane at the observed rate.\",\"option_c\":\"She would argue that the space probe's instruments probably did not detect methane at all.\",\"option_d\":\"She would argue that his reasoning is weakened because a non-living process could produce methane at a comparable rate, though this does not rule out his explanation.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Hakimov reasons that microbes are likely because no known geological process could produce the methane. Abdullayeva shows that rock and water can produce methane at comparable rates, which undercuts that reasoning. But she stresses that her results “do not show how the moon's methane actually forms,” so she would not rule out microbes. Choice D reflects both parts.\\n\\nChoice A overstates her claim: she explicitly says her results do not show how the methane actually forms. Choice B contradicts her findings, which show a non-living process producing comparable rates. Choice C is wrong because she accepts the measured rates and compares her results to them.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Hakimovning mulohazasi: ma’lum geologik jarayonlarning hech biri bunday metan hosil qila olmaydi, demak, ehtimol, mikroblar sabab. Abdullayeva esa tosh va suv shunga yaqin tezlikda metan hosil qilishi mumkinligini ko‘rsatadi — bu Hakimovning mulohazasini zaiflashtiradi. Ammo u natijalari “oydagi metan aslida qanday hosil bo‘lishini ko‘rsatmaydi” deb ta’kidlaydi, ya’ni mikroblarni istisno qilmaydi. D javob ikkala jihatni ham aks ettiradi.\\n\\nA uning da’vosini bo‘rttiradi: u natijalari metan qanday hosil bo‘lishini ko‘rsatmasligini ochiq aytadi. B uning natijalariga zid: ular jonsiz jarayon ham shunga yaqin tezlik berishini ko‘rsatadi. C noto‘g‘ri: u o‘lchangan tezliklarni qabul qiladi va o‘z natijalarini ular bilan taqqoslaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":105,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science\",\"respond-to-reasoning\",\"too-extreme-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"equivalent_expressions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Which expression is equivalent to $12x^3y - 18x^2y^2$?\",\"option_a\":\"$6x^2y(2x - 3y)$\",\"option_b\":\"$6xy(2x^2 - 3y)$\",\"option_c\":\"$6x^2y(2x + 3y)$\",\"option_d\":\"$2x^2y(6x - 9y^2)$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The greatest common factor of $12x^3y$ and $18x^2y^2$ is $6x^2y$. Dividing each term by it gives $2x$ and $3y$, so the expression equals $6x^2y(2x - 3y)$. Check: $6x^2y \\\\cdot 2x = 12x^3y$ and $6x^2y \\\\cdot 3y = 18x^2y^2$.\\n\\nChoice B expands to $12x^3y - 18xy^2$ (only one factor of $x$ in the second term). Choice C has the wrong sign. Choice D expands to $12x^3y - 18x^2y^3$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$12x^3y$ va $18x^2y^2$ ning eng katta umumiy bo‘luvchisi $6x^2y$. Har bir hadni unga bo‘lsak, $2x$ va $3y$ chiqadi, demak ifoda $6x^2y(2x - 3y)$ ga teng. Tekshiruv: $6x^2y \\\\cdot 2x = 12x^3y$ va $6x^2y \\\\cdot 3y = 18x^2y^2$.\\n\\nB variant qavs ochilganda $12x^3y - 18xy^2$ beradi (ikkinchi hadda $x$ faqat bitta). C variantda ishora noto‘g‘ri. D variant $12x^3y - 18x^2y^3$ beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"factor_gcf\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"equivalent_expressions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The expression $(3x - 2)(x + 5) - x^2$ is equivalent to $ax^2 + bx + c$, where $a$, $b$, and $c$ are constants. What is the value of $a + b + c$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"5\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Expand: $(3x - 2)(x + 5) = 3x^2 + 15x - 2x - 10 = 3x^2 + 13x - 10$. Subtracting $x^2$ gives $2x^2 + 13x - 10$, so $a = 2$, $b = 13$, $c = -10$, and $a + b + c = 5$.\\n\\nShortcut: $a + b + c$ is the value of the expression at $x = 1$: $(1)(6) - 1 = 5$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qavslarni ochamiz: $(3x - 2)(x + 5) = 3x^2 + 15x - 2x - 10 = 3x^2 + 13x - 10$. $x^2$ ni ayirsak, $2x^2 + 13x - 10$, ya’ni $a = 2$, $b = 13$, $c = -10$ va $a + b + c = 5$.\\n\\nQisqa yo‘l: $a + b + c$ — ifodaning $x = 1$ dagi qiymati: $(1)(6) - 1 = 5$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"expand_and_combine\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"equivalent_expressions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Which expression is equivalent to $\\\\sqrt[3]{x^2} \\\\cdot \\\\sqrt{x}$, where $x > 0$?\",\"option_a\":\"$x^{\\\\frac{7}{6}}$\",\"option_b\":\"$x^{\\\\frac{1}{3}}$\",\"option_c\":\"$x^{\\\\frac{3}{5}}$\",\"option_d\":\"$x^{\\\\frac{2}{5}}$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Write each root as a power: $\\\\sqrt[3]{x^2} = x^{\\\\frac{2}{3}}$ and $\\\\sqrt{x} = x^{\\\\frac{1}{2}}$. When multiplying powers with the same base, add the exponents: $\\\\frac{2}{3} + \\\\frac{1}{2} = \\\\frac{4}{6} + \\\\frac{3}{6} = \\\\frac{7}{6}$.\\n\\nChoice B multiplies the exponents instead of adding them. Choice C adds the numerators and the denominators separately, $\\\\frac{2 + 1}{3 + 2}$. Choice D keeps the exponent $2$ and adds the root indexes, $3 + 2$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Har bir ildizni daraja ko‘rinishida yozamiz: $\\\\sqrt[3]{x^2} = x^{\\\\frac{2}{3}}$ va $\\\\sqrt{x} = x^{\\\\frac{1}{2}}$. Asoslari bir xil darajalar ko‘paytirilganda ko‘rsatkichlar qo‘shiladi: $\\\\frac{2}{3} + \\\\frac{1}{2} = \\\\frac{4}{6} + \\\\frac{3}{6} = \\\\frac{7}{6}$.\\n\\nB variant ko‘rsatkichlarni qo‘shish o‘rniga ko‘paytiradi. C variant suratlar va maxrajlarni alohida qo‘shadi: $\\\\frac{2 + 1}{3 + 2}$. D variant $2$ darajani saqlab, ildiz ko‘rsatkichlarini qo‘shadi: $3 + 2$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"rational_exponents\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"equivalent_expressions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The kinetic energy $E$ of a moving object is given by the formula $E = \\\\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2$, where $m$ is the mass of the object and $v$ is its speed. Which equation gives $v$ in terms of $E$ and $m$, for $v > 0$?\",\"option_a\":\"$v = \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2E}{m}}$\",\"option_b\":\"$v = \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{E}{2m}}$\",\"option_c\":\"$v = \\\\dfrac{2E}{m}$\",\"option_d\":\"$v = \\\\sqrt{2Em}$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Multiply both sides by $2$: $2E = mv^2$. Divide by $m$: $v^2 = \\\\frac{2E}{m}$. Since $v > 0$, take the positive square root: $v = \\\\sqrt{\\\\frac{2E}{m}}$.\\n\\nChoice B divides by $2$ instead of multiplying. Choice C forgets the square root. Choice D multiplies by $m$ instead of dividing.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkala tomonni $2$ ga ko‘paytiramiz: $2E = mv^2$. $m$ ga bo‘lamiz: $v^2 = \\\\frac{2E}{m}$. $v > 0$ bo‘lgani uchun musbat kvadrat ildiz olamiz: $v = \\\\sqrt{\\\\frac{2E}{m}}$.\\n\\nB variant $2$ ga ko‘paytirish o‘rniga bo‘ladi. C variant kvadrat ildizni unutadi. D variant $m$ ga bo‘lish o‘rniga ko‘paytiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"rearrange_formula\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"equivalent_expressions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Which expression is equivalent to $4x^2 - (x - 3)^2$?\",\"option_a\":\"$3x^2 - 6x + 9$\",\"option_b\":\"$3x^2 + 6x - 9$\",\"option_c\":\"$3x^2 - 9$\",\"option_d\":\"$3x^2 - 6x - 9$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"First expand the square: $(x - 3)^2 = x^2 - 6x + 9$. Then subtract the whole expression: $4x^2 - (x^2 - 6x + 9) = 4x^2 - x^2 + 6x - 9 = 3x^2 + 6x - 9$.\\n\\nChoice A does not distribute the negative sign. Choice C uses $(x - 3)^2 = x^2 + 9$, dropping the middle term $-6x$. Choice D changes the sign of only the last term.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Avval kvadratni ochamiz: $(x - 3)^2 = x^2 - 6x + 9$. Keyin butun ifodani ayiramiz: $4x^2 - (x^2 - 6x + 9) = 4x^2 - x^2 + 6x - 9 = 3x^2 + 6x - 9$.\\n\\nA variant minus ishorasini qavs ichidagi hadlarga tarqatmaydi. C variant $(x - 3)^2 = x^2 + 9$ deb, o‘rta had $-6x$ ni tushirib qoldiradi. D variant faqat oxirgi hadning ishorasini o‘zgartiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"subtract_squared_binomial\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"equivalent_expressions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Which expression is equivalent to $(2x^3 + 5x - 1) - (x^3 - 7x + 4)$?\",\"option_a\":\"$x^3 + 12x - 5$\",\"option_b\":\"$x^3 - 2x + 3$\",\"option_c\":\"$x^3 - 2x - 5$\",\"option_d\":\"$x^3 + 12x + 3$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Distribute the subtraction to every term in the second polynomial: $2x^3 + 5x - 1 - x^3 + 7x - 4$. Combine like terms: $(2x^3 - x^3) + (5x + 7x) + (-1 - 4) = x^3 + 12x - 5$.\\n\\nChoice B adds the polynomials instead of subtracting. Choice C subtracts only the constant correctly. Choice D subtracts only the $x$-term correctly.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ayirishni ikkinchi ko‘phadning har bir hadiga tarqatamiz: $2x^3 + 5x - 1 - x^3 + 7x - 4$. O‘xshash hadlarni birlashtiramiz: $(2x^3 - x^3) + (5x + 7x) + (-1 - 4) = x^3 + 12x - 5$.\\n\\nB variant ko‘phadlarni ayirish o‘rniga qo‘shadi. C variant faqat ozod hadni to‘g‘ri ayiradi. D variant faqat $x$ li hadni to‘g‘ri ayiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"polynomial_add_subtract\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"equivalent_expressions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Which expression is equivalent to $2x^2 - 7x - 15$?\",\"option_a\":\"$(2x + 3)(x - 5)$\",\"option_b\":\"$(2x - 3)(x + 5)$\",\"option_c\":\"$(2x + 5)(x - 3)$\",\"option_d\":\"$(2x - 5)(x + 3)$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Expand choice A: $(2x + 3)(x - 5) = 2x^2 - 10x + 3x - 15 = 2x^2 - 7x - 15$. This matches.\\n\\nChoice B gives $2x^2 + 7x - 15$ (signs reversed). Choice C gives $2x^2 - x - 15$, and choice D gives $2x^2 + x - 15$. All four have the right first and last terms, so only the middle term decides.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"A variantni ochamiz: $(2x + 3)(x - 5) = 2x^2 - 10x + 3x - 15 = 2x^2 - 7x - 15$. Mos keladi.\\n\\nB variant $2x^2 + 7x - 15$ beradi (ishoralar teskari). C variant $2x^2 - x - 15$, D variant esa $2x^2 + x - 15$ beradi. To‘rttalasida ham birinchi va oxirgi hadlar to‘g‘ri, shuning uchun faqat o‘rta had hal qiladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"factor_trinomial\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"equivalent_expressions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The equation $(ax + 3)(2x - b) = 6x^2 - 6x - 12$ is true for all values of $x$, where $a$ and $b$ are constants. What is the value of $a + b$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"7\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Expand the left side: $2ax^2 - abx + 6x - 3b = 2ax^2 + (6 - ab)x - 3b$. Matching coefficients: $2a = 6$, so $a = 3$; and $-3b = -12$, so $b = 4$. Check the middle term: $6 - ab = 6 - 12 = -6$. So $a + b = 7$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Chap tomonni ochamiz: $2ax^2 - abx + 6x - 3b = 2ax^2 + (6 - ab)x - 3b$. Koeffitsiyentlarni tenglashtiramiz: $2a = 6$, ya’ni $a = 3$; $-3b = -12$, ya’ni $b = 4$. O‘rta hadni tekshiramiz: $6 - ab = 6 - 12 = -6$. Demak $a + b = 7$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"identity_coefficients\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"equivalent_expressions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Which expression is equivalent to $\\\\sqrt{48x^6}$, where $x > 0$?\",\"option_a\":\"$4x^3\\\\sqrt{3}$\",\"option_b\":\"$16x^3\\\\sqrt{3}$\",\"option_c\":\"$24x^3$\",\"option_d\":\"$4x^4\\\\sqrt{3}$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Split the radicand into a perfect square and the rest: $48x^6 = 16x^6 \\\\cdot 3 = (4x^3)^2 \\\\cdot 3$. So $\\\\sqrt{48x^6} = 4x^3\\\\sqrt{3}$.\\n\\nChoice B takes $16$ out of the root without taking its square root. Choice C divides $48$ by $2$ as if the square root halved the number. Choice D subtracts $2$ from the exponent instead of dividing it by $2$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ildiz ostidagi ifodani to‘la kvadrat va qolgan qismga ajratamiz: $48x^6 = 16x^6 \\\\cdot 3 = (4x^3)^2 \\\\cdot 3$. Demak $\\\\sqrt{48x^6} = 4x^3\\\\sqrt{3}$.\\n\\nB variant $16$ ni ildizdan kvadrat ildizini olmasdan chiqaradi. C variant kvadrat ildiz sonni ikkiga bo‘ladi deb, $48$ ni $2$ ga bo‘ladi. D variant daraja ko‘rsatkichini $2$ ga bo‘lish o‘rniga undan $2$ ni ayiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"radicals\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"equivalent_expressions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"When two resistors with resistances $a$ and $b$ are connected in parallel, their combined resistance $R$ satisfies $\\\\dfrac{1}{R} = \\\\dfrac{1}{a} + \\\\dfrac{1}{b}$. Which equation gives $b$ in terms of $a$ and $R$?\",\"option_a\":\"$b = \\\\dfrac{aR}{a - R}$\",\"option_b\":\"$b = \\\\dfrac{aR}{R - a}$\",\"option_c\":\"$b = a - R$\",\"option_d\":\"$b = \\\\dfrac{a - R}{aR}$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Isolate $\\\\frac{1}{b}$: $\\\\frac{1}{b} = \\\\frac{1}{R} - \\\\frac{1}{a} = \\\\frac{a - R}{aR}$. Take reciprocals of both sides: $b = \\\\frac{aR}{a - R}$.\\n\\nChoice B subtracts in the wrong order ($\\\\frac{1}{a} - \\\\frac{1}{R}$). Choice C treats the reciprocals as if they could be dropped. Choice D gives $\\\\frac{1}{b}$ instead of $b$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$\\\\frac{1}{b}$ ni ajratamiz: $\\\\frac{1}{b} = \\\\frac{1}{R} - \\\\frac{1}{a} = \\\\frac{a - R}{aR}$. Ikkala tomonning teskarisini olamiz: $b = \\\\frac{aR}{a - R}$.\\n\\nB variant ayirish tartibini almashtiradi ($\\\\frac{1}{a} - \\\\frac{1}{R}$). C variant teskari kasrlarni tashlab yuboradi. D variant $b$ ni emas, $\\\\frac{1}{b}$ ni beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"rearrange_formula\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"equivalent_expressions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"For $x \\\\ne -2$ and $x \\\\ne 4$, the expression $\\\\dfrac{3}{x + 2} + \\\\dfrac{5}{x - 4}$ is equivalent to $\\\\dfrac{ax + b}{(x + 2)(x - 4)}$, where $a$ and $b$ are constants. What is the value of $a - b$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"10\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Use the common denominator $(x + 2)(x - 4)$: the numerator is $3(x - 4) + 5(x + 2) = 3x - 12 + 5x + 10 = 8x - 2$. So $a = 8$ and $b = -2$, and $a - b = 8 - (-2) = 10$.\\n\\nAnswering $6$ computes $a + b$ or drops the sign of $b$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Umumiy maxraj $(x + 2)(x - 4)$: surat $3(x - 4) + 5(x + 2) = 3x - 12 + 5x + 10 = 8x - 2$. Demak $a = 8$, $b = -2$, va $a - b = 8 - (-2) = 10$.\\n\\n$6$ javobi $a + b$ ni hisoblaydi yoki $b$ ning ishorasini tashlab yuboradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"rational_expressions\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"equivalent_expressions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Which expression is equivalent to $(2x + 3)(x - 1) - (x + 2)^2$?\",\"option_a\":\"$x^2 - 3x - 7$\",\"option_b\":\"$x^2 + 5x + 1$\",\"option_c\":\"$x^2 + x - 7$\",\"option_d\":\"$3x^2 + 5x + 1$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Expand each part: $(2x + 3)(x - 1) = 2x^2 + x - 3$ and $(x + 2)^2 = x^2 + 4x + 4$. Subtract: $2x^2 + x - 3 - x^2 - 4x - 4 = x^2 - 3x - 7$.\\n\\nChoice B subtracts only the $x^2$ term and adds the other two. Choice C uses $(x + 2)^2 = x^2 + 4$, missing the middle term $4x$. Choice D adds the $x^2$ terms instead of subtracting them.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Har bir qismni ochamiz: $(2x + 3)(x - 1) = 2x^2 + x - 3$ va $(x + 2)^2 = x^2 + 4x + 4$. Ayiramiz: $2x^2 + x - 3 - x^2 - 4x - 4 = x^2 - 3x - 7$.\\n\\nB variant faqat $x^2$ hadni ayirib, qolgan ikkitasini qo‘shadi. C variant $(x + 2)^2 = x^2 + 4$ deb, o‘rta had $4x$ ni tushirib qoldiradi. D variant $x^2$ hadlarni ayirish o‘rniga qo‘shadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"product_minus_square\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"equivalent_expressions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Which of the following is equivalent to $x^4 - 13x^2 + 36$?\",\"option_a\":\"$(x^2 + 4)(x^2 + 9)$\",\"option_b\":\"$(x - 2)(x + 2)(x - 3)(x + 3)$\",\"option_c\":\"$(x - 2)^2(x - 3)^2$\",\"option_d\":\"$(x^2 - 4)(x^2 + 9)$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Treat $x^2$ as a single variable $u$: $u^2 - 13u + 36 = (u - 4)(u - 9)$. So $x^4 - 13x^2 + 36 = (x^2 - 4)(x^2 - 9)$, and each factor is a difference of squares: $(x - 2)(x + 2)(x - 3)(x + 3)$.\\n\\nChoice A expands to $x^4 + 13x^2 + 36$ (wrong middle sign). Choice C expands to a polynomial with odd powers of $x$, such as $-10x^3$. Choice D expands to $x^4 + 5x^2 - 36$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$x^2$ ni bitta o‘zgaruvchi $u$ deb olamiz: $u^2 - 13u + 36 = (u - 4)(u - 9)$. Demak $x^4 - 13x^2 + 36 = (x^2 - 4)(x^2 - 9)$, va har bir ko‘paytuvchi kvadratlar ayirmasi: $(x - 2)(x + 2)(x - 3)(x + 3)$.\\n\\nA variant ochilganda $x^4 + 13x^2 + 36$ chiqadi (o‘rta hadning ishorasi noto‘g‘ri). C variant ochilganda $x$ ning toq darajalari, masalan $-10x^3$, paydo bo‘ladi. D variant $x^4 + 5x^2 - 36$ beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"quadratic_form_factoring\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"equivalent_expressions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"For all values of $x$, the expression $(2x^2 - 5)^2 - (2x^2 + 3)^2$ is equivalent to $ax^2 + b$, where $a$ and $b$ are constants. What is the value of $a + b$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"-16\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Use the difference of squares $P^2 - Q^2 = (P - Q)(P + Q)$ with $P = 2x^2 - 5$ and $Q = 2x^2 + 3$: $P - Q = -8$ and $P + Q = 4x^2 - 2$. So the expression equals $-8(4x^2 - 2) = -32x^2 + 16$.\\n\\nThus $a = -32$, $b = 16$, and $a + b = -16$. Expanding both squares directly gives the same result: $(4x^4 - 20x^2 + 25) - (4x^4 + 12x^2 + 9) = -32x^2 + 16$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Kvadratlar ayirmasi $P^2 - Q^2 = (P - Q)(P + Q)$ ni $P = 2x^2 - 5$ va $Q = 2x^2 + 3$ bilan qo‘llaymiz: $P - Q = -8$ va $P + Q = 4x^2 - 2$. Demak ifoda $-8(4x^2 - 2) = -32x^2 + 16$ ga teng.\\n\\nShunday qilib $a = -32$, $b = 16$ va $a + b = -16$. Ikkala kvadratni to‘g‘ridan-to‘g‘ri ochsak ham shu chiqadi: $(4x^4 - 20x^2 + 25) - (4x^4 + 12x^2 + 9) = -32x^2 + 16$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"difference_of_squares_binomial\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"equivalent_expressions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"$$(ax + 3)(bx - 5) = 12x^2 + 11x - 15$$ The given equation is true for all values of $x$, where $a$ and $b$ are integers. What is the value of $a + b$?\",\"option_a\":\"$-7$\",\"option_b\":\"$-1$\",\"option_c\":\"$1$\",\"option_d\":\"$7$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Expanding the left side gives $abx^2 + (3b - 5a)x - 15$. So $ab = 12$ and $3b - 5a = 11$. Try integer pairs with $ab = 12$: $a = 4, b = 3$ gives $9 - 20 = -11$, which has the wrong sign. The pair $a = -4, b = -3$ gives $3(-3) - 5(-4) = -9 + 20 = 11$, which works.\\n\\nCheck: $(-4x + 3)(-3x - 5) = 12x^2 + 20x - 9x - 15 = 12x^2 + 11x - 15$. So $a + b = -7$.\\n\\nChoice D comes from $a = 4$, $b = 3$, which produces $-11x$, not $11x$. Choices B and C are impossible: integer pairs with $ab = 12$ have sums $\\\\pm 7$, $\\\\pm 8$, or $\\\\pm 13$, never $\\\\pm 1$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Chap tomonni ochsak, $abx^2 + (3b - 5a)x - 15$. Demak $ab = 12$ va $3b - 5a = 11$. $ab = 12$ bo‘ladigan butun juftlarni sinaymiz: $a = 4, b = 3$ da $9 - 20 = -11$, ishora noto‘g‘ri. $a = -4, b = -3$ da $3(-3) - 5(-4) = -9 + 20 = 11$, mos keladi.\\n\\nTekshiruv: $(-4x + 3)(-3x - 5) = 12x^2 + 20x - 9x - 15 = 12x^2 + 11x - 15$. Demak $a + b = -7$.\\n\\nD variant $a = 4$, $b = 3$ dan chiqadi, bu $11x$ emas, $-11x$ beradi. B va C variantlar bo‘lishi mumkin emas: $ab = 12$ bo‘ladigan butun juftlarning yig‘indisi $\\\\pm 7$, $\\\\pm 8$ yoki $\\\\pm 13$, hech qachon $\\\\pm 1$ emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"identity_integer_constants\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"equivalent_expressions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Which of the following is equivalent to $\\\\dfrac{x^2 - 9}{x^2 + 5x + 6} \\\\div \\\\dfrac{2x - 6}{x + 2}$, for $x > 3$?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\dfrac{1}{2}$\",\"option_b\":\"$2$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\dfrac{2(x - 3)^2}{(x + 2)^2}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\dfrac{x + 3}{2(x + 2)}$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Factor everything: $x^2 - 9 = (x - 3)(x + 3)$, $x^2 + 5x + 6 = (x + 2)(x + 3)$, and $2x - 6 = 2(x - 3)$. So the first fraction is $\\\\frac{x - 3}{x + 2}$.\\n\\nDividing means multiplying by the reciprocal: $\\\\frac{x - 3}{x + 2} \\\\cdot \\\\frac{x + 2}{2(x - 3)} = \\\\frac{1}{2}$.\\n\\nChoice B inverts the wrong fraction. Choice C multiplies by the second fraction instead of by its reciprocal. Choice D results from factoring $x^2 + 5x + 6$ incorrectly as $(x + 2)^2$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Hammasini ko‘paytuvchilarga ajratamiz: $x^2 - 9 = (x - 3)(x + 3)$, $x^2 + 5x + 6 = (x + 2)(x + 3)$ va $2x - 6 = 2(x - 3)$. Demak birinchi kasr $\\\\frac{x - 3}{x + 2}$ ga teng.\\n\\nBo‘lish — teskari kasrga ko‘paytirish: $\\\\frac{x - 3}{x + 2} \\\\cdot \\\\frac{x + 2}{2(x - 3)} = \\\\frac{1}{2}$.\\n\\nB variant noto‘g‘ri kasrni teskarisiga aylantiradi. C variant ikkinchi kasrning teskarisiga emas, o‘ziga ko‘paytiradi. D variant $x^2 + 5x + 6$ ni noto‘g‘ri, $(x + 2)^2$ deb ajratishdan chiqadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"rational_division\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"evaluating_statistical_claims\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A town council wants to estimate how many hours per week the adult residents of the town exercise. A council member surveyed 150 adults as they were leaving the town's largest fitness center one evening. Which of the following is the best reason the results of this survey may not be used to draw a valid conclusion about all adult residents of the town?\",\"option_a\":\"The survey was given in the evening instead of in the morning.\",\"option_b\":\"A sample of 150 adults is too small to give any information about the town.\",\"option_c\":\"Adults leaving a fitness center are likely to exercise more than typical adult residents of the town.\",\"option_d\":\"Some of the adults surveyed may not have answered honestly.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The sample was chosen from people at a fitness center, who are not representative of all adults in the town: they are very likely to exercise more than a typical resident, so the survey would overestimate exercise time. This is a biased (convenience) sample. Choice A is a minor detail compared with who was sampled. Choice B is wrong because a properly selected random sample of 150 adults could give useful information. Choice D could be a concern in any survey, but the main flaw here is how the sample was chosen.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tanlanma fitnes markazidagi odamlardan olingan, ular shaharning barcha kattalarini ifodalamaydi: ular oddiy aholidan ko‘ra ko‘proq shug‘ullanishi ehtimoli juda yuqori, shuning uchun so‘rovnoma mashg‘ulot vaqtini oshirib ko‘rsatadi. Bu — xolis bo‘lmagan (qulaylik bo‘yicha olingan) tanlanma. A javob kim tanlanganiga nisbatan ahamiyatsiz tafsilot. B javob noto‘g‘ri, chunki to‘g‘ri tanlangan 150 kishilik tasodifiy tanlanma foydali ma’lumot berishi mumkin. D javob har qanday so‘rovnomada uchrashi mumkin bo‘lgan muammo, lekin bu yerdagi asosiy kamchilik — tanlanmaning qanday olingani.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"sampling bias\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"evaluating_statistical_claims\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A researcher selected a random sample of 600 adults in a city and asked each adult how many servings of fruit they eat per day and how many days they were sick during the past year. The adults who ate more fruit tended to report fewer sick days. Which of the following conclusions is best supported by this study?\",\"option_a\":\"Eating more fruit causes adults in the city to have fewer sick days.\",\"option_b\":\"Among adults in the city, eating more fruit is associated with having fewer sick days.\",\"option_c\":\"Having fewer sick days causes adults in the city to eat more fruit.\",\"option_d\":\"There is no relationship between eating fruit and sick days for adults in the city.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Because the sample was selected at random from the adults in the city, the association found in the sample can be generalized to all adults in the city. However, this is an observational study: the adults were not randomly assigned to eat different amounts of fruit, so it cannot show cause and effect. Other factors, such as general lifestyle, could explain both. Choices A and C make causal claims that the study cannot support. Choice D contradicts the association that was found.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tanlanma shahar kattalaridan tasodifiy olingani uchun tanlanmada topilgan bog‘liqlikni shaharning barcha kattalariga umumlashtirish mumkin. Biroq bu kuzatuv tadqiqoti: kattalar turli miqdorda meva yeyishga tasodifiy taqsimlanmagan, shuning uchun u sabab-oqibat bog‘lanishini ko‘rsata olmaydi. Umumiy turmush tarzi kabi boshqa omillar ikkalasini ham tushuntirishi mumkin. A va C javoblar tadqiqot asoslay olmaydigan sababiy da’volarni qiladi. D javob topilgan bog‘liqlikka zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"association vs causation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"evaluating_statistical_claims\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"In an experiment, 80 tomato plants were randomly assigned to two groups of 40 plants each. One group was given a new fertilizer, and the other group was not. At the end of the season, the plants given the new fertilizer produced a total of 96 kilograms of tomatoes, and the other plants produced a total of 78 kilograms. Based on this experiment, how many more kilograms of tomatoes per plant, on average, did the plants given the new fertilizer produce?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"0.45\",\".45\",\"9/20\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The mean for the fertilizer group is $\\\\frac{96}{40} = 2.4$ kilograms per plant, and the mean for the other group is $\\\\frac{78}{40} = 1.95$ kilograms per plant. The difference is $2.4 - 1.95 = 0.45$ kilogram per plant. Answering 18 gives the difference in the total amounts, not per plant. Enter 0.45 or 9/20.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"O‘g‘it berilgan guruhning o‘rtachasi $\\\\frac{96}{40} = 2.4$ kilogramm/tup, ikkinchi guruhniki $\\\\frac{78}{40} = 1.95$ kilogramm/tup. Farq $2.4 - 1.95 = 0.45$ kilogramm/tup. 18 deb javob berish har bir tupga emas, umumiy miqdorlar farqini beradi. Javob: 0.45 yoki 9/20.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"experiment results\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"evaluating_statistical_claims\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A city library selected a random sample of 400 people from its list of all registered library members and asked how many books they had borrowed in the past month. The mean number of books for the sample was 3.1. To which of the following groups can the results of this survey most appropriately be generalized?\",\"option_a\":\"All registered members of the city library\",\"option_b\":\"All residents of the city\",\"option_c\":\"All people who read books in the city\",\"option_d\":\"Only the 400 people in the sample\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The sample was selected at random from the list of all registered members of the library, so the results can be generalized to that population. Choices B and C are too broad: residents who are not library members, including people who buy books instead of borrowing them, had no chance of being selected. Choice D is too narrow, because random sampling allows conclusions about the whole population the sample was drawn from.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tanlanma kutubxonaning barcha ro‘yxatdagi a’zolari orasidan tasodifiy olingan, shuning uchun natijalarni aynan shu to‘plamga umumlashtirish mumkin. B va C javoblar juda keng: kutubxona a’zosi bo‘lmagan aholi, jumladan kitobni olib o‘qish o‘rniga sotib oladiganlar tanlanishga imkoniga ega bo‘lmagan. D javob juda tor, chunki tasodifiy tanlash tanlanma olingan butun to‘plam haqida xulosa chiqarishga imkon beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"generalization\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"evaluating_statistical_claims\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A large company with 3,000 employees wants to estimate the percentage of its employees who would use a new on-site day care center. Which of the following methods of choosing a sample is most likely to give an unbiased estimate?\",\"option_a\":\"Surveying the first 100 employees who arrive at work on Monday\",\"option_b\":\"Surveying 100 employees who are selected at random from a list of all 3,000 employees\",\"option_c\":\"Posting the survey online and using the first 100 responses\",\"option_d\":\"Surveying 100 employees who already have young children\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Selecting employees at random from a list of all employees gives every employee the same chance of being chosen, so the sample is likely to represent the whole company. Choice A depends on arrival time, which may be related to having children to drop off. Choice C uses volunteers, who may care more about the topic than other employees. Choice D chooses only employees who are the most likely to want day care, so it would overestimate the percentage.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Barcha xodimlar ro‘yxatidan tasodifiy tanlash har bir xodimga tanlanish uchun bir xil imkoniyat beradi, shuning uchun tanlanma butun kompaniyani ifodalashi ehtimoli yuqori. A javob ishga kelish vaqtiga bog‘liq, bu esa farzandini bog‘chaga olib borish zarurati bilan bog‘liq bo‘lishi mumkin. C javob ko‘ngillilardan foydalanadi, ular mavzuga boshqa xodimlardan ko‘ra ko‘proq qiziqishi mumkin. D javob faqat bog‘chaga eng ko‘p ehtiyoji bor xodimlarni tanlaydi, shuning uchun foizni oshirib ko‘rsatadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"sampling method\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"evaluating_statistical_claims\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A city selected a random sample of 500 residents to learn their opinions about a plan to close a street in the city center to cars. Each resident in the sample was asked, \\\"Don't you agree that closing the street to cars will make the city center cleaner and safer?\\\" Of the residents surveyed, 82% answered yes. Which of the following is the best reason the result may not accurately reflect the opinions of all residents of the city?\",\"option_a\":\"The sample was selected at random.\",\"option_b\":\"The wording of the question encourages residents to answer yes.\",\"option_c\":\"The sample size of 500 is too large.\",\"option_d\":\"Residents of the city center were able to be selected for the sample.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The question describes only benefits of the plan and is phrased to suggest the answer \\\"yes\\\" (\\\"Don't you agree…\\\"). Such a leading question can make the percentage who answer yes higher than the true level of support. Choice A is a strength of the survey, not a weakness. Choice C is wrong because a larger sample does not cause bias. Choice D is wrong because residents of the city center are part of the population and should have a chance to be selected.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Savol rejaning faqat foydali tomonlarini aytadi va \\\"ha\\\" javobiga undaydigan tarzda tuzilgan (\\\"Qo‘shilmaysizmi…\\\"). Bunday yo‘naltiruvchi savol \\\"ha\\\" deb javob berganlar foizini haqiqiy qo‘llab-quvvatlashdan yuqori qilib ko‘rsatishi mumkin. A javob — so‘rovnomaning kamchiligi emas, kuchli tomoni. C javob noto‘g‘ri, chunki kattaroq tanlanma xatolikka olib kelmaydi. D javob noto‘g‘ri, chunki shahar markazi aholisi to‘plamning bir qismi va ular ham tanlanish imkoniga ega bo‘lishi kerak.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"question wording\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"evaluating_statistical_claims\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A researcher recruited 90 adults who responded to an advertisement for a study on memory. The researcher randomly assigned 45 of the adults to sleep at least 8 hours each night for two weeks and the other 45 adults to sleep their usual amount. At the end of the two weeks, the adults who slept at least 8 hours scored significantly higher on a memory test. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?\",\"option_a\":\"Sleeping at least 8 hours each night causes better memory test scores for all adults.\",\"option_b\":\"Sleeping at least 8 hours each night is likely to cause better memory test scores for adults similar to those in the study.\",\"option_c\":\"Sleeping at least 8 hours each night is associated with better memory test scores, but no cause-and-effect conclusion is possible.\",\"option_d\":\"No conclusion is possible, because the adults were not selected at random.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Because the adults were **randomly assigned** to the two groups, the difference in scores can be attributed to the amount of sleep: a cause-and-effect conclusion is appropriate. But the adults were volunteers, not a random sample of all adults, so the conclusion applies only to adults similar to those in the study. Choice A generalizes too far. Choice C ignores the random assignment, which is exactly what allows a causal conclusion. Choice D goes too far the other way: a lack of random sampling limits the population the conclusion applies to, but it does not prevent every conclusion.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Kattalar ikki guruhga **tasodifiy taqsimlangani** uchun ballardagi farqni uyqu miqdoriga bog‘lash mumkin: sabab-oqibat xulosasi o‘rinli. Lekin kattalar barcha kattalardan olingan tasodifiy tanlanma emas, ko‘ngillilar edi, shuning uchun xulosa faqat tadqiqotdagilarga o‘xshash kattalarga tegishli. A javob haddan tashqari keng umumlashtirgan. C javob tasodifiy taqsimlashni e’tiborsiz qoldiradi, holbuki aynan u sababiy xulosaga imkon beradi. D javob boshqa tomonga haddan oshgan: tasodifiy tanlanma yo‘qligi xulosa tegishli bo‘lgan to‘plamni cheklaydi, lekin har qanday xulosani taqiqlamaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"random assignment\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"evaluating_statistical_claims\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A survey company selected a random sample of 1,000 adults from all adults in a region. Each adult reported whether they use a fitness tracking app and their resting heart rate. The adults who use a fitness tracking app had a lower average resting heart rate than the adults who do not. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?\",\"option_a\":\"Using a fitness tracking app causes a lower resting heart rate for adults in the region.\",\"option_b\":\"Using a fitness tracking app causes a lower resting heart rate, but only for the 1,000 adults in the sample.\",\"option_c\":\"Among adults in the region, using a fitness tracking app is associated with a lower resting heart rate.\",\"option_d\":\"The result cannot be generalized to adults in the region, because the adults chose whether to use the app.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The random sample allows the result to be generalized to all adults in the region. However, the adults chose for themselves whether to use the app; they were not randomly assigned. People who use a fitness app may also exercise more, for example, so the study shows only an association, not cause and effect. Choices A and B make causal claims. Choice D confuses random assignment with random sampling: random sampling is what allows generalization, and it was used here.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tasodifiy tanlanma natijani mintaqadagi barcha kattalarga umumlashtirishga imkon beradi. Biroq kattalar ilovadan foydalanish-foydalanmaslikni o‘zlari tanlagan, ular tasodifiy taqsimlanmagan. Masalan, fitnes ilovasidan foydalanadiganlar ko‘proq sport bilan shug‘ullanishi ham mumkin, shuning uchun tadqiqot sabab-oqibatni emas, faqat bog‘liqlikni ko‘rsatadi. A va B javoblar sababiy da’vo qiladi. D javob tasodifiy taqsimlashni tasodifiy tanlash bilan adashtirgan: umumlashtirishga tasodifiy tanlash imkon beradi va u bu yerda qo‘llangan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"association vs causation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"evaluating_statistical_claims\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A researcher randomly assigned 150 volunteers who wanted to improve their sleep to two groups of 75. One group used a meditation app every evening for a month, and the other group did not. At the end of the month, 48 people in the app group and 33 people in the other group reported improved sleep. By how many percentage points was the percentage of people who reported improved sleep greater in the app group than in the other group?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"20\"],\"explanation_en\":\"In the app group, $\\\\frac{48}{75} = 0.64$, or 64%, reported improved sleep. In the other group, $\\\\frac{33}{75} = 0.44$, or 44%, did. The difference is $64 - 44 = 20$ percentage points. Answering 15 gives the difference in the numbers of people, not in the percentages. Enter 20.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ilova guruhida $\\\\frac{48}{75} = 0.64$, ya’ni 64% uyqusi yaxshilanganini aytgan. Ikkinchi guruhda $\\\\frac{33}{75} = 0.44$, ya’ni 44%. Farq $64 - 44 = 20$ foiz punkti. 15 deb javob berish foizlar emas, odamlar soni farqini beradi. Javob: 20.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"experiment results\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"evaluating_statistical_claims\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A university wanted to know how satisfied its 8,000 students are with the student dormitories. It e-mailed a satisfaction survey to a random sample of 1,000 students, and 140 of these students returned the survey. Of the students who returned it, 71% said they were dissatisfied. Which of the following is the best reason the university should be cautious about concluding that about 71% of all its students are dissatisfied with the dormitories?\",\"option_a\":\"The sample was selected at random from all students.\",\"option_b\":\"Only 140 of the 1,000 students selected responded, and students with strong opinions may have been more likely to respond.\",\"option_c\":\"A sample of 1,000 students is too large for a university of 8,000 students.\",\"option_d\":\"Some students who returned the survey may live in the dormitories.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Although the 1,000 students were chosen at random, only 14% of them responded. The students who chose to respond may differ from those who did not; for example, dissatisfied students may be more motivated to complain. This nonresponse can make the result biased. Choice A is a strength, not a reason for caution. Choice C is wrong: a large sample is not a problem. Choice D is wrong because students who live in the dormitories are exactly the people whose opinions are most relevant.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"1,000 nafar talaba tasodifiy tanlangan bo‘lsa ham, ularning faqat 14% i javob bergan. Javob berishni tanlaganlar javob bermaganlardan farq qilishi mumkin; masalan, norozi talabalar shikoyat qilishga ko‘proq intiladi. Bunday javob bermaslik natijani xolis bo‘lmagan qilishi mumkin. A javob — ehtiyot bo‘lish sababi emas, kuchli tomon. C javob noto‘g‘ri: katta tanlanma muammo emas. D javob noto‘g‘ri, chunki yotoqxonada yashaydigan talabalar — fikri eng muhim bo‘lgan odamlar.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"nonresponse bias\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"evaluating_statistical_claims\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A town has 35,000 adult residents. Two surveys were conducted to estimate the percentage of adult residents who support building a new swimming pool. Survey 1 selected 400 adult residents at random from a list of all adult residents of the town, and 58% of them supported the pool. Survey 2 used the responses of 1,200 adult residents who chose to answer a poll on the town's sports club website, and 81% of them supported the pool. Using the survey that gives the more reliable estimate, what is the estimated number of adult residents of the town who support building the new pool?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"20300\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Survey 1 used a random sample of all adult residents, so its estimate is reliable. Survey 2 is larger, but its respondents chose themselves and visited a sports club's website, so they are likely to support a pool more than typical residents. Using Survey 1: $0.58 \\\\times 35{,}000 = 20{,}300$ residents. Using Survey 2 would give $0.81 \\\\times 35{,}000 = 28{,}350$, an overestimate based on a biased sample. Enter 20300.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"1-so‘rovnoma barcha katta yoshli aholidan olingan tasodifiy tanlanmadan foydalangan, shuning uchun uning bahosi ishonchli. 2-so‘rovnoma kattaroq, lekin uning ishtirokchilari o‘zlarini o‘zlari tanlagan va sport klubi saytiga kirgan, shuning uchun ular basseynni oddiy aholidan ko‘ra ko‘proq qo‘llab-quvvatlashi ehtimoli bor. 1-so‘rovnomadan: $0.58 \\\\times 35{,}000 = 20{,}300$ nafar. 2-so‘rovnomadan foydalanish $0.81 \\\\times 35{,}000 = 28{,}350$ ni beradi — bu xolis bo‘lmagan tanlanmaga asoslangan oshirilgan baho. Javob: 20300.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"choose reliable survey\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"evaluating_statistical_claims\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A researcher wants to determine whether drinking green tea every day lowers blood pressure for adults who work at a large factory. Which of the following study designs is most appropriate for reaching this conclusion?\",\"option_a\":\"Select a random sample of the factory's adult workers, and compare the blood pressure of those who already drink green tea every day with those who do not.\",\"option_b\":\"Ask the factory's workers who already drink green tea every day to volunteer, and measure their blood pressure at the start and end of a month.\",\"option_c\":\"Select a random sample of the factory's adult workers, randomly assign half of them to drink green tea every day for two months and the other half not to, and then compare the changes in their blood pressure.\",\"option_d\":\"Select a random sample of adults from a different city, randomly assign half of them to drink green tea every day, and compare the changes in their blood pressure.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"To show cause and effect, the researcher needs random assignment to treatment groups, and to apply the result to the factory's workers, the sample should be selected at random from those workers. Choice C does both. Choice A is an observational study, so it can show only an association. Choice B has no comparison group and uses volunteers. Choice D uses random assignment, but its sample comes from a different population, so the results would not necessarily apply to the factory's workers.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Sabab-oqibatni ko‘rsatish uchun ishtirokchilarni guruhlarga tasodifiy taqsimlash kerak, natijani zavod ishchilariga qo‘llash uchun esa tanlanma aynan shu ishchilardan tasodifiy olinishi kerak. C javob ikkalasini ham bajaradi. A javob kuzatuv tadqiqoti, u faqat bog‘liqlikni ko‘rsata oladi. B javobda taqqoslash guruhi yo‘q va ko‘ngillilardan foydalaniladi. D javobda tasodifiy taqsimlash bor, lekin tanlanma boshqa to‘plamdan olingan, shuning uchun natijalar zavod ishchilariga albatta tegishli bo‘lmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"study design\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"evaluating_statistical_claims\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A grocery delivery company selected a random sample of 2,000 customers from all of its customers in Tashkent. Half of the selected customers were randomly assigned to see a new version of the company's app, and the other half saw the old version. Over the next month, the customers who saw the new version placed significantly more orders, on average, than the customers who saw the old version. Which of the following conclusions is best supported by the study?\",\"option_a\":\"The new version of the app causes an increase in the number of orders for customers of any delivery company in Tashkent.\",\"option_b\":\"The new version of the app is likely to cause an increase in the number of orders for the company's customers in Tashkent.\",\"option_c\":\"The new version of the app is associated with more orders for the company's customers in Tashkent, but it cannot be the cause of the increase.\",\"option_d\":\"The new version of the app is likely to cause an increase in the number of orders, but only for the 2,000 customers in the study.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The study used both random sampling from all of the company's customers in Tashkent and random assignment to the two versions. Random assignment supports a cause-and-effect conclusion, and random sampling allows it to be generalized to the population sampled: the company's customers in Tashkent. Choice A extends the conclusion to customers of other companies, who were not in the population. Choice C ignores the random assignment. Choice D ignores the random sampling, which allows a conclusion beyond the 2,000 customers.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tadqiqotda kompaniyaning Toshkentdagi barcha mijozlaridan tasodifiy tanlash ham, ikki versiyaga tasodifiy taqsimlash ham qo‘llangan. Tasodifiy taqsimlash sabab-oqibat xulosasini asoslaydi, tasodifiy tanlash esa uni tanlanma olingan to‘plamga — kompaniyaning Toshkentdagi mijozlariga umumlashtirishga imkon beradi. A javob xulosani to‘plamga kirmagan boshqa kompaniyalarning mijozlariga kengaytirgan. C javob tasodifiy taqsimlashni e’tiborsiz qoldiradi. D javob 2,000 nafar mijozdan tashqariga xulosa chiqarishga imkon beruvchi tasodifiy tanlashni e’tiborsiz qoldiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":110,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"scope of conclusion\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"evaluating_statistical_claims\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A high school has 2,400 students. The school selected a random sample of 300 of its students and randomly assigned 150 of them to receive text message reminders about homework deadlines; the other 150 received no reminders. During the term, 120 of the students who received reminders and 96 of the students who did not receive reminders submitted all their homework on time. Based on this study, if all 2,400 students at the school received the reminders instead of none of them, what is the best estimate of the increase in the number of students who would submit all their homework on time?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"384\"],\"explanation_en\":\"With reminders, $\\\\frac{120}{150} = 80\\\\%$ of students submitted all homework on time; without reminders, $\\\\frac{96}{150} = 64\\\\%$ did. Because the students were randomly assigned, the 16-percentage-point difference can be attributed to the reminders, and because the sample was random, it applies to all students at the school. The expected increase is $0.16 \\\\times 2{,}400 = 384$ students. Answering 24 gives only the difference within the study ($120 - 96$). Enter 384.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Eslatma olganlarning $\\\\frac{120}{150} = 80\\\\%$ i, eslatma olmaganlarning esa $\\\\frac{96}{150} = 64\\\\%$ i barcha uy vazifalarini o‘z vaqtida topshirgan. O‘quvchilar tasodifiy taqsimlangani uchun 16 foiz punktlik farqni eslatmalarga bog‘lash mumkin, tanlanma tasodifiy bo‘lgani uchun esa bu maktabning barcha o‘quvchilariga tegishli. Kutilgan o‘sish $0.16 \\\\times 2{,}400 = 384$ nafar o‘quvchi. 24 deb javob berish faqat tadqiqot ichidagi farqni ($120 - 96$) beradi. Javob: 384.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"experiment results\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"evaluating_statistical_claims\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Using data for all 120 districts in a country, an economist found that districts with more cafés per 10,000 residents tend to have a higher average household income. A local official claims that the data show that opening more cafés in a district will raise household incomes there. Which of the following is the best evaluation of the official's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"The claim is supported, because the data include every district in the country rather than a sample.\",\"option_b\":\"The claim is supported, because the association between cafés and income is positive.\",\"option_c\":\"The claim is not supported, because the districts were not randomly assigned to have more or fewer cafés; for example, higher incomes may lead to more cafés.\",\"option_d\":\"The claim is not supported, because 120 districts are too few to show an association.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"This is an observational study: no one assigned districts to have more or fewer cafés. A positive association does not show which variable affects the other, or whether some third factor, such as the size of the district's businesses, affects both. In fact, it is reasonable that districts where people earn more can support more cafés. Choice A is a trap: even data for the whole population show only an association, not a cause. Choice B treats association as causation. Choice D is wrong because an association was observed among all 120 districts; the problem is the type of study, not its size.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bu kuzatuv tadqiqoti: hech kim tumanlarni ko‘proq yoki kamroq kafeli bo‘lishga taqsimlamagan. Musbat bog‘liqlik qaysi o‘zgaruvchi qaysi biriga ta’sir qilishini yoki ikkalasiga ham ta’sir qiladigan uchinchi omil (masalan, tumandagi korxonalar ko‘lami) bor-yo‘qligini ko‘rsatmaydi. Aslida, odamlar ko‘proq daromad oladigan tumanlarda ko‘proq kafe faoliyat yuritishi mantiqan to‘g‘ri. A javob — tuzoq: hatto butun to‘plam bo‘yicha ma’lumotlar ham sababni emas, faqat bog‘liqlikni ko‘rsatadi. B javob bog‘liqlikni sabab deb hisoblaydi. D javob noto‘g‘ri, chunki bog‘liqlik barcha 120 ta tumanda kuzatilgan; muammo tadqiqot hajmida emas, uning turida.\",\"estimated_seconds\":110,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"association vs causation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"evaluating_statistical_claims\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Two studies examined whether drinking a glass of warm milk before bed affects how quickly adults fall asleep.\\\\n\\\\nStudy 1: A random sample of 800 adults in a city was surveyed. The adults who reported drinking warm milk before bed also reported falling asleep faster, on average, than the adults who did not.\\\\n\\\\nStudy 2: 60 adults in the same city volunteered for a study. Half were randomly assigned to drink warm milk before bed for two weeks, and the other half drank nothing before bed. The adults who drank warm milk fell asleep faster, on average.\\\\n\\\\nWhich of the following statements is best supported by the two studies?\",\"option_a\":\"Study 1 shows that drinking warm milk causes adults in the city to fall asleep faster.\",\"option_b\":\"Study 2 shows that drinking warm milk causes all adults in the city to fall asleep faster.\",\"option_c\":\"Study 1 shows an association for adults in the city, and Study 2 suggests that warm milk causes faster sleep for adults similar to its volunteers.\",\"option_d\":\"Neither study supports any conclusion, because each study has a weakness.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Study 1 used random sampling but no random assignment, so it supports an association that can be generalized to adults in the city, not a causal conclusion. Study 2 used random assignment but volunteers, so it supports a causal conclusion only for adults similar to the volunteers. Choice A claims causation from an observational study. Choice B generalizes Study 2's causal result to all adults in the city, but the volunteers were not a random sample. Choice D is too extreme: each study has a limitation, but each still supports a limited conclusion.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"1-tadqiqotda tasodifiy tanlash bor, lekin tasodifiy taqsimlash yo‘q, shuning uchun u sababiy xulosani emas, shahar kattalariga umumlashtirish mumkin bo‘lgan bog‘liqlikni asoslaydi. 2-tadqiqotda tasodifiy taqsimlash bor, lekin ishtirokchilar ko‘ngillilar, shuning uchun u sababiy xulosani faqat ko‘ngillilarga o‘xshash kattalar uchun asoslaydi. A javob kuzatuv tadqiqotidan sababiy xulosa chiqargan. B javob 2-tadqiqotning sababiy natijasini shaharning barcha kattalariga umumlashtirgan, lekin ko‘ngillilar tasodifiy tanlanma emas edi. D javob haddan oshgan: har bir tadqiqotning cheklovi bor, lekin har biri baribir cheklangan xulosani asoslaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"compare designs\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Weaver birds build woven nests of grass that hang from the tips of thin branches, often directly over water. The nests of the village weaver, which ties each strand in place with its beak and feet, ______ very difficult for snakes and other predators to reach.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"is\",\"option_b\":\"are\",\"option_c\":\"was\",\"option_d\":\"has been\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The subject of the main clause is the plural noun “nests,” not “village weaver” or the singular words inside the “which” clause. A plural subject needs a plural verb, and the passage is written in the present tense, so “are” (choice B) is correct.\\n\\nChoices A, C, and D are all singular verbs (“is,” “was,” “has been”), which would agree with “weaver” rather than with the true subject, “nests.” Choice C is also in the past tense, which does not fit the present-tense description.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bosh gapning egasi — ko‘plikdagi “nests,” “village weaver” yoki “which” ergash gapidagi birlikdagi so‘zlar emas. Ko‘plikdagi egaga ko‘plikdagi fe’l kerak, matn esa hozirgi zamonda yozilgan, shuning uchun “are” (B) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA, C va D — birlikdagi fe’llar (“is,” “was,” “has been”); ular haqiqiy ega “nests” bilan emas, “weaver” bilan moslashadi. C bundan tashqari o‘tgan zamonda, bu hozirgi zamondagi tasvirga mos kelmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"sva-interrupter\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"To study how imagination develops, psychologist Hana Okafor asked 120 children between the ages of four and nine to draw an animal that does not exist. Many of the ______ drawings combined parts of familiar animals, such as a fish with bird wings, while the older children more often invented entirely new body shapes.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"childrens'\",\"option_b\":\"childrens\",\"option_c\":\"children\",\"option_d\":\"children's\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The blank needs the plural possessive of “child”: the drawings belong to the children. “Children” is an irregular plural that does not end in -s, so its possessive is formed by adding 's: “children's” (choice D).\\n\\nChoice A adds -s and an apostrophe to a word that is already plural; “childrens” is not an English word. Choice B has the same error and is also not possessive. Choice C is plural but not possessive, so it does not show that the drawings belong to the children.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bo‘sh joyga “child” so‘zining ko‘plikdagi qaratqich shakli kerak: rasmlar bolalarga tegishli. “Children” — -s bilan tugamaydigan noqonuniy ko‘plik shakli, shuning uchun uning qaratqich shakli 's qo‘shib yasaladi: “children's” (D).\\n\\nA allaqachon ko‘plikdagi so‘zga -s va apostrof qo‘shadi; “childrens” degan so‘z ingliz tilida yo‘q. B da ham xuddi shu xato bor va u qaratqich shaklda ham emas. C ko‘plikda, lekin qaratqich shaklda emas, shuning uchun rasmlar bolalarga tegishli ekanini ko‘rsatmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"plural-possessive-irregular\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Strong winds carry salt and dust from the dried-up bed of the Aral Sea across the surrounding region. To help hold the soil in place, last spring a group of volunteers ______ about 2,000 saxaul seedlings on a stretch of the exposed seabed. By autumn, most of the young shrubs had taken root.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"plants\",\"option_b\":\"will plant\",\"option_c\":\"planted\",\"option_d\":\"has been planting\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The phrase “last spring” shows that the planting happened at a specific time in the past, and the next sentence (“By autumn, most of the young shrubs had taken root”) confirms that it is finished. The simple past tense, “planted” (choice C), is correct.\\n\\nChoice A, “plants,” is present tense. Choice B, “will plant,” is future tense. Choice D, “has been planting,” describes an action continuing up to the present, which conflicts with the completed action at a specific past time.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“last spring” birikmasi ekish o‘tmishdagi aniq bir vaqtda bo‘lganini ko‘rsatadi, keyingi gap (“By autumn, most of the young shrubs had taken root”) esa bu ish tugaganini tasdiqlaydi. Oddiy o‘tgan zamon — “planted” (C) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA — “plants” hozirgi zamon. B — “will plant” kelasi zamon. D — “has been planting” hozirgacha davom etayotgan harakatni bildiradi, bu o‘tmishdagi aniq vaqtda tugagan harakatga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"tense-time-marker\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The small energy company Lumora sells solar lanterns to families in villages without electricity. After customers complained that the lanterns' batteries wore out too quickly, the company redesigned ______ lanterns so that the batteries could be replaced without special tools.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"it's\",\"option_b\":\"their\",\"option_c\":\"its\",\"option_d\":\"they're\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The pronoun refers to “the company,” which is a singular noun, and it must show possession (the company's lanterns). The singular possessive pronoun is “its,” so choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A, “it's,” is a contraction of “it is” or “it has,” not a possessive. Choice B, “their,” is plural and does not agree with the singular “company.” Choice D, “they're,” is a contraction of “they are.”\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Olmosh birlikdagi “the company” ga ishora qiladi va egalikni ko‘rsatishi kerak (kompaniyaning chiroqlari). Birlikdagi egalik olmoshi — “its,” shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA — “it's” “it is” yoki “it has” ning qisqartmasi, egalik olmoshi emas. B — “their” ko‘plikda, birlikdagi “company” bilan moslashmaydi. D — “they're” esa “they are” ning qisqartmasi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"pronoun-its-their\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The Gur-e-Amir mausoleum in Samarkand, with its ribbed turquoise dome, was completed in the early fifteenth century. Inside the main chamber, a large block of dark green jade ______ the burial place of the conqueror Timur.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"marks\",\"option_b\":\"marking\",\"option_c\":\"to mark\",\"option_d\":\"having marked\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The second sentence has a subject, “a large block of dark green jade,” but no main verb. The blank must supply a finite verb that can serve as the sentence's main verb, so “marks” (choice A) is correct.\\n\\nChoices B (“marking”), C (“to mark”), and D (“having marked”) are nonfinite forms—a participle, an infinitive, and a perfect participle. None of them can act as the main verb of a sentence, so each leaves the sentence as a fragment.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkinchi gapda ega (“a large block of dark green jade”) bor, ammo bosh kesim yo‘q. Bo‘sh joyga gapning bosh kesimi bo‘la oladigan shaxsli (finite) fe’l kerak, shuning uchun “marks” (A) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nB (“marking”), C (“to mark”) va D (“having marked”) — shaxssiz (nonfinite) shakllar: sifatdosh, infinitiv va tugallangan sifatdosh. Ularning hech biri gapning bosh kesimi bo‘la olmaydi, shuning uchun gap to‘liq bo‘lmay qoladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"finite-verb-needed\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"A network of automatic weather stations is spread across the mountain valleys of the national park. The readings from each weather ______ sent by radio to the park's central office twice a day, where forecasters use them to warn hikers about sudden storms.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"station are\",\"option_b\":\"station is\",\"option_c\":\"station was\",\"option_d\":\"station has been\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The subject of the sentence is the plural noun “readings”; “from each weather station” is only a prepositional phrase describing them. A plural subject needs a plural verb, and the passage describes a regular, present-day routine, so “are” (choice A) is correct.\\n\\nChoices B, C, and D use singular verbs (“is,” “was,” “has been”) that agree with “station,” the noun closest to the verb, rather than with the true subject, “readings.” Choice C is also in the past tense, which does not fit the present-tense routine.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapning egasi — ko‘plikdagi “readings”; “from each weather station” esa uni tasvirlovchi predlogli birikma xolos. Ko‘plikdagi egaga ko‘plikdagi fe’l kerak, matn esa hozirgi muntazam jarayonni tasvirlaydi, shuning uchun “are” (A) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nB, C va D birlikdagi fe’llardan (“is,” “was,” “has been”) foydalanadi; ular haqiqiy ega “readings” bilan emas, fe’lga eng yaqin turgan “station” bilan moslashadi. C bundan tashqari o‘tgan zamonda, bu hozirgi muntazam jarayonga mos emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"sva-of-phrase\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In 2007, the African Union launched the Great Green Wall initiative, a project ______ to restore degraded land in a broad belt across the Sahel region, from Senegal in the west to Djibouti in the east.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"intends\",\"option_b\":\"is intended\",\"option_c\":\"intended\",\"option_d\":\"was intended\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The sentence already has a subject and a main verb: “the African Union launched.” The phrase that follows, “a project ___ to restore degraded land …,” describes the initiative, so the blank needs a nonfinite form that can modify “project.” The participle “intended” (choice C) does this: “a project intended to restore degraded land.”\\n\\nChoices A (“intends”), B (“is intended”), and D (“was intended”) are finite verbs. Adding any of them would create a second main clause attached to the first with only a comma, and the sentence would no longer be grammatical.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapda allaqachon ega va bosh kesim bor: “the African Union launched.” Undan keyingi “a project ___ to restore degraded land …” birikmasi tashabbusni tasvirlaydi, shuning uchun bo‘sh joyga “project” ni aniqlay oladigan shaxssiz (nonfinite) shakl kerak. “intended” sifatdoshi (C) aynan shu vazifani bajaradi: “a project intended to restore degraded land.”\\n\\nA (“intends”), B (“is intended”) va D (“was intended”) — shaxsli fe’llar. Ularning birortasi qo‘yilsa, birinchi gapga faqat vergul bilan ulangan ikkinchi bosh gap hosil bo‘ladi va gap grammatik jihatdan noto‘g‘ri bo‘lib qoladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"nonfinite-modifier\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Cracks have appeared in the concrete of the city's sixty-year-old dam. The team of engineers, geologists, and hydrologists that the city hired to study the structure ______ that the dam be reinforced before the spring floods arrive.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"has recommended\",\"option_b\":\"have recommended\",\"option_c\":\"are recommending\",\"option_d\":\"recommend\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The subject of the second sentence is “team,” a singular collective noun. The plural nouns “engineers, geologists, and hydrologists” are part of a prepositional phrase that describes the team, and the “that” clause also describes it. A singular subject needs a singular verb, so “has recommended” (choice A) is correct.\\n\\nChoices B (“have recommended”), C (“are recommending”), and D (“recommend”) are plural verbs. They agree with the nearby plural nouns in the list rather than with the true subject, “team.”\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkinchi gapning egasi — birlikdagi jamlovchi ot “team.” Ko‘plikdagi “engineers, geologists, and hydrologists” jamoani tasvirlovchi predlogli birikmaning bir qismi, “that” ergash gapi ham uni tasvirlaydi. Birlikdagi egaga birlikdagi fe’l kerak, shuning uchun “has recommended” (A) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nB (“have recommended”), C (“are recommending”) va D (“recommend”) — ko‘plikdagi fe’llar. Ular haqiqiy ega “team” bilan emas, ro‘yxatdagi yaqin turgan ko‘plikdagi otlar bilan moslashadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"sva-collective-noun\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Seed banks store seeds at low temperatures so that rare plant varieties are not lost. To find out how long such seeds stay alive, a research team tested samples of wild wheat that had been placed in storage decades earlier. Stored in a cool, dark vault since ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"1983, the researchers found that the wild wheat seeds still sprouted when planted in 2023.\",\"option_b\":\"1983, the wild wheat seeds' ability to sprout surprised the researchers when they planted them in 2023.\",\"option_c\":\"1983, sprouting still occurred when the researchers planted the wild wheat seeds in 2023.\",\"option_d\":\"1983, the wild wheat seeds still sprouted when the researchers planted them in 2023.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The opening phrase “Stored in a cool, dark vault since 1983” must be followed immediately by the noun that was stored. The seeds were stored, so choice D, which places “the wild wheat seeds” right after the comma, is correct.\\n\\nChoice A makes it seem that the researchers were stored in the vault. Choice B makes the seeds' “ability to sprout” the thing that was stored. Choice C makes “sprouting” the thing that was stored. In each case, the modifier dangles because the word after the comma is not what was stored.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Kirish birikmasi “Stored in a cool, dark vault since 1983” dan keyin darhol saqlangan narsani bildiruvchi ot kelishi kerak. Saqlangan narsa — urug‘lar, shuning uchun vergul ortidan “the wild wheat seeds” ni qo‘yadigan D to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA tadqiqotchilar omborda saqlangandek ko‘rsatadi. B saqlangan narsa urug‘larning “unib chiqish qobiliyati” bo‘lib qoladi. C da saqlangan narsa “sprouting” (unib chiqish) bo‘lib qoladi. Har uchala holatda ham vergul ortidagi so‘z saqlangan narsa emas, shuning uchun aniqlovchi “osilib” qoladi (dangling modifier).\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"dangling-modifier\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In 2009, a handful of neighbors began clearing trash from an empty lot and planting vegetables there. Since its founding that year, the volunteer group ______ more than forty abandoned lots across the city into community gardens, and it continues to add new sites every season.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"turns\",\"option_b\":\"will turn\",\"option_c\":\"has turned\",\"option_d\":\"had turned\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The phrase “Since its founding that year” describes an action that began in the past and continues up to the present, and the sentence goes on to say that the group “continues to add new sites.” The present perfect tense, “has turned” (choice C), expresses exactly this.\\n\\nChoice A, “turns,” is the simple present, which does not connect the past starting point to the present. Choice B, “will turn,” is future tense. Choice D, “had turned,” is past perfect, which describes an action completed before another past event and conflicts with “continues.”\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“Since its founding that year” birikmasi o‘tmishda boshlanib, hozirgacha davom etayotgan harakatni bildiradi, gap davomida esa guruh “continues to add new sites” deyiladi. Aynan shuni hozirgi tugallangan zamon (present perfect) — “has turned” (C) ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA — “turns” oddiy hozirgi zamon, u o‘tmishdagi boshlanish nuqtasini hozirgi vaqt bilan bog‘lamaydi. B — “will turn” kelasi zamon. D — “had turned” o‘tgan tugallangan zamon (past perfect), u o‘tmishdagi boshqa voqeadan oldin tugagan harakatni bildiradi va “continues” ga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"tense-present-perfect\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Unlike orb-weaving spiders, which build webs and wait for insects to fly into them, jumping spiders stalk and pounce on their prey. To judge the distance of a jump accurately, these hunters rely on sight: ______ two large forward-facing eyes give them unusually sharp vision for animals of their size.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"its\",\"option_b\":\"their\",\"option_c\":\"they're\",\"option_d\":\"there\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The pronoun refers to “these hunters,” that is, the jumping spiders, which is plural. The eyes belong to the spiders, so a plural possessive pronoun is needed: “their” (choice B).\\n\\nChoice A, “its,” is singular and does not agree with the plural “hunters.” Choice C, “they're,” is a contraction of “they are,” not a possessive. Choice D, “there,” indicates a place or begins a “there is/are” sentence; it does not show possession.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Olmosh “these hunters,” ya’ni ko‘plikdagi sakrovchi o‘rgimchaklarga ishora qiladi. Ko‘zlar o‘rgimchaklarga tegishli, shuning uchun ko‘plikdagi egalik olmoshi kerak: “their” (B).\\n\\nA — “its” birlikda, ko‘plikdagi “hunters” bilan moslashmaydi. C — “they're” “they are” ning qisqartmasi, egalik olmoshi emas. D — “there” joyni bildiradi yoki “there is/are” gapini boshlaydi; egalikni ko‘rsatmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"pronoun-antecedent\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Historians who study medieval trade routes often visit the city archive. One of the oldest surviving maps of the region, drawn in ink on cotton paper by an unknown fifteenth-century traveler, ______ there in a climate-controlled case.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"are kept\",\"option_b\":\"were kept\",\"option_c\":\"have been kept\",\"option_d\":\"is kept\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The subject of the second sentence is “One,” which is singular; “of the oldest surviving maps of the region” is a prepositional phrase, and “drawn in ink …” is a descriptive phrase. A singular subject needs a singular verb, and the first sentence describes the present, so “is kept” (choice D) is correct.\\n\\nChoices A (“are kept”), B (“were kept”), and C (“have been kept”) are plural verbs that agree with “maps” instead of with the true subject, “One.” Choice B is also in the past tense, which conflicts with the present-tense context.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkinchi gapning egasi — birlikdagi “One”; “of the oldest surviving maps of the region” — predlogli birikma, “drawn in ink …” esa tasvirlovchi birikma. Birlikdagi egaga birlikdagi fe’l kerak, birinchi gap esa hozirgi zamonni tasvirlaydi, shuning uchun “is kept” (D) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA (“are kept”), B (“were kept”) va C (“have been kept”) — ko‘plikdagi fe’llar; ular haqiqiy ega “One” bilan emas, “maps” bilan moslashadi. B bundan tashqari o‘tgan zamonda, bu hozirgi zamon kontekstiga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"sva-one-of\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"For years, a small portrait of a merchant in a fur-trimmed coat was listed in the museum's catalog as the work of an unknown painter. Having examined the panel under ultraviolet light, ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"the painting's hidden signature was discovered by the conservators beneath a layer of old varnish.\",\"option_b\":\"a hidden signature beneath a layer of old varnish became visible to the conservators.\",\"option_c\":\"conservation work revealed a hidden signature beneath a layer of old varnish.\",\"option_d\":\"the conservators discovered a hidden signature beneath a layer of old varnish.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The opening phrase “Having examined the panel under ultraviolet light” describes people who did the examining. The noun that follows the comma must name those people, so choice D, which begins with “the conservators,” is correct.\\n\\nChoice A places “the painting's hidden signature” after the comma, suggesting that the signature examined the panel; the passive voice hides the real actors. Choice B suggests that “a hidden signature” did the examining. Choice C suggests that “conservation work,” an activity rather than a person, examined the panel. In each, the modifier dangles.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Kirish birikmasi “Having examined the panel under ultraviolet light” tekshirishni bajargan shaxslarni tasvirlaydi. Verguldan keyingi ot aynan shu shaxslarni nomlashi kerak, shuning uchun “the conservators” bilan boshlanadigan D to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA verguldan keyin “the painting's hidden signature” ni qo‘yadi — go‘yo panelni imzo tekshirgandek; majhul nisbat haqiqiy bajaruvchilarni yashiradi. B da panelni “a hidden signature” tekshirgandek ko‘rinadi. C da tekshiruvchi shaxs emas, faoliyat — “conservation work” bo‘lib qoladi. Har bir holatda aniqlovchi “osilib” qoladi (dangling modifier).\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"dangling-modifier\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Divers exploring a ship that sank off the coast of Sicily in the 1600s have recovered thousands of objects, most of them ordinary goods rather than treasure. Among the most informative items brought up from the wreck so far ______ a brass navigational instrument, a set of lead weights, and several dozen glazed bowls stacked as cargo.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"were\",\"option_b\":\"was\",\"option_c\":\"is\",\"option_d\":\"has been\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"This sentence is inverted: the verb comes before its subject. The subject is the compound list “a brass navigational instrument, a set of lead weights, and several dozen glazed bowls,” which is plural, and the context describes completed recoveries. The plural verb “were” (choice A) agrees with this subject.\\n\\nChoices B (“was”), C (“is”), and D (“has been”) are singular. They might seem to agree with “the wreck,” the singular noun closest to the blank, or with the first item in the list, but the subject is all three items together.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bu gapda tartib teskari: kesim egadan oldin keladi. Ega — ko‘plikdagi uyushiq ro‘yxat “a brass navigational instrument, a set of lead weights, and several dozen glazed bowls,” kontekst esa topib olingan narsalarni tasvirlaydi. Bu egaga ko‘plikdagi “were” (A) mos keladi.\\n\\nB (“was”), C (“is”) va D (“has been”) — birlikdagi fe’llar. Ular bo‘sh joyga eng yaqin turgan birlikdagi “the wreck” yoki ro‘yxatdagi birinchi narsa bilan moslashgandek tuyulishi mumkin, lekin ega uchala narsaning hammasi birgalikda.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"sva-inverted\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"When an insect lands on the sensitive plant, its leaflets snap shut in seconds. Plants of the genus *Mimosa*, whose feathery leaves fold inward and droop soon after being touched or shaken, ______ this rapid movement by quickly shifting water out of swollen cells at the base of each leaflet.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"producing\",\"option_b\":\"produce\",\"option_c\":\"which produce\",\"option_d\":\"having produced\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The second sentence's subject is “Plants of the genus *Mimosa*.” The long “whose” clause that follows contains its own verbs (“fold,” “droop”), but these belong to the clause, not to the main sentence. The main clause still needs a finite verb, so “produce” (choice B) is correct; it is also plural, agreeing with “Plants.”\\n\\nChoice A (“producing”) and choice D (“having produced”) are participles, which cannot serve as the main verb, leaving the sentence a fragment. Choice C (“which produce”) creates a second relative clause, so the sentence still has no main verb.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkinchi gapning egasi — “Plants of the genus *Mimosa*.” Undan keyingi uzun “whose” ergash gapida o‘z fe’llari (“fold,” “droop”) bor, lekin ular bosh gapga emas, ergash gapga tegishli. Bosh gapga hali ham shaxsli fe’l kerak, shuning uchun “produce” (B) to‘g‘ri; u ko‘plikda ham bo‘lib, “Plants” bilan moslashadi.\\n\\nA (“producing”) va D (“having produced”) — sifatdoshlar, ular bosh kesim bo‘la olmaydi va gap to‘liq bo‘lmay qoladi. C (“which produce”) yana bir nisbiy ergash gap hosil qiladi, shuning uchun gapda baribir bosh kesim bo‘lmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"finite-verb-long-subject\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Ancient teeth and bones hold chemical traces of what people once ate. Reconstructing the diets of people who lived thousands of years ago, using isotopes preserved in their teeth and bones, ______ researchers to combine laboratory methods from chemistry with careful excavation techniques from archaeology.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"require\",\"option_b\":\"requires\",\"option_c\":\"have required\",\"option_d\":\"are requiring\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The subject of the second sentence is the gerund phrase “Reconstructing the diets of people who lived thousands of years ago.” A gerund phrase acts as a single thing and takes a singular verb, so “requires” (choice B) is correct. The plural nouns “diets,” “people,” “isotopes,” “teeth,” and “bones” are all inside the subject phrase or the interrupting phrase and do not control the verb.\\n\\nChoices A (“require”), C (“have required”), and D (“are requiring”) are plural verbs that wrongly agree with one of those nearby plural nouns.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkinchi gapning egasi — gerundli birikma “Reconstructing the diets of people who lived thousands of years ago.” Gerundli birikma bitta harakatni bildiradi va birlikdagi fe’l talab qiladi, shuning uchun “requires” (B) to‘g‘ri. Ko‘plikdagi “diets,” “people,” “isotopes,” “teeth” va “bones” otlari ega birikmasi yoki kiritma birikma ichida joylashgan va fe’lning shaklini belgilamaydi.\\n\\nA (“require”), C (“have required”) va D (“are requiring”) — ko‘plikdagi fe’llar; ular yaqin turgan ko‘plikdagi otlardan biri bilan noto‘g‘ri moslashadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"sva-gerund-subject\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inference_margin_of_error\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A transport company selected a random sample of its taxi drivers in Tashkent and recorded the distance each driver traveled on one workday. Based on the sample, the mean distance traveled by all of the company's taxi drivers on that day is estimated to be 182 kilometers, with an associated margin of error of 9 kilometers. Which of the following is a plausible value for the mean distance, in kilometers, traveled by all of the company's taxi drivers on that day?\",\"option_a\":\"168\",\"option_b\":\"175\",\"option_c\":\"193\",\"option_d\":\"198\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The plausible values are from $182 - 9 = 173$ to $182 + 9 = 191$ kilometers. Only 175 is in this interval. Choice A is below 173. Choice C, 193, is just above 191, and choice D is even farther above the interval; these would be plausible only with a larger margin of error.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ehtimoliy qiymatlar $182 - 9 = 173$ dan $182 + 9 = 191$ kilometrgacha. Bu oraliqda faqat 175 bor. A javob 173 dan kichik. C javob (193) 191 dan sal katta, D javob esa oraliqdan yanada uzoqda; ular faqat xatolik chegarasi kattaroq bo‘lganda ehtimoliy bo‘lardi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"plausible values\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inference_margin_of_error\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A school has 4,500 students. A random sample of 150 of the students was asked whether they play a musical instrument, and 27 of the students in the sample said they do. Based on the sample, what is the best estimate of the number of students at the school who play a musical instrument?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"810\"],\"explanation_en\":\"In the random sample, $\\\\frac{27}{150} = 0.18$, or 18%, of the students play an instrument. Applying this proportion to the whole school gives $0.18 \\\\times 4{,}500 = 810$ students. Answering 27 counts only the students in the sample. Enter 810.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tasodifiy tanlanmada o‘quvchilarning $\\\\frac{27}{150} = 0.18$ qismi, ya’ni 18% i cholg‘u asbobida chaladi. Bu ulushni butun maktabga qo‘llasak, $0.18 \\\\times 4{,}500 = 810$ nafar o‘quvchi chiqadi. 27 deb javob berish faqat tanlanmadagi o‘quvchilarni sanaydi. Javob: 810.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"estimate from sample\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inference_margin_of_error\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Based on a random sample of farmers in a region, researchers estimated the percentage of all farmers in the region who use drip irrigation. They reported that the plausible values for this percentage are from 31% to 39%. If the interval was found by adding and subtracting the margin of error from the sample estimate, what is the margin of error, in percentage points?\",\"option_a\":\"4\",\"option_b\":\"8\",\"option_c\":\"31\",\"option_d\":\"35\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The estimate is the midpoint of the interval, $\\\\frac{31 + 39}{2} = 35\\\\%$, and the margin of error is the distance from the estimate to either endpoint: $39 - 35 = 4$ percentage points. Choice B is the full width of the interval, which is twice the margin of error. Choice C is the lower endpoint, and choice D is the estimate itself.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Baho — oraliqning o‘rtasi: $\\\\frac{31 + 39}{2} = 35\\\\%$, xatolik chegarasi esa bahodan istalgan chetgacha bo‘lgan masofa: $39 - 35 = 4$ foiz punkti. B javob — oraliqning to‘liq kengligi, u xatolik chegarasidan ikki marta katta. C javob — quyi chegara, D javob esa bahoning o‘zi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"margin from interval\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inference_margin_of_error\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A researcher plans to estimate the mean number of hours per week that university students in a city spend on part-time jobs. The researcher will select a random sample of students from all university students in the city. Which of the following sample sizes would most likely result in the smallest margin of error for the estimate?\",\"option_a\":\"50\",\"option_b\":\"200\",\"option_c\":\"800\",\"option_d\":\"1,600\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"When the samples are selected the same way from the same population, a larger random sample usually gives a more precise estimate, which means a smaller margin of error. The largest sample, 1,600 students, would most likely give the smallest margin of error. Choice A, the smallest sample, would most likely give the **largest** margin of error, and choices B and C are smaller than 1,600.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tanlanmalar bir xil to‘plamdan bir xil usulda olinganda, kattaroq tasodifiy tanlanma odatda aniqroq baho beradi, ya’ni xatolik chegarasi kichikroq bo‘ladi. Eng katta tanlanma — 1,600 nafar talaba — eng kichik xatolik chegarasini berishi ehtimoli eng yuqori. A javob, eng kichik tanlanma, aksincha **eng katta** xatolik chegarasini berishi mumkin, B va C javoblar esa 1,600 dan kichik.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"sample size\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inference_margin_of_error\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A random sample of adults in Namangan was asked whether they shop at an online marketplace at least once a month. Based on the sample, it is estimated that 62% of all adults in Namangan shop at an online marketplace at least once a month, with an associated margin of error of 3.8 percentage points. Based on this estimate and margin of error, what is the greatest plausible percentage of all adults in Namangan who shop at an online marketplace at least once a month?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"65.8\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The plausible values are the estimate plus or minus the margin of error, from $62 - 3.8 = 58.2\\\\%$ to $62 + 3.8 = 65.8\\\\%$. The greatest plausible percentage is 65.8%. Enter 65.8 (without the percent sign).\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ehtimoliy qiymatlar — baho plyus-minus xatolik chegarasi: $62 - 3.8 = 58.2\\\\%$ dan $62 + 3.8 = 65.8\\\\%$ gacha. Eng katta ehtimoliy foiz 65.8%. Javob: 65.8 (foiz belgisisiz).\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"plausible values\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inference_margin_of_error\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A random sample of 120 bus passengers in Samarkand was surveyed. Based on the sample, the mean waiting time at a bus stop for all bus passengers in Samarkand is estimated to be 11 minutes, with an associated margin of error of 2 minutes. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?\",\"option_a\":\"Every bus passenger in Samarkand waits between 9 and 13 minutes at a bus stop.\",\"option_b\":\"It is plausible that the mean waiting time for all bus passengers in Samarkand is between 9 and 13 minutes.\",\"option_c\":\"The mean waiting time for all bus passengers in Samarkand is exactly 11 minutes.\",\"option_d\":\"The mean waiting time for the 120 passengers in the sample is between 9 and 13 minutes, but it is not known exactly.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"An estimate with a margin of error gives an interval of plausible values for a population **mean**: from $11 - 2 = 9$ to $11 + 2 = 13$ minutes. Choice A is about individual passengers; many passengers may wait less than 9 or more than 13 minutes. Choice C claims too much: 11 minutes is only an estimate. Choice D is wrong because the sample mean is known exactly (11 minutes); the uncertainty is about the population mean.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Xatolik chegarali baho to‘plam **o‘rtachasi** uchun ehtimoliy qiymatlar oralig‘ini beradi: $11 - 2 = 9$ dan $11 + 2 = 13$ daqiqagacha. A javob alohida yo‘lovchilar haqida; ko‘p yo‘lovchilar 9 daqiqadan kam yoki 13 daqiqadan ko‘p kutishi mumkin. C javob ortiqcha da’vo qiladi: 11 daqiqa — faqat baho. D javob noto‘g‘ri, chunki tanlanma o‘rtachasi aniq ma’lum (11 daqiqa); noaniqlik to‘plam o‘rtachasiga tegishli.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"interpretation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inference_margin_of_error\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A district has 80,000 households. Based on a random sample of the district's households, it is estimated that 35% of all households in the district have a pet, with an associated margin of error of 4 percentage points. Which of the following is a plausible number of households in the district that have a pet?\",\"option_a\":\"24,000\",\"option_b\":\"26,500\",\"option_c\":\"32,000\",\"option_d\":\"35,000\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The plausible percentages are from $35 - 4 = 31\\\\%$ to $35 + 4 = 39\\\\%$. Of 80,000 households, that is from $0.31 \\\\times 80{,}000 = 24{,}800$ to $0.39 \\\\times 80{,}000 = 31{,}200$ households. Only 26,500 is in this range. Choice A (30% of the households) is too small. Choice C (40%) and choice D (about 44%) are too large; choice D uses the number 35 as if it were thousands of households.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ehtimoliy foizlar $35 - 4 = 31\\\\%$ dan $35 + 4 = 39\\\\%$ gacha. 80,000 ta xonadon uchun bu $0.31 \\\\times 80{,}000 = 24{,}800$ dan $0.39 \\\\times 80{,}000 = 31{,}200$ gacha. Bu oraliqda faqat 26,500 bor. A javob (xonadonlarning 30% i) juda kichik. C javob (40%) va D javob (taxminan 44%) juda katta; D javob 35 sonini go‘yo minglab xonadonlar soni deb olgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"population count\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inference_margin_of_error\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A town has 12,500 adult residents. Based on a random sample of the town's adult residents, it is estimated that 18% of all adult residents of the town ride a bicycle to work, with an associated margin of error of 2.4 percentage points. Based on this estimate and margin of error, what is the greatest plausible number of adult residents of the town who ride a bicycle to work?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"2550\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The greatest plausible percentage is $18 + 2.4 = 20.4\\\\%$. Of 12,500 residents, that is $0.204 \\\\times 12{,}500 = 2{,}550$. Using only the estimate, $0.18 \\\\times 12{,}500 = 2{,}250$, gives the best estimate, not the greatest plausible value. Enter 2550.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Eng katta ehtimoliy foiz $18 + 2.4 = 20.4\\\\%$. 12,500 nafar aholidan bu $0.204 \\\\times 12{,}500 = 2{,}550$. Faqat bahodan foydalanish, $0.18 \\\\times 12{,}500 = 2{,}250$, eng katta ehtimoliy qiymatni emas, eng yaxshi bahoni beradi. Javob: 2550.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"population count\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inference_margin_of_error\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Two researchers each estimated the mean daily water use of households in the same city. Each researcher selected a random sample of households from all households in the city. Researcher A used a sample of 150 households, and Researcher B used a sample of 900 households. The two researchers used the same method to calculate their margins of error. Which of the following is most likely true?\",\"option_a\":\"The margin of error for Researcher A's estimate is smaller than the margin of error for Researcher B's estimate.\",\"option_b\":\"The margin of error for Researcher B's estimate is smaller than the margin of error for Researcher A's estimate.\",\"option_c\":\"The margins of error are equal, because both samples come from the same city.\",\"option_d\":\"The margins of error cannot be compared, because the two samples will give different estimates.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"When everything else is the same, a larger random sample usually gives a smaller margin of error. Researcher B's sample (900 households) is larger than Researcher A's (150), so B's margin of error is most likely smaller. Choice A reverses this relationship. Choice C is wrong because the margin of error depends on the sample size, not only on the population. Choice D is wrong because the sample sizes, not the particular estimates, tell which margin is likely smaller.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Boshqa barcha shartlar bir xil bo‘lsa, kattaroq tasodifiy tanlanma odatda kichikroq xatolik chegarasini beradi. B tadqiqotchining tanlanmasi (900 xonadon) A nikidan (150) katta, demak B ning xatolik chegarasi kichikroq bo‘lishi ehtimoli yuqori. A javob bu bog‘liqlikni teskari qilgan. C javob noto‘g‘ri, chunki xatolik chegarasi faqat to‘plamga emas, tanlanma hajmiga ham bog‘liq. D javob noto‘g‘ri, chunki qaysi chegara kichikroq ekanini aniq bahoning o‘zi emas, tanlanma hajmlari ko‘rsatadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"sample size\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inference_margin_of_error\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Researchers selected a random sample of adults in City P and a separate random sample of adults in City Q and asked each adult whether they read the news online every day. Based on the samples, 46% of all adults in City P read the news online every day, with a margin of error of 4 percentage points, and 41% of all adults in City Q do, with a margin of error of 3 percentage points. Which of the following conclusions is best supported by these results?\",\"option_a\":\"The percentage of all adults who read the news online every day is greater in City P than in City Q.\",\"option_b\":\"The percentage of all adults who read the news online every day is greater in City Q than in City P.\",\"option_c\":\"It is plausible that the percentage of all adults who read the news online every day is the same in the two cities.\",\"option_d\":\"Exactly 5% more adults in City P than in City Q read the news online every day.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The plausible values are 42% to 50% for City P and 38% to 44% for City Q. These intervals overlap (for example, 43% is plausible for both cities), so it is plausible that the two percentages are equal. Choice A goes too far: although the estimate for City P is greater, the intervals overlap, so the data do not show that City P's percentage is greater. Choice B has no support in the data. Choice D is wrong because 5 is the difference between the two estimates, in percentage points, not a known difference, and it is not a percent of adults.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ehtimoliy qiymatlar P shahar uchun 42% dan 50% gacha, Q shahar uchun 38% dan 44% gacha. Bu oraliqlar kesishadi (masalan, 43% ikkala shahar uchun ham ehtimoliy), demak ikki foizning teng bo‘lishi ehtimoliy. A javob ortiqcha xulosa chiqaradi: P shahar bahosi katta bo‘lsa ham, oraliqlar kesishgani uchun ma’lumotlar P shahar foizi katta ekanini ko‘rsatmaydi. B javobni ma’lumotlar qo‘llab-quvvatlamaydi. D javob noto‘g‘ri, chunki 5 — ikki baho orasidagi foiz punktlardagi farq, ma’lum farq emas, va u kattalar sonining foizi ham emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"compare estimates\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inference_margin_of_error\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A beekeeper has 400 beehives. A random sample of 60 of the beehives was selected, and the amount of honey from each hive in one season was measured. Based on the sample, the mean amount of honey per hive for all 400 hives is estimated to be 18.5 kilograms, with an associated margin of error of 1.2 kilograms. Based on this estimate and margin of error, what is the greatest plausible total amount of honey, in kilograms, from all 400 hives?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"7880\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The greatest plausible mean is $18.5 + 1.2 = 19.7$ kilograms per hive. For 400 hives, the greatest plausible total is $19.7 \\\\times 400 = 7{,}880$ kilograms. Multiplying by 60 uses only the hives in the sample, and $18.5 \\\\times 400 = 7{,}400$ is the best estimate, not the greatest plausible value. Enter 7880.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Eng katta ehtimoliy o‘rtacha har bir uyaga $18.5 + 1.2 = 19.7$ kilogramm. 400 ta uya uchun eng katta ehtimoliy jami miqdor $19.7 \\\\times 400 = 7{,}880$ kilogramm. 60 ga ko‘paytirish faqat tanlanmadagi uyalarni hisobga oladi, $18.5 \\\\times 400 = 7{,}400$ esa eng katta ehtimoliy qiymat emas, eng yaxshi baho. Javob: 7880.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"mean total\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inference_margin_of_error\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A researcher selected a random sample of 400 students from all students at one university in Fergana and asked how many hours they spend on social media each day. Based on the sample, the mean time for all students at the university is estimated to be 3.2 hours, with an associated margin of error of 0.3 hour. Which of the following statements is best supported by this information?\",\"option_a\":\"It is plausible that the mean time spent on social media each day by all students at this university is between 2.9 and 3.5 hours.\",\"option_b\":\"It is plausible that the mean time spent on social media each day by all university students in Uzbekistan is between 2.9 and 3.5 hours.\",\"option_c\":\"Most students at this university spend between 2.9 and 3.5 hours on social media each day.\",\"option_d\":\"The mean time spent on social media each day by all students at this university is between 3.2 and 3.5 hours.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The sample was selected at random from the students at this one university, so the interval $3.2 \\\\pm 0.3$, from 2.9 to 3.5 hours, gives plausible values for the mean of **that** population. Choice B extends the conclusion to all university students in the country, who were not sampled. Choice C describes individual students instead of the mean. Choice D uses only the upper half of the interval; values below 3.2 are equally plausible.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tanlanma faqat shu bitta universitet talabalaridan tasodifiy olingan, shuning uchun $3.2 \\\\pm 0.3$ oraliq, ya’ni 2.9 dan 3.5 soatgacha, aynan **shu** to‘plam o‘rtachasining ehtimoliy qiymatlarini beradi. B javob xulosani tanlanma olinmagan butun mamlakatdagi talabalarga kengaytirgan. C javob o‘rtachani emas, alohida talabalarni tasvirlaydi. D javob oraliqning faqat yuqori yarmini olgan; 3.2 dan kichik qiymatlar ham xuddi shunday ehtimoliy.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"interpretation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inference_margin_of_error\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Researchers selected a random sample of households in District A, which has 6,000 households, and a separate random sample of households in District B, which has 9,000 households. Based on the samples, 30% of the households in District A have solar panels, with a margin of error of 4 percentage points, and 22% of the households in District B have solar panels, with a margin of error of 3 percentage points. Which of the following statements is best supported by these estimates?\",\"option_a\":\"District A has more households with solar panels than District B does.\",\"option_b\":\"District B has more households with solar panels than District A does.\",\"option_c\":\"It is plausible that the percentage of households with solar panels is the same in the two districts.\",\"option_d\":\"It is plausible that the number of households with solar panels is the same in the two districts.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The plausible percentages are 26% to 34% for District A and 19% to 25% for District B. These intervals do not overlap, so choice C is not supported. Converting to numbers of households: District A has from $0.26 \\\\times 6{,}000 = 1{,}560$ to $0.34 \\\\times 6{,}000 = 2{,}040$, and District B has from $0.19 \\\\times 9{,}000 = 1{,}710$ to $0.25 \\\\times 9{,}000 = 2{,}250$. These ranges overlap (for example, 1,800 is plausible for both), so the numbers may be equal. Choice A confuses a greater percentage with a greater number. Choice B is not certain either: the best estimates are 1,800 and 1,980, but because the ranges overlap, District A could have more.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ehtimoliy foizlar A tuman uchun 26% dan 34% gacha, B tuman uchun 19% dan 25% gacha. Bu oraliqlar kesishmaydi, shuning uchun C javob asoslanmagan. Xonadonlar soniga o‘tkazamiz: A tumanda $0.26 \\\\times 6{,}000 = 1{,}560$ dan $0.34 \\\\times 6{,}000 = 2{,}040$ gacha, B tumanda $0.19 \\\\times 9{,}000 = 1{,}710$ dan $0.25 \\\\times 9{,}000 = 2{,}250$ gacha. Bu oraliqlar kesishadi (masalan, 1,800 ikkalasi uchun ham ehtimoliy), demak sonlar teng bo‘lishi mumkin. A javob kattaroq foizni kattaroq son bilan adashtirgan. B javob ham aniq emas: eng yaxshi baholar 1,800 va 1,980, lekin oraliqlar kesishgani uchun A tumanda ko‘proq bo‘lishi ham mumkin.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"compare estimates\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inference_margin_of_error\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"An orchard has 6,000 apple trees. A random sample of 250 of the trees was inspected for a certain pest. Based on the sample, the plausible numbers of trees in the entire orchard that have the pest are from 1,140 to 1,500, where this range was found by applying the sample percentage plus or minus a margin of error to all 6,000 trees. How many of the trees in the sample had the pest?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"55\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The estimate is the middle of the range: $\\\\frac{1{,}140 + 1{,}500}{2} = 1{,}320$ trees. As a percentage of the orchard, that is $\\\\frac{1{,}320}{6{,}000} = 0.22$, or 22%, which is the sample percentage. In the sample of 250 trees, $0.22 \\\\times 250 = 55$ trees had the pest. (The margin of error is $\\\\frac{180}{6{,}000} = 3$ percentage points.) Enter 55.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Baho — oraliqning o‘rtasi: $\\\\frac{1{,}140 + 1{,}500}{2} = 1{,}320$ ta daraxt. Bog‘ga nisbatan foizda bu $\\\\frac{1{,}320}{6{,}000} = 0.22$, ya’ni 22% — bu tanlanmadagi foiz. 250 ta daraxtli tanlanmada zararkunanda $0.22 \\\\times 250 = 55$ ta daraxtda bo‘lgan. (Xatolik chegarasi $\\\\frac{180}{6{,}000} = 3$ foiz punkti.) Javob: 55.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"reverse interval\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inference_margin_of_error\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A mobile phone company selected a random sample of 500 of its customers and asked whether they were satisfied with the company's service. Based on the sample, it is estimated that 64% of all of the company's customers are satisfied, with an associated margin of error of 4.3 percentage points. The company's advertisement claims that at least 70% of its customers are satisfied with its service. Which of the following is the best evaluation of the advertisement's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"The claim is supported, because 70% is within two margins of error of 64%.\",\"option_b\":\"The claim is not supported, because all of the plausible values for the percentage of satisfied customers are less than 70%.\",\"option_c\":\"The data prove that exactly 64% of the company's customers are satisfied, so the claim is false.\",\"option_d\":\"The claim cannot be evaluated, because a sample of 500 customers is too small to give information about all customers.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The plausible values for the percentage of all customers who are satisfied are from $64 - 4.3 = 59.7\\\\%$ to $64 + 4.3 = 68.3\\\\%$. Every plausible value is less than 70%, so the data do not support the claim. Choice A uses two margins of error, but the plausible values are found with one margin of error. Choice C overstates the result: 64% is an estimate, not an exact value. Choice D is wrong because a random sample of 500 customers can give useful information about all customers; that is what the margin of error measures.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Barcha mijozlar orasida mamnunlar foizining ehtimoliy qiymatlari $64 - 4.3 = 59.7\\\\%$ dan $64 + 4.3 = 68.3\\\\%$ gacha. Har bir ehtimoliy qiymat 70% dan kichik, demak ma’lumotlar da’voni qo‘llab-quvvatlamaydi. A javob ikki xatolik chegarasini olgan, lekin ehtimoliy qiymatlar bitta xatolik chegarasi bilan topiladi. C javob natijani bo‘rttirgan: 64% — aniq qiymat emas, baho. D javob noto‘g‘ri, chunki 500 nafar mijozdan iborat tasodifiy tanlanma barcha mijozlar haqida foydali ma’lumot beradi; xatolik chegarasi aynan shuni o‘lchaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":110,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"evaluate claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inference_margin_of_error\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A city district has 24,000 adult residents. Based on a random sample of the district's adult residents, a researcher estimated the percentage of all adult residents who exercise at least three times a week, with an associated margin of error. Applying the plausible percentages to all 24,000 adult residents gives plausible numbers from 8,160 to 9,600. What is the margin of error, in percentage points?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"3\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Half the width of the range of plausible numbers is $\\\\frac{9{,}600 - 8{,}160}{2} = 720$ residents. As a percentage of 24,000 residents, that is $\\\\frac{720}{24{,}000} = 0.03$, or 3 percentage points. (The estimate is $\\\\frac{8{,}880}{24{,}000} = 37\\\\%$, so the plausible percentages are 34% to 40%.) Answering 6 uses the whole width of the range instead of half of it. Enter 3.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ehtimoliy sonlar oralig‘i kengligining yarmi $\\\\frac{9{,}600 - 8{,}160}{2} = 720$ nafar aholi. 24,000 nafar aholiga nisbatan foizda bu $\\\\frac{720}{24{,}000} = 0.03$, ya’ni 3 foiz punkti. (Baho $\\\\frac{8{,}880}{24{,}000} = 37\\\\%$, demak ehtimoliy foizlar 34% dan 40% gacha.) 6 deb javob berish oraliqning yarmini emas, to‘liq kengligini olishdir. Javob: 3.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"margin from interval\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Botanist Leila Haddad grew two groups of identical basil plants in the same type of soil and gave every plant the same amount of water. One group received eight hours of light each day, and the other received fourteen hours. After six weeks, the plants that had received fourteen hours of light weighed, on average, twice as much as the other plants. Because the amount of light was the only condition that differed between the groups, Haddad concluded that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"basil plants need more water than most other herbs do.\",\"option_b\":\"the extra hours of light most likely caused the greater growth.\",\"option_c\":\"the soil used in the experiment lacked nutrients that basil needs.\",\"option_d\":\"basil plants given only eight hours of light cannot survive longer than six weeks.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The two groups were treated the same in every way except the hours of light, and the group with more light grew much heavier. When only one condition differs, that condition is the most likely cause of the difference, so choice B follows.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because both groups received the same water and the text never compares basil with other herbs. Choice C is unsupported: both groups grew in the same soil, and the text gives no evidence that it lacked nutrients. Choice D goes too far; the text reports that these plants were lighter, not that they would die.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikki guruhga yorug‘lik soatlaridan boshqa barcha jihatdan bir xil sharoit yaratilgan va ko‘proq yorug‘lik olgan guruh ancha og‘irroq bo‘lib o‘sgan. Faqat bitta shart farq qilsa, aynan shu shart farqning eng ehtimoliy sababi bo‘ladi, shuning uchun B mantiqan kelib chiqadi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: ikkala guruh bir xil miqdorda suv olgan va matnda rayhon boshqa o‘tlar bilan solishtirilmaydi. C asossiz: ikkala guruh ham bir xil tuproqda o‘sgan, tuproqda ozuqa yetishmagani haqida dalil yo‘q. D haddan oshib ketadi: matnda bu o‘simliklar yengilroq bo‘lgani aytiladi, nobud bo‘lishi haqida emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science-controlled-experiment\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The merchant Yusuf Karimov kept a detailed diary throughout his trading journeys, and the surviving volumes cover the years 1871 to 1879. Entries for the year 1874, however, are missing entirely. In a letter written in 1880, Karimov's son mentioned that one volume of his father's diary had been lent to a family friend and was never returned. Given this information, it is reasonable to suspect that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Karimov did not make any trading journeys in 1874.\",\"option_b\":\"the family friend wrote the diary entries for 1874.\",\"option_c\":\"the volume that was lent and never returned contained the entries for 1874.\",\"option_d\":\"Karimov stopped keeping a diary after 1879 because he had lost one volume.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The surviving diary has a gap at 1874, and the son's letter says that one volume was lent out and never came back. Putting these two facts together, the missing volume most likely held the 1874 entries, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice A ignores the letter; the son's explanation suggests a lost volume, not a year without travel. Choice B has no support: the friend borrowed a volume but nothing suggests the friend wrote in it. Choice D invents a reason for something the text does not discuss; we are not told why the diary ends in 1879.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Saqlanib qolgan kundalikda 1874-yil uchun bo‘shliq bor, o‘g‘lining maktubida esa bir jild kimgadir berilgani va qaytarilmagani aytiladi. Bu ikki faktni birlashtirsak, yo‘qolgan jildda 1874-yil yozuvlari bo‘lgan bo‘lishi ehtimoli katta — C.\\n\\nA maktubni e’tiborsiz qoldiradi; o‘g‘il yo‘qolgan jild haqida gapiradi, safar qilinmagan yil haqida emas. B asossiz: oila do‘sti jildni olgan, lekin unga yozuv qilgani haqida hech narsa aytilmagan. D matnda muhokama qilinmagan narsaga sabab o‘ylab topadi; kundalik nega 1879-yilda tugashi aytilmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"history-missing-record\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"To find out whether longer hours would attract more visitors, a city library stayed open until 10 p.m. instead of 6 p.m. on weekdays for three months. Total weekday visits rose by only 4 percent. Nearly all of the additional visits took place between 6 p.m. and 7 p.m., and very few people came to the library after 8 p.m. These results suggest that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"most of the city's residents prefer to visit the library on weekends.\",\"option_b\":\"the library's weekday visits more than doubled during the three-month trial.\",\"option_c\":\"most of the people who came to the library after 8 p.m. were students.\",\"option_d\":\"keeping the library open until about 7 p.m. would attract most of the additional visitors that the later closing time brought in.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Almost all of the extra visits happened between 6 and 7 p.m., and very few came after 8 p.m. So extending hours only to around 7 p.m. would capture most of the gain, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A is unsupported because the study did not examine weekend visits. Choice B contradicts the text, which reports a rise of only 4 percent. Choice C is unsupported: the text says very few people came after 8 p.m. but says nothing about who they were.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qo‘shimcha tashriflarning deyarli hammasi soat 18:00 dan 19:00 gacha bo‘lgan, 20:00 dan keyin esa juda kam odam kelgan. Demak, ish vaqtini faqat soat 19:00 gacha uzaytirish ham o‘sishning katta qismini saqlab qoladi — D.\\n\\nA asossiz, chunki tadqiqot dam olish kunlaridagi tashriflarni o‘rganmagan. B matnga zid: matnda faqat 4 foizlik o‘sish haqida aytilgan. C asossiz: matnda soat 20:00 dan keyin juda kam odam kelgani aytiladi, lekin ular kimligi haqida hech narsa deyilmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"social-science-results\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Many lizards control their body temperature partly by changing the color of their skin. Darker skin absorbs more sunlight, and therefore more heat, than lighter skin does. Biologist Tomas Elling observed that the lizards of one desert population had dark skin in the cool early morning but became noticeably lighter by midday, when the ground was hottest. This pattern suggests that the lizards ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"are unable to control their body temperature through any behavior.\",\"option_b\":\"absorb more sunlight at midday than they do in the early morning.\",\"option_c\":\"darken when it is cool to absorb heat and lighten when it is hot to avoid overheating.\",\"option_d\":\"change color mainly to hide from predators that hunt at midday.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Dark skin absorbs more heat. The lizards are dark when it is cool and light when it is hot, which matches a strategy of gaining heat in the morning and limiting heat gain at midday, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice A contradicts the first sentence, which says lizards control their temperature partly by changing color. Choice B reverses the logic: the lizards are lighter at midday, so their skin absorbs less of the sunlight that reaches it. Choice D introduces predators, which the text never mentions.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qora teri ko‘proq issiqlik yutadi. Kaltakesaklar salqin paytda qoramtir, issiq paytda esa och rangda bo‘ladi; bu ertalab issiqlik to‘plash va tushda ortiqcha qizib ketmaslik strategiyasiga mos keladi — C.\\n\\nA birinchi gapga zid: unda kaltakesaklar tana haroratini qisman rang o‘zgartirish orqali boshqarishi aytilgan. B mantiqni teskari qiladi: tushda kaltakesaklar och rangda, demak terisi o‘ziga tushgan quyosh nurining kamroq qismini yutadi. D yirtqichlarni tilga oladi, matnda esa ular haqida gap yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science-mechanism\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"An unsigned painting of a fishing harbor has long been attributed to the landscape painter Hendrika Vos, who died in 1862. A recent chemical analysis, however, found that the green paint used for the harbor's water contains a pigment that, according to manufacturers' records, was not produced anywhere before 1871. Unless that paint was added by someone else at a later date, this finding indicates that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Vos experimented with newly invented pigments late in her career.\",\"option_b\":\"the painting was not made by Vos.\",\"option_c\":\"the harbor shown in the painting no longer exists.\",\"option_d\":\"Vos signed most of the paintings that she completed.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Vos died in 1862, but the painting contains a pigment that did not exist until 1871. If the paint was not added later by someone else, Vos could not have painted it, so choice B follows.\\n\\nChoice A is impossible on the text's facts: the pigment did not exist during Vos's lifetime. Choice C concerns the harbor itself, which the analysis tells us nothing about. Choice D is about Vos's other paintings, which the text never discusses.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Vos 1862-yilda vafot etgan, rasmda esa 1871-yilgacha mavjud bo‘lmagan pigment bor. Agar bo‘yoqni keyinroq boshqa birov qo‘shmagan bo‘lsa, rasmni Vos chizgan bo‘lishi mumkin emas — B.\\n\\nA matndagi faktlarga ko‘ra imkonsiz: bu pigment Vos hayotligida mavjud bo‘lmagan. C portning o‘zi haqida, tahlil esa bu haqda hech narsa aytmaydi. D Vosning boshqa rasmlari haqida, matnda ular muhokama qilinmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"humanities-dating\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The seeds of bloodroot, a woodland wildflower of eastern North America, carry a small fatty attachment called an elaiosome. Ants find elaiosomes attractive: they carry the seeds back to their nests, eat the elaiosomes, and then discard the seeds, which remain unharmed. Because the soil in and around ant nests is often richer in nutrients than the surrounding forest floor, researchers suggest that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"ants usually eat the entire bloodroot seed, including the part that would grow.\",\"option_b\":\"bloodroot seeds without elaiosomes are more likely to be carried away by ants.\",\"option_c\":\"ants prefer bloodroot seeds to every other kind of food in the forest.\",\"option_d\":\"bloodroot plants benefit when ants move their seeds.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Ants carry bloodroot seeds to their nests and leave the seeds unharmed in soil that is richer in nutrients than the surrounding ground. That arrangement places the seeds in a good spot to grow, so the plant benefits, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A contradicts the text: the ants eat only the elaiosome and the seeds “remain unharmed.” Choice B reverses the text's logic, since the elaiosome is what attracts the ants. Choice C goes beyond the text, which never compares bloodroot seeds with all other foods.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Chumolilar urug‘larni uyalariga olib boradi va ularni atrofdagi yerdan ozuqaga boyroq tuproqda zararsiz qoldiradi. Bu urug‘larni o‘sish uchun qulay joyga joylashtiradi, demak o‘simlik foyda ko‘radi — D.\\n\\nA matnga zid: chumolilar faqat elaiosomani yeydi, urug‘lar esa “zararsiz qoladi.” B matn mantiqini teskari qiladi, chunki chumolilarni aynan elaiosoma jalb qiladi. C matndan tashqariga chiqadi: matnda urug‘lar boshqa barcha ozuqalar bilan solishtirilmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science-mutual-benefit\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"To evaluate a new fertilizer, agronomist Dilnoza Rahimova compared tomato harvests on two farms with similar soil. Farm A used the fertilizer and harvested 30 percent more tomatoes per hectare than Farm B, which did not use it. However, Farm A also installed a drip irrigation system that season, while Farm B continued to rely on rainfall alone. Because of this difference between the farms, the comparison ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"proves that drip irrigation, not the fertilizer, caused Farm A's larger harvest.\",\"option_b\":\"cannot show how much of Farm A's larger harvest, if any, resulted from the fertilizer.\",\"option_c\":\"shows that the fertilizer is effective only when it is combined with drip irrigation.\",\"option_d\":\"suggests that Farm B received more rainfall during the season than Farm A did.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Farm A differed from Farm B in two ways: it used the fertilizer and it had drip irrigation. Either change, or both, could explain the larger harvest, so the comparison cannot separate the fertilizer's effect, as choice B says.\\n\\nChoice A overcorrects: the irrigation is a possible cause, but nothing proves that it was the only one. Choice C claims a specific interaction that was never tested, since no farm used irrigation without the fertilizer or the fertilizer without irrigation. Choice D is unsupported; the text says nothing about how much rain each farm received.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"A ferma B fermadan ikki jihatdan farq qilgan: u o‘g‘itdan foydalangan va unda tomchilatib sug‘orish tizimi bo‘lgan. Hosilning ko‘pligini ikkala o‘zgarishning istalgan biri yoki ikkalasi birga tushuntirishi mumkin, shuning uchun bu taqqoslash o‘g‘itning ta’sirini ajratib ko‘rsata olmaydi — B.\\n\\nA haddan oshadi: sug‘orish mumkin bo‘lgan sabab, ammo uning yagona sabab ekanini hech narsa isbotlamaydi. C hech qachon sinab ko‘rilmagan o‘zaro ta’sirni da’vo qiladi, chunki birorta ferma o‘g‘itsiz sug‘orish yoki sug‘orishsiz o‘g‘itni qo‘llamagan. D asossiz: matnda har bir fermaga qancha yomg‘ir yoqqani haqida hech narsa aytilmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science-confound\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"A technology company wanted to estimate how much time adults in a large country spend reading printed books. It posted a survey on its own website, and 12,000 visitors responded, reporting an average of just 9 minutes of print reading per day. The people who visit the company's website, however, tend to spend far more of their free time online than most adults in the country do. For this reason, the survey's result ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"proves that people who read printed books rarely use the internet.\",\"option_b\":\"shows that adults in the country spend more time reading online than reading printed books.\",\"option_c\":\"may not accurately reflect the reading habits of adults in the country as a whole.\",\"option_d\":\"would have been accurate if the company had collected more than 12,000 responses.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The survey reached only visitors to one website, a group whose free time differs from that of typical adults. Because the sample is not representative, its average may not describe the country's adults in general, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice B is unsupported because the survey measured print reading only, not online reading. Choice A is far too strong and is not about the survey's accuracy at all. Choice D misses the problem: collecting more responses from the same unrepresentative group would not remove the bias.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"So‘rovnoma faqat bitta veb-sayt tashrifchilarini qamrab olgan, ularning bo‘sh vaqti esa oddiy kattalarnikidan farq qiladi. Tanlanma (sample) reprezentativ bo‘lmagani uchun uning o‘rtacha ko‘rsatkichi mamlakatdagi kattalarni umuman to‘g‘ri aks ettirmasligi mumkin — C.\\n\\nB asossiz: so‘rovnoma faqat bosma kitob o‘qishni o‘lchagan, onlayn o‘qishni emas. A juda keskin va umuman so‘rovnomaning aniqligi haqida emas. D muammoni ko‘rmaydi: xuddi shu reprezentativ bo‘lmagan guruhdan ko‘proq javob yig‘ish xatolikni yo‘qotmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"social-science-sampling\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Two anonymous travel accounts describing Bukhara in the 1840s have often been attributed to a single writer, because both describe several of the same buildings in very similar words. Historian Aziza Nurmatova points out, however, that a widely read guidebook published in 1838 describes those buildings in almost identical language, and that copying passages from popular guidebooks was common among travel writers of that period. If Nurmatova is correct, the similar descriptions ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"do not by themselves establish that the two accounts share an author.\",\"option_b\":\"prove that the two accounts were written by two different authors.\",\"option_c\":\"show that both accounts were written before the guidebook appeared in 1838.\",\"option_d\":\"indicate that the author of the guidebook copied passages from the two accounts.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Nurmatova shows another way the similarity could have arisen: two different writers could each have copied the same guidebook. The similar wording therefore no longer points only to a single author, so it cannot on its own establish one, as choice A says.\\n\\nChoice B is too strong; the evidence is now inconclusive, which does not prove there were two authors. Choice C conflicts with the text, which dates the accounts to the 1840s, after the guidebook. Choice D reverses the direction of copying: a guidebook from 1838 could not have copied accounts written in the 1840s.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Nurmatova o‘xshashlik boshqa yo‘l bilan ham paydo bo‘lishi mumkinligini ko‘rsatadi: ikki xil muallif bir xil yo‘riqnomadan ko‘chirgan bo‘lishi mumkin. Demak, o‘xshash ifodalar endi faqat bitta muallifga ishora qilmaydi va o‘z-o‘zidan yagona muallifni isbotlay olmaydi — A.\\n\\nB juda keskin: dalil endi aniq emas, bu esa ikki muallif bo‘lganini isbotlamaydi. C matnga zid: matnda yozuvlar 1840-yillarga, ya’ni yo‘riqnomadan keyingi davrga tegishli. D ko‘chirish yo‘nalishini teskari qiladi: 1838-yilgi yo‘riqnoma 1840-yillarda yozilgan yozuvlardan ko‘chira olmas edi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"humanities-authorship\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Low-pitched traffic noise can drown out bird songs in cities. Ornithologist Pavel Orlov found that great tits living in city parks sing at higher pitches than great tits in nearby forests. To learn more, Orlov recorded the same city birds on a public holiday when traffic nearly stopped, and he found that on that day their songs dropped to pitches similar to those of the forest birds. This finding suggests that the city birds' higher pitch ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"is an adjustment the birds make in response to the noise around them at the time.\",\"option_b\":\"is an inherited trait passed down from parents that also lived in cities.\",\"option_c\":\"makes city great tits less attractive to potential mates than forest great tits.\",\"option_d\":\"is caused by differences in the food available in city parks and forests.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The same city birds lowered their pitch on the one day when traffic noise nearly disappeared. A trait that changes from day to day with the current noise level is an adjustment, not a fixed feature, as choice A says.\\n\\nChoice B conflicts with the finding: a fixed inherited pitch would not drop on a quiet day. Choice C introduces mate choice, which the text never discusses. Choice D cannot explain the holiday result, because the birds' food did not change in one day while their pitch did.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Aynan o‘sha shahar qushlari transport shovqini deyarli yo‘qolgan kuni ovoz balandligini pasaytirgan. Kundan kunga joriy shovqin darajasiga qarab o‘zgaradigan xususiyat — moslashuv, o‘zgarmas belgi emas — A.\\n\\nB topilmaga zid: irsiy, o‘zgarmas ovoz balandligi tinch kunda pasaymas edi. C juft tanlash masalasini kiritadi, matnda esa bu haqda gap yo‘q. D bayram kunidagi natijani tushuntira olmaydi: bir kun ichida qushlarning ozuqasi o‘zgarmagan, ovoz balandligi esa o‘zgargan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science-alternative-explanation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"At a tenth-century trading settlement on the Baltic coast, archaeologists found hundreds of silver coins minted in cities of the Abbasid Caliphate, thousands of kilometers to the southeast. Some researchers have treated the find as evidence that merchants from the settlement traveled to those distant cities. But coins often changed hands many times, and written sources describe traders along the Volga River regularly exchanging such coins for furs from the north. The find is therefore ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"proof that no one from the settlement ever traveled to the Abbasid Caliphate.\",\"option_b\":\"evidence that the settlement's residents valued furs more highly than silver.\",\"option_c\":\"consistent with the coins having reached the settlement through intermediaries rather than through direct travel.\",\"option_d\":\"a sign that the coins were made in the settlement and stamped to look foreign.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text offers a second way the coins could have arrived: through a chain of trades, such as the Volga exchanges of coins for furs. So the find fits that explanation as well as the direct-travel one, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice A is too strong; showing another possible route does not prove that no one ever made the journey. Choice B is unsupported, since nothing compares how the settlement's residents valued furs and silver. Choice D contradicts the text, which says the coins were minted in Abbasid cities.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn tangalar yetib kelishining ikkinchi yo‘lini ko‘rsatadi: Volga bo‘yida tangalarni mo‘ynaga almashtirish kabi bir qator savdo bitimlari orqali. Demak, topilma to‘g‘ridan-to‘g‘ri safar haqidagi izohga ham, bu izohga ham mos keladi — C.\\n\\nA juda keskin: boshqa mumkin bo‘lgan yo‘lni ko‘rsatish hech kim u yerga bormaganini isbotlamaydi. B asossiz, chunki aholining mo‘yna va kumushni qanday qadrlagani solishtirilmaydi. D matnga zid: unda tangalar Abbosiylar shaharlarida zarb qilingani aytiladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"history-intermediaries\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Literary scholars have generally assumed that the novelist Ramón Iturbe wrote the final chapter of *The Glass Orchard* last, because it neatly resolves plot threads introduced throughout the book. However, Iturbe's notebooks show that he drafted that chapter in 1931, two years before he began writing the novel's earlier chapters, and that he changed it very little before the book was published. This evidence suggests that Iturbe ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"decided how the novel would end before writing most of the material that leads up to the ending.\",\"option_b\":\"added the final chapter only after his publisher asked for a clearer ending.\",\"option_c\":\"did not intend the final chapter to resolve the novel's plot threads.\",\"option_d\":\"wrote the chapters of the novel in the same order in which they appear in the book.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The notebooks show that the final chapter was drafted first and barely revised, yet it resolves threads introduced earlier in the book. Iturbe must therefore have settled on the ending before writing most of the chapters that lead to it, as choice A says.\\n\\nChoice B conflicts with the evidence that the chapter existed two years before the rest and was changed very little. Choice C ignores the text's statement that the chapter neatly resolves the plot threads. Choice D is exactly the assumption the notebooks disprove.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Daftarlar so‘nggi bob birinchi bo‘lib yozilgani va deyarli o‘zgartirilmaganini ko‘rsatadi, shunga qaramay u kitobda avvalroq boshlangan syujet chiziqlarini yakunlaydi. Demak, Iturbe yakunga olib boradigan boblarning ko‘pini yozishdan oldin romanning oxirini belgilab olgan — A.\\n\\nB dalilga zid: bob qolgan qismdan ikki yil oldin mavjud bo‘lgan va juda kam o‘zgartirilgan. C matndagi bob syujet chiziqlarini puxta yakunlaydi degan fikrni e’tiborsiz qoldiradi. D aynan daftarlar rad etgan taxminning o‘zi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"literature-composition\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Some marine bacteria in the genus *Vibrio* glow only when their population is dense, a behavior controlled by a signaling molecule that each cell both releases and detects. Microbiologist Inês Carvalho found that a normal strain grown at low density did not glow, but it began glowing within an hour after she added signaling molecules collected from a dense culture. A mutant strain, by contrast, failed to glow at low density, at high density, or after the added molecules—even though chemical tests confirmed that its cells released the signaling molecule in normal amounts. These results suggest that the mutant strain's failure to glow most likely arises from ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"the low population density at which the mutant cells were grown.\",\"option_b\":\"a defect in some step that follows the release of the signaling molecule, not in its release.\",\"option_c\":\"the mutant cells' inability to release the signaling molecule.\",\"option_d\":\"the added signaling molecules being too weak to affect bacterial cells.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The tests rule out the obvious explanations. The mutant released the molecule normally, so release is not the problem; it stayed dark even at high density and with molecules added, so low density or too little signal is not the problem either. What remains is something after release, such as detecting the molecule or producing light in response, as choice B says.\\n\\nChoice A is ruled out because the mutant also failed at high density and with added molecules. Choice C directly contradicts the chemical tests. Choice D is ruled out because the same added molecules made the normal strain glow.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Sinovlar aniq ko‘rinadigan izohlarni istisno qiladi. Mutant molekulani normal ajratgan, demak muammo ajratishda emas; u yuqori zichlikda ham, molekula qo‘shilgandan keyin ham yorug‘lik chiqarmagan, demak muammo past zichlik yoki signal yetishmasligida ham emas. Faqat ajratishdan keyingi bosqich qoladi — masalan, molekulani sezish yoki unga javoban yorug‘lik hosil qilish — B.\\n\\nA istisno qilinadi: mutant yuqori zichlikda va molekula qo‘shilganda ham yorug‘lik chiqarmagan. C kimyoviy sinovlar natijasiga bevosita zid. D ham istisno qilinadi, chunki aynan shu qo‘shilgan molekulalar normal shtammni yorug‘lik chiqarishga majbur qilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science-elimination\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"A widely used ecological model holds that, all else being equal, larger islands support more species than smaller ones do. Ecologist Farrukh Toshev surveyed beetle species on fourteen islands in a large lake and found that the model's predictions held for thirteen of them. The exception, Kemer Island, is mid-sized but has fewer beetle species than several much smaller islands nearby. Kemer is also the only island in the lake whose forests were cleared for grazing during the twentieth century; trees have regrown there only over the past thirty years. Unless beetles colonize islands much faster than Toshev's other data suggest, this history implies that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"the model should be revised to state that mid-sized islands support fewer species than small islands do.\",\"option_b\":\"the smaller islands near Kemer were also cleared for grazing at some point in the twentieth century.\",\"option_c\":\"beetles are unable to survive on any island that has ever been used for grazing.\",\"option_d\":\"Kemer's low beetle count may reflect habitat loss from which its beetle populations have not yet recovered, rather than a failure of the model.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The model compares islands when “all else” is equal, but Kemer differs in an important way: its forests were removed and have regrown only recently. If beetles recolonize slowly, Kemer may simply not have regained its species yet. That explains the exception without rejecting the model, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A overreacts to one exception that has a likely explanation; the model held for the other thirteen islands. Choice B contradicts the text, which says Kemer is the only island that was cleared. Choice C is too extreme: Kemer still has some beetles, and the text implies only slow recovery, not complete impossibility.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Model orollarni “boshqa sharoitlar teng bo‘lganda” solishtiradi, Kemer esa muhim jihatdan farq qiladi: uning o‘rmonlari kesilgan va yaqindagina qayta o‘sgan. Agar qo‘ng‘izlar orollarni sekin qayta egallasa, Kemer o‘z turlarini hali tiklab ulgurmagan bo‘lishi mumkin. Bu istisnoni modelni rad etmasdan tushuntiradi — D.\\n\\nA ehtimoliy izohi bor bitta istisnoga haddan ortiq javob beradi; model qolgan o‘n uchta orol uchun to‘g‘ri chiqqan. B matnga zid: unda faqat Kemer o‘rmonlari kesilgani aytiladi. C juda keskin: Kemerda baribir ba’zi qo‘ng‘izlar bor, matn esa faqat sekin tiklanishni nazarda tutadi, mutlaq imkonsizlikni emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science-model-exception\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Dendrochronologists can determine the year in which a tree was cut down by matching its growth rings to a regional record. Samples from six roof beams in the old meeting hall of the village of Dornick show that the trees were felled between 1508 and 1512, and the hall is therefore often dated to around 1512. A village record from 1630, however, states that the meeting hall was built in that year “with the good timber of the old church,” which had been taken down after a fire. If the record is accurate and refers to this hall, then ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"the tree-ring method must give inaccurate felling dates for timber cut before 1600.\",\"option_b\":\"the old church was most likely built in 1630, at the same time as the hall.\",\"option_c\":\"the hall was built around 1512 and later repaired with timber from the old church in 1630.\",\"option_d\":\"the hall is likely more than a century younger than the trees that supplied its roof beams.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The tree-ring dates tell when the trees were cut (1508–1512), not when the timber was placed in this building. If the hall was built in 1630 from timber reused from the old church, the beams are older than the hall by more than a century, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the record does not challenge the felling dates themselves; it explains why they differ from the building date. Choice B misreads the record: in 1630 the old church was already gone and its timber was reused. Choice C contradicts the record, which says the hall was built, not repaired, in 1630.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Daraxt halqalari bo‘yicha sanalar daraxtlar qachon kesilganini (1508–1512) ko‘rsatadi, yog‘och shu binoga qachon qo‘yilganini emas. Agar zal 1630-yilda eski cherkovdan qolgan yog‘ochdan qurilgan bo‘lsa, to‘sinlar zaldan yuz yildan ham ko‘proq qadimiyroq — D.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: yozuv kesilish sanalarining o‘ziga shubha uyg‘otmaydi, faqat ular nega qurilish sanasidan farq qilishini tushuntiradi. B yozuvni noto‘g‘ri tushunadi: 1630-yilda eski cherkov allaqachon buzilgan va uning yog‘ochi qayta ishlatilgan. C yozuvga zid: unda zal 1630-yilda ta’mirlangani emas, qurilgani aytiladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"history-reused-material\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Economists Nodira Yusupova and Lars Eklund studied a mid-sized city that eliminated fares on all of its buses in 2019. Over the following year, bus ridership rose by 35 percent, yet car traffic in the city center fell by less than 2 percent. Surveys showed that most of the new bus riders had previously walked or cycled to their destinations rather than driven. Yusupova and Eklund argue that a fare-free policy can substantially reduce traffic congestion only if a large share of its new riders would otherwise have traveled by car. Their findings thus suggest that, in this city, ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"eliminating fares did little to reduce congestion, largely because it attracted people who had not been driving.\",\"option_b\":\"eliminating fares caused more people to drive into the city center than before.\",\"option_c\":\"bus ridership would have risen even more if fares had been lowered rather than eliminated.\",\"option_d\":\"people who walk or cycle are more likely than drivers to support fare-free buses.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The economists' condition for a large reduction in congestion is that many new riders would otherwise have driven. In this city, most new riders had walked or cycled, and car traffic fell by less than 2 percent. The condition was not met, so the policy did little for congestion, as choice A says.\\n\\nChoice B contradicts the text, since traffic fell slightly rather than rose. Choice C makes a claim about a policy the study never examined. Choice D concerns political support, which the text does not discuss.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Iqtisodchilarning fikricha, tirbandlik sezilarli kamayishi uchun yangi yo‘lovchilarning ko‘pchiligi aks holda mashinada yurgan bo‘lishi kerak. Bu shaharda esa yangi yo‘lovchilarning aksariyati avval piyoda yoki velosipedda yurgan va avtomobil harakati 2 foizdan kam qisqargan. Shart bajarilmagan, demak siyosat tirbandlikni kamaytirishga deyarli yordam bermagan — A.\\n\\nB matnga zid: harakat oshmagan, aksincha biroz kamaygan. C tadqiqotda umuman o‘rganilmagan siyosat haqida da’vo qiladi. D siyosiy qo‘llab-quvvatlash haqida, matnda esa bu muhokama qilinmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"social-science-conditional\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_one_variable\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"What value of $x$ is the solution to the equation $6(x + 2) - 4 = 2x + 24$?\",\"option_a\":\"$2$\",\"option_b\":\"$4$\",\"option_c\":\"$6.5$\",\"option_d\":\"$8$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Distribute the $6$: $6x + 12 - 4 = 2x + 24$, so $6x + 8 = 2x + 24$. Subtract $2x$ and $8$ from both sides: $4x = 16$, so $x = 4$. Check: $6(6) - 4 = 32$ and $2(4) + 24 = 32$.\\n\\nChoice A comes from adding $2x$ to the left side instead of subtracting it ($8x + 8 = 24$). Choice C comes from multiplying only $x$ by $6$ ($6x + 2 - 4 = 2x + 24$). Choice D comes from adding $8$ to the right side instead of subtracting it ($4x = 32$).\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$6$ ni qavsga ko‘paytiramiz: $6x + 12 - 4 = 2x + 24$, ya’ni $6x + 8 = 2x + 24$. Ikkala tomondan $2x$ va $8$ ni ayiramiz: $4x = 16$, demak $x = 4$. Tekshiruv: $6(6) - 4 = 32$ va $2(4) + 24 = 32$.\\n\\nA variant $2x$ ni ayirish o‘rniga chap tomonga qo‘shishdan chiqadi ($8x + 8 = 24$). C variant faqat $x$ ni $6$ ga ko‘paytirishdan kelib chiqadi ($6x + 2 - 4 = 2x + 24$). D variant $8$ ni o‘ng tomondan ayirish o‘rniga qo‘shishdan chiqadi ($4x = 32$).\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"solve-distribute\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_one_variable\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"If $4x + 6 = 30$, what is the value of $2x + 3$?\",\"option_a\":\"$6$\",\"option_b\":\"$12$\",\"option_c\":\"$15$\",\"option_d\":\"$60$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The expression $2x + 3$ is half of $4x + 6$. So $2x + 3 = \\\\frac{30}{2} = 15$. (Solving also works: $x = 6$ and $2(6) + 3 = 15$.)\\n\\nChoice A is the value of $x$, not of $2x + 3$. Choice B is the value of $2x$. Choice D doubles $30$ instead of halving it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$2x + 3$ ifodasi $4x + 6$ ning yarmiga teng. Demak, $2x + 3 = \\\\frac{30}{2} = 15$. (Tenglamani yechsak ham bo‘ladi: $x = 6$ va $2(6) + 3 = 15$.)\\n\\nA variant — $x$ ning qiymati, $2x + 3$ niki emas. B variant — $2x$ ning qiymati. D variant $30$ ni ikkiga bo‘lish o‘rniga ikkiga ko‘paytirgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"expression-without-solving\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_one_variable\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A fitness club charges a one-time registration fee of $50{,}000$ soums plus $120{,}000$ soums for each month of membership. Kamola paid a total of $770{,}000$ soums for registration and $m$ months of membership. If $50{,}000 + 120{,}000m = 770{,}000$, what is the value of $m$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"6\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Subtract the fee: $120{,}000m = 770{,}000 - 50{,}000 = 720{,}000$. Divide by $120{,}000$: $m = 6$. Dividing $770{,}000$ by $120{,}000$ without removing the fee first gives about $6.4$, which is wrong.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Avval ro‘yxatdan o‘tish to‘lovini ayiramiz: $120{,}000m = 770{,}000 - 50{,}000 = 720{,}000$. $120{,}000$ ga bo‘lamiz: $m = 6$. To‘lovni ayirmasdan $770{,}000$ ni $120{,}000$ ga bo‘lsak, taxminan $6.4$ chiqadi — bu xato.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"word-problem\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_one_variable\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Botir is reading a book that has $420$ pages. He has already read $70$ pages, and he plans to read $35$ pages each day until he finishes. The equation $70 + 35d = 420$ represents this situation. What is the value of $d$?\",\"option_a\":\"$2$\",\"option_b\":\"$10$\",\"option_c\":\"$12$\",\"option_d\":\"$14$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Subtract $70$ from both sides: $35d = 350$. Divide by $35$: $d = 10$. So Botir needs $10$ more days.\\n\\nChoice A is $70 \\\\div 35$, the number of days for the pages already read. Choice C is $420 \\\\div 35$, which ignores the $70$ pages already read. Choice D comes from adding $70$ instead of subtracting it: $490 \\\\div 35 = 14$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkala tomondan $70$ ni ayiramiz: $35d = 350$. $35$ ga bo‘lamiz: $d = 10$. Demak, Botirga yana $10$ kun kerak.\\n\\nA variant — $70 \\\\div 35$, ya’ni allaqachon o‘qilgan sahifalar uchun kunlar soni. C variant — $420 \\\\div 35$, bunda o‘qilgan $70$ sahifa hisobga olinmagan. D variant $70$ ni ayirish o‘rniga qo‘shishdan chiqadi: $490 \\\\div 35 = 14$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"word-problem\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_one_variable\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"What value of $x$ is the solution to the equation $\\\\dfrac{x - 3}{5} = 4$?\",\"option_a\":\"$3.8$\",\"option_b\":\"$7$\",\"option_c\":\"$17$\",\"option_d\":\"$23$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Multiply both sides by $5$: $x - 3 = 20$. Add $3$: $x = 23$. Check: $\\\\frac{23 - 3}{5} = \\\\frac{20}{5} = 4$.\\n\\nChoice A divides $4$ by $5$ instead of multiplying ($\\\\frac{4}{5} + 3$). Choice B ignores the denominator ($x - 3 = 4$). Choice C subtracts $3$ instead of adding it ($20 - 3$).\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkala tomonni $5$ ga ko‘paytiramiz: $x - 3 = 20$. $3$ ni qo‘shamiz: $x = 23$. Tekshiruv: $\\\\frac{23 - 3}{5} = \\\\frac{20}{5} = 4$.\\n\\nA variant $4$ ni $5$ ga ko‘paytirish o‘rniga bo‘lgan ($\\\\frac{4}{5} + 3$). B variant maxrajni e’tiborsiz qoldirgan ($x - 3 = 4$). C variant $3$ ni qo‘shish o‘rniga ayirgan ($20 - 3$).\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"solve-fraction\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_one_variable\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"How many solutions does the equation $2(x + 3) = 2x + 5$ have?\",\"option_a\":\"Zero\",\"option_b\":\"Exactly one\",\"option_c\":\"Exactly two\",\"option_d\":\"Infinitely many\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Distribute: $2x + 6 = 2x + 5$. Subtracting $2x$ from both sides gives $6 = 5$, which is never true. So no value of $x$ works, and the equation has zero solutions.\\n\\nExactly one solution would need different coefficients of $x$ on the two sides, but both sides have $2x$. A linear equation can never have exactly two solutions. Infinitely many solutions would need the constants to match too ($6 = 6$), but here they are $6$ and $5$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qavsni ochamiz: $2x + 6 = 2x + 5$. Ikkala tomondan $2x$ ni ayirsak, $6 = 5$ hosil bo‘ladi — bu hech qachon to‘g‘ri emas. Demak, hech bir $x$ mos kelmaydi va tenglamaning yechimi yo‘q.\\n\\nBitta yechim bo‘lishi uchun ikki tomondagi $x$ koeffitsiyentlari har xil bo‘lishi kerak, lekin ikkalasida ham $2x$. Chiziqli tenglama hech qachon aynan ikkita yechimga ega bo‘lmaydi. Cheksiz ko‘p yechim uchun ozod hadlar ham teng bo‘lishi kerak edi ($6 = 6$), bu yerda esa ular $6$ va $5$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"number-of-solutions\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_one_variable\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\frac{3x}{4} - 2 = \\\\frac{x}{6} + 5$$\\n\\nWhat is the solution to the given equation?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"12\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Multiply every term by $12$, the least common denominator: $9x - 24 = 2x + 60$. Subtract $2x$ and add $24$: $7x = 84$, so $x = 12$. Check: $\\\\frac{3(12)}{4} - 2 = 7$ and $\\\\frac{12}{6} + 5 = 7$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Har bir hadni eng kichik umumiy maxraj $12$ ga ko‘paytiramiz: $9x - 24 = 2x + 60$. $2x$ ni ayirib, $24$ ni qo‘shamiz: $7x = 84$, demak $x = 12$. Tekshiruv: $\\\\frac{3(12)}{4} - 2 = 7$ va $\\\\frac{12}{6} + 5 = 7$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"solve-fraction\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_one_variable\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"If $5(2x + 1) - 3(2x + 1) = 18$, what is the value of $2x + 1$?\",\"option_a\":\"$4$\",\"option_b\":\"$6$\",\"option_c\":\"$9$\",\"option_d\":\"$18$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Treat $2x + 1$ as one quantity. Five of it minus three of it is two of it: $2(2x + 1) = 18$, so $2x + 1 = 9$. (Then $x = 4$, but the question asks for $2x + 1$.)\\n\\nChoice A is the value of $x$, not of $2x + 1$. Choice B divides $18$ by $3$ instead of by $5 - 3 = 2$. Choice D forgets to divide by $2$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$2x + 1$ ni bitta miqdor deb qaraymiz. Uning beshtasidan uchtasini ayirsak, ikkitasi qoladi: $2(2x + 1) = 18$, demak $2x + 1 = 9$. ($x = 4$ bo‘ladi, lekin savol $2x + 1$ haqida.)\\n\\nA variant — $x$ ning qiymati, $2x + 1$ niki emas. B variant $18$ ni $5 - 3 = 2$ ga emas, $3$ ga bo‘lgan. D variantda $2$ ga bo‘lish unutilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"expression-without-solving\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_one_variable\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Two candles are lit at the same time. Candle A is $30$ centimeters tall and burns down at a constant rate of $1.5$ centimeters per hour. Candle B is $22$ centimeters tall and burns down at a constant rate of $0.7$ centimeter per hour. After how many hours will the two candles have the same height?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{40}{11}$\",\"option_b\":\"$8$\",\"option_c\":\"$10$\",\"option_d\":\"$20$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"After $t$ hours the heights are $30 - 1.5t$ and $22 - 0.7t$. Set them equal: $30 - 1.5t = 22 - 0.7t$, so $8 = 0.8t$ and $t = 10$. Both candles are then $15$ cm tall, and candle A has not burned out yet.\\n\\nChoice A adds the rates ($8 \\\\div 2.2$); the gap closes at the difference of the rates, $0.8$ cm per hour. Choice B is the starting difference in height, in centimeters. Choice D is the time for candle A to burn out ($30 \\\\div 1.5$).\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$t$ soatdan keyin balandliklar $30 - 1.5t$ va $22 - 0.7t$ bo‘ladi. Ularni tenglaymiz: $30 - 1.5t = 22 - 0.7t$, demak $8 = 0.8t$ va $t = 10$. Shunda ikkala sham ham $15$ sm bo‘ladi va A sham hali yonib tugamagan.\\n\\nA variant tezliklarni qo‘shgan ($8 \\\\div 2.2$); farq esa tezliklar ayirmasi, ya’ni soatiga $0.8$ sm dan qisqaradi. B variant — boshlang‘ich balandliklar farqi (santimetrda). D variant — A shamning to‘liq yonib bo‘lish vaqti ($30 \\\\div 1.5$).\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"word-problem\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_one_variable\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The sum of three consecutive odd integers is $135$. What is the greatest of the three integers?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"47\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Let the integers be $n$, $n + 2$, and $n + 4$. Then $3n + 6 = 135$, so $3n = 129$ and $n = 43$. The integers are $43$, $45$, and $47$, so the greatest is $47$. Answering $45$ (the middle one, $135 \\\\div 3$) or $43$ (the smallest) is a common slip.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Sonlar $n$, $n + 2$ va $n + 4$ bo‘lsin. U holda $3n + 6 = 135$, demak $3n = 129$ va $n = 43$. Sonlar $43$, $45$ va $47$, eng kattasi $47$. O‘rtadagi son $45$ ni ($135 \\\\div 3$) yoki eng kichigi $43$ ni yozib qo‘yish ko‘p uchraydigan xato.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"consecutive-integers\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_one_variable\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"$$4(3x - k) = 12x - 20$$\\n\\nIn the given equation, $k$ is a constant. For what value of $k$ does the equation have infinitely many solutions?\",\"option_a\":\"$-5$\",\"option_b\":\"$5$\",\"option_c\":\"$16$\",\"option_d\":\"$20$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Distribute: $12x - 4k = 12x - 20$. The $x$-terms already match, so the equation is true for every $x$ exactly when the constants match: $-4k = -20$, so $k = 5$.\\n\\nChoice A has the wrong sign: with $k = -5$ the left side is $12x + 20$. Choice C comes from subtracting $4$ instead of dividing by $4$. Choice D forgets to distribute the $4$ to $k$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qavsni ochamiz: $12x - 4k = 12x - 20$. $x$ li hadlar allaqachon teng, shuning uchun tenglama har qanday $x$ uchun to‘g‘ri bo‘lishi uchun ozod hadlar teng bo‘lishi kerak: $-4k = -20$, demak $k = 5$.\\n\\nA variantda ishora xato: $k = -5$ bo‘lsa, chap tomon $12x + 20$ bo‘ladi. C variant $4$ ga bo‘lish o‘rniga $4$ ni ayirishdan chiqadi. D variantda $4$ ni $k$ ga ko‘paytirish unutilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"infinitely-many-constant\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_one_variable\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"What is the solution to the equation $0.15x + 0.6 = 0.05(x + 40)$?\",\"option_a\":\"$1.4$\",\"option_b\":\"$14$\",\"option_c\":\"$26$\",\"option_d\":\"$140$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Distribute: $0.15x + 0.6 = 0.05x + 2$. Subtract $0.05x$ and $0.6$: $0.1x = 1.4$, so $x = 14$. Check: $0.15(14) + 0.6 = 2.7$ and $0.05(54) = 2.7$.\\n\\nChoice A stops at $0.1x = 1.4$ without dividing by $0.1$. Choice C adds $0.6$ instead of subtracting it ($0.1x = 2.6$). Choice D misplaces the decimal point when dividing by $0.1$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qavsni ochamiz: $0.15x + 0.6 = 0.05x + 2$. $0.05x$ va $0.6$ ni ayiramiz: $0.1x = 1.4$, demak $x = 14$. Tekshiruv: $0.15(14) + 0.6 = 2.7$ va $0.05(54) = 2.7$.\\n\\nA variant $0.1x = 1.4$ da to‘xtab, $0.1$ ga bo‘lmagan. C variant $0.6$ ni ayirish o‘rniga qo‘shgan ($0.1x = 2.6$). D variantda $0.1$ ga bo‘lishda vergul (o‘nli kasr nuqtasi) noto‘g‘ri qo‘yilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"solve-decimal\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_one_variable\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\frac{2}{5}(15x - a) + 3 = bx - 7$$\\n\\nIn the given equation, $a$ and $b$ are constants. If the equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $a + b$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"31\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Distribute: $6x - \\\\frac{2a}{5} + 3 = bx - 7$. For infinitely many solutions, both sides must be the same expression. Matching the $x$-coefficients gives $b = 6$. Matching the constants gives $-\\\\frac{2a}{5} + 3 = -7$, so $-\\\\frac{2a}{5} = -10$ and $a = 25$. Therefore $a + b = 25 + 6 = 31$. A common error is to multiply only $15x$ by $\\\\frac{2}{5}$ and forget $a$, which gives $a = 10$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qavsni ochamiz: $6x - \\\\frac{2a}{5} + 3 = bx - 7$. Cheksiz ko‘p yechim bo‘lishi uchun ikkala tomon bir xil ifoda bo‘lishi kerak. $x$ koeffitsiyentlarini tenglasak, $b = 6$. Ozod hadlarni tenglasak, $-\\\\frac{2a}{5} + 3 = -7$, ya’ni $-\\\\frac{2a}{5} = -10$ va $a = 25$. Demak, $a + b = 25 + 6 = 31$. Ko‘p uchraydigan xato — $\\\\frac{2}{5}$ ni faqat $15x$ ga ko‘paytirib, $a$ ni unutish; bu $a = 10$ ni beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"infinitely-many-constant\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_one_variable\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\frac{cx + 4}{3} = \\\\frac{x - c}{2}$$\\n\\nIn the given equation, $c$ is a constant. If $x = -2$ is the solution to the equation, what is the value of $c$?\",\"option_a\":\"$-14$\",\"option_b\":\"$2$\",\"option_c\":\"$4$\",\"option_d\":\"$14$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Substitute $x = -2$: $\\\\frac{-2c + 4}{3} = \\\\frac{-2 - c}{2}$. Cross-multiply: $2(-2c + 4) = 3(-2 - c)$, so $-4c + 8 = -6 - 3c$. Then $14 = c$. Check: $\\\\frac{-28 + 4}{3} = -8$ and $\\\\frac{-2 - 14}{2} = -8$.\\n\\nChoice A has the sign wrong at the last step. Choice B comes from distributing $3(-2 - c)$ as $-6 + 3c$. Choice C comes from cross-multiplying the wrong way, $3(-2c + 4) = 2(-2 - c)$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$x = -2$ ni qo‘yamiz: $\\\\frac{-2c + 4}{3} = \\\\frac{-2 - c}{2}$. Proporsiya qoidasi bo‘yicha ko‘paytiramiz: $2(-2c + 4) = 3(-2 - c)$, ya’ni $-4c + 8 = -6 - 3c$. Bundan $14 = c$. Tekshiruv: $\\\\frac{-28 + 4}{3} = -8$ va $\\\\frac{-2 - 14}{2} = -8$.\\n\\nA variantda oxirgi qadamda ishora xato. B variant $3(-2 - c)$ ni $-6 + 3c$ deb ochishdan chiqadi. C variant noto‘g‘ri tomonlarni ko‘paytirishdan chiqadi: $3(-2c + 4) = 2(-2 - c)$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"parameter-from-solution\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_one_variable\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"Tank A contains $1{,}200$ liters of water and is being drained at a constant rate of $45$ liters per minute. At the same moment, tank B contains $300$ liters of water and is being filled at a constant rate of $30$ liters per minute. After how many minutes will tank A contain exactly twice as much water as tank B?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"40/7\",\"5.714\"],\"explanation_en\":\"After $t$ minutes, tank A has $1{,}200 - 45t$ liters and tank B has $300 + 30t$ liters. Set A equal to twice B: $1{,}200 - 45t = 2(300 + 30t) = 600 + 60t$. Then $600 = 105t$, so $t = \\\\frac{600}{105} = \\\\frac{40}{7} \\\\approx 5.714$. Doubling only the $300$ (and not the $30t$) gives $600 = 75t$, or $t = 8$, which is wrong.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$t$ daqiqadan keyin A bakda $1{,}200 - 45t$ litr, B bakda $300 + 30t$ litr suv bo‘ladi. A ni B ning ikki baravariga tenglaymiz: $1{,}200 - 45t = 2(300 + 30t) = 600 + 60t$. Bundan $600 = 105t$, ya’ni $t = \\\\frac{600}{105} = \\\\frac{40}{7} \\\\approx 5.714$. Faqat $300$ ni ikkilantirib ($30t$ ni emas), $600 = 75t$, ya’ni $t = 8$ deb topish xato.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"word-problem\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_one_variable\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"$$ax + 12 = 4(x + 3) + bx$$\\n\\nIn the given equation, $a$ and $b$ are constants. If the equation has infinitely many solutions, which of the following must be true?\",\"option_a\":\"$a - b = 4$\",\"option_b\":\"$a + b = 4$\",\"option_c\":\"$a = 4$ and $b = 0$\",\"option_d\":\"$a - b = 12$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Distribute: $ax + 12 = 4x + 12 + bx = (4 + b)x + 12$. The constants already match ($12 = 12$), so the equation holds for every $x$ exactly when $a = 4 + b$, that is, $a - b = 4$.\\n\\nChoice B has the wrong sign: $b$ is on the same side as $4$. Choice C is one pair that works, but it is not required; for example, $a = 7$ and $b = 3$ also work. Choice D compares a coefficient with the constant $12$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qavsni ochamiz: $ax + 12 = 4x + 12 + bx = (4 + b)x + 12$. Ozod hadlar allaqachon teng ($12 = 12$), shuning uchun tenglama har qanday $x$ uchun aynan $a = 4 + b$, ya’ni $a - b = 4$ bo‘lganda bajariladi.\\n\\nB variantda ishora xato: $b$ va $4$ bir tomonda turibdi. C variant mos keladigan juftliklardan biri, lekin majburiy emas; masalan, $a = 7$ va $b = 3$ ham mos keladi. D variant koeffitsiyentni ozod had $12$ bilan solishtirgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"infinitely-many-constant\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_two_variables\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A courier service charges $15{,}000$ soums to deliver a small parcel and $40{,}000$ soums to deliver a large parcel. On Tuesday, the service earned $1{,}100{,}000$ soums by delivering $s$ small parcels and $L$ large parcels, so $15{,}000s + 40{,}000L = 1{,}100{,}000$. What does $40{,}000$ represent in this equation?\",\"option_a\":\"The number of large parcels delivered on Tuesday\",\"option_b\":\"The total amount, in soums, earned from large parcels on Tuesday\",\"option_c\":\"The amount, in soums, charged to deliver one small parcel\",\"option_d\":\"The amount, in soums, charged to deliver one large parcel\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The term $40{,}000L$ is the money earned from large parcels: the number of large parcels, $L$, times the charge for each one. So $40{,}000$ is the charge, in soums, for one large parcel.\\n\\nChoice A describes $L$, not $40{,}000$. Choice B describes the whole term $40{,}000L$. Choice C describes $15{,}000$, the coefficient of $s$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$40{,}000L$ hadi katta posilkalardan tushgan pul: katta posilkalar soni $L$ ni har biri uchun to‘lovga ko‘paytirilgan. Demak, $40{,}000$ — bitta katta posilkani yetkazish narxi (so‘mda).\\n\\nA variant $40{,}000$ ni emas, $L$ ni tasvirlaydi. B variant butun $40{,}000L$ hadini tasvirlaydi. C variant $s$ ning koeffitsiyenti $15{,}000$ ni tasvirlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"interpret-coefficient\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_two_variables\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In the equation $4x - 3y = 30$, what is the value of $y$ when $x = 3$?\",\"option_a\":\"$-14$\",\"option_b\":\"$-6$\",\"option_c\":\"$6$\",\"option_d\":\"$14$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Substitute $x = 3$: $12 - 3y = 30$. Subtract $12$: $-3y = 18$. Divide by $-3$: $y = -6$. Check: $4(3) - 3(-6) = 12 + 18 = 30$.\\n\\nChoice C drops the negative sign when dividing by $-3$. Choices A and D come from adding $12$ to $30$ instead of subtracting it ($\\\\pm 42 \\\\div 3$).\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$x = 3$ ni qo‘yamiz: $12 - 3y = 30$. $12$ ni ayiramiz: $-3y = 18$. $-3$ ga bo‘lamiz: $y = -6$. Tekshiruv: $4(3) - 3(-6) = 12 + 18 = 30$.\\n\\nC variant $-3$ ga bo‘lishda manfiy ishorani yo‘qotgan. A va D variantlar $12$ ni $30$ dan ayirish o‘rniga qo‘shishdan chiqadi ($\\\\pm 42 \\\\div 3$).\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"find-other-variable\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_two_variables\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"At a school bookstore, each notebook costs $3{,}000$ soums and each pen costs $2{,}000$ soums. Shahzoda spent exactly $32{,}000$ soums on $n$ notebooks and $p$ pens, so $3{,}000n + 2{,}000p = 32{,}000$. If she bought $6$ notebooks, how many pens did she buy?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"7\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Substitute $n = 6$: $18{,}000 + 2{,}000p = 32{,}000$. Then $2{,}000p = 14{,}000$, so $p = 7$. Substituting $6$ for $p$ instead of $n$ swaps the items and gives a non-whole number of notebooks.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$n = 6$ ni qo‘yamiz: $18{,}000 + 2{,}000p = 32{,}000$. Bundan $2{,}000p = 14{,}000$, demak $p = 7$. Agar $6$ ni $n$ o‘rniga emas, $p$ o‘rniga qo‘ysak, buyumlar almashib ketadi va daftarlar soni butun son chiqmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"find-other-variable\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_two_variables\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"At what point does the graph of $6x + 5y = 30$ cross the $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane?\",\"option_a\":\"$(0, 6)$\",\"option_b\":\"$(5, 0)$\",\"option_c\":\"$(0, 5)$\",\"option_d\":\"$(6, 0)$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"On the $x$-axis, $y = 0$. Then $6x = 30$, so $x = 5$, and the point is $(5, 0)$.\\n\\nChoice C is where the graph crosses the $y$-axis ($x = 0$ gives $5y = 30$). Choices A and D use the coefficient $6$ as a coordinate instead of solving.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$x$ o‘qida $y = 0$ bo‘ladi. U holda $6x = 30$, demak $x = 5$ va nuqta $(5, 0)$.\\n\\nC variant — grafikning $y$ o‘qini kesib o‘tish nuqtasi ($x = 0$ bo‘lsa, $5y = 30$). A va D variantlar tenglamani yechish o‘rniga $6$ koeffitsiyentini koordinata sifatida olgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"intercepts\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_two_variables\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A honey producer packs $180$ jars of honey into boxes. Each small box holds $6$ jars and each large box holds $10$ jars. If all $180$ jars are packed into $s$ small boxes and $b$ large boxes and every box is full, which equation represents this situation?\",\"option_a\":\"$6s + 10b = 180$\",\"option_b\":\"$10s + 6b = 180$\",\"option_c\":\"$s + b = 180$\",\"option_d\":\"$16(s + b) = 180$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The $s$ small boxes hold $6s$ jars and the $b$ large boxes hold $10b$ jars. Together they hold all $180$ jars: $6s + 10b = 180$.\\n\\nChoice B swaps which box holds which number of jars. Choice C counts boxes, not jars; the total number of boxes is not $180$. Choice D assumes every box holds $16$ jars.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$s$ ta kichik qutida $6s$ ta, $b$ ta katta qutida $10b$ ta banka bor. Birgalikda ular barcha $180$ ta bankani sig‘diradi: $6s + 10b = 180$.\\n\\nB variant qaysi quti nechta banka sig‘dirishini almashtirib yuborgan. C variant bankalarni emas, qutilarni sanaydi; qutilar soni $180$ emas. D variant har bir qutiga $16$ ta banka sig‘adi deb hisoblaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"write-equation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_two_variables\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In the $xy$-plane, the graph of $y = \\\\frac{3}{4}x - 2$ passes through the point $(8, k)$. What is the value of $k$?\",\"option_a\":\"$4$\",\"option_b\":\"$6$\",\"option_c\":\"$8$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{40}{3}$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The point $(8, k)$ means $x = 8$ and $y = k$. So $k = \\\\frac{3}{4}(8) - 2 = 6 - 2 = 4$.\\n\\nChoice B forgets to subtract $2$. Choice C uses $8 + 0$, repeating the $x$-coordinate. Choice D swaps the coordinates: it solves $8 = \\\\frac{3}{4}k - 2$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$(8, k)$ nuqta $x = 8$ va $y = k$ ekanini bildiradi. Demak, $k = \\\\frac{3}{4}(8) - 2 = 6 - 2 = 4$.\\n\\nB variantda $2$ ni ayirish unutilgan. C variant shunchaki $x$ koordinatasini takrorlagan. D variant koordinatalarni almashtirgan: $8 = \\\\frac{3}{4}k - 2$ ni yechgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"point-on-line\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_two_variables\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A school has $3{,}600$ thousand soums to spend on new chairs and tables. Each chair costs $45$ thousand soums and each table costs $120$ thousand soums. The equation $45x + 120y = 3{,}600$ represents buying $x$ chairs and $y$ tables for exactly the whole amount. What is the best interpretation of the $x$-intercept of the graph of this equation in the $xy$-plane?\",\"option_a\":\"The school can buy $80$ chairs if it buys no tables.\",\"option_b\":\"The school can buy $30$ tables if it buys no chairs.\",\"option_c\":\"The school can buy $80$ chairs and $30$ tables.\",\"option_d\":\"Each chair costs $45$ thousand soums.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The $x$-intercept is the point where $y = 0$, meaning no tables are bought. Then $45x = 3{,}600$, so $x = 80$: the school can buy $80$ chairs and no tables.\\n\\nChoice B describes the $y$-intercept, $(0, 30)$. Choice C combines both intercepts, but $80$ chairs and $30$ tables would cost $7{,}200$ thousand soums. Choice D is the meaning of the coefficient $45$, not of an intercept.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$x$ o‘qi bilan kesishish nuqtasida $y = 0$, ya’ni stol sotib olinmaydi. U holda $45x = 3{,}600$, demak $x = 80$: maktab stol olmasa, $80$ ta stul sotib olishi mumkin.\\n\\nB variant $y$ o‘qi bilan kesishish nuqtasini, $(0, 30)$ ni tasvirlaydi. C variant ikkala nuqtani birlashtirgan, lekin $80$ stul va $30$ stol $7{,}200$ ming so‘m turadi. D variant — kesishish nuqtasi emas, $45$ koeffitsiyentining ma’nosi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"intercepts\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_two_variables\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A line in the $xy$-plane passes through the points $(-4, 5)$ and $(6, -1)$. What is the slope of the line?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"-3/5\",\"-.6\",\"-0.6\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Slope $= \\\\frac{\\\\text{change in } y}{\\\\text{change in } x} = \\\\frac{-1 - 5}{6 - (-4)} = \\\\frac{-6}{10} = -\\\\frac{3}{5}$. Writing $\\\\frac{10}{-6}$ (change in $x$ over change in $y$) or dropping the negative sign are common errors.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qiyalik $= \\\\frac{y \\\\text{ ning o‘zgarishi}}{x \\\\text{ ning o‘zgarishi}} = \\\\frac{-1 - 5}{6 - (-4)} = \\\\frac{-6}{10} = -\\\\frac{3}{5}$. $\\\\frac{10}{-6}$ deb yozish ($x$ ning o‘zgarishini $y$ nikiga bo‘lish) yoki manfiy ishorani tushirib qoldirish ko‘p uchraydigan xatolar.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"slope\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_two_variables\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A gardener spends exactly $60$ thousand soums on fertilizer and seeds each month. Fertilizer costs $2.5$ thousand soums per kilogram and seeds cost $4$ thousand soums per packet, so $2.5w + 4h = 60$, where $w$ is the number of kilograms of fertilizer and $h$ is the number of packets of seeds. If the gardener buys $5$ more packets of seeds next month and still spends exactly $60$ thousand soums, how does the amount of fertilizer change?\",\"option_a\":\"It decreases by $2$ kilograms.\",\"option_b\":\"It decreases by $3.125$ kilograms.\",\"option_c\":\"It decreases by $8$ kilograms.\",\"option_d\":\"It increases by $8$ kilograms.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Five more packets cost $4 \\\\times 5 = 20$ thousand soums more. To keep the total at $60$, fertilizer spending must drop by $20$ thousand soums, which is $20 \\\\div 2.5 = 8$ kilograms less.\\n\\nChoice A divides $5$ packets by $2.5$. Choice B computes $5 \\\\times 2.5 \\\\div 4$, swapping the two prices. Choice D has the right size but the wrong direction: spending more on seeds leaves less for fertilizer.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Yana $5$ paket urug‘ $4 \\\\times 5 = 20$ ming so‘m qimmatroq. Jami $60$ da qolishi uchun o‘g‘itga sarf $20$ ming so‘mga kamayishi kerak, bu $20 \\\\div 2.5 = 8$ kilogramm kam degani.\\n\\nA variant $5$ paketni $2.5$ ga bo‘lgan. B variant $5 \\\\times 2.5 \\\\div 4$ ni hisoblagan, ya’ni ikki narxni almashtirgan. D variantda son to‘g‘ri, lekin yo‘nalish xato: urug‘ga ko‘proq sarflansa, o‘g‘itga kamroq qoladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"interpret-coefficient\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_two_variables\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"In the $xy$-plane, the point $(k, k + 3)$ lies on the graph of $5x - 2y = 12$, where $k$ is a constant. What is the value of $k$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"6\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Substitute $x = k$ and $y = k + 3$: $5k - 2(k + 3) = 12$. Then $5k - 2k - 6 = 12$, so $3k = 18$ and $k = 6$. Check: the point is $(6, 9)$, and $5(6) - 2(9) = 30 - 18 = 12$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$x = k$ va $y = k + 3$ ni qo‘yamiz: $5k - 2(k + 3) = 12$. Bundan $5k - 2k - 6 = 12$, ya’ni $3k = 18$ va $k = 6$. Tekshiruv: nuqta $(6, 9)$, va $5(6) - 2(9) = 30 - 18 = 12$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"point-on-line\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_two_variables\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Which of the following equations has the same graph in the $xy$-plane as $3x - 2y = 8$?\",\"option_a\":\"$6x - 4y = 8$\",\"option_b\":\"$2x - 3y = 8$\",\"option_c\":\"$y = \\\\frac{3}{2}x + 4$\",\"option_d\":\"$6x - 4y = 16$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Multiplying both sides of $3x - 2y = 8$ by $2$ gives $6x - 4y = 16$, an equivalent equation, so it has the same graph.\\n\\nChoice A doubles only the left side; its graph is parallel to the original line but different. Choice B swaps the coefficients, giving a different slope. Choice C: solving the original for $y$ gives $y = \\\\frac{3}{2}x - 4$, not $+4$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$3x - 2y = 8$ ning ikkala tomonini $2$ ga ko‘paytirsak, $6x - 4y = 16$ hosil bo‘ladi — bu teng kuchli tenglama, demak grafigi ham bir xil.\\n\\nA variant faqat chap tomonni ikkilantirgan; uning grafigi asl to‘g‘ri chiziqqa parallel, lekin boshqa chiziq. B variant koeffitsiyentlarni almashtirgan, qiyalik boshqacha bo‘ladi. C variant: asl tenglamadan $y$ ni topsak, $y = \\\\frac{3}{2}x - 4$ chiqadi, $+4$ emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"equivalent-lines\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_two_variables\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Gulnora sells small jars of honey for $35{,}000$ soums each and large jars for $60{,}000$ soums each. Last week she sold $4$ more large jars than small jars and earned a total of $1{,}190{,}000$ soums. How many large jars did she sell?\",\"option_a\":\"$10$\",\"option_b\":\"$14$\",\"option_c\":\"$24$\",\"option_d\":\"$34$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Let $s$ be the number of small jars; then she sold $s + 4$ large jars. So $35{,}000s + 60{,}000(s + 4) = 1{,}190{,}000$, which gives $95{,}000s + 240{,}000 = 1{,}190{,}000$, so $95{,}000s = 950{,}000$ and $s = 10$. The number of large jars is $10 + 4 = 14$.\\n\\nChoice A is the number of small jars. Choice C is the total number of jars. Choice D is $1{,}190{,}000 \\\\div 35{,}000$, as if every jar were small.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Kichik bankalar soni $s$ bo‘lsin; u holda katta bankalar $s + 4$ ta. Demak, $35{,}000s + 60{,}000(s + 4) = 1{,}190{,}000$, ya’ni $95{,}000s + 240{,}000 = 1{,}190{,}000$, bundan $95{,}000s = 950{,}000$ va $s = 10$. Katta bankalar soni $10 + 4 = 14$.\\n\\nA variant — kichik bankalar soni. C variant — jami bankalar soni. D variant — $1{,}190{,}000 \\\\div 35{,}000$, go‘yo barcha bankalar kichik bo‘lgandek.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"write-equation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_two_variables\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"In the $xy$-plane, the graph of $2x + 3y = c$, where $c$ is a positive constant, and the two coordinate axes form a triangle with an area of $27$ square units. What is the value of $c$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"18\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The $x$-intercept is $\\\\left(\\\\frac{c}{2}, 0\\\\right)$ and the $y$-intercept is $\\\\left(0, \\\\frac{c}{3}\\\\right)$. The triangle is a right triangle with legs $\\\\frac{c}{2}$ and $\\\\frac{c}{3}$, so its area is $\\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\cdot \\\\frac{c}{2} \\\\cdot \\\\frac{c}{3} = \\\\frac{c^2}{12}$. Then $\\\\frac{c^2}{12} = 27$, so $c^2 = 324$ and, since $c > 0$, $c = 18$. Check: the intercepts are $9$ and $6$, and $\\\\frac{1}{2}(9)(6) = 27$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$x$ o‘qi bilan kesishish nuqtasi $\\\\left(\\\\frac{c}{2}, 0\\\\right)$, $y$ o‘qi bilan esa $\\\\left(0, \\\\frac{c}{3}\\\\right)$. Hosil bo‘lgan uchburchak to‘g‘ri burchakli, katetlari $\\\\frac{c}{2}$ va $\\\\frac{c}{3}$, shuning uchun yuzi $\\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\cdot \\\\frac{c}{2} \\\\cdot \\\\frac{c}{3} = \\\\frac{c^2}{12}$. Demak, $\\\\frac{c^2}{12} = 27$, ya’ni $c^2 = 324$ va $c > 0$ bo‘lgani uchun $c = 18$. Tekshiruv: kesishish nuqtalari $9$ va $6$, $\\\\frac{1}{2}(9)(6) = 27$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"intercepts\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_two_variables\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"At a café, a cup of tea costs $4{,}000$ soums and a samsa costs $7{,}000$ soums. A customer spent exactly $61{,}000$ soums on $t$ cups of tea and $s$ samsas and bought at least one of each. What is the greatest possible value of $s$?\",\"option_a\":\"$3$\",\"option_b\":\"$7$\",\"option_c\":\"$8$\",\"option_d\":\"$10$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The equation is $4{,}000t + 7{,}000s = 61{,}000$, or $4t + 7s = 61$, with $t$ and $s$ positive integers. Try the largest values of $s$: $s = 8$ gives $4t = 5$ (not a whole number); $s = 7$ gives $4t = 12$, so $t = 3$. Thus the greatest possible value of $s$ is $7$.\\n\\nChoice A ($s = 3$, $t = 10$) is possible but not the greatest. Choice C is $61 \\\\div 7$ rounded down, but then $t$ is not a whole number. Choice D is the number of cups of tea when $s = 3$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tenglama: $4{,}000t + 7{,}000s = 61{,}000$, ya’ni $4t + 7s = 61$, bunda $t$ va $s$ musbat butun sonlar. $s$ ning eng katta qiymatlarini sinaymiz: $s = 8$ bo‘lsa, $4t = 5$ (butun son emas); $s = 7$ bo‘lsa, $4t = 12$, ya’ni $t = 3$. Demak, $s$ ning eng katta mumkin bo‘lgan qiymati $7$.\\n\\nA variant ($s = 3$, $t = 10$) mumkin, lekin eng kattasi emas. C variant — $61 \\\\div 7$ ning butun qismi, lekin bunda $t$ butun son chiqmaydi. D variant — $s = 3$ bo‘lgandagi choy stakanlari soni.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"integer-solutions\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_two_variables\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In the $xy$-plane, the graph of $(2k - 1)x + 4y = 12$, where $k$ is a constant, is parallel to the line that passes through the points $(-2, 5)$ and $(6, 11)$. What is the value of $k$?\",\"option_a\":\"$-\\\\frac{13}{6}$\",\"option_b\":\"$-1$\",\"option_c\":\"$2$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{19}{6}$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The line through $(-2, 5)$ and $(6, 11)$ has slope $\\\\frac{11 - 5}{6 - (-2)} = \\\\frac{6}{8} = \\\\frac{3}{4}$. Solving the given equation for $y$ gives $y = -\\\\frac{2k - 1}{4}x + 3$, so its slope is $-\\\\frac{2k - 1}{4}$. Parallel lines have equal slopes: $-\\\\frac{2k - 1}{4} = \\\\frac{3}{4}$, so $2k - 1 = -3$ and $k = -1$.\\n\\nChoice A uses the slope $\\\\frac{8}{6} = \\\\frac{4}{3}$ (change in $x$ over change in $y$). Choice C forgets the negative sign in the slope $-\\\\frac{A}{B}$. Choice D uses the perpendicular slope $-\\\\frac{4}{3}$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$(-2, 5)$ va $(6, 11)$ nuqtalardan o‘tuvchi to‘g‘ri chiziqning qiyaligi $\\\\frac{11 - 5}{6 - (-2)} = \\\\frac{6}{8} = \\\\frac{3}{4}$. Berilgan tenglamadan $y$ ni topsak, $y = -\\\\frac{2k - 1}{4}x + 3$, demak uning qiyaligi $-\\\\frac{2k - 1}{4}$. Parallel to‘g‘ri chiziqlarning qiyaliklari teng: $-\\\\frac{2k - 1}{4} = \\\\frac{3}{4}$, bundan $2k - 1 = -3$ va $k = -1$.\\n\\nA variant qiyalikni $\\\\frac{8}{6} = \\\\frac{4}{3}$ deb olgan ($x$ ning o‘zgarishini $y$ nikiga bo‘lgan). C variant $-\\\\frac{A}{B}$ qiyalikdagi manfiy ishorani unutgan. D variant perpendikulyar qiyalik $-\\\\frac{4}{3}$ ni ishlatgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"slope-parameter\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_equations_two_variables\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"In the $xy$-plane, the points $(a, b)$ and $(a + 6, b + c)$ both lie on the graph of $4x + 3y = 21$, where $a$, $b$, and $c$ are constants. What is the value of $c$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"-8\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The slope of $4x + 3y = 21$ is $-\\\\frac{4}{3}$. Moving from $(a, b)$ to $(a + 6, b + c)$, $x$ increases by $6$, so $y$ changes by $6 \\\\cdot \\\\left(-\\\\frac{4}{3}\\\\right) = -8$. Thus $c = -8$. Algebraically: $4(a + 6) + 3(b + c) = 21$ and $4a + 3b = 21$; subtracting gives $24 + 3c = 0$, so $c = -8$. Answering $8$ (dropping the sign) or $-4.5$ (using $-\\\\frac{3}{4}$ as the slope) are common errors.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$4x + 3y = 21$ ning qiyaligi $-\\\\frac{4}{3}$. $(a, b)$ dan $(a + 6, b + c)$ ga o‘tganda $x$ $6$ ga ortadi, demak $y$ $6 \\\\cdot \\\\left(-\\\\frac{4}{3}\\\\right) = -8$ ga o‘zgaradi. Shunday qilib, $c = -8$. Algebraik usul: $4(a + 6) + 3(b + c) = 21$ va $4a + 3b = 21$; ayirsak, $24 + 3c = 0$, ya’ni $c = -8$. Ishorani tushirib $8$ deb yozish yoki qiyalikni $-\\\\frac{3}{4}$ deb olib $-4.5$ topish ko‘p uchraydigan xatolar.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"slope-parameter\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A taxi service in Samarkand charges a fare that depends on the distance traveled. The fare $F$, in soums, for a ride of $d$ kilometers is given by $F(d) = 2{,}500d + 6{,}000$. What is the best interpretation of $6{,}000$ in this context?\",\"option_a\":\"The fare increases by $6{,}000$ soums for each kilometer traveled.\",\"option_b\":\"The fare includes a fixed charge of $6{,}000$ soums before any distance is added.\",\"option_c\":\"The total fare for a $1$-kilometer ride is $6{,}000$ soums.\",\"option_d\":\"The maximum distance of a ride is $6{,}000$ kilometers.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The constant term $6{,}000$ is the value of $F$ when $d = 0$, so it is the part of the fare charged regardless of distance, a fixed starting charge. The coefficient $2{,}500$ is the charge per kilometer.\\n\\nChoice A describes the slope, which is $2{,}500$. Choice C is wrong because $F(1) = 8{,}500$. Choice D misreads a fare in soums as a distance.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ozod had $6{,}000$ — $d = 0$ bo‘lgandagi $F$ qiymati, ya’ni masofadan qat’i nazar olinadigan qat’iy boshlang‘ich to‘lov. $2{,}500$ koeffitsiyenti esa har kilometr uchun to‘lov.\\n\\nA variant qiyalikni tasvirlaydi, u $2{,}500$ ga teng. C variant noto‘g‘ri, chunki $F(1) = 8{,}500$. D variant so‘mdagi to‘lovni masofa deb o‘qiydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"interpret_intercept\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = 7x - 9$. If $f(a) = 33$, what is the value of $a$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"6\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Set $7a - 9 = 33$. Adding $9$ gives $7a = 42$, so $a = 6$.\\n\\nAnswering $222$ computes $f(33)$ instead of solving $f(a) = 33$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$7a - 9 = 33$ deb olamiz. $9$ ni qo‘shsak, $7a = 42$, demak $a = 6$.\\n\\n$222$ javobi $f(a) = 33$ ni yechish o‘rniga $f(33)$ ni hisoblaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"solve_for_input\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A line in the $xy$-plane passes through the points $(-2, 5)$ and $(4, -7)$. What is the slope of the line?\",\"option_a\":\"$-2$\",\"option_b\":\"$2$\",\"option_c\":\"$-\\\\dfrac{1}{2}$\",\"option_d\":\"$-6$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Slope $= \\\\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1} = \\\\frac{-7 - 5}{4 - (-2)} = \\\\frac{-12}{6} = -2$.\\n\\nChoice B loses the negative sign. Choice C divides the change in $x$ by the change in $y$. Choice D uses $4 - 2 = 2$ for the change in $x$, forgetting that subtracting $-2$ means adding $2$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qiyalik $= \\\\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1} = \\\\frac{-7 - 5}{4 - (-2)} = \\\\frac{-12}{6} = -2$.\\n\\nB variant manfiy ishorani yo‘qotadi. C variant $x$ o‘zgarishini $y$ o‘zgarishiga bo‘ladi. D variant $x$ o‘zgarishi uchun $4 - 2 = 2$ ni oladi, $-2$ ni ayirish $2$ ni qo‘shish ekanini unutadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"slope_two_points\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A bicycle rental shop charges $20{,}000$ soums for the first hour and $12{,}000$ soums for each additional hour. Which function gives the total cost $C$, in soums, of renting a bicycle for $h$ hours, where $h$ is a whole number and $h \\\\ge 1$?\",\"option_a\":\"$C(h) = 20{,}000 + 12{,}000h$\",\"option_b\":\"$C(h) = 32{,}000h$\",\"option_c\":\"$C(h) = 20{,}000 + 12{,}000(h - 1)$\",\"option_d\":\"$C(h) = 12{,}000 + 20{,}000(h - 1)$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first hour costs $20{,}000$ soums. The remaining $h - 1$ hours cost $12{,}000$ soums each, so $C(h) = 20{,}000 + 12{,}000(h - 1)$. Check: for $1$ hour, $C(1) = 20{,}000$; for $3$ hours, $C(3) = 20{,}000 + 24{,}000 = 44{,}000$.\\n\\nChoice A charges $12{,}000$ for the first hour as well, so $C(1) = 32{,}000$. Choice B charges $32{,}000$ for every hour. Choice D swaps the two rates.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi soat $20{,}000$ so‘m turadi. Qolgan $h - 1$ soatning har biri $12{,}000$ so‘mdan, demak $C(h) = 20{,}000 + 12{,}000(h - 1)$. Tekshiruv: $1$ soat uchun $C(1) = 20{,}000$; $3$ soat uchun $C(3) = 20{,}000 + 24{,}000 = 44{,}000$.\\n\\nA variant birinchi soat uchun ham $12{,}000$ qo‘shadi, natijada $C(1) = 32{,}000$. B variant har soat uchun $32{,}000$ oladi. D variant ikki narxni almashtiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"each_additional_model\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"For the linear function $f$, $f(1) = 7$ and $f(3) = 15$. What is the value of $f(10)$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"43\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The slope is $\\\\frac{15 - 7}{3 - 1} = 4$. Then $f(1) = 4(1) + b = 7$ gives $b = 3$, so $f(x) = 4x + 3$ and $f(10) = 40 + 3 = 43$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qiyalik $\\\\frac{15 - 7}{3 - 1} = 4$. Keyin $f(1) = 4(1) + b = 7$ dan $b = 3$, demak $f(x) = 4x + 3$ va $f(10) = 40 + 3 = 43$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"build_from_points\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In the $xy$-plane, the graph of $y = -3x + 8$ is shifted down $5$ units. What is the $y$-intercept of the resulting graph?\",\"option_a\":\"$(0, 13)$\",\"option_b\":\"$(0, 3)$\",\"option_c\":\"$(0, 8)$\",\"option_d\":\"$(1, 0)$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Shifting down $5$ units subtracts $5$ from every $y$-value: $y = -3x + 8 - 5 = -3x + 3$. At $x = 0$, $y = 3$, so the $y$-intercept is $(0, 3)$.\\n\\nChoice A shifts up instead of down. Choice C is the $y$-intercept of the original graph. Choice D is the $x$-intercept of the new graph, not its $y$-intercept.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$5$ birlik pastga surish har bir $y$ qiymatdan $5$ ni ayiradi: $y = -3x + 8 - 5 = -3x + 3$. $x = 0$ da $y = 3$, demak $y$ o‘qi bilan kesishish nuqtasi $(0, 3)$.\\n\\nA variant pastga emas, yuqoriga suradi. C variant dastlabki grafikning kesishish nuqtasi. D variant yangi grafikning $x$ o‘qi bilan kesishish nuqtasi, $y$ o‘qi bilan emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"vertical_shift\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Line $\\\\ell$ is defined by $3x - 4y = 12$. Line $n$ is perpendicular to line $\\\\ell$ in the $xy$-plane. What is the slope of line $n$?\",\"option_a\":\"$-\\\\dfrac{4}{3}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\dfrac{3}{4}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\dfrac{4}{3}$\",\"option_d\":\"$-\\\\dfrac{3}{4}$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Solve for $y$: $-4y = -3x + 12$, so $y = \\\\frac{3}{4}x - 3$. The slope of $\\\\ell$ is $\\\\frac{3}{4}$. A perpendicular line has the negative reciprocal slope, $-\\\\frac{4}{3}$.\\n\\nChoice B is the slope of $\\\\ell$ itself (a parallel line). Choice C takes the reciprocal but not the opposite sign. Choice D reads the slope as $-\\\\frac{A}{B}$ with the sign of $B$ lost, $-\\\\frac{3}{4}$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$y$ ni ajratamiz: $-4y = -3x + 12$, ya’ni $y = \\\\frac{3}{4}x - 3$. $\\\\ell$ ning qiyaligi $\\\\frac{3}{4}$. Perpendikulyar chiziqning qiyaligi teskari va qarama-qarshi ishorali son: $-\\\\frac{4}{3}$.\\n\\nB variant $\\\\ell$ ning o‘z qiyaligi (parallel chiziq uchun). C variant teskari sonni oladi, lekin ishorani o‘zgartirmaydi. D variant $B$ ning ishorasini yo‘qotib, qiyalikni $-\\\\frac{3}{4}$ deb o‘qiydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"perpendicular_slope\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A textile company bought a new sewing machine for $84$ million soums. Three years later, the machine was worth $60$ million soums. Assuming the value of the machine decreases linearly, which function gives its value $V$, in millions of soums, $t$ years after it was bought?\",\"option_a\":\"$V(t) = 84 - 24t$\",\"option_b\":\"$V(t) = 84 - 8t$\",\"option_c\":\"$V(t) = 60 - 8t$\",\"option_d\":\"$V(t) = 84 - 3t$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The value drops by $84 - 60 = 24$ million soums over $3$ years, which is $\\\\frac{24}{3} = 8$ million soums per year. The starting value is $84$, so $V(t) = 84 - 8t$. Check: $V(3) = 84 - 24 = 60$.\\n\\nChoice A uses the total $3$-year drop as the yearly drop. Choice C starts from the value after $3$ years instead of the purchase price. Choice D uses the number of years as the rate.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qiymat $3$ yilda $84 - 60 = 24$ million so‘mga kamaygan, ya’ni yiliga $\\\\frac{24}{3} = 8$ million so‘m. Boshlang‘ich qiymat $84$, demak $V(t) = 84 - 8t$. Tekshiruv: $V(3) = 84 - 24 = 60$.\\n\\nA variant $3$ yillik umumiy kamayishni yillik deb oladi. C variant xarid narxi o‘rniga $3$ yildan keyingi qiymatdan boshlaydi. D variant yillar sonini sur’at deb oladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"build_from_context\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = ax + 5$, where $a$ is a constant. If $f(4) = -7$, what is the value of $f(-2)$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"11\"],\"explanation_en\":\"From $f(4) = -7$: $4a + 5 = -7$, so $4a = -12$ and $a = -3$. Then $f(x) = -3x + 5$, and $f(-2) = -3(-2) + 5 = 6 + 5 = 11$.\\n\\nAnswering $-1$ uses $a = -3$ but computes $-3(-2)$ as $-6$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$f(4) = -7$ dan: $4a + 5 = -7$, ya’ni $4a = -12$ va $a = -3$. U holda $f(x) = -3x + 5$ va $f(-2) = -3(-2) + 5 = 6 + 5 = 11$.\\n\\n$-1$ javobi $a = -3$ ni to‘g‘ri topadi, lekin $-3(-2)$ ni $-6$ deb hisoblaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"find_constant_then_evaluate\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A veterinarian uses the equation $W = 0.8n + 3.2$ to estimate the weight $W$, in kilograms, of a puppy of a certain breed that is $n$ weeks old, for $2 \\\\le n \\\\le 16$. According to the equation, by how many kilograms does the estimated weight increase over a period of $5$ weeks?\",\"option_a\":\"$0.8$\",\"option_b\":\"$3.2$\",\"option_c\":\"$4$\",\"option_d\":\"$7.2$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The slope $0.8$ means the estimated weight increases by $0.8$ kilogram per week. Over $5$ weeks, the increase is $5(0.8) = 4$ kilograms.\\n\\nChoice A is the increase for $1$ week. Choice B is the constant term, which does not describe a change. Choice D is the estimated weight $W$ at $n = 5$, not the increase over $5$ weeks.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$0.8$ qiyalik taxminiy vazn har hafta $0.8$ kilogrammga oshishini bildiradi. $5$ haftada o‘sish $5(0.8) = 4$ kilogramm.\\n\\nA variant $1$ haftalik o‘sish. B variant ozod had, u o‘zgarishni tasvirlamaydi. D variant $n = 5$ dagi taxminiy vazn $W$, $5$ hafta davomidagi o‘sish emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"interpret_slope\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"In the $xy$-plane, line $p$ is parallel to the line $y = 4x - 1$ and passes through the point $(2, 9)$. What is the $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept of line $p$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"-1/4\",\"-.25\",\"-0.25\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Parallel lines have equal slopes, so line $p$ has slope $4$: $y = 4x + b$. Using $(2, 9)$: $9 = 8 + b$, so $b = 1$ and $y = 4x + 1$.\\n\\nAt the $x$-intercept, $y = 0$: $4x + 1 = 0$, so $x = -\\\\frac{1}{4} = -0.25$.\\n\\nAnswering $1$ gives the $y$-intercept, and answering $\\\\frac{1}{4}$ drops the sign.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Parallel chiziqlarning qiyaliklari teng, demak $p$ ning qiyaligi $4$: $y = 4x + b$. $(2, 9)$ nuqtadan: $9 = 8 + b$, ya’ni $b = 1$ va $y = 4x + 1$.\\n\\n$x$ o‘qi bilan kesishish nuqtasida $y = 0$: $4x + 1 = 0$, demak $x = -\\\\frac{1}{4} = -0.25$.\\n\\n$1$ javobi $y$ o‘qi bilan kesishish nuqtasini beradi, $\\\\frac{1}{4}$ javobi esa ishorani tushirib qoldiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"parallel_line_intercept\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A water tank is being drained at a constant rate. The volume $V$, in liters, of water in the tank $t$ minutes after draining began is given by $V(t) = 9{,}000 - 360t$. Which of the following is the best interpretation of the $t$-intercept of the graph of $V$ in the $tV$-plane?\",\"option_a\":\"The $t$-intercept is $(0, 9{,}000)$; the tank held $9{,}000$ liters when draining began.\",\"option_b\":\"The $t$-intercept is $(25, 0)$; water drains at $25$ liters per minute.\",\"option_c\":\"The $t$-intercept is $(25, 0)$; the tank is empty $25$ minutes after draining began.\",\"option_d\":\"The $t$-intercept is $(360, 0)$; the tank is empty $360$ minutes after draining began.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The $t$-intercept is where $V = 0$: $9{,}000 - 360t = 0$ gives $t = 25$. So the point is $(25, 0)$, meaning that the tank contains $0$ liters (is empty) $25$ minutes after draining began.\\n\\nChoice A describes the $V$-intercept, not the $t$-intercept. Choice B has the right point but the wrong meaning: the rate is $360$ liters per minute. Choice D uses the rate as the time.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$t$ o‘qi bilan kesishish nuqtasida $V = 0$: $9{,}000 - 360t = 0$ dan $t = 25$. Demak nuqta $(25, 0)$, ya’ni bo‘shatish boshlanganidan $25$ daqiqa o‘tgach bakda $0$ litr suv qoladi (bak bo‘sh).\\n\\nA variant $t$ o‘qi bilan emas, $V$ o‘qi bilan kesishish nuqtasini tasvirlaydi. B variantda nuqta to‘g‘ri, lekin ma’nosi noto‘g‘ri: sur’at daqiqasiga $360$ litr. D variant sur’atni vaqt deb oladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"interpret_intercept\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The functions $f$ and $g$ are defined by $f(x) = 2x + k$ and $g(x) = 3x - 1$, where $k$ is a constant. If $f(g(2)) = 17$, what is the value of $g(f(1))$?\",\"option_a\":\"$11$\",\"option_b\":\"$17$\",\"option_c\":\"$26$\",\"option_d\":\"$33$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"First, $g(2) = 3(2) - 1 = 5$, so $f(g(2)) = f(5) = 10 + k = 17$, giving $k = 7$ and $f(x) = 2x + 7$.\\n\\nThen $f(1) = 9$ and $g(f(1)) = g(9) = 27 - 1 = 26$.\\n\\nChoice A computes $f(g(1)) = f(2) = 11$, applying the functions in the wrong order. Choice B repeats the given value. Choice D is $g(f(1)) + k$, adding the constant again.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Avval $g(2) = 3(2) - 1 = 5$, demak $f(g(2)) = f(5) = 10 + k = 17$, bundan $k = 7$ va $f(x) = 2x + 7$.\\n\\nKeyin $f(1) = 9$ va $g(f(1)) = g(9) = 27 - 1 = 26$.\\n\\nA variant $f(g(1)) = f(2) = 11$ ni hisoblaydi, ya’ni funksiyalarni teskari tartibda qo‘llaydi. B variant berilgan qiymatni takrorlaydi. D variant $g(f(1)) + k$, o‘zgarmasni yana bir marta qo‘shadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"composition\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"In the $xy$-plane, the points $(-1, 4)$, $(3, -8)$, and $(k, 19)$ lie on the same line. What is the value of $k$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"-6\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The slope through the first two points is $\\\\frac{-8 - 4}{3 - (-1)} = \\\\frac{-12}{4} = -3$. The slope from $(-1, 4)$ to $(k, 19)$ must also be $-3$: $\\\\frac{19 - 4}{k - (-1)} = -3$, so $15 = -3(k + 1)$, $k + 1 = -5$, and $k = -6$.\\n\\nCheck: the line is $y = -3x + 1$, and $-3(-6) + 1 = 19$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi ikki nuqtadan o‘tuvchi chiziq qiyaligi $\\\\frac{-8 - 4}{3 - (-1)} = \\\\frac{-12}{4} = -3$. $(-1, 4)$ dan $(k, 19)$ gacha qiyalik ham $-3$ bo‘lishi kerak: $\\\\frac{19 - 4}{k - (-1)} = -3$, ya’ni $15 = -3(k + 1)$, $k + 1 = -5$ va $k = -6$.\\n\\nTekshiruv: chiziq tenglamasi $y = -3x + 1$, va $-3(-6) + 1 = 19$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"collinearity\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In the $xy$-plane, the lines with equations $kx + 3y = 7$ and $4x - 6y = 5$ are perpendicular, where $k$ is a constant. What is the value of $k$?\",\"option_a\":\"$-2$\",\"option_b\":\"$2$\",\"option_c\":\"$-\\\\dfrac{9}{2}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\dfrac{9}{2}$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The slope of $kx + 3y = 7$ is $-\\\\frac{k}{3}$, and the slope of $4x - 6y = 5$ is $-\\\\frac{4}{-6} = \\\\frac{2}{3}$. Perpendicular slopes multiply to $-1$: $-\\\\frac{k}{3} \\\\cdot \\\\frac{2}{3} = -1$, so $\\\\frac{2k}{9} = 1$ and $k = \\\\frac{9}{2}$.\\n\\nCheck: the first slope is $-\\\\frac{3}{2}$, the negative reciprocal of $\\\\frac{2}{3}$.\\n\\nChoice A makes the lines parallel ($-\\\\frac{k}{3} = \\\\frac{2}{3}$). Choice B uses the wrong sign for a parallel condition. Choice C makes the product of the slopes $+1$ instead of $-1$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$kx + 3y = 7$ ning qiyaligi $-\\\\frac{k}{3}$, $4x - 6y = 5$ ning qiyaligi esa $-\\\\frac{4}{-6} = \\\\frac{2}{3}$. Perpendikulyar chiziqlar qiyaliklari ko‘paytmasi $-1$: $-\\\\frac{k}{3} \\\\cdot \\\\frac{2}{3} = -1$, ya’ni $\\\\frac{2k}{9} = 1$ va $k = \\\\frac{9}{2}$.\\n\\nTekshiruv: birinchi qiyalik $-\\\\frac{3}{2}$, bu $\\\\frac{2}{3}$ ning teskari va qarama-qarshi ishorali soni.\\n\\nA variant chiziqlarni parallel qiladi ($-\\\\frac{k}{3} = \\\\frac{2}{3}$). B variant parallellik shartida ishorani adashtiradi. C variant qiyaliklar ko‘paytmasini $-1$ emas, $+1$ qiladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"perpendicular_parameter\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"For the linear function $f$, $f(2) = 11$ and $f(6) = 23$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x) - 4$. In the $xy$-plane, what is the $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept of the graph of $y = g(x)$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"-1/6\",\"-.1666\",\"-.1667\",\"-0.166\",\"-0.167\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The slope of $f$ is $\\\\frac{23 - 11}{6 - 2} = 3$, and $f(2) = 6 + b = 11$ gives $b = 5$, so $f(x) = 3x + 5$.\\n\\nThen $g(x) = f(2x) - 4 = 3(2x) + 5 - 4 = 6x + 1$. Setting $6x + 1 = 0$ gives $x = -\\\\frac{1}{6}$.\\n\\nA common error is writing $f(2x) = 2f(x) = 6x + 10$, which gives $g(x) = 6x + 6$ and $x = -1$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$f$ ning qiyaligi $\\\\frac{23 - 11}{6 - 2} = 3$, va $f(2) = 6 + b = 11$ dan $b = 5$, demak $f(x) = 3x + 5$.\\n\\nU holda $g(x) = f(2x) - 4 = 3(2x) + 5 - 4 = 6x + 1$. $6x + 1 = 0$ dan $x = -\\\\frac{1}{6}$.\\n\\nKo‘p uchraydigan xato: $f(2x) = 2f(x) = 6x + 10$ deb yozish, bu $g(x) = 6x + 6$ va $x = -1$ ni beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"build_then_transform\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_inequalities\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Aziz has $150{,}000$ soums to spend on school supplies. Notebooks cost $8{,}000$ soums each and pens cost $3{,}000$ soums each. Which inequality represents the possible numbers of notebooks $n$ and pens $p$ that Aziz can buy without spending more than he has?\",\"option_a\":\"$8{,}000n + 3{,}000p \\\\ge 150{,}000$\",\"option_b\":\"$3{,}000n + 8{,}000p \\\\le 150{,}000$\",\"option_c\":\"$8{,}000n + 3{,}000p \\\\le 150{,}000$\",\"option_d\":\"$8{,}000n + 3{,}000p < 150{,}000$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The cost of $n$ notebooks is $8{,}000n$ and the cost of $p$ pens is $3{,}000p$. The total can be at most $150{,}000$ soums (spending exactly $150{,}000$ is allowed), so $8{,}000n + 3{,}000p \\\\le 150{,}000$.\\n\\nChoice A requires spending at least $150{,}000$. Choice B matches the prices with the wrong items. Choice D excludes spending exactly $150{,}000$, which Aziz can do.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$n$ ta daftar narxi $8{,}000n$, $p$ ta ruchka narxi $3{,}000p$. Jami ko‘pi bilan $150{,}000$ so‘m bo‘lishi mumkin (aynan $150{,}000$ sarflash ham mumkin), demak $8{,}000n + 3{,}000p \\\\le 150{,}000$.\\n\\nA variant kamida $150{,}000$ sarflashni talab qiladi. B variant narxlarni noto‘g‘ri buyumlarga bog‘laydi. D variant aynan $150{,}000$ sarflashni chiqarib tashlaydi, holbuki Aziz buni qila oladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"write_budget_inequality\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_inequalities\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"$$5 - 3x \\\\ge -16$$ What is the greatest value of $x$ that satisfies the given inequality?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"7\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Subtract $5$ from both sides: $-3x \\\\ge -21$. Dividing by $-3$ reverses the inequality: $x \\\\le 7$. The greatest value is $7$.\\n\\nForgetting to reverse the sign gives $x \\\\ge 7$, which has no greatest value.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkala tomondan $5$ ni ayiramiz: $-3x \\\\ge -21$. $-3$ ga bo‘lganda tengsizlik belgisi teskarisiga o‘zgaradi: $x \\\\le 7$. Eng katta qiymat $7$.\\n\\nBelgini o‘zgartirishni unutish $x \\\\ge 7$ ni beradi, bunda eng katta qiymat yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"solve_inequality_flip\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_inequalities\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Which of the following values of $x$ satisfies the inequality $4x + 9 < 2x - 5$?\",\"option_a\":\"$-8$\",\"option_b\":\"$-7$\",\"option_c\":\"$0$\",\"option_d\":\"$7$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Subtract $2x$ and $9$ from both sides: $2x < -14$, so $x < -7$. Of the choices, only $-8$ is less than $-7$. Check: $4(-8) + 9 = -23$ and $2(-8) - 5 = -21$, and $-23 < -21$.\\n\\nChoice B makes the two sides equal ($-19 = -19$), so it does not satisfy a strict inequality. Choices C and D are greater than $-7$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkala tomondan $2x$ va $9$ ni ayiramiz: $2x < -14$, demak $x < -7$. Variantlardan faqat $-8$ son $-7$ dan kichik. Tekshiruv: $4(-8) + 9 = -23$ va $2(-8) - 5 = -21$, va $-23 < -21$.\\n\\nB variantda ikkala tomon teng bo‘ladi ($-19 = -19$), shuning uchun qat’iy tengsizlik bajarilmaydi. C va D variantlar $-7$ dan katta.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"test_values\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_inequalities\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Lola tutors students in mathematics and earns $45{,}000$ soums per hour. She has already earned $270{,}000$ soums this month and wants to earn at least $900{,}000$ soums in total this month. Which inequality represents the number of additional hours $h$ she must tutor this month to reach her goal?\",\"option_a\":\"$45{,}000h + 270{,}000 \\\\ge 900{,}000$\",\"option_b\":\"$45{,}000h + 270{,}000 \\\\le 900{,}000$\",\"option_c\":\"$45{,}000h \\\\ge 900{,}000 + 270{,}000$\",\"option_d\":\"$270{,}000h + 45{,}000 \\\\ge 900{,}000$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"After $h$ more hours, her total is the $270{,}000$ soums already earned plus $45{,}000h$. “At least $900{,}000$” means the total is greater than or equal to $900{,}000$: $45{,}000h + 270{,}000 \\\\ge 900{,}000$.\\n\\nChoice B uses “at most” instead of “at least”. Choice C adds the amount already earned to the goal instead of counting it toward the goal. Choice D swaps the hourly rate and the amount already earned.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Yana $h$ soatdan keyin uning jami daromadi — allaqachon topilgan $270{,}000$ so‘m va $45{,}000h$ yig‘indisi. “Kamida $900{,}000$” — jami $900{,}000$ dan katta yoki teng degani: $45{,}000h + 270{,}000 \\\\ge 900{,}000$.\\n\\nB variant “kamida” o‘rniga “ko‘pi bilan” ni ishlatadi. C variant topilgan pulni maqsadga hisoblash o‘rniga uni maqsadga qo‘shadi. D variant soatlik haq bilan topilgan pulni almashtiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"write_goal_inequality\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_inequalities\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"An elevator has a maximum load of $630$ kilograms. A worker who weighs $80$ kilograms is loading boxes that weigh $24$ kilograms each into the elevator and will ride with them. What is the greatest number of boxes the worker can load so that the total load does not exceed the maximum?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"22\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The total load is $80 + 24n \\\\le 630$, so $24n \\\\le 550$ and $n \\\\le 22.91\\\\ldots$. Since $n$ must be a whole number, the greatest possible value is $22$.\\n\\nRounding up to $23$ gives $80 + 24(23) = 632$ kilograms, which exceeds the limit. Ignoring the worker's weight gives $26$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Umumiy yuk $80 + 24n \\\\le 630$, ya’ni $24n \\\\le 550$ va $n \\\\le 22.91\\\\ldots$. $n$ butun son bo‘lishi kerak, demak eng katta qiymat $22$.\\n\\n$23$ ga yaxlitlash $80 + 24(23) = 632$ kilogrammni beradi, bu chegaradan oshadi. Ishchining vaznini hisobga olmaslik $26$ ni beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"integer_max_context\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_inequalities\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Which point $(x, y)$ is a solution to the inequality $y \\\\ge 2x - 3$?\",\"option_a\":\"$(3, 2)$\",\"option_b\":\"$(1, 0)$\",\"option_c\":\"$(4, 4)$\",\"option_d\":\"$(2, 0)$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Substitute each point. For $(1, 0)$: $2(1) - 3 = -1$, and $0 \\\\ge -1$ is true.\\n\\nFor $(3, 2)$: $2 \\\\ge 3$ is false. For $(4, 4)$: $4 \\\\ge 5$ is false. For $(2, 0)$: $0 \\\\ge 1$ is false.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Har bir nuqtani qo‘yamiz. $(1, 0)$ uchun: $2(1) - 3 = -1$, va $0 \\\\ge -1$ to‘g‘ri.\\n\\n$(3, 2)$ uchun: $2 \\\\ge 3$ noto‘g‘ri. $(4, 4)$ uchun: $4 \\\\ge 5$ noto‘g‘ri. $(2, 0)$ uchun: $0 \\\\ge 1$ noto‘g‘ri.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"point_in_region\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_inequalities\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A storage tank initially holds $60$ liters of water. Because of a leak, the tank loses between $1.5$ and $2.5$ liters of water per hour, inclusive. Assuming no water is added, which inequality describes the possible amounts of water $A$, in liters, in the tank after $8$ hours?\",\"option_a\":\"$12 \\\\le A \\\\le 20$\",\"option_b\":\"$40 \\\\le A \\\\le 48$\",\"option_c\":\"$48 \\\\le A \\\\le 60$\",\"option_d\":\"$72 \\\\le A \\\\le 80$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"In $8$ hours, the tank loses between $8(1.5) = 12$ and $8(2.5) = 20$ liters. The greatest possible amount left is $60 - 12 = 48$ liters and the least is $60 - 20 = 40$ liters, so $40 \\\\le A \\\\le 48$.\\n\\nChoice A gives the amount lost, not the amount left. Choice C uses only the smallest loss as a lower bound. Choice D adds the losses instead of subtracting them.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$8$ soatda bak $8(1.5) = 12$ dan $8(2.5) = 20$ litrgacha suv yo‘qotadi. Eng ko‘p qoladigan miqdor $60 - 12 = 48$ litr, eng kami $60 - 20 = 40$ litr, demak $40 \\\\le A \\\\le 48$.\\n\\nA variant qolgan emas, yo‘qotilgan miqdorni beradi. C variant faqat eng kichik yo‘qotishni quyi chegara deb oladi. D variant yo‘qotishlarni ayirish o‘rniga qo‘shadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"range_of_rates\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_inequalities\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"In the $xy$-plane, the graph of the inequality $3x + by \\\\le 18$, where $b$ is a constant, is a region bounded by a line. This boundary line passes through the point $(2, -4)$. What is the value of $b$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"-3\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The boundary line is $3x + by = 18$. Substituting $(2, -4)$: $3(2) + b(-4) = 18$, so $6 - 4b = 18$, $-4b = 12$, and $b = -3$.\\n\\nAnswering $3$ comes from a sign error when dividing by $-4$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Chegara chizig‘i $3x + by = 18$. $(2, -4)$ ni qo‘yamiz: $3(2) + b(-4) = 18$, ya’ni $6 - 4b = 18$, $-4b = 12$ va $b = -3$.\\n\\n$3$ javobi $-4$ ga bo‘lishdagi ishora xatosidan chiqadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"boundary_line_constant\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_inequalities\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A minibus can carry at most $16$ passengers. The fare is $4{,}000$ soums for an adult and $2{,}000$ soums for a child. On each trip, the driver wants to collect at least $50{,}000$ soums in fares. Which system of inequalities represents the numbers of adults $a$ and children $c$ on a trip that meets both conditions?\",\"option_a\":\"$a + c \\\\le 16$ and $4{,}000a + 2{,}000c \\\\ge 50{,}000$\",\"option_b\":\"$a + c \\\\ge 16$ and $4{,}000a + 2{,}000c \\\\le 50{,}000$\",\"option_c\":\"$a + c \\\\le 16$ and $2{,}000a + 4{,}000c \\\\ge 50{,}000$\",\"option_d\":\"$4{,}000a + 2{,}000c \\\\le 16$ and $a + c \\\\ge 50{,}000$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"“At most $16$ passengers” gives $a + c \\\\le 16$. The fares collected are $4{,}000a + 2{,}000c$, and “at least $50{,}000$” gives $4{,}000a + 2{,}000c \\\\ge 50{,}000$.\\n\\nChoice B reverses both inequality signs. Choice C matches the fares with the wrong passengers. Choice D pairs the passenger limit with the fare expression and the fare goal with the passenger count.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“Ko‘pi bilan $16$ yo‘lovchi” — $a + c \\\\le 16$. Yig‘ilgan to‘lov $4{,}000a + 2{,}000c$, “kamida $50{,}000$” esa $4{,}000a + 2{,}000c \\\\ge 50{,}000$ ni beradi.\\n\\nB variant ikkala tengsizlik belgisini teskari qiladi. C variant narxlarni noto‘g‘ri yo‘lovchilarga bog‘laydi. D variant yo‘lovchilar chegarasini to‘lov ifodasiga, to‘lov maqsadini esa yo‘lovchilar soniga bog‘laydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"write_system_context\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_inequalities\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Which of the following describes all values of $x$ that satisfy $-3 < 5 - 2x \\\\le 11$?\",\"option_a\":\"$-3 \\\\le x < 4$\",\"option_b\":\"$-3 < x \\\\le 4$\",\"option_c\":\"$-4 < x \\\\le 3$\",\"option_d\":\"$-8 < x \\\\le 6$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Subtract $5$ from all three parts: $-8 < -2x \\\\le 6$. Divide all parts by $-2$ and reverse both signs: $4 > x \\\\ge -3$, which is $-3 \\\\le x < 4$.\\n\\nChoice B puts the strict and non-strict signs on the wrong ends: $x = 4$ gives $5 - 2x = -3$, which is excluded, while $x = -3$ gives $11$, which is included. Choice C does not reverse the order of the bounds. Choice D stops before dividing by $-2$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Uchala qismdan $5$ ni ayiramiz: $-8 < -2x \\\\le 6$. Hammasini $-2$ ga bo‘lamiz va ikkala belgini teskarisiga o‘zgartiramiz: $4 > x \\\\ge -3$, ya’ni $-3 \\\\le x < 4$.\\n\\nB variant qat’iy va noqat’iy belgilarni noto‘g‘ri uchlarga qo‘yadi: $x = 4$ da $5 - 2x = -3$, bu qiymat kirmaydi, $x = -3$ da esa $11$, bu qiymat kiradi. C variant chegaralar tartibini almashtirmaydi. D variant $-2$ ga bo‘lishdan oldin to‘xtaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"compound_inequality\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_inequalities\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A juice vendor sells each cup of fresh juice for $6{,}000$ soums. Each cup costs the vendor $2{,}500$ soums to make, and the vendor also pays $190{,}000$ soums per day for the stand. What is the least number of cups the vendor must sell in one day to make a profit of at least $100{,}000$ soums that day?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"83\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Each cup gives $6{,}000 - 2{,}500 = 3{,}500$ soums toward profit, so the daily profit is $3{,}500c - 190{,}000$. We need $3{,}500c - 190{,}000 \\\\ge 100{,}000$, so $3{,}500c \\\\ge 290{,}000$ and $c \\\\ge 82.857\\\\ldots$.\\n\\nSince $c$ is a whole number, the least value is $83$. Rounding down to $82$ gives a profit of $97{,}000$ soums, which is not enough.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Har bir stakan foydaga $6{,}000 - 2{,}500 = 3{,}500$ so‘m qo‘shadi, demak kunlik foyda $3{,}500c - 190{,}000$. Kerak: $3{,}500c - 190{,}000 \\\\ge 100{,}000$, ya’ni $3{,}500c \\\\ge 290{,}000$ va $c \\\\ge 82.857\\\\ldots$.\\n\\n$c$ butun son bo‘lgani uchun eng kichik qiymat $83$. $82$ ga yaxlitlash $97{,}000$ so‘m foyda beradi, bu yetarli emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"profit_threshold\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_inequalities\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In the $xy$-plane, a line passes through the points $(0, 2)$ and $(3, 8)$. Which inequality describes the region of all points that lie above this line, not including the points on the line?\",\"option_a\":\"$y \\\\ge 2x + 2$\",\"option_b\":\"$y > 2x + 2$\",\"option_c\":\"$y < 2x + 2$\",\"option_d\":\"$y > \\\\dfrac{1}{2}x + 2$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The slope is $\\\\frac{8 - 2}{3 - 0} = 2$ and the $y$-intercept is $2$, so the line is $y = 2x + 2$. Points above the line have $y$-values greater than the line's, and the line itself is excluded, so the inequality is strict: $y > 2x + 2$.\\n\\nChoice A includes the line. Choice C describes the region below the line. Choice D uses the reciprocal of the slope.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qiyalik $\\\\frac{8 - 2}{3 - 0} = 2$ va $y$ o‘qi bilan kesishish $2$, demak chiziq $y = 2x + 2$. Chiziqdan yuqoridagi nuqtalarda $y$ qiymati chiziqnikidan katta, chiziqning o‘zi kirmaydi, shuning uchun tengsizlik qat’iy: $y > 2x + 2$.\\n\\nA variant chiziqning o‘zini ham qo‘shadi. C variant chiziqdan pastdagi sohani tasvirlaydi. D variant qiyalikning teskarisini oladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"region_from_line\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_inequalities\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A truck delivers sacks of rice and sacks of flour to a market. Each sack of rice weighs $50$ kilograms and each sack of flour weighs $40$ kilograms. The truck can carry at most $2{,}000$ kilograms and has room for at most $45$ sacks. On one trip, the truck must carry at least $15$ sacks of rice. What is the greatest number of sacks of flour the truck can carry on this trip?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"30\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Let $r$ be the number of sacks of rice and $f$ the number of sacks of flour. The conditions are $50r + 40f \\\\le 2{,}000$, $r + f \\\\le 45$, and $r \\\\ge 15$. To make $f$ as large as possible, use the least rice, $r = 15$.\\n\\nWeight: $750 + 40f \\\\le 2{,}000$, so $f \\\\le 31.25$, which allows at most $31$ sacks. Space: $15 + f \\\\le 45$, so $f \\\\le 30$. Both conditions must hold, so the greatest value is $30$.\\n\\nAnswering $31$ checks only the weight limit and ignores the limit on the number of sacks.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Guruch qoplari soni $r$, un qoplari soni $f$ bo‘lsin. Shartlar: $50r + 40f \\\\le 2{,}000$, $r + f \\\\le 45$ va $r \\\\ge 15$. $f$ ni eng katta qilish uchun guruchni eng kam, $r = 15$ olamiz.\\n\\nVazn: $750 + 40f \\\\le 2{,}000$, ya’ni $f \\\\le 31.25$, demak ko‘pi bilan $31$ qop. Joy: $15 + f \\\\le 45$, ya’ni $f \\\\le 30$. Ikkala shart ham bajarilishi kerak, demak eng katta qiymat $30$.\\n\\n$31$ javobi faqat vazn chegarasini tekshirib, qoplar soni chegarasini e’tiborsiz qoldiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"two_constraint_integer_max\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_inequalities\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"$$ax - 4 \\\\ge 3x + 2$$ In the given inequality, $a$ is a constant. The solution set of the inequality is $x \\\\le -3$. What is the value of $a$?\",\"option_a\":\"$-1$\",\"option_b\":\"$1$\",\"option_c\":\"$3$\",\"option_d\":\"$5$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Rearrange: $ax - 3x \\\\ge 6$, so $(a - 3)x \\\\ge 6$. The solution set has the form $x \\\\le \\\\ldots$, so dividing by $a - 3$ must reverse the sign, which means $a - 3 < 0$. Then $x \\\\le \\\\frac{6}{a - 3}$, and $\\\\frac{6}{a - 3} = -3$ gives $a - 3 = -2$, so $a = 1$.\\n\\nChoice D comes from setting $\\\\frac{6}{a - 3} = 3$; it gives $x \\\\ge 3$. Choice C makes the inequality $0 \\\\ge 6$, which has no solution. Choice A gives $x \\\\le -\\\\frac{3}{2}$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Hadlarni ko‘chiramiz: $ax - 3x \\\\ge 6$, ya’ni $(a - 3)x \\\\ge 6$. Yechimlar to‘plami $x \\\\le \\\\ldots$ ko‘rinishida, demak $a - 3$ ga bo‘lganda belgi teskarisiga o‘zgarishi kerak, ya’ni $a - 3 < 0$. U holda $x \\\\le \\\\frac{6}{a - 3}$, va $\\\\frac{6}{a - 3} = -3$ dan $a - 3 = -2$, demak $a = 1$.\\n\\nD variant $\\\\frac{6}{a - 3} = 3$ deb olishdan chiqadi; u $x \\\\ge 3$ ni beradi. C variant tengsizlikni $0 \\\\ge 6$ ga aylantiradi, bu yechimga ega emas. A variant $x \\\\le -\\\\frac{3}{2}$ ni beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"parameter_solution_set\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_inequalities\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"How many integers $x$ satisfy both $3x - 7 > 11$ and $2x + 1 < 35$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"10\"],\"explanation_en\":\"From $3x - 7 > 11$: $3x > 18$, so $x > 6$. From $2x + 1 < 35$: $2x < 34$, so $x < 17$. The integers with $6 < x < 17$ are $7, 8, \\\\ldots, 16$, which is $16 - 7 + 1 = 10$ integers.\\n\\nAnswering $11$ or $12$ includes one or both endpoints, $6$ and $17$, which make the strict inequalities false.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$3x - 7 > 11$ dan: $3x > 18$, ya’ni $x > 6$. $2x + 1 < 35$ dan: $2x < 34$, ya’ni $x < 17$. $6 < x < 17$ bo‘ladigan butun sonlar $7, 8, \\\\ldots, 16$, ularning soni $16 - 7 + 1 = 10$.\\n\\n$11$ yoki $12$ javobi chegaraviy $6$ va $17$ sonlaridan birini yoki ikkalasini qo‘shadi, lekin ular qat’iy tengsizliklarni qanoatlantirmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"count_integer_solutions\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"linear_inequalities\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A phone's battery is at $92\\\\%$ charge. While a navigation app runs, the battery charge drops by at least $6$ and at most $9$ percentage points per hour, depending on conditions. What is the greatest whole number of hours $h$ for which the battery is guaranteed to still have at least $20\\\\%$ charge after running the app for $h$ hours?\",\"option_a\":\"$8$\",\"option_b\":\"$9$\",\"option_c\":\"$12$\",\"option_d\":\"$15$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"A guarantee must hold even in the worst case, when the battery drops $9$ percentage points per hour. Then the charge after $h$ hours is $92 - 9h$, and $92 - 9h \\\\ge 20$ gives $9h \\\\le 72$, so $h \\\\le 8$. The greatest whole number is $8$.\\n\\nChoice C uses the best-case rate of $6$ points per hour ($92 - 6h \\\\ge 20$ gives $h \\\\le 12$), which is possible but not guaranteed. Choice B is too large: in the worst case the charge after $9$ hours is $92 - 81 = 11\\\\%$. Choice D divides $92$ by $6$ and ignores the $20\\\\%$ requirement.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Kafolat eng yomon holatda ham bajarilishi kerak, ya’ni batareya soatiga $9$ foiz punktga kamayganda. U holda $h$ soatdan keyingi zaryad $92 - 9h$, va $92 - 9h \\\\ge 20$ dan $9h \\\\le 72$, ya’ni $h \\\\le 8$. Eng katta butun son $8$.\\n\\nC variant eng yaxshi holatdagi soatiga $6$ punkt sur’atni oladi ($92 - 6h \\\\ge 20$ dan $h \\\\le 12$); bu mumkin, lekin kafolatlanmagan. B variant juda katta: eng yomon holatda $9$ soatdan keyin zaryad $92 - 81 = 11\\\\%$. D variant $92$ ni $6$ ga bo‘ladi va $20\\\\%$ talabini e’tiborsiz qoldiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"worst_case_reasoning\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"lines_angles_triangles\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Two lines intersect at a point and form four angles. One of the angles is 4 times as large as an angle adjacent to it. What is the measure, in degrees, of the smaller of these two adjacent angles?\",\"option_a\":\"$18^\\\\circ$\",\"option_b\":\"$36^\\\\circ$\",\"option_c\":\"$72^\\\\circ$\",\"option_d\":\"$144^\\\\circ$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Adjacent angles formed by two intersecting lines form a straight line, so they add to $180^\\\\circ$. If the smaller angle is $x^\\\\circ$, then $x + 4x = 180$, so $x = 36$. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A uses a sum of $90^\\\\circ$, and choice C uses a sum of $360^\\\\circ$. Choice D is the larger of the two angles.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Kesishuvchi ikki to‘g‘ri chiziq hosil qilgan qo‘shni burchaklar yoyiq burchak hosil qiladi, ya’ni yig‘indisi $180^\\\\circ$. Kichik burchak $x^\\\\circ$ bo‘lsa, $x + 4x = 180$, demak $x = 36$. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA javobda yig‘indi $90^\\\\circ$, C javobda $360^\\\\circ$ deb olingan. D — ikki burchakdan kattasi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"vertical_linear_pair\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"lines_angles_triangles\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"In a right triangle, the measure of one acute angle is $18^\\\\circ$ greater than the measure of the other acute angle. What is the measure, in degrees, of the larger acute angle?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"54\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The two acute angles of a right triangle add to $180^\\\\circ - 90^\\\\circ = 90^\\\\circ$. If the smaller angle is $x^\\\\circ$, then $x + (x + 18) = 90$, so $x = 36$, and the larger angle is $36 + 18 = 54$ degrees. If you got 36, you gave the smaller angle.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"To‘g‘ri burchakli uchburchakning ikki o‘tkir burchagi yig‘indisi $180^\\\\circ - 90^\\\\circ = 90^\\\\circ$. Kichik burchak $x^\\\\circ$ bo‘lsa, $x + (x + 18) = 90$, demak $x = 36$, katta burchak esa $36 + 18 = 54$ gradus. Agar 36 chiqqan bo‘lsa, siz kichik burchakni yozgansiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"triangle_angle_sum\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"lines_angles_triangles\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Parallel lines $p$ and $q$ are intersected by transversal $t$. Angle 1 is formed where $t$ meets $p$, and angle 2 is formed where $t$ meets $q$. Both angles lie between lines $p$ and $q$, on the same side of $t$. If the measure of angle 1 is $63^\\\\circ$, what is the measure of angle 2?\",\"option_a\":\"$27^\\\\circ$\",\"option_b\":\"$63^\\\\circ$\",\"option_c\":\"$117^\\\\circ$\",\"option_d\":\"$153^\\\\circ$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Angles 1 and 2 are same-side interior angles. When the lines are parallel, same-side interior angles are supplementary, so angle 2 measures $180^\\\\circ - 63^\\\\circ = 117^\\\\circ$. Choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A is the complement of $63^\\\\circ$. Choice B would be correct for alternate interior or corresponding angles, which are congruent, but these angles are on the same side of $t$. Choice D is $90^\\\\circ + 63^\\\\circ$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"1- va 2-burchaklar — bir tomonli ichki burchaklar. To‘g‘ri chiziqlar parallel bo‘lsa, bir tomonli ichki burchaklar yig‘indisi $180^\\\\circ$, demak 2-burchak $180^\\\\circ - 63^\\\\circ = 117^\\\\circ$. To‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA — $63^\\\\circ$ ni $90^\\\\circ$ gacha to‘ldiruvchi burchak. B ichki almashinuvchi yoki mos burchaklar uchun to‘g‘ri bo‘lardi (ular teng), lekin bu burchaklar $t$ ning bir tomonida joylashgan. D — bu $90^\\\\circ + 63^\\\\circ$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"parallel_lines\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"lines_angles_triangles\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Triangle $ABC$ is similar to triangle $DEF$, where vertices $A$, $B$, and $C$ correspond to vertices $D$, $E$, and $F$, respectively. If $AB = 6$, $DE = 15$, and $BC = 8$, what is the length of $\\\\overline{EF}$?\",\"option_a\":\"3.2\",\"option_b\":\"11.25\",\"option_c\":\"17\",\"option_d\":\"20\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Corresponding sides are proportional: $\\\\frac{EF}{BC} = \\\\frac{DE}{AB} = \\\\frac{15}{6} = 2.5$. So $EF = 2.5 \\\\cdot 8 = 20$. Choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoice A multiplies by $\\\\frac{6}{15}$, the scale factor in the wrong direction. Choice B, $\\\\frac{15 \\\\cdot 6}{8}$, pairs sides that do not correspond. Choice C adds the difference $15 - 6 = 9$ to 8; similar figures are related by multiplication, not addition.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Mos tomonlar proporsional: $\\\\frac{EF}{BC} = \\\\frac{DE}{AB} = \\\\frac{15}{6} = 2.5$. Demak $EF = 2.5 \\\\cdot 8 = 20$. To‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\nA javobda teskari koeffitsiyent $\\\\frac{6}{15}$ ga ko‘paytirilgan. B, ya’ni $\\\\frac{15 \\\\cdot 6}{8}$, mos kelmaydigan tomonlarni juftlaydi. C javobda 8 ga $15 - 6 = 9$ ayirma qo‘shilgan; o‘xshash shakllar qo‘shish bilan emas, ko‘paytirish bilan bog‘lanadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"similar_triangles\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"lines_angles_triangles\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"In triangle $ABC$, $AB = BC$, and the measure of angle $B$ is twice the measure of angle $A$. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle $B$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"90\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Since $AB = BC$, the angles opposite these sides are equal: angle $C$ (opposite $\\\\overline{AB}$) equals angle $A$ (opposite $\\\\overline{BC}$). If angle $A$ is $x^\\\\circ$, then $x + 2x + x = 180$, so $x = 45$ and angle $B = 2 \\\\cdot 45 = 90$ degrees. If you got 45, you gave angle $A$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$AB = BC$ bo‘lgani uchun bu tomonlar qarshisidagi burchaklar teng: $C$ burchak ($\\\\overline{AB}$ qarshisida) $A$ burchakka ($\\\\overline{BC}$ qarshisida) teng. $A$ burchak $x^\\\\circ$ bo‘lsa, $x + 2x + x = 180$, demak $x = 45$ va $B = 2 \\\\cdot 45 = 90$ gradus. Agar 45 chiqqan bo‘lsa, siz $A$ burchakni yozgansiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"isosceles\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"lines_angles_triangles\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A courtyard is shaped like a pentagon (a polygon with 5 sides). What is the sum of the measures, in degrees, of the interior angles of the pentagon?\",\"option_a\":\"$360^\\\\circ$\",\"option_b\":\"$540^\\\\circ$\",\"option_c\":\"$720^\\\\circ$\",\"option_d\":\"$900^\\\\circ$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"A polygon with $n$ sides can be divided into $n - 2$ triangles from one vertex, so its interior angles add to $(n - 2) \\\\cdot 180^\\\\circ$. For $n = 5$, the sum is $3 \\\\cdot 180^\\\\circ = 540^\\\\circ$. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A is the sum for a quadrilateral (and the sum of the exterior angles of any polygon). Choice C is the sum for a hexagon, and choice D is $5 \\\\cdot 180^\\\\circ$, which uses $n$ triangles instead of $n - 2$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$n$ tomonli ko‘pburchakni bir uchidan $n - 2$ ta uchburchakka bo‘lish mumkin, shuning uchun ichki burchaklari yig‘indisi $(n - 2) \\\\cdot 180^\\\\circ$. $n = 5$ uchun yig‘indi $3 \\\\cdot 180^\\\\circ = 540^\\\\circ$. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA — to‘rtburchak uchun yig‘indi (shuningdek, istalgan ko‘pburchak tashqi burchaklari yig‘indisi). C — oltiburchak uchun yig‘indi, D esa $5 \\\\cdot 180^\\\\circ$; unda $n - 2$ o‘rniga $n$ ta uchburchak olingan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"polygon_angles\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"lines_angles_triangles\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In triangle $ABC$, point $D$ lies on $\\\\overline{AB}$ and point $E$ lies on $\\\\overline{AC}$ so that $\\\\overline{DE}$ is parallel to $\\\\overline{BC}$. If $AD = 4$, $DB = 6$, and $DE = 5$, what is the length of $\\\\overline{BC}$?\",\"option_a\":\"2\",\"option_b\":\"7.5\",\"option_c\":\"12.5\",\"option_d\":\"15\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Because $\\\\overline{DE} \\\\parallel \\\\overline{BC}$, triangle $ADE$ is similar to triangle $ABC$. The ratio is $\\\\frac{AB}{AD} = \\\\frac{4 + 6}{4} = \\\\frac{5}{2}$, so $BC = \\\\frac{5}{2} \\\\cdot 5 = 12.5$. Choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A multiplies by $\\\\frac{4}{10}$ instead of $\\\\frac{10}{4}$. Choice B uses $\\\\frac{DB}{AD} = \\\\frac{6}{4}$; $DB$ is not a side of triangle $ABC$. Choice D adds $AB = 10$ to $DE$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$\\\\overline{DE} \\\\parallel \\\\overline{BC}$ bo‘lgani uchun $ADE$ uchburchak $ABC$ uchburchakka o‘xshash. Nisbat $\\\\frac{AB}{AD} = \\\\frac{4 + 6}{4} = \\\\frac{5}{2}$, demak $BC = \\\\frac{5}{2} \\\\cdot 5 = 12.5$. To‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA javobda $\\\\frac{10}{4}$ o‘rniga $\\\\frac{4}{10}$ ga ko‘paytirilgan. B javobda $\\\\frac{DB}{AD} = \\\\frac{6}{4}$ ishlatilgan; $DB$ $ABC$ uchburchakning tomoni emas. D javobda $DE$ ga $AB = 10$ qo‘shilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"parallel_segment_similarity\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"lines_angles_triangles\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"Parallel lines $\\\\ell$ and $m$ are intersected by transversal $t$. Two angles lie between $\\\\ell$ and $m$ on the same side of $t$, one at each line, and they measure $(4x + 10)^\\\\circ$ and $(2x + 20)^\\\\circ$. What is the measure, in degrees, of the larger of these two angles?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"110\"],\"explanation_en\":\"These are same-side interior angles, so they are supplementary: $(4x + 10) + (2x + 20) = 180$. Then $6x = 150$ and $x = 25$. The angles measure $4(25) + 10 = 110$ and $2(25) + 20 = 70$ degrees, so the larger is 110. If you set the angles equal, you get $x = 5$ and two $30^\\\\circ$ angles, which do not add to $180^\\\\circ$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bular bir tomonli ichki burchaklar, demak yig‘indisi $180^\\\\circ$: $(4x + 10) + (2x + 20) = 180$. U holda $6x = 150$ va $x = 25$. Burchaklar $4(25) + 10 = 110$ va $2(25) + 20 = 70$ gradus, kattasi 110. Agar burchaklarni tenglasangiz, $x = 5$ va ikkita $30^\\\\circ$ li burchak chiqadi, ularning yig‘indisi $180^\\\\circ$ emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"parallel_lines_algebra\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"lines_angles_triangles\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In triangle $ABC$, the measure of angle $A$ is $40^\\\\circ$ and the measure of angle $B$ is $75^\\\\circ$. In triangle $DEF$, the measure of angle $D$ is $40^\\\\circ$ and the measure of angle $F$ is $65^\\\\circ$. Which of the following statements is true?\",\"option_a\":\"Triangle $ABC$ is similar to triangle $DEF$.\",\"option_b\":\"Triangle $ABC$ is similar to triangle $DFE$.\",\"option_c\":\"Triangle $ABC$ is similar to triangle $FED$.\",\"option_d\":\"The two triangles are not similar.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The third angles are $C = 180^\\\\circ - 40^\\\\circ - 75^\\\\circ = 65^\\\\circ$ and $E = 180^\\\\circ - 40^\\\\circ - 65^\\\\circ = 75^\\\\circ$. So $A = D = 40^\\\\circ$, $B = E = 75^\\\\circ$, and $C = F = 65^\\\\circ$, and the triangles are similar by angle-angle with $A \\\\leftrightarrow D$, $B \\\\leftrightarrow E$, $C \\\\leftrightarrow F$. Choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoice B pairs $B$ ($75^\\\\circ$) with $F$ ($65^\\\\circ$). Choice C pairs $A$ ($40^\\\\circ$) with $F$ ($65^\\\\circ$). Choice D is wrong because two pairs of equal angles are enough to prove similarity.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Uchinchi burchaklar: $C = 180^\\\\circ - 40^\\\\circ - 75^\\\\circ = 65^\\\\circ$ va $E = 180^\\\\circ - 40^\\\\circ - 65^\\\\circ = 75^\\\\circ$. Demak $A = D = 40^\\\\circ$, $B = E = 75^\\\\circ$, $C = F = 65^\\\\circ$, va uchburchaklar ikki burchak bo‘yicha $A \\\\leftrightarrow D$, $B \\\\leftrightarrow E$, $C \\\\leftrightarrow F$ moslikda o‘xshash. To‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\nB javob $B$ ($75^\\\\circ$) ni $F$ ($65^\\\\circ$) bilan juftlaydi. C javob $A$ ($40^\\\\circ$) ni $F$ ($65^\\\\circ$) bilan juftlaydi. D noto‘g‘ri, chunki o‘xshashlikni isbotlash uchun ikki juft teng burchak yetarli.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"similarity_criteria\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"lines_angles_triangles\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Two parallel lines are intersected by a transversal, forming eight angles. One of these angles measures $(2k + 30)^\\\\circ$, and another measures $(6k - 10)^\\\\circ$. These two angles are not congruent. What is the value of $k$?\",\"option_a\":\"5\",\"option_b\":\"10\",\"option_c\":\"20\",\"option_d\":\"25\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"When two parallel lines are cut by a transversal, any two of the eight angles are either congruent or supplementary. Since these two angles are not congruent, they are supplementary: $(2k + 30) + (6k - 10) = 180$, so $8k = 160$ and $k = 20$. The angles are $70^\\\\circ$ and $110^\\\\circ$. Choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice B comes from setting the angles equal, which gives two $50^\\\\circ$ angles; that contradicts the statement that they are not congruent. Choices A and D give angle pairs ($40^\\\\circ$ and $20^\\\\circ$; $80^\\\\circ$ and $140^\\\\circ$) that are neither congruent nor supplementary.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikki parallel to‘g‘ri chiziq kesuvchi bilan kesilganda sakkiz burchakdan istalgan ikkitasi yo teng, yo yig‘indisi $180^\\\\circ$ bo‘ladi. Bu ikki burchak teng emas, demak $(2k + 30) + (6k - 10) = 180$, ya’ni $8k = 160$ va $k = 20$. Burchaklar $70^\\\\circ$ va $110^\\\\circ$. To‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nB javob burchaklarni tenglashdan kelib chiqadi va ikkita $50^\\\\circ$ li burchak beradi; bu ular teng emasligiga zid. A va D javoblar na teng, na yig‘indisi $180^\\\\circ$ bo‘lgan burchak juftlarini beradi ($40^\\\\circ$ va $20^\\\\circ$; $80^\\\\circ$ va $140^\\\\circ$).\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"parallel_lines_algebra\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"lines_angles_triangles\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A triangle has side lengths of 5 centimeters, 12 centimeters, and 13 centimeters. The triangle is dilated by a scale factor greater than 1 to form a new triangle with an area of 270 square centimeters. What is the perimeter, in centimeters, of the new triangle?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"90\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Since $5^2 + 12^2 = 13^2$, the original triangle is a right triangle with legs 5 and 12, so its area is $\\\\frac{1}{2}(5)(12) = 30$. Areas scale by the square of the scale factor $k$: $30k^2 = 270$, so $k^2 = 9$ and $k = 3$. The original perimeter is $5 + 12 + 13 = 30$, so the new perimeter is $3 \\\\cdot 30 = 90$ centimeters. If you got 270, you multiplied the perimeter by $k^2 = 9$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$5^2 + 12^2 = 13^2$ bo‘lgani uchun dastlabki uchburchak katetlari 5 va 12 bo‘lgan to‘g‘ri burchakli uchburchak, yuzi $\\\\frac{1}{2}(5)(12) = 30$. Yuzalar koeffitsiyent $k$ ning kvadratiga proporsional: $30k^2 = 270$, demak $k^2 = 9$ va $k = 3$. Dastlabki perimetr $5 + 12 + 13 = 30$, yangi perimetr $3 \\\\cdot 30 = 90$ santimetr. Agar 270 chiqqan bo‘lsa, siz perimetrni $k^2 = 9$ ga ko‘paytirgansiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"dilation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"lines_angles_triangles\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In triangle $PQR$, side $\\\\overline{PR}$ is extended beyond $R$ to point $S$. The measure of angle $QRS$ is $(5x)^\\\\circ$, the measure of angle $P$ is $(2x + 12)^\\\\circ$, and the measure of angle $Q$ is $(x + 30)^\\\\circ$. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle $PRQ$?\",\"option_a\":\"$21^\\\\circ$\",\"option_b\":\"$54^\\\\circ$\",\"option_c\":\"$75^\\\\circ$\",\"option_d\":\"$105^\\\\circ$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Angle $QRS$ is an exterior angle of the triangle, so it equals the sum of the two remote interior angles: $5x = (2x + 12) + (x + 30)$, which gives $2x = 42$ and $x = 21$. Then angle $QRS = 105^\\\\circ$, and angle $PRQ = 180^\\\\circ - 105^\\\\circ = 75^\\\\circ$. Choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A is the value of $x$. Choice B is angle $P$. Choice D is the exterior angle $QRS$, not the interior angle $PRQ$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$QRS$ — uchburchakning tashqi burchagi, shuning uchun u o‘ziga qo‘shni bo‘lmagan ikki ichki burchak yig‘indisiga teng: $5x = (2x + 12) + (x + 30)$, bundan $2x = 42$ va $x = 21$. U holda $QRS = 105^\\\\circ$ va $PRQ = 180^\\\\circ - 105^\\\\circ = 75^\\\\circ$. To‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA — $x$ ning qiymati. B — $P$ burchak. D — ichki $PRQ$ burchak emas, tashqi $QRS$ burchak.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"exterior_angle\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"lines_angles_triangles\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"In triangle $ABC$, $AB = 9$, $AC = 15$, and $BC = 16$. The bisector of angle $A$ intersects $\\\\overline{BC}$ at point $D$. What is the length of $\\\\overline{CD}$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"10\"],\"explanation_en\":\"By the angle bisector theorem, the bisector divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent sides: $\\\\frac{BD}{CD} = \\\\frac{AB}{AC} = \\\\frac{9}{15} = \\\\frac{3}{5}$. So $CD = \\\\frac{5}{8} \\\\cdot 16 = 10$ (and $BD = 6$). If you got 6, you found $BD$; if you got 8, you assumed $D$ is the midpoint of $\\\\overline{BC}$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bissektrisa teoremasiga ko‘ra bissektrisa qarama-qarshi tomonni yon tomonlar nisbatida bo‘ladi: $\\\\frac{BD}{CD} = \\\\frac{AB}{AC} = \\\\frac{9}{15} = \\\\frac{3}{5}$. Demak $CD = \\\\frac{5}{8} \\\\cdot 16 = 10$ (va $BD = 6$). Agar 6 chiqqan bo‘lsa, siz $BD$ ni topgansiz; 8 chiqqan bo‘lsa, $D$ ni $\\\\overline{BC}$ ning o‘rtasi deb olgansiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"angle_bisector\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"lines_angles_triangles\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In triangle $ABC$, $AB = AC$, and the measure of angle $A$ is $40^\\\\circ$. Point $D$ lies on $\\\\overline{AC}$, between $A$ and $C$, so that $BD = BC$. What is the measure of angle $ABD$?\",\"option_a\":\"$30^\\\\circ$\",\"option_b\":\"$40^\\\\circ$\",\"option_c\":\"$70^\\\\circ$\",\"option_d\":\"$110^\\\\circ$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Since $AB = AC$, angles $B$ and $C$ are equal: each is $\\\\frac{180^\\\\circ - 40^\\\\circ}{2} = 70^\\\\circ$. In triangle $BDC$, $BD = BC$, so angle $BDC$ equals angle $BCD = 70^\\\\circ$, and angle $DBC = 180^\\\\circ - 70^\\\\circ - 70^\\\\circ = 40^\\\\circ$. Then angle $ABD = 70^\\\\circ - 40^\\\\circ = 30^\\\\circ$. Choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoice B is angle $DBC$. Choice C is all of angle $ABC$. Choice D is angle $ADB$, which is $180^\\\\circ - 70^\\\\circ$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$AB = AC$ bo‘lgani uchun $B$ va $C$ burchaklar teng: har biri $\\\\frac{180^\\\\circ - 40^\\\\circ}{2} = 70^\\\\circ$. $BDC$ uchburchakda $BD = BC$, demak $BDC$ burchak $BCD = 70^\\\\circ$ ga teng va $DBC = 180^\\\\circ - 70^\\\\circ - 70^\\\\circ = 40^\\\\circ$. U holda $ABD = 70^\\\\circ - 40^\\\\circ = 30^\\\\circ$. To‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\nB — $DBC$ burchak. C — butun $ABC$ burchak. D — $ADB$ burchak, ya’ni $180^\\\\circ - 70^\\\\circ$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"isosceles_multistep\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"lines_angles_triangles\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"In triangle $ABC$, point $D$ lies on $\\\\overline{AB}$ and point $E$ lies on $\\\\overline{AC}$ so that $\\\\overline{DE}$ is parallel to $\\\\overline{BC}$. The area of triangle $ADE$ is 18 square units, and the area of quadrilateral $DBCE$ is 32 square units. If $AB = 20$, what is the length of $\\\\overline{DB}$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"8\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Triangle $ADE$ is similar to triangle $ABC$, whose area is $18 + 32 = 50$. The ratio of the areas is $\\\\frac{18}{50} = \\\\frac{9}{25}$, so the ratio of corresponding sides is $\\\\sqrt{\\\\frac{9}{25}} = \\\\frac{3}{5}$. Then $AD = \\\\frac{3}{5} \\\\cdot 20 = 12$ and $DB = 20 - 12 = 8$. If you used $\\\\frac{18}{32}$, you compared the triangle with the quadrilateral instead of with the whole triangle.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$ADE$ uchburchak yuzi $18 + 32 = 50$ bo‘lgan $ABC$ uchburchakka o‘xshash. Yuzalar nisbati $\\\\frac{18}{50} = \\\\frac{9}{25}$, demak mos tomonlar nisbati $\\\\sqrt{\\\\frac{9}{25}} = \\\\frac{3}{5}$. U holda $AD = \\\\frac{3}{5} \\\\cdot 20 = 12$ va $DB = 20 - 12 = 8$. Agar $\\\\frac{18}{32}$ ni olgan bo‘lsangiz, uchburchakni butun uchburchak bilan emas, to‘rtburchak bilan solishtirgansiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"similar_area_ratio\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"lines_angles_triangles\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In triangle $ABC$, the measure of angle $B$ is $20^\\\\circ$ greater than the measure of angle $A$, and the measure of angle $C$ is greater than $70^\\\\circ$. Which of the following could be the measure of angle $A$?\",\"option_a\":\"$44^\\\\circ$\",\"option_b\":\"$45^\\\\circ$\",\"option_c\":\"$52^\\\\circ$\",\"option_d\":\"$60^\\\\circ$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"If angle $A$ is $a^\\\\circ$, then angle $B$ is $(a + 20)^\\\\circ$ and angle $C = 180 - a - (a + 20) = 160 - 2a$ degrees. The condition $160 - 2a > 70$ gives $a < 45$. Only $44^\\\\circ$ satisfies this (then $B = 64^\\\\circ$ and $C = 72^\\\\circ$). Choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoice B gives $C = 70^\\\\circ$ exactly, which is not greater than $70^\\\\circ$. Choices C and D give $C = 56^\\\\circ$ and $C = 40^\\\\circ$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$A$ burchak $a^\\\\circ$ bo‘lsa, $B$ burchak $(a + 20)^\\\\circ$ va $C = 180 - a - (a + 20) = 160 - 2a$ gradus. $160 - 2a > 70$ shartdan $a < 45$. Faqat $44^\\\\circ$ bu shartni qanoatlantiradi (u holda $B = 64^\\\\circ$, $C = 72^\\\\circ$). To‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\nB javobda $C$ aynan $70^\\\\circ$ bo‘ladi, bu $70^\\\\circ$ dan katta emas. C va D javoblarda $C = 56^\\\\circ$ va $C = 40^\\\\circ$ chiqadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"angle_inequality\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_equations_systems\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"What are all the solutions to the equation $2x^2 = 72$?\",\"option_a\":\"$6$\",\"option_b\":\"$-6$ and $6$\",\"option_c\":\"$36$\",\"option_d\":\"$-36$ and $36$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Divide both sides by $2$: $x^2 = 36$. Both $6^2$ and $(-6)^2$ equal $36$, so the solutions are $x = -6$ and $x = 6$.\\n\\nChoice A misses the negative solution. Choices C and D stop at $x^2 = 36$ and give the value of $x^2$, not $x$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkala tomonni $2$ ga bo‘lamiz: $x^2 = 36$. $6^2$ ham, $(-6)^2$ ham $36$ ga teng, demak yechimlar $x = -6$ va $x = 6$.\\n\\nA variant manfiy yechimni tushirib qoldiradi. C va D variantlar $x^2 = 36$ da to‘xtab, $x$ ning emas, $x^2$ ning qiymatini beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"solve_quadratic\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_equations_systems\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A rectangular vegetable garden has a length that is $4$ meters greater than its width. The area of the garden is $96$ square meters. What is the width, in meters, of the garden?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"8\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Let $w$ be the width. Then the length is $w + 4$ and $w(w + 4) = 96$, so $w^2 + 4w - 96 = 0$, which factors as $(w + 12)(w - 8) = 0$. The width must be positive, so $w = 8$.\\n\\nThe solution $w = -12$ is rejected because a length cannot be negative. The answer $12$ is the length, not the width.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Eni $w$ bo‘lsin. U holda bo‘yi $w + 4$ va $w(w + 4) = 96$, ya’ni $w^2 + 4w - 96 = 0$, bu $(w + 12)(w - 8) = 0$ ko‘rinishda ko‘paytuvchilarga ajraladi. Eni musbat bo‘lishi kerak, demak $w = 8$.\\n\\n$w = -12$ yechimi rad etiladi, chunki uzunlik manfiy bo‘lmaydi. $12$ javobi bo‘yi, eni emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"quadratic_word_problem\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_equations_systems\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"What is the solution to the equation $\\\\sqrt{x + 7} = 5$?\",\"option_a\":\"$-2$\",\"option_b\":\"$12$\",\"option_c\":\"$18$\",\"option_d\":\"$32$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Square both sides: $x + 7 = 25$, so $x = 18$. Check: $\\\\sqrt{18 + 7} = \\\\sqrt{25} = 5$.\\n\\nChoice A subtracts $7$ from $5$ without squaring. Choice B adds $7$ to $5$ instead of squaring. Choice D adds $7$ to $25$ instead of subtracting it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkala tomonni kvadratga ko‘taramiz: $x + 7 = 25$, demak $x = 18$. Tekshiruv: $\\\\sqrt{18 + 7} = \\\\sqrt{25} = 5$.\\n\\nA variant kvadratga ko‘tarmasdan $5$ dan $7$ ni ayiradi. B variant kvadratga ko‘tarish o‘rniga $5$ ga $7$ ni qo‘shadi. D variant $25$ dan $7$ ni ayirish o‘rniga qo‘shadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"radical_equation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_equations_systems\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"How many distinct real solutions does the equation $x^2 + 4x + 7 = 0$ have?\",\"option_a\":\"Exactly one\",\"option_b\":\"Exactly two\",\"option_c\":\"Infinitely many\",\"option_d\":\"Zero\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The discriminant is $b^2 - 4ac = 4^2 - 4(1)(7) = 16 - 28 = -12$. A negative discriminant means the equation has no real solutions.\\n\\nChoice A would need a discriminant of $0$, and choice B a positive discriminant. Choice C is not possible for a quadratic equation, which has at most two solutions.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Diskriminant $b^2 - 4ac = 4^2 - 4(1)(7) = 16 - 28 = -12$. Diskriminant manfiy bo‘lsa, tenglama haqiqiy yechimga ega emas.\\n\\nA variant uchun diskriminant $0$, B variant uchun esa musbat bo‘lishi kerak. C variant kvadrat tenglama uchun mumkin emas, u ko‘pi bilan ikkita yechimga ega.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"discriminant_count\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_equations_systems\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"$$|x - 4| = 9$$ What is the positive solution to the given equation?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"13\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The expression inside the absolute value is either $9$ or $-9$. If $x - 4 = 9$, then $x = 13$. If $x - 4 = -9$, then $x = -5$. The positive solution is $13$.\\n\\nAnswering $5$ comes from $x = 9 - 4$, which ignores how the absolute value works.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Modul ichidagi ifoda yo $9$ ga, yo $-9$ ga teng. Agar $x - 4 = 9$ bo‘lsa, $x = 13$. Agar $x - 4 = -9$ bo‘lsa, $x = -5$. Musbat yechim $13$.\\n\\n$5$ javobi $x = 9 - 4$ dan chiqadi, bu modulning ma’nosini hisobga olmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"absolute_value_equation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_equations_systems\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\begin{aligned} y &= x^2 + 2x \\\\\\\\ y &= 8 \\\\end{aligned}$$ Which ordered pair $(x, y)$ is a solution to the given system of equations?\",\"option_a\":\"$(2, 8)$\",\"option_b\":\"$(8, 2)$\",\"option_c\":\"$(4, 8)$\",\"option_d\":\"$(-2, 8)$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Substitute $y = 8$: $x^2 + 2x = 8$, so $x^2 + 2x - 8 = 0$ and $(x + 4)(x - 2) = 0$. So $x = 2$ or $x = -4$, and the solutions are $(2, 8)$ and $(-4, 8)$. Only $(2, 8)$ is listed.\\n\\nChoice B swaps the coordinates. Choice C gives $4^2 + 2(4) = 24 \\\\ne 8$. Choice D uses the solution $x = -4$ with the wrong sign: $(-2)^2 + 2(-2) = 0 \\\\ne 8$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$y = 8$ ni qo‘yamiz: $x^2 + 2x = 8$, ya’ni $x^2 + 2x - 8 = 0$ va $(x + 4)(x - 2) = 0$. Demak $x = 2$ yoki $x = -4$, yechimlar $(2, 8)$ va $(-4, 8)$. Variantlarda faqat $(2, 8)$ bor.\\n\\nB variant koordinatalarni almashtiradi. C variantda $4^2 + 2(4) = 24 \\\\ne 8$. D variant $x = -4$ yechimini teskari ishora bilan oladi: $(-2)^2 + 2(-2) = 0 \\\\ne 8$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"line_quadratic_system\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_equations_systems\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"$$2x^2 + bx + 18 = 0$$ In the given equation, $b$ is a positive constant. The equation has exactly one real solution. What is the value of $b$?\",\"option_a\":\"$6$\",\"option_b\":\"$12$\",\"option_c\":\"$36$\",\"option_d\":\"$144$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"A quadratic equation has exactly one real solution when its discriminant is $0$: $b^2 - 4(2)(18) = 0$, so $b^2 = 144$. Since $b$ is positive, $b = 12$.\\n\\nChoice A comes from $b^2 = 2 \\\\cdot 18 = 36$, leaving out the factor $4$ in $4ac$. Choice C is $ac = 2 \\\\cdot 18$. Choice D is the value of $b^2$, not $b$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Kvadrat tenglama diskriminanti $0$ bo‘lganda aynan bitta haqiqiy yechimga ega: $b^2 - 4(2)(18) = 0$, ya’ni $b^2 = 144$. $b$ musbat bo‘lgani uchun $b = 12$.\\n\\nA variant $b^2 = 2 \\\\cdot 18 = 36$ dan chiqadi, $4ac$ dagi $4$ ko‘paytuvchi unutilgan. C variant $ac = 2 \\\\cdot 18$ ning o‘zi. D variant $b$ ning emas, $b^2$ ning qiymati.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"discriminant_parameter\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_equations_systems\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\begin{aligned} x^2 + y^2 &= 25 \\\\\\\\ y &= x + 1 \\\\end{aligned}$$ The solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$, where $x > 0$. What is the value of $xy$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"12\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Substitute $y = x + 1$: $x^2 + (x + 1)^2 = 25$, so $2x^2 + 2x - 24 = 0$, or $x^2 + x - 12 = 0$. This factors as $(x + 4)(x - 3) = 0$, so $x = 3$ or $x = -4$.\\n\\nSince $x > 0$, $x = 3$ and $y = 4$, so $xy = 12$. (The other solution, $(-4, -3)$, also gives $xy = 12$, but it does not satisfy $x > 0$.)\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$y = x + 1$ ni qo‘yamiz: $x^2 + (x + 1)^2 = 25$, ya’ni $2x^2 + 2x - 24 = 0$ yoki $x^2 + x - 12 = 0$. Bu $(x + 4)(x - 3) = 0$ ko‘rinishda ajraladi, demak $x = 3$ yoki $x = -4$.\\n\\n$x > 0$ bo‘lgani uchun $x = 3$ va $y = 4$, demak $xy = 12$. (Ikkinchi yechim $(-4, -3)$ ham $xy = 12$ beradi, lekin u $x > 0$ shartini qanoatlantirmaydi.)\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"line_circle_system\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_equations_systems\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"What are all the solutions to the equation $\\\\sqrt{2x + 9} = x - 3$?\",\"option_a\":\"$0$ and $8$\",\"option_b\":\"$8$ only\",\"option_c\":\"$0$ only\",\"option_d\":\"There are no solutions.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Square both sides: $2x + 9 = x^2 - 6x + 9$, so $x^2 - 8x = 0$ and $x(x - 8) = 0$. The candidates are $x = 0$ and $x = 8$.\\n\\nCheck each one. For $x = 8$: $\\\\sqrt{25} = 5$ and $8 - 3 = 5$, so it works. For $x = 0$: $\\\\sqrt{9} = 3$ but $0 - 3 = -3$, so $x = 0$ is extraneous (a square root is never negative). The only solution is $8$.\\n\\nChoice A keeps the extraneous root. Choice C keeps only the extraneous root. Choice D rejects a valid solution.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkala tomonni kvadratga ko‘taramiz: $2x + 9 = x^2 - 6x + 9$, ya’ni $x^2 - 8x = 0$ va $x(x - 8) = 0$. Nomzodlar: $x = 0$ va $x = 8$.\\n\\nHar birini tekshiramiz. $x = 8$ da: $\\\\sqrt{25} = 5$ va $8 - 3 = 5$, mos keladi. $x = 0$ da: $\\\\sqrt{9} = 3$, lekin $0 - 3 = -3$, demak $x = 0$ — begona ildiz (kvadrat ildiz manfiy bo‘lmaydi). Yagona yechim $8$.\\n\\nA variant begona ildizni ham qoldiradi. C variant faqat begona ildizni qoldiradi. D variant to‘g‘ri yechimni ham rad etadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"radical_extraneous\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_equations_systems\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"What is the sum of the solutions to the equation $(x - 4)(2x + 3) = 5x$?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\dfrac{5}{2}$\",\"option_b\":\"$5$\",\"option_c\":\"$-6$\",\"option_d\":\"$10$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Expand and move all terms to one side: $2x^2 + 3x - 8x - 12 = 5x$, so $2x^2 - 10x - 12 = 0$, or $x^2 - 5x - 6 = 0$. This factors as $(x - 6)(x + 1) = 0$, so the solutions are $6$ and $-1$, and their sum is $5$. (For $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$ the sum is $-\\\\frac{b}{a} = \\\\frac{10}{2} = 5$.)\\n\\nChoice A sums the zeros of the left side, $4$ and $-\\\\frac{3}{2}$, as if the right side were $0$. Choice C is the product of the solutions. Choice D uses $-b$ without dividing by $a = 2$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qavslarni ochib, barcha hadlarni bir tomonga o‘tkazamiz: $2x^2 + 3x - 8x - 12 = 5x$, ya’ni $2x^2 - 10x - 12 = 0$ yoki $x^2 - 5x - 6 = 0$. Bu $(x - 6)(x + 1) = 0$ ko‘rinishda ajraladi, yechimlar $6$ va $-1$, yig‘indisi $5$. ($ax^2 + bx + c = 0$ uchun yig‘indi $-\\\\frac{b}{a} = \\\\frac{10}{2} = 5$.)\\n\\nA variant chap tomonning nollari $4$ va $-\\\\frac{3}{2}$ ni qo‘shadi, go‘yo o‘ng tomon $0$ bo‘lgandek. C variant yechimlar ko‘paytmasi. D variant $-b$ ni $a = 2$ ga bo‘lmasdan oladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"sum_of_solutions\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_equations_systems\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The product of two consecutive positive even integers is $168$. What is the greater of the two integers?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"14\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Let the integers be $n$ and $n + 2$. Then $n(n + 2) = 168$, so $n^2 + 2n - 168 = 0$, which factors as $(n + 14)(n - 12) = 0$. Since $n$ is positive, $n = 12$, and the greater integer is $n + 2 = 14$.\\n\\nAnswering $12$ gives the smaller integer. The root $n = -14$ is rejected because the integers are positive.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Sonlar $n$ va $n + 2$ bo‘lsin. U holda $n(n + 2) = 168$, ya’ni $n^2 + 2n - 168 = 0$, bu $(n + 14)(n - 12) = 0$ ko‘rinishda ajraladi. $n$ musbat, demak $n = 12$ va kattasi $n + 2 = 14$.\\n\\n$12$ javobi kichik sonni beradi. $n = -14$ ildizi rad etiladi, chunki sonlar musbat.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"quadratic_word_problem\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_equations_systems\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\frac{x}{x - 3} = \\\\frac{3}{x - 3} + 4$$ Which of the following is true about the given equation?\",\"option_a\":\"Its only solution is $x = 3$.\",\"option_b\":\"Its only solution is $x = -3$.\",\"option_c\":\"Its only solution is $x = 4$.\",\"option_d\":\"It has no solution.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Multiply both sides by $x - 3$: $x = 3 + 4(x - 3)$, so $x = 4x - 9$ and $x = 3$. But $x = 3$ makes the denominators $0$, so it is not allowed. The equation has no solution.\\n\\nAnother way to see it: for $x \\\\ne 3$, the left side minus the first term on the right is $\\\\frac{x - 3}{x - 3} = 1$, and $1 \\\\ne 4$.\\n\\nChoice A keeps the excluded value. Choices B and C come from algebra errors; for example, $x = 4$ gives $4 = 3 + 4 = 7$, which is false.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkala tomonni $x - 3$ ga ko‘paytiramiz: $x = 3 + 4(x - 3)$, ya’ni $x = 4x - 9$ va $x = 3$. Lekin $x = 3$ da maxrajlar $0$ bo‘ladi, shuning uchun bu qiymat mumkin emas. Tenglama yechimga ega emas.\\n\\nBoshqa yo‘l: $x \\\\ne 3$ uchun chap tomondan o‘ngdagi birinchi kasrni ayirsak, $\\\\frac{x - 3}{x - 3} = 1$ chiqadi, va $1 \\\\ne 4$.\\n\\nA variant taqiqlangan qiymatni qoldiradi. B va C variantlar hisob xatolaridan chiqadi; masalan, $x = 4$ da $4 = 3 + 4 = 7$, bu noto‘g‘ri.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"rational_extraneous\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_equations_systems\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"$$x^2 + bx + 20 = 0$$ In the given equation, $b$ is a positive integer. The equation has no real solution. What is the greatest possible value of $b$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"8\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The equation has no real solution when the discriminant is negative: $b^2 - 4(1)(20) < 0$, so $b^2 < 80$. Since $\\\\sqrt{80} \\\\approx 8.94$, the positive integers that work are $1$ through $8$. The greatest is $8$.\\n\\n$b = 9$ fails because $81 - 80 = 1 > 0$, which gives two real solutions. Answering $80$ gives the bound on $b^2$, not on $b$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Diskriminant manfiy bo‘lganda tenglama haqiqiy yechimga ega emas: $b^2 - 4(1)(20) < 0$, ya’ni $b^2 < 80$. $\\\\sqrt{80} \\\\approx 8.94$ bo‘lgani uchun mos musbat butun sonlar $1$ dan $8$ gacha. Eng kattasi $8$.\\n\\n$b = 9$ to‘g‘ri kelmaydi, chunki $81 - 80 = 1 > 0$ va ikkita haqiqiy yechim chiqadi. $80$ javobi $b$ uchun emas, $b^2$ uchun chegarani beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"discriminant_integer\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_equations_systems\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\begin{aligned} y &= 3x^2 - 7 \\\\\\\\ y &= mx - 10 \\\\end{aligned}$$ In the given system of equations, $m$ is a positive constant. The system has exactly one solution $(x, y)$. What is the value of $x$?\",\"option_a\":\"$-1$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\dfrac{1}{6}$\",\"option_c\":\"$1$\",\"option_d\":\"$6$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Set the expressions equal: $3x^2 - 7 = mx - 10$, so $3x^2 - mx + 3 = 0$. Exactly one solution means the discriminant is $0$: $m^2 - 4(3)(3) = 0$, so $m^2 = 36$ and, since $m > 0$, $m = 6$.\\n\\nThen $3x^2 - 6x + 3 = 0$, or $3(x - 1)^2 = 0$, so $x = 1$ (and $y = -4$).\\n\\nChoice A is the $x$-value for $m = -6$, which is not allowed. Choice B divides $1$ by $m$ instead of computing $\\\\frac{m}{2a} = \\\\frac{6}{6}$. Choice D is the value of $m$, not $x$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ifodalarni tenglashtiramiz: $3x^2 - 7 = mx - 10$, ya’ni $3x^2 - mx + 3 = 0$. Aynan bitta yechim — diskriminant $0$: $m^2 - 4(3)(3) = 0$, demak $m^2 = 36$ va $m > 0$ bo‘lgani uchun $m = 6$.\\n\\nU holda $3x^2 - 6x + 3 = 0$, ya’ni $3(x - 1)^2 = 0$, demak $x = 1$ (va $y = -4$).\\n\\nA variant $m = -6$ uchun $x$ qiymati, bunga ruxsat yo‘q. B variant $\\\\frac{m}{2a} = \\\\frac{6}{6}$ ni hisoblash o‘rniga $1$ ni $m$ ga bo‘ladi. D variant $x$ ning emas, $m$ ning qiymati.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"tangent_line_parameter\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_equations_systems\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"$$2x^2 - 16x + k = 0$$ In the given equation, $k$ is a constant. The equation has two real solutions, and one solution is $3$ times the other. What is the value of $k$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"24\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Let the solutions be $r$ and $3r$. Dividing the equation by $2$ gives $x^2 - 8x + \\\\frac{k}{2} = 0$, so the sum of the solutions is $8$ and their product is $\\\\frac{k}{2}$.\\n\\nFrom the sum, $r + 3r = 8$, so $r = 2$ and the solutions are $2$ and $6$. From the product, $2 \\\\cdot 6 = \\\\frac{k}{2}$, so $k = 24$. Check: $2x^2 - 16x + 24 = 2(x - 2)(x - 6)$.\\n\\nAnswering $12$ forgets that the product of the solutions is $\\\\frac{k}{2}$, not $k$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Yechimlar $r$ va $3r$ bo‘lsin. Tenglamani $2$ ga bo‘lsak, $x^2 - 8x + \\\\frac{k}{2} = 0$, demak yechimlar yig‘indisi $8$, ko‘paytmasi esa $\\\\frac{k}{2}$.\\n\\nYig‘indidan $r + 3r = 8$, ya’ni $r = 2$, yechimlar $2$ va $6$. Ko‘paytmadan $2 \\\\cdot 6 = \\\\frac{k}{2}$, demak $k = 24$. Tekshiruv: $2x^2 - 16x + 24 = 2(x - 2)(x - 6)$.\\n\\n$12$ javobi yechimlar ko‘paytmasi $k$ emas, $\\\\frac{k}{2}$ ekanini unutadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"vieta_condition\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_equations_systems\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\begin{aligned} x^2 + y^2 &= 20 \\\\\\\\ y &= 2x + b \\\\end{aligned}$$ In the given system of equations, $b$ is a positive constant. The system has exactly one real solution. What is the value of $b$?\",\"option_a\":\"$5$\",\"option_b\":\"$10$\",\"option_c\":\"$20$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\sqrt{20}$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Substitute $y = 2x + b$: $x^2 + (2x + b)^2 = 20$, so $5x^2 + 4bx + b^2 - 20 = 0$. There is exactly one solution when the discriminant is $0$: $(4b)^2 - 4(5)(b^2 - 20) = 0$, so $16b^2 - 20b^2 + 400 = 0$, giving $b^2 = 100$ and $b = 10$.\\n\\nGeometrically, the line is tangent to the circle of radius $\\\\sqrt{20}$ centered at the origin. Choice D is the radius, which would be correct only for a horizontal line $y = b$. Choices A and C give discriminants that are not $0$: for $b = 5$ the line crosses the circle twice, and for $b = 20$ it misses the circle.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$y = 2x + b$ ni qo‘yamiz: $x^2 + (2x + b)^2 = 20$, ya’ni $5x^2 + 4bx + b^2 - 20 = 0$. Diskriminant $0$ bo‘lganda aynan bitta yechim bor: $(4b)^2 - 4(5)(b^2 - 20) = 0$, ya’ni $16b^2 - 20b^2 + 400 = 0$, bundan $b^2 = 100$ va $b = 10$.\\n\\nGeometrik ma’nosi: to‘g‘ri chiziq markazi koordinatalar boshida, radiusi $\\\\sqrt{20}$ bo‘lgan aylanaga urinadi. D variant radius; u faqat gorizontal $y = b$ chiziq uchun to‘g‘ri bo‘lar edi. A va C variantlarda diskriminant $0$ emas: $b = 5$ da chiziq aylanani ikki nuqtada kesadi, $b = 20$ da esa umuman kesmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"tangent_line_circle\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A new online store tracks the number of weekly visitors to its website. The number of visitors $V$ during week $w$ is modeled by the function $V(w) = 1{,}800(1.12)^w$. Which of the following is the best interpretation of $1.12$ in this context?\",\"option_a\":\"The number of weekly visitors increases by $1.12\\\\%$ each week.\",\"option_b\":\"The number of weekly visitors increases by $12\\\\%$ each week.\",\"option_c\":\"The number of weekly visitors increases by $112\\\\%$ each week.\",\"option_d\":\"The number of weekly visitors increases by $1.12$ visitors each week.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"In an exponential model $V(w) = a(b)^w$, the base $b$ is the growth factor. Here $b = 1.12 = 1 + 0.12$, so each week the number of visitors is multiplied by $1.12$, which is an increase of $12\\\\%$.\\n\\nChoice A treats the factor $1.12$ as the percent itself. Choice C confuses the new amount ($112\\\\%$ of the old amount) with the increase. Choice D describes a linear change of a fixed number of visitors, not a percent change.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$V(w) = a(b)^w$ ko‘rsatkichli modelida $b$ asos — o‘sish ko‘paytuvchisi. Bu yerda $b = 1.12 = 1 + 0.12$, ya’ni har hafta tashrif buyuruvchilar soni $1.12$ ga ko‘paytiriladi, bu $12\\\\%$ o‘sish demakdir.\\n\\nA variant $1.12$ ko‘paytuvchini foizning o‘zi deb oladi. C variant yangi miqdorni (eskisining $112\\\\%$ i) o‘sish bilan adashtiradi. D variant foizli emas, balki har hafta bir xil songa o‘zgaradigan chiziqli o‘sishni tasvirlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"exponential_interpret_base\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = -3(x - 4)^2 + 19$. What is the maximum value of $f(x)$?\",\"option_a\":\"$-3$\",\"option_b\":\"$4$\",\"option_c\":\"$19$\",\"option_d\":\"$-4$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"In vertex form $f(x) = a(x - h)^2 + k$ with $a < 0$, the graph opens downward and the maximum value of $f(x)$ is $k$. Here $a = -3$ and $k = 19$, so the maximum value is $19$, reached when $x = 4$.\\n\\nChoice B is the $x$-value where the maximum occurs, not the maximum value. Choice D has the wrong sign for that $x$-value. Choice A is the leading coefficient $a$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$f(x) = a(x - h)^2 + k$ uch ko‘rinishida $a < 0$ bo‘lsa, parabola pastga ochiladi va $f(x)$ ning eng katta qiymati $k$ ga teng. Bu yerda $a = -3$, $k = 19$, demak eng katta qiymat $19$, u $x = 4$ da erishiladi.\\n\\nB variant eng katta qiymat erishiladigan $x$ qiymati, eng katta qiymatning o‘zi emas. D variantda shu $x$ qiymatining ishorasi noto‘g‘ri. A variant esa bosh koeffitsiyent $a$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"vertex_form_max_min\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = 5(3)^x - 7$. What is the value of $g(2)$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"38\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Substitute $x = 2$: $g(2) = 5(3)^2 - 7 = 5(9) - 7 = 45 - 7 = 38$.\\n\\nA common error is computing $(5 \\\\cdot 3)^2 = 225$ before subtracting, which gives $218$. The exponent applies only to the base $3$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$x = 2$ ni qo‘yamiz: $g(2) = 5(3)^2 - 7 = 5(9) - 7 = 45 - 7 = 38$.\\n\\nKo‘p uchraydigan xato — ayirishdan oldin $(5 \\\\cdot 3)^2 = 225$ ni hisoblash, bu $218$ ni beradi. Daraja faqat $3$ asosiga tegishli.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"evaluate_function\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The function $h$ is defined by $h(x) = (x + 6)(x - 2)(2x - 5)$. In the $xy$-plane, which of the following is an $x$-intercept of the graph of $y = h(x)$?\",\"option_a\":\"$(6, 0)$\",\"option_b\":\"$(-2, 0)$\",\"option_c\":\"$(5, 0)$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{5}{2}, 0\\\\right)$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"An $x$-intercept occurs where $h(x) = 0$, which happens when one of the factors is $0$: $x + 6 = 0$, $x - 2 = 0$, or $2x - 5 = 0$. So the zeros are $x = -6$, $x = 2$, and $x = \\\\frac{5}{2}$, and $\\\\left(\\\\frac{5}{2}, 0\\\\right)$ is an $x$-intercept.\\n\\nChoices A and B use the numbers in the factors with the wrong sign. Choice C uses the constant $5$ in $2x - 5$ without dividing by the coefficient $2$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$x$ o‘qi bilan kesishish nuqtasida $h(x) = 0$ bo‘ladi, bu ko‘paytuvchilardan biri $0$ ga teng bo‘lganda yuz beradi: $x + 6 = 0$, $x - 2 = 0$ yoki $2x - 5 = 0$. Demak, nollar $x = -6$, $x = 2$ va $x = \\\\frac{5}{2}$, va $\\\\left(\\\\frac{5}{2}, 0\\\\right)$ — $x$ o‘qi bilan kesishish nuqtasi.\\n\\nA va B variantlar ko‘paytuvchilardagi sonlarni teskari ishora bilan oladi. C variant $2x - 5$ dagi $5$ ni $2$ koeffitsiyentga bo‘lmasdan oladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"polynomial_zeros_factored\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = 4\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{1}{2}\\\\right)^x + 3$. What is the $y$-intercept of the graph of $y = f(x)$ in the $xy$-plane?\",\"option_a\":\"$(0, 3)$\",\"option_b\":\"$(0, 4)$\",\"option_c\":\"$(0, 5)$\",\"option_d\":\"$(0, 7)$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The $y$-intercept is the point where $x = 0$. Since $\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\right)^0 = 1$, $f(0) = 4(1) + 3 = 7$, so the $y$-intercept is $(0, 7)$.\\n\\nChoice A ignores the exponential term. Choice B ignores the added $3$. Choice C is $f(1) = 4\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\right) + 3 = 5$, the value at $x = 1$, not at $x = 0$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$y$ o‘qi bilan kesishish nuqtasida $x = 0$. $\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\right)^0 = 1$ bo‘lgani uchun $f(0) = 4(1) + 3 = 7$, demak nuqta $(0, 7)$.\\n\\nA variant ko‘rsatkichli hadni hisobga olmaydi. B variant qo‘shilgan $3$ ni unutadi. C variant $f(1) = 4\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\right) + 3 = 5$, ya’ni $x = 1$ dagi qiymat, $x = 0$ dagi emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"exponential_initial_value\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = x^2 - 1$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(x - 3) + 2$. What is the value of $g(5)$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"5\"],\"explanation_en\":\"By the definition of $g$, $g(5) = f(5 - 3) + 2 = f(2) + 2$. Since $f(2) = 2^2 - 1 = 3$, $g(5) = 3 + 2 = 5$.\\n\\nUsing $f(5 + 3) = f(8) = 63$ (shifting the wrong way) gives $65$, and forgetting the $+2$ gives $3$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$g$ ning ta’rifiga ko‘ra $g(5) = f(5 - 3) + 2 = f(2) + 2$. $f(2) = 2^2 - 1 = 3$ bo‘lgani uchun $g(5) = 3 + 2 = 5$.\\n\\n$f(5 + 3) = f(8) = 63$ ni ishlatish (noto‘g‘ri tomonga siljitish) $65$ ni, $+2$ ni unutish esa $3$ ni beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"transformations\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A biologist places $400$ bacteria in a nutrient solution. The number of bacteria doubles every $6$ hours. Which function $B$ gives the number of bacteria $h$ hours after they were placed in the solution?\",\"option_a\":\"$B(h) = 400(2)^{6h}$\",\"option_b\":\"$B(h) = 400(2)^{\\\\frac{h}{6}}$\",\"option_c\":\"$B(h) = 400(6)^{\\\\frac{h}{2}}$\",\"option_d\":\"$B(h) = 800^{\\\\frac{h}{6}}$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The amount is multiplied by $2$ once every $6$ hours, so after $h$ hours it has doubled $\\\\frac{h}{6}$ times: $B(h) = 400(2)^{\\\\frac{h}{6}}$. Check: $B(0) = 400$, $B(6) = 800$, $B(12) = 1{,}600$.\\n\\nChoice A doubles $6$ times every hour ($B(1) = 25{,}600$). Choice C swaps the roles of $2$ and $6$. Choice D gives $B(0) = 1$, not $400$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Miqdor har $6$ soatda bir marta $2$ ga ko‘paytiriladi, demak $h$ soatdan keyin u $\\\\frac{h}{6}$ marta ikki baravar ortgan: $B(h) = 400(2)^{\\\\frac{h}{6}}$. Tekshiruv: $B(0) = 400$, $B(6) = 800$, $B(12) = 1{,}600$.\\n\\nA variant har soatda $6$ marta ikki baravar oshiradi ($B(1) = 25{,}600$). C variant $2$ va $6$ ning rollarini almashtiradi. D variantda $B(0) = 1$, $400$ emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"exponential_doubling_model\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = 3x^2 - 18x + 31$. What is the minimum value of $f(x)$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"4\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The vertex is at $x = -\\\\frac{b}{2a} = -\\\\frac{-18}{2(3)} = 3$. The minimum value is $f(3) = 3(9) - 18(3) + 31 = 27 - 54 + 31 = 4$.\\n\\nEquivalently, completing the square gives $f(x) = 3(x - 3)^2 + 4$. The answer $3$ is the $x$-value where the minimum occurs, not the minimum value.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Parabola uchi $x = -\\\\frac{b}{2a} = -\\\\frac{-18}{2(3)} = 3$ da. Eng kichik qiymat $f(3) = 3(9) - 18(3) + 31 = 27 - 54 + 31 = 4$.\\n\\nTo‘la kvadrat ajratsak ham $f(x) = 3(x - 3)^2 + 4$ chiqadi. $3$ javobi eng kichik qiymat erishiladigan $x$, eng kichik qiymatning o‘zi emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"standard_to_vertex\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The population of a species of fish in a reservoir is modeled by $P(t) = 600(1.21)^{\\\\frac{t}{2}}$, where $t$ is the number of years since the population was first measured. Which of the following best describes how the population changes, according to the model?\",\"option_a\":\"It increases by $21\\\\%$ every year.\",\"option_b\":\"It increases by $10\\\\%$ every year.\",\"option_c\":\"It increases by $10.5\\\\%$ every year.\",\"option_d\":\"It increases by $42\\\\%$ every year.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Rewrite the model with an exponent of $t$: $(1.21)^{\\\\frac{t}{2}} = \\\\left((1.21)^{\\\\frac{1}{2}}\\\\right)^t = (1.1)^t$. So $P(t) = 600(1.1)^t$, and the population is multiplied by $1.1$ each year, an increase of $10\\\\%$ per year.\\n\\nChoice A is the increase every $2$ years, not every year. Choice C divides $21\\\\%$ by $2$, but percent growth compounds, so it cannot be split evenly. Choice D doubles $21\\\\%$ instead of taking a square root.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Modelni daraja $t$ bo‘ladigan qilib yozamiz: $(1.21)^{\\\\frac{t}{2}} = \\\\left((1.21)^{\\\\frac{1}{2}}\\\\right)^t = (1.1)^t$. Demak $P(t) = 600(1.1)^t$, ya’ni populyatsiya har yili $1.1$ ga ko‘paytiriladi — yiliga $10\\\\%$ o‘sish.\\n\\nA variant har $2$ yildagi o‘sish, har yilgi emas. C variant $21\\\\%$ ni $2$ ga bo‘ladi, lekin foizli o‘sish murakkab foiz kabi yig‘iladi, uni teng bo‘lib bo‘lmaydi. D variant kvadrat ildiz olish o‘rniga $21\\\\%$ ni ikki baravar oshiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"exponential_scaled_exponent\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The graph of the function $f$ in the $xy$-plane passes through the point $(2, -5)$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(x + 4) - 1$. Which of the following points must lie on the graph of $y = g(x)$?\",\"option_a\":\"$(6, -6)$\",\"option_b\":\"$(-2, -6)$\",\"option_c\":\"$(-2, -4)$\",\"option_d\":\"$(6, -4)$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"We need an input for $g$ that turns $x + 4$ into $2$: $x + 4 = 2$ gives $x = -2$. Then $g(-2) = f(2) - 1 = -5 - 1 = -6$, so $(-2, -6)$ is on the graph of $g$. The graph of $g$ is the graph of $f$ moved $4$ units left and $1$ unit down.\\n\\nChoices A and D shift $4$ units to the right instead of left. Choices C and D move the graph up $1$ unit instead of down.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$g$ uchun shunday kirish qiymati kerakki, $x + 4$ ifoda $2$ ga aylansin: $x + 4 = 2$ dan $x = -2$. U holda $g(-2) = f(2) - 1 = -5 - 1 = -6$, demak $(-2, -6)$ nuqta $g$ grafigida yotadi. $g$ grafigi — $f$ grafigini $4$ birlik chapga va $1$ birlik pastga surish natijasi.\\n\\nA va D variantlar $4$ birlik chapga emas, o‘ngga suradi. C va D variantlar grafikni $1$ birlik pastga emas, yuqoriga suradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"transformations\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = \\\\dfrac{x^2 + kx - 12}{x + 5}$, where $k$ is a constant. In the $xy$-plane, the graph of $y = f(x)$ has an $x$-intercept at $(2, 0)$. What is the $y$-coordinate of the $y$-intercept of the graph?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"-12/5\",\"-2.4\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Since $f(2) = 0$, the numerator is $0$ at $x = 2$: $4 + 2k - 12 = 0$, so $k = 4$. Then $f(0) = \\\\frac{0 + 0 - 12}{0 + 5} = -\\\\frac{12}{5} = -2.4$.\\n\\nAnswering $-12$ forgets to divide by the denominator, and answering $4$ gives the value of $k$ instead of the $y$-coordinate.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$f(2) = 0$ bo‘lgani uchun $x = 2$ da surat $0$ ga teng: $4 + 2k - 12 = 0$, demak $k = 4$. U holda $f(0) = \\\\frac{0 + 0 - 12}{0 + 5} = -\\\\frac{12}{5} = -2.4$.\\n\\n$-12$ javobi maxrajga bo‘lishni unutadi, $4$ javobi esa $y$ koordinata o‘rniga $k$ ning qiymatini beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"rational_function_parameter\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"For the exponential function $f$, $f(1) = 12$ and $f(3) = 108$. Which equation could define $f$?\",\"option_a\":\"$f(x) = 4(3)^x$\",\"option_b\":\"$f(x) = 12(3)^x$\",\"option_c\":\"$f(x) = 3(4)^x$\",\"option_d\":\"$f(x) = 4(9)^x$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Write $f(x) = a(b)^x$. Dividing $f(3)$ by $f(1)$ gives $b^2 = \\\\frac{108}{12} = 9$, so $b = 3$ (the base of an exponential function is positive). Then $a(3) = 12$, so $a = 4$ and $f(x) = 4(3)^x$. Check: $4(3)^3 = 108$.\\n\\nChoice B uses $f(1)$ as the initial value, but the initial value is $f(0) = 4$. Choice C swaps $a$ and $b$. Choice D uses the factor for a $2$-unit change, $9$, as the factor for a $1$-unit change.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$f(x) = a(b)^x$ deb yozamiz. $f(3)$ ni $f(1)$ ga bo‘lsak, $b^2 = \\\\frac{108}{12} = 9$, demak $b = 3$ (ko‘rsatkichli funksiya asosi musbat). Keyin $a(3) = 12$, ya’ni $a = 4$ va $f(x) = 4(3)^x$. Tekshiruv: $4(3)^3 = 108$.\\n\\nB variant $f(1)$ ni boshlang‘ich qiymat deb oladi, lekin boshlang‘ich qiymat $f(0) = 4$. C variant $a$ va $b$ ni almashtiradi. D variant $2$ birlik o‘zgarishga mos ko‘paytuvchi $9$ ni $1$ birlik o‘zgarish uchun ishlatadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"exponential_from_points\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The function $f$ is a quadratic function. In the $xy$-plane, the graph of $y = f(x)$ has its vertex at $(3, -8)$ and passes through the point $(5, 4)$. What is the $y$-coordinate of the $y$-intercept of the graph of $y = -2f(x + 1)$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"-8\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Write $f(x) = a(x - 3)^2 - 8$. Using $(5, 4)$: $4 = a(2)^2 - 8$, so $4a = 12$ and $a = 3$. Thus $f(x) = 3(x - 3)^2 - 8$.\\n\\nThe $y$-intercept of $y = -2f(x + 1)$ is at $x = 0$: $-2f(0 + 1) = -2f(1) = -2\\\\left(3(1 - 3)^2 - 8\\\\right) = -2(12 - 8) = -8$.\\n\\nCommon errors: using $f(0) = 19$ (ignoring the shift) gives $-38$; using $f(-1) = 40$ (shifting the wrong way) gives $-80$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$f(x) = a(x - 3)^2 - 8$ deb yozamiz. $(5, 4)$ nuqtadan: $4 = a(2)^2 - 8$, ya’ni $4a = 12$ va $a = 3$. Demak $f(x) = 3(x - 3)^2 - 8$.\\n\\n$y = -2f(x + 1)$ grafigining $y$ o‘qi bilan kesishish nuqtasi $x = 0$ da: $-2f(0 + 1) = -2f(1) = -2\\\\left(3(1 - 3)^2 - 8\\\\right) = -2(12 - 8) = -8$.\\n\\nKo‘p uchraydigan xatolar: siljishni hisobga olmay $f(0) = 19$ ni olish $-38$ ni beradi; noto‘g‘ri tomonga siljitib $f(-1) = 40$ ni olish $-80$ ni beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"vertex_point_transformation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = a(x - h)^2 + k$, where $a$, $h$, and $k$ are constants. In the $xy$-plane, the graph of $y = f(x)$ has $x$-intercepts at $(-3, 0)$ and $(7, 0)$ and a $y$-intercept at $(0, 42)$. Which of the following is true about $f$?\",\"option_a\":\"The minimum value of $f(x)$ is $50$.\",\"option_b\":\"The maximum value of $f(x)$ is $2$.\",\"option_c\":\"The maximum value of $f(x)$ is $50$.\",\"option_d\":\"The maximum value of $f(x)$ is $42$.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"From the $x$-intercepts, $f(x) = a(x + 3)(x - 7)$. Then $f(0) = a(3)(-7) = -21a = 42$, so $a = -2$, and the parabola opens downward, so $f$ has a maximum. The vertex is halfway between the $x$-intercepts, at $h = \\\\frac{-3 + 7}{2} = 2$, and $k = f(2) = -2(5)(-5) = 50$. So the maximum value of $f(x)$ is $50$.\\n\\nChoice A misses that $a < 0$, so $50$ is a maximum, not a minimum. Choice B gives the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. Choice D gives the $y$-intercept, which is not the highest point because the vertex is at $x = 2$, not $x = 0$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$x$ o‘qi bilan kesishish nuqtalaridan $f(x) = a(x + 3)(x - 7)$. U holda $f(0) = a(3)(-7) = -21a = 42$, demak $a = -2$ va parabola pastga ochiladi, ya’ni $f$ eng katta qiymatga ega. Uch $x$ o‘qidagi nuqtalarning o‘rtasida: $h = \\\\frac{-3 + 7}{2} = 2$, va $k = f(2) = -2(5)(-5) = 50$. Demak $f(x)$ ning eng katta qiymati $50$.\\n\\nA variant $a < 0$ ekanini e’tiborsiz qoldiradi, shuning uchun $50$ eng kichik emas, eng katta qiymat. B variant parabola uchining $x$ koordinatasi. D variant $y$ o‘qi bilan kesishish nuqtasi; u eng yuqori nuqta emas, chunki uch $x = 0$ da emas, $x = 2$ da.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"intercept_to_vertex\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The function $p$ is defined by $p(x) = (x - 3)(x + k)(2x - 1)$, where $k$ is a constant. In the $xy$-plane, the graph of $y = p(x)$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(0, 30)$. What is the sum of the $x$-coordinates of all the $x$-intercepts of the graph?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"-13/2\",\"-6.5\"],\"explanation_en\":\"At $x = 0$: $p(0) = (-3)(k)(-1) = 3k$. Since $p(0) = 30$, $k = 10$, so $p(x) = (x - 3)(x + 10)(2x - 1)$.\\n\\nThe $x$-intercepts are at $x = 3$, $x = -10$, and $x = \\\\frac{1}{2}$. Their sum is $3 - 10 + \\\\frac{1}{2} = -\\\\frac{13}{2} = -6.5$.\\n\\nUsing $x = 10$ instead of $x = -10$ gives $13.5$, and using $x = 1$ instead of $x = \\\\frac{1}{2}$ gives $-6$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$x = 0$ da: $p(0) = (-3)(k)(-1) = 3k$. $p(0) = 30$ bo‘lgani uchun $k = 10$, demak $p(x) = (x - 3)(x + 10)(2x - 1)$.\\n\\n$x$ o‘qi bilan kesishish nuqtalari: $x = 3$, $x = -10$ va $x = \\\\frac{1}{2}$. Ularning yig‘indisi $3 - 10 + \\\\frac{1}{2} = -\\\\frac{13}{2} = -6.5$.\\n\\n$x = -10$ o‘rniga $x = 10$ ni olish $13.5$ ni, $x = \\\\frac{1}{2}$ o‘rniga $x = 1$ ni olish esa $-6$ ni beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"polynomial_parameter\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"nonlinear_functions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = a(x - h)^2 + k$, where $a$, $h$, and $k$ are constants with $a < 0$, $h > 0$, and $k < 0$. Which of the following must be true?\",\"option_a\":\"The graph of $y = f(x)$ has two $x$-intercepts.\",\"option_b\":\"The graph of $y = f(x)$ has no $x$-intercepts.\",\"option_c\":\"$f(0) > k$\",\"option_d\":\"The maximum value of $f(x)$ is $h$.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Because $a < 0$, the parabola opens downward and its highest point is the vertex $(h, k)$. The maximum value $k$ is negative, so every value of $f(x)$ is at most $k < 0$, and the graph never reaches the $x$-axis: it has no $x$-intercepts.\\n\\nChoice A would require the maximum value to be positive. Choice C is false: $f(0) = ah^2 + k$, and $ah^2 < 0$, so $f(0) < k$. Choice D confuses the $x$-coordinate of the vertex with the maximum value, which is $k$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$a < 0$ bo‘lgani uchun parabola pastga ochiladi va eng yuqori nuqtasi — $(h, k)$ uch. Eng katta qiymat $k$ manfiy, demak $f(x)$ ning barcha qiymatlari $k < 0$ dan oshmaydi va grafik $x$ o‘qiga yetmaydi: $x$ o‘qi bilan kesishish nuqtalari yo‘q.\\n\\nA variant eng katta qiymat musbat bo‘lishini talab qiladi. C variant noto‘g‘ri: $f(0) = ah^2 + k$, va $ah^2 < 0$, demak $f(0) < k$. D variant uchning $x$ koordinatasini eng katta qiymat bilan adashtiradi, eng katta qiymat esa $k$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"parameter_must_be_true\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"one_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The list shows the number of pages Dilnoza read on each of 5 days.\\n\\n24, 31, 18, 40, 27\\n\\nWhat is the mean number of pages she read per day?\",\"option_a\":\"22\",\"option_b\":\"27\",\"option_c\":\"28\",\"option_d\":\"35\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The mean is the sum divided by the number of values: $\\\\frac{24 + 31 + 18 + 40 + 27}{5} = \\\\frac{140}{5} = 28$. Choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A is the range, $40 - 18$. Choice B is the median. Choice D divides the sum by 4 instead of 5.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"O‘rta qiymat — yig‘indining qiymatlar soniga nisbati: $\\\\frac{24 + 31 + 18 + 40 + 27}{5} = \\\\frac{140}{5} = 28$. To‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA — qamrov, $40 - 18$. B — mediana. D javobda yig‘indi 5 ga emas, 4 ga bo‘lingan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"mean_list\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"one_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"passage\":\"The frequency table shows the number of siblings of each of the 20 students in a class.\\n\\n| Number of siblings | Number of students |\\n| ---: | ---: |\\n| 0 | 6 |\\n| 1 | 5 |\\n| 2 | 3 |\\n| 3 | 4 |\\n| 4 | 2 |\",\"stem\":\"What is the median number of siblings for these 20 students?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"1\"],\"explanation_en\":\"With 20 values, the median is the mean of the 10th and 11th values in order. Counting up the table: 6 students have 0 siblings (values 1 to 6) and 5 students have 1 sibling (values 7 to 11). So the 10th and 11th values are both 1, and the median is 1. If you got 2, you took the middle of the list of possible values 0 to 4 instead of the middle student.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"20 ta qiymatda mediana tartiblangan qiymatlardan 10- va 11-sining o‘rtachasi. Jadval bo‘yicha sanaymiz: 6 o‘quvchida 0 ta aka-uka/opa-singil bor (1–6-qiymatlar), 5 o‘quvchida 1 ta (7–11-qiymatlar). Demak 10- va 11-qiymatlar ikkalasi ham 1, mediana 1. Agar 2 chiqqan bo‘lsa, siz o‘rtadagi o‘quvchini emas, 0 dan 4 gacha bo‘lgan mumkin qiymatlarning o‘rtasini olgansiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"median_frequency_table\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"one_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A data set consists of the 6 values 4, 9, 9, 12, 15, and 21. If the value 21 is changed to 31, which of the following statistics of the data set does NOT change?\",\"option_a\":\"The mean\",\"option_b\":\"The median\",\"option_c\":\"The range\",\"option_d\":\"The standard deviation\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The median of 6 ordered values is the mean of the 3rd and 4th values: $\\\\frac{9 + 12}{2} = 10.5$. Changing the greatest value from 21 to 31 keeps it the greatest value, so the 3rd and 4th values stay 9 and 12, and the median stays 10.5. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A changes: the sum increases by 10, so the mean increases by $\\\\frac{10}{6}$. Choice C changes from $21 - 4 = 17$ to $31 - 4 = 27$. Choice D changes because the greatest value moves farther from the rest of the data, so the spread increases.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"6 ta tartiblangan qiymatning medianasi 3- va 4-qiymatlarning o‘rtachasi: $\\\\frac{9 + 12}{2} = 10.5$. Eng katta qiymat 21 dan 31 ga o‘zgarganda u eng kattaligicha qoladi, shuning uchun 3- va 4-qiymatlar 9 va 12 bo‘lib qoladi, mediana esa 10.5. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA o‘zgaradi: yig‘indi 10 ga ortadi, demak o‘rta qiymat $\\\\frac{10}{6}$ ga ortadi. C $21 - 4 = 17$ dan $31 - 4 = 27$ ga o‘zgaradi. D o‘zgaradi, chunki eng katta qiymat qolgan ma’lumotlardan uzoqlashadi va tarqoqlik ortadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"effect_of_change\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"one_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A box plot summarizes the lengths, in centimeters, of fish caught in a lake. The box plot shows a minimum of 12, a first quartile of 18, a median of 25, a third quartile of 31, and a maximum of 44. What is the interquartile range of the lengths, in centimeters?\",\"option_a\":\"6\",\"option_b\":\"13\",\"option_c\":\"25\",\"option_d\":\"32\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The interquartile range is the third quartile minus the first quartile: $31 - 18 = 13$ centimeters. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A is the third quartile minus the median. Choice C is the median. Choice D is the range, $44 - 12$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Kvartillararo oraliq — uchinchi kvartildan birinchi kvartilni ayirish: $31 - 18 = 13$ santimetr. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA — uchinchi kvartildan medianani ayirish. C — mediana. D — qamrov, $44 - 12$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"box_plot_iqr\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"one_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"passage\":\"Each of 10 students rated a new school lunch menu on a scale from 1 to 4. The table shows how many students gave each rating.\\n\\n| Rating | Number of students |\\n| ---: | ---: |\\n| 1 | 1 |\\n| 2 | 2 |\\n| 3 | 5 |\\n| 4 | 2 |\",\"stem\":\"What is the mean rating given by these 10 students?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"2.8\",\"14/5\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Multiply each rating by its frequency and add: $1(1) + 2(2) + 3(5) + 4(2) = 1 + 4 + 15 + 8 = 28$. Divide by the 10 students: $\\\\frac{28}{10} = 2.8$. If you got 2.5, you averaged the ratings 1, 2, 3, 4 without using the frequencies.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Har bir bahoni uning chastotasiga ko‘paytirib qo‘shamiz: $1(1) + 2(2) + 3(5) + 4(2) = 1 + 4 + 15 + 8 = 28$. 10 o‘quvchiga bo‘lamiz: $\\\\frac{28}{10} = 2.8$. Agar 2.5 chiqqan bo‘lsa, siz chastotalarni hisobga olmay 1, 2, 3, 4 baholarning o‘rtachasini olgansiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"mean_frequency_table\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"one_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Data set A consists of the values 48, 49, 50, 51, and 52. Data set B consists of the values 30, 40, 50, 60, and 70. Which of the following statements is true?\",\"option_a\":\"The means are equal, and the standard deviation of data set A is greater.\",\"option_b\":\"The means are equal, and the standard deviation of data set B is greater.\",\"option_c\":\"The mean of data set B is greater, and the standard deviations are equal.\",\"option_d\":\"The means are equal, and the standard deviations are equal.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Each data set is symmetric about 50, so both means are 50. The values in data set B are much farther from 50 (up to 20 away) than the values in data set A (at most 2 away), so data set B has the greater standard deviation. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A reverses the comparison of spread. Choice C is wrong because both means are 50. Choice D ignores that B is much more spread out.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Har ikki to‘plam 50 ga nisbatan simmetrik, demak ikkala o‘rta qiymat 50. B to‘plamdagi qiymatlar 50 dan ancha uzoqroq (20 gacha), A to‘plamdagilar esa ko‘pi bilan 2 birlik uzoqda, shuning uchun B ning standart chetlanishi katta. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA javob tarqoqlik taqqoslashini teskari qiladi. C noto‘g‘ri, chunki ikkala o‘rta qiymat ham 50. D javob B ancha tarqoqroq ekanini e’tiborga olmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"compare_spread\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"one_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A group of 12 students took a test, and their mean score was 70. Later, 8 more students took the same test, and the mean score of all 20 students was 74. What was the mean score of the 8 additional students?\",\"option_a\":\"72\",\"option_b\":\"74\",\"option_c\":\"78\",\"option_d\":\"80\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The total of all 20 scores is $20 \\\\cdot 74 = 1{,}480$, and the total of the first 12 scores is $12 \\\\cdot 70 = 840$. The 8 additional students scored $1{,}480 - 840 = 640$ in total, so their mean is $\\\\frac{640}{8} = 80$. Choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoice A is the mean of 70 and 74. Choice B is the overall mean. Choice C, $2 \\\\cdot 74 - 70$, assumes the two groups have the same size.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"20 ta natijaning yig‘indisi $20 \\\\cdot 74 = 1{,}480$, dastlabki 12 tasiniki $12 \\\\cdot 70 = 840$. Qo‘shimcha 8 o‘quvchi jami $1{,}480 - 840 = 640$ ball to‘plagan, demak ularning o‘rta qiymati $\\\\frac{640}{8} = 80$. To‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\nA — 70 va 74 ning o‘rtachasi. B — umumiy o‘rta qiymat. C, ya’ni $2 \\\\cdot 74 - 70$, ikki guruhni teng sonli deb hisoblaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"combined_mean\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"one_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The list shows 6 numbers in order from least to greatest.\\n\\n5, 8, $x$, 13, 16, 20\\n\\nThe median of the 6 numbers is 11. What is the value of $x$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"9\"],\"explanation_en\":\"With 6 values, the median is the mean of the 3rd and 4th values: $\\\\frac{x + 13}{2} = 11$. So $x + 13 = 22$ and $x = 9$, which fits the order $8 \\\\le 9 \\\\le 13$. If you set the mean of all 6 numbers equal to 11, you get $x = 4$, which does not fit the order.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"6 ta qiymatda mediana 3- va 4-qiymatlarning o‘rtachasi: $\\\\frac{x + 13}{2} = 11$. Demak $x + 13 = 22$ va $x = 9$; bu $8 \\\\le 9 \\\\le 13$ tartibga mos keladi. Agar 6 ta sonning o‘rta qiymatini 11 ga tenglasangiz, $x = 4$ chiqadi, u tartibga mos kelmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"median_unknown\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"one_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"passage\":\"The table summarizes the daily step counts, in thousands, of 30 people on one day.\\n\\n| Steps (thousands) | Number of people |\\n| --- | ---: |\\n| 0 to less than 4 | 2 |\\n| 4 to less than 8 | 6 |\\n| 8 to less than 12 | 8 |\\n| 12 to less than 16 | 10 |\\n| 16 to less than 20 | 4 |\",\"stem\":\"Which interval contains the median daily step count of these 30 people?\",\"option_a\":\"4 to less than 8\",\"option_b\":\"8 to less than 12\",\"option_c\":\"12 to less than 16\",\"option_d\":\"16 to less than 20\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"With 30 values, the median is the mean of the 15th and 16th values in order. The cumulative counts are 2 (below 4), $2 + 6 = 8$ (below 8), and $8 + 8 = 16$ (below 12). So the 15th and 16th values are both in the interval 8 to less than 12. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice C is the interval with the most people (the mode), not the median. Choices A and D contain values that are too low or too high to be in the middle position.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"30 ta qiymatda mediana tartiblangan qiymatlardan 15- va 16-sining o‘rtachasi. Jamlangan sonlar: 2 (4 dan kichik), $2 + 6 = 8$ (8 dan kichik) va $8 + 8 = 16$ (12 dan kichik). Demak 15- va 16-qiymatlar ikkalasi ham 8 dan 12 gacha bo‘lgan oraliqda. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nC — eng ko‘p odam bor oraliq (moda), mediana emas. A va D oraliqlardagi qiymatlar o‘rtadagi o‘rin uchun juda kichik yoki juda katta.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"median_grouped\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"one_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A data set consists of 9 values. The mean of the data set is 40, and the standard deviation is 6. A 10th value, 40, is added to the data set. Which of the following statements about the new data set of 10 values is true?\",\"option_a\":\"The mean and the standard deviation are both unchanged.\",\"option_b\":\"The mean is unchanged, and the standard deviation decreases.\",\"option_c\":\"The mean increases, and the standard deviation is unchanged.\",\"option_d\":\"The mean is unchanged, and the standard deviation increases.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Adding a value equal to the mean keeps the mean at 40, since the new sum $9 \\\\cdot 40 + 40 = 400$ divided by 10 is 40. The new value is at distance 0 from the mean, so the typical distance from the mean becomes smaller: the standard deviation decreases. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A ignores that one more value with zero deviation lowers the average spread. Choice C is wrong because the mean stays 40. Choice D would be true only if the new value were far from the mean.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"O‘rta qiymatga teng qiymat qo‘shilganda o‘rta qiymat 40 bo‘lib qoladi: yangi yig‘indi $9 \\\\cdot 40 + 40 = 400$, 10 ga bo‘linsa 40. Yangi qiymat o‘rta qiymatdan 0 masofada, shuning uchun o‘rta qiymatdan odatiy uzoqlik kamayadi: standart chetlanish kamayadi. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA javob chetlanishi nol bo‘lgan yana bir qiymat o‘rtacha tarqoqlikni kamaytirishini e’tiborga olmaydi. C noto‘g‘ri, chunki o‘rta qiymat 40 ligicha qoladi. D faqat yangi qiymat o‘rta qiymatdan uzoq bo‘lganda to‘g‘ri bo‘lardi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"effect_on_sd\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"one_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The mean of a list of 8 numbers is 15. When one number is removed from the list, the mean of the remaining 7 numbers is 14. What number was removed?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"22\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The sum of the 8 numbers is $8 \\\\cdot 15 = 120$, and the sum of the remaining 7 numbers is $7 \\\\cdot 14 = 98$. The removed number is $120 - 98 = 22$. If you got 1, you subtracted the means instead of the sums.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"8 ta son yig‘indisi $8 \\\\cdot 15 = 120$, qolgan 7 tasiniki $7 \\\\cdot 14 = 98$. Olib tashlangan son $120 - 98 = 22$. Agar 1 chiqqan bo‘lsa, siz yig‘indilarni emas, o‘rta qiymatlarni ayirgansiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"removed_value\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"one_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"passage\":\"The table shows the number of cups of tea that each of 20 people drink per day.\\n\\n| Cups of tea per day | Number of people |\\n| ---: | ---: |\\n| 1 | 6 |\\n| 2 | 8 |\\n| 3 | 4 |\\n| 4 | 1 |\\n| 10 | 1 |\",\"stem\":\"Which of the following gives the mean and the median of the numbers of cups of tea these 20 people drink per day?\",\"option_a\":\"Mean 2.4, median 2\",\"option_b\":\"Mean 2, median 2.4\",\"option_c\":\"Mean 2.4, median 3\",\"option_d\":\"Mean 4, median 2\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The total is $1(6) + 2(8) + 3(4) + 4(1) + 10(1) = 6 + 16 + 12 + 4 + 10 = 48$, so the mean is $\\\\frac{48}{20} = 2.4$. The median is the mean of the 10th and 11th values; the first 6 values are 1 and the next 8 (7th to 14th) are 2, so the median is 2. Choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoice B swaps the mean and the median. Choice C uses the wrong position for the median. Choice D averages the five listed values 1, 2, 3, 4, and 10 without using the frequencies.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Yig‘indi $1(6) + 2(8) + 3(4) + 4(1) + 10(1) = 6 + 16 + 12 + 4 + 10 = 48$, demak o‘rta qiymat $\\\\frac{48}{20} = 2.4$. Mediana 10- va 11-qiymatlarning o‘rtachasi; dastlabki 6 ta qiymat 1, keyingi 8 tasi (7–14-qiymatlar) 2, shuning uchun mediana 2. To‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\nB javobda o‘rta qiymat va mediana almashtirilgan. C javobda mediana uchun noto‘g‘ri o‘rin olingan. D javobda chastotalarsiz 1, 2, 3, 4 va 10 qiymatlarning o‘rtachasi olingan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"mean_median_skew\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"one_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"passage\":\"Data set X and data set Y each consist of 10 integers from 1 to 5. The table shows how many times each integer appears in each data set.\\n\\n| Value | Frequency in X | Frequency in Y |\\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| 1 | 1 | 4 |\\n| 2 | 2 | 1 |\\n| 3 | 4 | 0 |\\n| 4 | 2 | 1 |\\n| 5 | 1 | 4 |\",\"stem\":\"Which of the following statements about data set X and data set Y is true?\",\"option_a\":\"The means are equal, and the standard deviation of data set X is greater.\",\"option_b\":\"The means are equal, and the standard deviation of data set Y is greater.\",\"option_c\":\"The mean of data set X is greater, and the standard deviations are equal.\",\"option_d\":\"The mean of data set Y is greater, and the standard deviation of data set Y is greater.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Both frequency distributions are symmetric about the value 3, so both means are 3. In data set X, most values are at or near 3 (4 values equal 3). In data set Y, 8 of the 10 values are at 1 or 5, the farthest possible from 3, and none equal 3. So the values of Y are farther from the mean, and Y has the greater standard deviation. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A reverses the comparison. Choices C and D are wrong because the means are equal: the sums are $1 + 4 + 12 + 8 + 5 = 30$ for X and $4 + 2 + 0 + 4 + 20 = 30$ for Y.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkala chastota taqsimoti ham 3 qiymatga nisbatan simmetrik, demak ikkala o‘rta qiymat 3. X to‘plamda ko‘p qiymatlar 3 ga teng yoki unga yaqin (4 ta qiymat 3 ga teng). Y to‘plamda 10 ta qiymatdan 8 tasi 1 yoki 5 da — 3 dan eng uzoq joyda, 3 ga tengi esa yo‘q. Demak Y qiymatlari o‘rta qiymatdan uzoqroq va Y ning standart chetlanishi katta. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA javob taqqoslashni teskari qiladi. C va D noto‘g‘ri, chunki o‘rta qiymatlar teng: X uchun yig‘indi $1 + 4 + 12 + 8 + 5 = 30$, Y uchun $4 + 2 + 0 + 4 + 20 = 30$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"compare_sd_frequency\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"one_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A data set consists of 7 different positive integers. The mean of the data set is 12, and the median is 10. What is the greatest possible value of the largest integer in the data set?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"45\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The sum of the 7 integers is $7 \\\\cdot 12 = 84$. In order, the 4th integer is the median, 10. To make the largest integer as great as possible, make the other five as small as possible: the three below 10 can be 1, 2, and 3, and the two above 10 (other than the largest) must be at least 11 and 12. Then the largest is $84 - (1 + 2 + 3 + 10 + 11 + 12) = 84 - 39 = 45$. If you got 51, you allowed repeated values (1, 1, 1 below the median and 10, 10 after it), but the integers must be different.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"7 ta butun son yig‘indisi $7 \\\\cdot 12 = 84$. Tartiblanganda 4-son mediana, ya’ni 10. Eng katta sonni iloji boricha katta qilish uchun qolgan beshtasini iloji boricha kichik olamiz: 10 dan kichik uchtasi 1, 2 va 3, 10 dan katta ikkitasi (eng kattasidan tashqari) kamida 11 va 12 bo‘lishi kerak. U holda eng katta son $84 - (1 + 2 + 3 + 10 + 11 + 12) = 84 - 39 = 45$. Agar 51 chiqqan bo‘lsa, siz takrorlanuvchi qiymatlarga (medianadan oldin 1, 1, 1 va keyin 10, 10) yo‘l qo‘ygansiz, lekin sonlar har xil bo‘lishi kerak.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"extreme_value_constraints\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"one_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A data set consists of 15 different numbers. A new data set is created by increasing the greatest number by 30 and decreasing the least number by 30; the other 13 numbers are unchanged. Which of the following statistics of the new data set must be equal to the corresponding statistic of the original data set?\\n\\nI. The mean\\nII. The median\\nIII. The standard deviation\",\"option_a\":\"I only\",\"option_b\":\"II only\",\"option_c\":\"I and II only\",\"option_d\":\"I, II, and III\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"I: The sum changes by $+30 - 30 = 0$, so the mean is unchanged. II: The greatest number stays the greatest and the least stays the least, so the order of the values does not change and the 8th value (the median) is the same. III: The two extreme values move 30 units farther from the unchanged mean, so the spread increases and the standard deviation changes. Choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoices A and B each leave out one statistic that stays the same. Choice D wrongly includes the standard deviation, which increases.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"I: Yig‘indi $+30 - 30 = 0$ ga o‘zgaradi, demak o‘rta qiymat o‘zgarmaydi. II: Eng katta son eng kattaligicha, eng kichigi eng kichikligicha qoladi, shuning uchun qiymatlar tartibi o‘zgarmaydi va 8-qiymat (mediana) avvalgidek. III: Ikki chetki qiymat o‘zgarmagan o‘rta qiymatdan 30 birlik uzoqlashadi, shuning uchun tarqoqlik ortadi va standart chetlanish o‘zgaradi. To‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA va B javoblar o‘zgarmaydigan statistikalardan birini tushirib qoldiradi. D javob ortadigan standart chetlanishni ham noto‘g‘ri qo‘shadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"effect_of_change\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"one_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"Data set A consists of 10 values with a mean of $m$. Data set B is formed by adding 4 to each value in data set A and then including one more value, 50. The mean of data set B is 7 greater than $m$. What is the value of $m$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"13\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The sum of data set A is $10m$. Adding 4 to each of the 10 values adds 40, and including 50 adds 50, so data set B has 11 values with sum $10m + 90$. Its mean is $\\\\frac{10m + 90}{11} = m + 7$, so $10m + 90 = 11m + 77$ and $m = 13$. Check: B has sum 220 and mean 20, which is $13 + 7$. If you divided by 10 instead of 11, you counted only the original values.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"A to‘plam yig‘indisi $10m$. 10 ta qiymatning har biriga 4 qo‘shish yig‘indiga 40 qo‘shadi, 50 ni kiritish esa yana 50 qo‘shadi, demak B to‘plamda yig‘indisi $10m + 90$ bo‘lgan 11 ta qiymat bor. Uning o‘rta qiymati $\\\\frac{10m + 90}{11} = m + 7$, demak $10m + 90 = 11m + 77$ va $m = 13$. Tekshiruv: B yig‘indisi 220, o‘rta qiymati 20, bu $13 + 7$. Agar 11 ga emas, 10 ga bo‘lgan bo‘lsangiz, faqat dastlabki qiymatlarni sanagansiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"mean_algebra\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"percentages\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A school library has $1{,}240$ books, and $15\\\\%$ of them are novels. How many novels does the library have?\",\"option_a\":\"$124$\",\"option_b\":\"$186$\",\"option_c\":\"$1{,}054$\",\"option_d\":\"$1{,}426$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"$15\\\\%$ of $1{,}240$ is $0.15 \\\\times 1{,}240 = 186$.\\n\\nChoice A is $10\\\\%$ of $1{,}240$. Choice C is the number of books that are not novels ($85\\\\%$). Choice D is $115\\\\%$ of $1{,}240$, which adds $15\\\\%$ to the total.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$1{,}240$ ning $15\\\\%$ i: $0.15 \\\\times 1{,}240 = 186$.\\n\\nA variant — $1{,}240$ ning $10\\\\%$ i. C variant — roman bo‘lmagan kitoblar soni ($85\\\\%$). D variant — $1{,}240$ ning $115\\\\%$ i, ya’ni jamiga $15\\\\%$ qo‘shilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"percent-of\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"percentages\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The price of a kilogram of cherries at a market rose from $80{,}000$ soums to $92{,}000$ soums. By what percent did the price increase?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"15\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The increase is $92{,}000 - 80{,}000 = 12{,}000$ soums. Divide by the original price: $\\\\frac{12{,}000}{80{,}000} = 0.15 = 15\\\\%$. Dividing by the new price ($\\\\frac{12{,}000}{92{,}000} \\\\approx 13\\\\%$) is a common error.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"O‘sish $92{,}000 - 80{,}000 = 12{,}000$ so‘m. Uni boshlang‘ich narxga bo‘lamiz: $\\\\frac{12{,}000}{80{,}000} = 0.15 = 15\\\\%$. Yangi narxga bo‘lish ($\\\\frac{12{,}000}{92{,}000} \\\\approx 13\\\\%$) — ko‘p uchraydigan xato.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"percent-change\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"percentages\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In a class of $48$ students, $36$ students passed a driving-theory test. What percent of the students in the class passed the test?\",\"option_a\":\"$25\\\\%$\",\"option_b\":\"$36\\\\%$\",\"option_c\":\"$75\\\\%$\",\"option_d\":\"$133\\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\%$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Divide the part by the whole: $\\\\frac{36}{48} = 0.75 = 75\\\\%$.\\n\\nChoice A is the percent of students who did not pass ($\\\\frac{12}{48}$). Choice B treats the number of students who passed as a percent. Choice D divides the whole by the part ($\\\\frac{48}{36}$).\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qismni butunga bo‘lamiz: $\\\\frac{36}{48} = 0.75 = 75\\\\%$.\\n\\nA variant — testdan o‘ta olmaganlar ulushi ($\\\\frac{12}{48}$). B variant testdan o‘tganlar sonini foiz deb olgan. D variant butunni qismga bo‘lgan ($\\\\frac{48}{36}$).\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"part-of-whole\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"percentages\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A winter coat normally costs $640{,}000$ soums. During a sale, the price is reduced by $35\\\\%$. What is the sale price of the coat, in soums?\",\"option_a\":\"$224{,}000$\",\"option_b\":\"$416{,}000$\",\"option_c\":\"$605{,}000$\",\"option_d\":\"$864{,}000$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"After a $35\\\\%$ discount, the customer pays $100\\\\% - 35\\\\% = 65\\\\%$ of the price: $0.65 \\\\times 640{,}000 = 416{,}000$ soums.\\n\\nChoice A is the amount of the discount, not the sale price. Choice C subtracts $35{,}000$ soums instead of $35\\\\%$. Choice D increases the price by $35\\\\%$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$35\\\\%$ chegirmadan keyin xaridor narxning $100\\\\% - 35\\\\% = 65\\\\%$ ini to‘laydi: $0.65 \\\\times 640{,}000 = 416{,}000$ so‘m.\\n\\nA variant — chegirma miqdori, chegirmali narx emas. C variant $35\\\\%$ o‘rniga $35{,}000$ so‘m ayirgan. D variant narxni $35\\\\%$ ga oshirgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"discount\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"percentages\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"If $30\\\\%$ of a number is $54$, what is the number?\",\"option_a\":\"$16.2$\",\"option_b\":\"$84$\",\"option_c\":\"$162$\",\"option_d\":\"$180$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Let the number be $n$. Then $0.3n = 54$, so $n = \\\\frac{54}{0.3} = 180$. Check: $0.3 \\\\times 180 = 54$.\\n\\nChoice A is $30\\\\%$ of $54$, not the number whose $30\\\\%$ is $54$. Choice B adds $30$ to $54$. Choice C multiplies $54$ by $3$ instead of dividing by $0.3$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Son $n$ bo‘lsin. U holda $0.3n = 54$, demak $n = \\\\frac{54}{0.3} = 180$. Tekshiruv: $0.3 \\\\times 180 = 54$.\\n\\nA variant — $54$ ning $30\\\\%$ i, $30\\\\%$ i $54$ ga teng bo‘lgan son emas. B variant $54$ ga $30$ ni qo‘shgan. C variant $0.3$ ga bo‘lish o‘rniga $54$ ni $3$ ga ko‘paytirgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"find-whole\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"percentages\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The price of a train ticket was $p$ soums. The price then increased by $7\\\\%$. Which expression represents the new price, in soums?\",\"option_a\":\"$p + 7$\",\"option_b\":\"$0.07p$\",\"option_c\":\"$1.07p$\",\"option_d\":\"$1.7p$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The new price is the old price plus $7\\\\%$ of it: $p + 0.07p = 1.07p$.\\n\\nChoice A adds $7$ soums, not $7\\\\%$. Choice B is only the amount of the increase. Choice D represents a $70\\\\%$ increase.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Yangi narx — eski narx va uning $7\\\\%$ i yig‘indisi: $p + 0.07p = 1.07p$.\\n\\nA variant $7\\\\%$ emas, $7$ so‘m qo‘shgan. B variant — faqat o‘sish miqdori. D variant $70\\\\%$ o‘sishni ifodalaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"percent-expression\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"percentages\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"After the number of members in a robotics club increased by $25\\\\%$, the club had $340$ members. How many members did the club have before the increase?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"272\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The new number is $125\\\\%$ of the original: $1.25n = 340$, so $n = \\\\frac{340}{1.25} = 272$. Check: $272 + 0.25(272) = 272 + 68 = 340$. Subtracting $25\\\\%$ of $340$ ($340 - 85 = 255$) is wrong, because the $25\\\\%$ is a percent of the original number, not of $340$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Yangi son dastlabki sonning $125\\\\%$ i: $1.25n = 340$, demak $n = \\\\frac{340}{1.25} = 272$. Tekshiruv: $272 + 0.25(272) = 272 + 68 = 340$. $340$ dan uning $25\\\\%$ ini ayirish ($340 - 85 = 255$) xato, chunki $25\\\\%$ — dastlabki sonning foizi, $340$ ning emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"reverse-percent\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"percentages\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A family used $4{,}800$ liters of water in May. After they repaired a leaking pipe, they used $3{,}600$ liters of water in June. By what percent did the family's water use decrease from May to June?\",\"option_a\":\"$12\\\\%$\",\"option_b\":\"$25\\\\%$\",\"option_c\":\"$33\\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\%$\",\"option_d\":\"$75\\\\%$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The decrease is $4{,}800 - 3{,}600 = 1{,}200$ liters. Divide by the original (May) amount: $\\\\frac{1{,}200}{4{,}800} = 0.25 = 25\\\\%$.\\n\\nChoice A treats the decrease of $1{,}200$ liters as if it were $12\\\\%$. Choice C divides by the June amount instead of the May amount ($\\\\frac{1{,}200}{3{,}600}$). Choice D is June's use as a percent of May's use, not the decrease.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Kamayish $4{,}800 - 3{,}600 = 1{,}200$ litr. Uni dastlabki (may) miqdorga bo‘lamiz: $\\\\frac{1{,}200}{4{,}800} = 0.25 = 25\\\\%$.\\n\\nA variant $1{,}200$ litr kamayishni $12\\\\%$ deb olgan. C variant may o‘rniga iyun miqdoriga bo‘lgan ($\\\\frac{1{,}200}{3{,}600}$). D variant — iyundagi sarf mayning necha foizi ekani, kamayish emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"percent-change\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"percentages\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A shop increased the price of a pair of headphones by $25\\\\%$. A month later, the shop offered a $20\\\\%$ discount on the increased price. How does the final price compare with the original price?\",\"option_a\":\"The final price is equal to the original price.\",\"option_b\":\"The final price is $5\\\\%$ greater than the original price.\",\"option_c\":\"The final price is $5\\\\%$ less than the original price.\",\"option_d\":\"The final price is $45\\\\%$ greater than the original price.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Let the original price be $p$. After the increase, the price is $1.25p$. After the discount, it is $0.80 \\\\times 1.25p = 1.00p$. So the final price equals the original price.\\n\\nChoice B subtracts the percents ($25\\\\% - 20\\\\% = 5\\\\%$) instead of multiplying the factors. Choice C makes the same mistake with the wrong sign. Choice D adds the two percents as if both were increases.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Dastlabki narx $p$ bo‘lsin. Oshirilgandan keyin narx $1.25p$. Chegirmadan keyin u $0.80 \\\\times 1.25p = 1.00p$ bo‘ladi. Demak, oxirgi narx dastlabki narxga teng.\\n\\nB variant ko‘paytuvchilarni ko‘paytirish o‘rniga foizlarni ayirgan ($25\\\\% - 20\\\\% = 5\\\\%$). C variant xuddi shu xatoni teskari ishora bilan qilgan. D variant ikkala foizni o‘sish deb qo‘shgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"successive-percent\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"percentages\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"In a survey of the residents of a town, $40\\\\%$ of the respondents were younger than $25$, and the rest were $25$ or older. Of the respondents younger than $25$, $65\\\\%$ said they prefer taking the bus to work. Of the respondents $25$ or older, $30\\\\%$ said they prefer taking the bus to work. What percent of all respondents said they prefer taking the bus to work?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"44\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Respondents younger than $25$ who prefer the bus: $0.65 \\\\times 40\\\\% = 26\\\\%$ of all respondents. Respondents $25$ or older make up $60\\\\%$, and $0.30 \\\\times 60\\\\% = 18\\\\%$ of all respondents prefer the bus. In total, $26\\\\% + 18\\\\% = 44\\\\%$. Averaging $65\\\\%$ and $30\\\\%$ ($47.5\\\\%$) is wrong because the two groups are different sizes.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$25$ yoshdan kichik va avtobusni afzal ko‘radiganlar: barcha respondentlarning $0.65 \\\\times 40\\\\% = 26\\\\%$ i. $25$ yosh va undan kattalar $60\\\\%$ ni tashkil qiladi, ulardan $0.30 \\\\times 60\\\\% = 18\\\\%$ (barcha respondentlarga nisbatan) avtobusni afzal ko‘radi. Jami $26\\\\% + 18\\\\% = 44\\\\%$. $65\\\\%$ va $30\\\\%$ ning o‘rta qiymatini olish ($47.5\\\\%$) xato, chunki ikki guruhning hajmi har xil.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"percent-of-percent\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"percentages\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A museum had $2{,}500$ visitors in March. The number of visitors in July was $160\\\\%$ greater than the number of visitors in March. How many visitors did the museum have in July?\",\"option_a\":\"$2{,}660$\",\"option_b\":\"$4{,}000$\",\"option_c\":\"$4{,}100$\",\"option_d\":\"$6{,}500$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"$160\\\\%$ greater means the March number plus $160\\\\%$ of it: $2{,}500 + 1.6 \\\\times 2{,}500 = 2.6 \\\\times 2{,}500 = 6{,}500$ visitors.\\n\\nChoice A adds $160$ visitors instead of $160\\\\%$. Choice B is $160\\\\%$ of the March number ($1.6 \\\\times 2{,}500$), not $160\\\\%$ greater. Choice C adds $1{,}600$ visitors, treating $160\\\\%$ as a number of visitors.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$160\\\\%$ ko‘p degani — mart sonining ustiga uning $160\\\\%$ ini qo‘shish: $2{,}500 + 1.6 \\\\times 2{,}500 = 2.6 \\\\times 2{,}500 = 6{,}500$ tashrifchi.\\n\\nA variant $160\\\\%$ o‘rniga $160$ tashrifchi qo‘shgan. B variant — mart sonining $160\\\\%$ i ($1.6 \\\\times 2{,}500$), $160\\\\%$ ko‘p emas. C variant $160\\\\%$ ni tashrifchilar soni deb, $1{,}600$ qo‘shgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"percent-greater\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"percentages\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"At a school, the percent of students who passed a mathematics exam rose from $40\\\\%$ last year to $50\\\\%$ this year. By what percent did the pass rate increase?\",\"option_a\":\"$10\\\\%$\",\"option_b\":\"$20\\\\%$\",\"option_c\":\"$25\\\\%$\",\"option_d\":\"$50\\\\%$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The pass rate rose by $50 - 40 = 10$ percentage points. As a percent of the original rate, the increase is $\\\\frac{10}{40} = 0.25 = 25\\\\%$.\\n\\nChoice A is the increase in percentage points, not the percent increase. Choice B divides by the new rate ($\\\\frac{10}{50}$). Choice D is the new pass rate itself.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"O‘tish ko‘rsatkichi $50 - 40 = 10$ foiz punktga oshgan. Dastlabki ko‘rsatkichga nisbatan foizda bu $\\\\frac{10}{40} = 0.25 = 25\\\\%$.\\n\\nA variant — foiz punktlardagi o‘sish, foizli o‘sish emas. B variant yangi ko‘rsatkichga bo‘lgan ($\\\\frac{10}{50}$). D variant — yangi ko‘rsatkichning o‘zi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"percent-change\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"percentages\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The positive number $x$ is $75\\\\%$ greater than the positive number $y$, and $y$ is $20\\\\%$ less than the positive number $z$. The value of $x$ is what percent greater than the value of $z$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"40\"],\"explanation_en\":\"From the conditions, $x = 1.75y$ and $y = 0.80z$. So $x = 1.75 \\\\times 0.80z = 1.4z$, which means $x$ is $40\\\\%$ greater than $z$. Adding and subtracting the percents ($75\\\\% - 20\\\\% = 55\\\\%$) is wrong, because the two percents are taken of different numbers.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Shartlardan $x = 1.75y$ va $y = 0.80z$. Demak, $x = 1.75 \\\\times 0.80z = 1.4z$, ya’ni $x$ soni $z$ dan $40\\\\%$ katta. Foizlarni qo‘shib-ayirish ($75\\\\% - 20\\\\% = 55\\\\%$) xato, chunki ikki foiz har xil sonlardan olingan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"chained-percent\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"percentages\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A cook has $200$ grams of sugar syrup that is $12\\\\%$ sugar by mass. How many grams of pure sugar must the cook add to the syrup to make a syrup that is $20\\\\%$ sugar by mass?\",\"option_a\":\"$16$\",\"option_b\":\"$20$\",\"option_c\":\"$24$\",\"option_d\":\"$40$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The syrup contains $0.12 \\\\times 200 = 24$ grams of sugar. After adding $s$ grams of sugar, there are $24 + s$ grams of sugar in $200 + s$ grams of syrup. Set $\\\\frac{24 + s}{200 + s} = 0.20$: $24 + s = 40 + 0.2s$, so $0.8s = 16$ and $s = 20$. Check: $\\\\frac{44}{220} = 0.20$.\\n\\nChoice A is $8\\\\%$ of $200$, which ignores that adding sugar also increases the total mass. Choice C is the amount of sugar already in the syrup. Choice D is the amount of sugar needed in $200$ grams of a $20\\\\%$ syrup, not the amount to add.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Sharbatda $0.12 \\\\times 200 = 24$ gramm shakar bor. $s$ gramm shakar qo‘shilgach, $200 + s$ gramm sharbatda $24 + s$ gramm shakar bo‘ladi. $\\\\frac{24 + s}{200 + s} = 0.20$ deb olamiz: $24 + s = 40 + 0.2s$, demak $0.8s = 16$ va $s = 20$. Tekshiruv: $\\\\frac{44}{220} = 0.20$.\\n\\nA variant — $200$ ning $8\\\\%$ i; bunda shakar qo‘shilganda umumiy massa ham ortishi hisobga olinmagan. C variant — sharbatda allaqachon bor shakar miqdori. D variant — $200$ gramm $20\\\\%$ li sharbatdagi shakar miqdori, qo‘shiladigan miqdor emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"mixture\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"percentages\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The number of students in Club A is $20\\\\%$ greater than the number of students in Club B. If $12$ students moved from Club A to Club B, the two clubs would have the same number of students. How many students are in Club A?\",\"option_a\":\"$120$\",\"option_b\":\"$132$\",\"option_c\":\"$144$\",\"option_d\":\"$264$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Let $B$ be the number of students in Club B; then Club A has $1.2B$. After the move: $1.2B - 12 = B + 12$, so $0.2B = 24$ and $B = 120$. Club A has $1.2 \\\\times 120 = 144$ students. Check: $144 - 12 = 132$ and $120 + 12 = 132$.\\n\\nChoice A is the number of students in Club B. Choice B is the number in each club after the move. Choice D is the total number of students in both clubs.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"B klubdagi o‘quvchilar soni $B$ bo‘lsin; u holda A klubda $1.2B$ o‘quvchi bor. Ko‘chishdan keyin: $1.2B - 12 = B + 12$, demak $0.2B = 24$ va $B = 120$. A klubda $1.2 \\\\times 120 = 144$ o‘quvchi bor. Tekshiruv: $144 - 12 = 132$ va $120 + 12 = 132$.\\n\\nA variant — B klubdagi o‘quvchilar soni. B variant — ko‘chishdan keyin har bir klubdagi o‘quvchilar soni. D variant — ikkala klubdagi jami o‘quvchilar soni.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"percent-greater\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"percentages\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The population of a town increased by $25\\\\%$ from $2020$ to $2022$. From $2022$ to $2024$, the population decreased by $p\\\\%$. The population in $2024$ was $10\\\\%$ greater than the population in $2020$. What is the value of $p$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"12\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Let the 2020 population be $P$. In 2022 it was $1.25P$, and in 2024 it was $1.25P\\\\left(1 - \\\\frac{p}{100}\\\\right)$. This equals $1.10P$, so $1 - \\\\frac{p}{100} = \\\\frac{1.10}{1.25} = 0.88$, and $p = 12$. Subtracting the percents ($25 - 10 = 15$) is wrong, because the decrease is a percent of the larger 2022 population.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"2020-yildagi aholi $P$ bo‘lsin. 2022-yilda u $1.25P$, 2024-yilda esa $1.25P\\\\left(1 - \\\\frac{p}{100}\\\\right)$ bo‘lgan. Bu $1.10P$ ga teng, demak $1 - \\\\frac{p}{100} = \\\\frac{1.10}{1.25} = 0.88$ va $p = 12$. Foizlarni ayirish ($25 - 10 = 15$) xato, chunki kamayish 2022-yildagi kattaroq aholi sonining foizi hisoblanadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"successive-percent\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"probability_conditional\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A tray contains 30 samsas: 12 are filled with meat, 10 are filled with pumpkin, and 8 are filled with potato. If one samsa is selected at random from the tray, what is the probability that it is **not** filled with pumpkin?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{1}{3}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{2}{5}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{1}{2}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{2}{3}$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"There are $30 - 10 = 20$ samsas that are not filled with pumpkin, so the probability is $\\\\frac{20}{30} = \\\\frac{2}{3}$. Choice A is the probability that the samsa **is** filled with pumpkin. Choice B counts only the 12 meat samsas. Choice C compares the 10 pumpkin samsas with the 20 others ($\\\\frac{10}{20}$) instead of dividing by the total.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qovoqli bo‘lmagan samsalar $30 - 10 = 20$ ta, demak ehtimollik $\\\\frac{20}{30} = \\\\frac{2}{3}$. A javob — samsa qovoqli **bo‘lish** ehtimolligi. B javob faqat 12 ta go‘shtli samsani sanagan. C javob 10 ta qovoqli samsani qolgan 20 tasi bilan solishtirgan ($\\\\frac{10}{20}$), umumiy songa bo‘lmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"complement\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"probability_conditional\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"For a certain variety of melon, the probability that a seed planted in a greenhouse sprouts is 0.85. If a farmer plants 360 of these seeds in a greenhouse, how many of the seeds are expected to sprout?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"306\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The expected number is the probability times the number of trials: $0.85 \\\\times 360 = 306$ seeds. Answering 54 gives the expected number of seeds that do **not** sprout ($0.15 \\\\times 360$). Enter 306.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Kutilgan son — ehtimollik ko‘paytirilgan sinovlar soni: $0.85 \\\\times 360 = 306$ ta urug‘. 54 deb javob berish unib **chiqmaydigan** urug‘lar sonini beradi ($0.15 \\\\times 360$). Javob: 306.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"expected count\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"probability_conditional\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"passage\":\"A museum in Samarkand surveyed 200 visitors. Each visitor was classified by age group and by whether it was the visitor's first visit to the museum. The results are shown in the table.\\n\\n| Age group | First visit | Returning visitor | Total |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Under 25 years | 48 | 32 | 80 |\\n| 25 years or older | 54 | 66 | 120 |\\n| Total | 102 | 98 | 200 |\",\"stem\":\"If one of the 200 visitors is selected at random, what is the probability that the visitor is a returning visitor?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{16}{49}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{2}{5}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{49}{100}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{33}{50}$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"There are 98 returning visitors among the 200 visitors, so the probability is $\\\\frac{98}{200} = \\\\frac{49}{100}$. Choice A is $\\\\frac{32}{98}$, the fraction of returning visitors who are under 25. Choice B is $\\\\frac{80}{200}$, the probability that the visitor is under 25. Choice D is $\\\\frac{66}{100}$; it uses only the returning visitors who are 25 or older and the wrong total.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"200 nafar tashrif buyuruvchidan 98 nafari qayta kelgan, demak ehtimollik $\\\\frac{98}{200} = \\\\frac{49}{100}$. A javob $\\\\frac{32}{98}$ — qayta kelganlar orasida 25 yoshdan kichiklar ulushi. B javob $\\\\frac{80}{200}$ — tashrif buyuruvchining 25 yoshdan kichik bo‘lish ehtimolligi. D javob $\\\\frac{66}{100}$; u faqat 25 va undan katta yoshdagi qayta kelganlarni va noto‘g‘ri umumiy sonni olgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"two-way table\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"probability_conditional\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"For a school raffle, 50 tickets are numbered with the consecutive integers from 101 to 150, with one number on each ticket. If one ticket is selected at random, what is the probability that its number is a multiple of 10?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{1}{10}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{1}{5}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{1}{50}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{3}{50}$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The multiples of 10 from 101 to 150 are 110, 120, 130, 140, and 150, which is 5 tickets. The probability is $\\\\frac{5}{50} = \\\\frac{1}{10}$. Choice B counts the numbers that end in 0 **or** 5 (multiples of 5). Choice C counts only one ticket, and choice D misses 110 and 150, counting only 120, 130 and 140.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"101 dan 150 gacha bo‘lgan 10 ga karrali sonlar: 110, 120, 130, 140 va 150 — jami 5 ta chipta. Ehtimollik $\\\\frac{5}{50} = \\\\frac{1}{10}$. B javob 0 **yoki** 5 bilan tugaydigan sonlarni (5 ga karralilarni) sanagan. C javob faqat bitta chiptani sanagan, D javob esa 110 va 150 ni tushirib qoldirib, faqat 120, 130 va 140 ni sanagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"simple probability\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"probability_conditional\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A jar contains only white beans and black beans. There are 45 white beans in the jar. If one bean is selected at random from the jar, the probability that it is white is 0.36. How many black beans are in the jar?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"80\"],\"explanation_en\":\"If $n$ is the total number of beans, then $\\\\frac{45}{n} = 0.36$, so $n = \\\\frac{45}{0.36} = 125$. The number of black beans is $125 - 45 = 80$. Answering 125 gives the total number of beans, not the number of black beans. Enter 80.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Agar $n$ — loviyalarning umumiy soni bo‘lsa, $\\\\frac{45}{n} = 0.36$, demak $n = \\\\frac{45}{0.36} = 125$. Qora loviyalar soni $125 - 45 = 80$. 125 deb javob berish qora loviyalar sonini emas, umumiy sonni beradi. Javob: 80.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"simple probability\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"probability_conditional\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"passage\":\"A museum in Samarkand surveyed 200 visitors. Each visitor was classified by age group and by whether it was the visitor's first visit to the museum. The results are shown in the table.\\n\\n| Age group | First visit | Returning visitor | Total |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Under 25 years | 48 | 32 | 80 |\\n| 25 years or older | 54 | 66 | 120 |\\n| Total | 102 | 98 | 200 |\",\"stem\":\"If one of the 200 visitors is selected at random, what is the probability that the visitor is under 25 years and is on a first visit?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{6}{25}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{8}{17}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{3}{5}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{51}{100}$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The table shows 48 visitors who are under 25 and on a first visit, out of 200 visitors in total. The probability is $\\\\frac{48}{200} = \\\\frac{6}{25}$. Choice B is $\\\\frac{48}{102}$, which divides only by the first-time visitors. Choice C is $\\\\frac{48}{80}$, which divides only by the visitors under 25. Choice D is $\\\\frac{102}{200}$, the probability of a first visit for any age.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Jadvalga ko‘ra, jami 200 nafar tashrif buyuruvchidan 48 nafari 25 yoshdan kichik va birinchi marta kelgan. Ehtimollik $\\\\frac{48}{200} = \\\\frac{6}{25}$. B javob $\\\\frac{48}{102}$ — faqat birinchi marta kelganlarga bo‘lgan. C javob $\\\\frac{48}{80}$ — faqat 25 yoshdan kichiklarga bo‘lgan. D javob $\\\\frac{102}{200}$ — har qanday yoshdagi birinchi tashrif ehtimolligi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"two-way table\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"probability_conditional\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"If one of the visitors who are under 25 years is selected at random, what is the probability that the visitor is a returning visitor?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{4}{25}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{16}{49}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{2}{5}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{49}{100}$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The condition limits the selection to the 80 visitors under 25. Of these, 32 are returning visitors, so the probability is $\\\\frac{32}{80} = \\\\frac{2}{5}$. Choice A divides by all 200 visitors. Choice B is $\\\\frac{32}{98}$, which answers the reverse question (the probability that a returning visitor is under 25). Choice D ignores the age condition.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Shart tanlovni 25 yoshdan kichik 80 nafar tashrif buyuruvchi bilan cheklaydi. Ulardan 32 nafari qayta kelgan, demak ehtimollik $\\\\frac{32}{80} = \\\\frac{2}{5}$. A javob barcha 200 nafarga bo‘lgan. B javob $\\\\frac{32}{98}$ — teskari savolning javobi (qayta kelgan tashrif buyuruvchining 25 yoshdan kichik bo‘lish ehtimolligi). D javob yosh shartini hisobga olmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"conditional\"],\"passage\":\"A museum in Samarkand surveyed 200 visitors. Each visitor was classified by age group and by whether it was the visitor's first visit to the museum. The results are shown in the table.\\n\\n| Age group | First visit | Returning visitor | Total |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Under 25 years | 48 | 32 | 80 |\\n| 25 years or older | 54 | 66 | 120 |\\n| Total | 102 | 98 | 200 |\"},{\"skill_code\":\"probability_conditional\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"passage\":\"A museum in Samarkand surveyed 200 visitors. Each visitor was classified by age group and by whether it was the visitor's first visit to the museum. The results are shown in the table.\\n\\n| Age group | First visit | Returning visitor | Total |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Under 25 years | 48 | 32 | 80 |\\n| 25 years or older | 54 | 66 | 120 |\\n| Total | 102 | 98 | 200 |\",\"stem\":\"If one of the returning visitors is selected at random, what is the probability that the visitor is 25 years or older? (Express your answer as a fraction or a decimal.)\",\"spr_answers\":[\"33/49\",\".6734\",\".6735\",\"0.673\",\"66/98\"],\"explanation_en\":\"There are 98 returning visitors, and 66 of them are 25 years or older. The probability is $\\\\frac{66}{98} = \\\\frac{33}{49} \\\\approx 0.6735$. Dividing by 120 (all visitors 25 or older) answers the reverse question, and dividing by 200 ignores the condition. Enter 33/49, .6734, .6735, or 0.673.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qayta kelganlar 98 nafar, ulardan 66 nafari 25 va undan katta yoshda. Ehtimollik $\\\\frac{66}{98} = \\\\frac{33}{49} \\\\approx 0.6735$. 120 ga (25 va undan katta yoshdagi barcha tashrif buyuruvchilarga) bo‘lish teskari savolga javob beradi, 200 ga bo‘lish esa shartni hisobga olmaydi. Javob: 33/49, .6734, .6735 yoki 0.673.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"conditional\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"probability_conditional\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A sports center has 50 members. Of these members, 28 swim, 17 play tennis, and 6 both swim and play tennis. If one member is selected at random, what is the probability that the member swims or plays tennis?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{11}{50}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{33}{50}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{39}{50}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{9}{10}$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Adding $28 + 17$ counts the 6 members who do both activities twice, so the number of members who swim or play tennis is $28 + 17 - 6 = 39$. The probability is $\\\\frac{39}{50}$. Choice D, $\\\\frac{45}{50}$, counts those 6 members twice. Choice B, $\\\\frac{33}{50}$, subtracts the overlap twice. Choice A, $\\\\frac{11}{50}$, is the probability that the member does **neither** activity.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$28 + 17$ ni qo‘shish ikkala mashg‘ulot bilan shug‘ullanadigan 6 kishini ikki marta sanaydi, demak suzadigan yoki tennis o‘ynaydigan a’zolar $28 + 17 - 6 = 39$ nafar. Ehtimollik $\\\\frac{39}{50}$. D javob ($\\\\frac{45}{50}$) o‘sha 6 kishini ikki marta sanagan. B javob ($\\\\frac{33}{50}$) umumiy qismni ikki marta ayirgan. A javob ($\\\\frac{11}{50}$) — a’zoning **hech qaysi** mashg‘ulot bilan shug‘ullanmaslik ehtimolligi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"union\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"probability_conditional\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A textile factory has two machines that operate independently of each other. On a given day, the probability that machine A breaks down is 0.05, and the probability that machine B breaks down is 0.08. What is the probability that **neither** machine breaks down on that day?\",\"option_a\":\"0.004\",\"option_b\":\"0.126\",\"option_c\":\"0.870\",\"option_d\":\"0.874\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The probability that machine A does not break down is $1 - 0.05 = 0.95$, and for machine B it is $1 - 0.08 = 0.92$. Because the machines are independent, the probability that neither breaks down is $0.95 \\\\times 0.92 = 0.874$. Choice A is the probability that **both** break down ($0.05 \\\\times 0.08$). Choice B is $1 - 0.874$, the probability that at least one breaks down. Choice C subtracts the sum of the two probabilities from 1, which ignores the chance that both break down.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"A mashinaning buzilmaslik ehtimolligi $1 - 0.05 = 0.95$, B mashina uchun esa $1 - 0.08 = 0.92$. Mashinalar bir-biriga bog‘liq bo‘lmagani uchun hech biri buzilmaslik ehtimolligi $0.95 \\\\times 0.92 = 0.874$. A javob — **ikkalasi** ham buzilish ehtimolligi ($0.05 \\\\times 0.08$). B javob $1 - 0.874$, ya’ni kamida bittasi buzilish ehtimolligi. C javob ikki ehtimollik yig‘indisini 1 dan ayirgan, bunda ikkalasi birga buzilish holati hisobga olinmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"independent events\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"probability_conditional\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"passage\":\"A researcher tested 300 seeds of two wheat varieties to see whether each seed germinated within 10 days. The results are shown in the table.\\n\\n| Variety | Germinated | Did not germinate | Total |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Variety X | 108 | 12 | 120 |\\n| Variety Y | 153 | 27 | 180 |\\n| Total | 261 | 39 | 300 |\",\"stem\":\"If one of the seeds that did **not** germinate is selected at random, what is the probability that it is a seed of Variety X?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{1}{25}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{1}{10}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{4}{13}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{9}{13}$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The condition restricts the selection to the 39 seeds that did not germinate. Of these, 12 are Variety X seeds, so the probability is $\\\\frac{12}{39} = \\\\frac{4}{13}$. Choice A divides by all 300 seeds. Choice B, $\\\\frac{12}{120}$, is the probability that a Variety X seed did not germinate, which is the reverse condition. Choice D is the probability that the seed is Variety Y.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Shart tanlovni unib chiqmagan 39 ta urug‘ bilan cheklaydi. Ulardan 12 tasi X navi, demak ehtimollik $\\\\frac{12}{39} = \\\\frac{4}{13}$. A javob barcha 300 ta urug‘ga bo‘lgan. B javob ($\\\\frac{12}{120}$) — X navi urug‘ining unib chiqmaslik ehtimolligi, ya’ni teskari shart. D javob — urug‘ning Y navi bo‘lish ehtimolligi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"conditional\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"probability_conditional\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A box contains 12 tea bags: 7 are green tea and 5 are black tea. Two tea bags are taken from the box at random, one after the other, without the first one being put back. What is the probability that both tea bags are green tea?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"7/22\",\".3181\",\".3182\",\"0.318\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The probability that the first bag is green tea is $\\\\frac{7}{12}$. After it is removed, 6 of the remaining 11 bags are green tea, so the probability that the second is green tea is $\\\\frac{6}{11}$. The probability that both are green tea is $\\\\frac{7}{12} \\\\cdot \\\\frac{6}{11} = \\\\frac{42}{132} = \\\\frac{7}{22} \\\\approx 0.3182$. Using $\\\\frac{7}{12} \\\\cdot \\\\frac{7}{12} = \\\\frac{49}{144}$ wrongly assumes the first bag is put back. Enter 7/22, .3181, .3182, or 0.318.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi paketning ko‘k choy bo‘lish ehtimolligi $\\\\frac{7}{12}$. U olingandan keyin qolgan 11 ta paketdan 6 tasi ko‘k choy, demak ikkinchisining ko‘k choy bo‘lish ehtimolligi $\\\\frac{6}{11}$. Ikkalasining ham ko‘k choy bo‘lish ehtimolligi $\\\\frac{7}{12} \\\\cdot \\\\frac{6}{11} = \\\\frac{42}{132} = \\\\frac{7}{22} \\\\approx 0.3182$. $\\\\frac{7}{12} \\\\cdot \\\\frac{7}{12} = \\\\frac{49}{144}$ ni hisoblash birinchi paket qaytarib solinadi deb noto‘g‘ri faraz qiladi. Javob: 7/22, .3181, .3182 yoki 0.318.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"without replacement\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"probability_conditional\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A city surveyed 400 adult residents. Of the residents surveyed, 55% own a car. Of the residents who own a car, 30% use the metro at least once a week. Of the residents who do not own a car, 70% use the metro at least once a week. If one of the surveyed residents who use the metro at least once a week is selected at random, what is the probability that the resident owns a car?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{3}{10}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{33}{200}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{11}{32}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{21}{32}$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Car owners: $0.55 \\\\times 400 = 220$, of whom $0.30 \\\\times 220 = 66$ use the metro weekly. Residents without a car: $400 - 220 = 180$, of whom $0.70 \\\\times 180 = 126$ use the metro weekly. So $66 + 126 = 192$ residents use the metro weekly, and the probability is $\\\\frac{66}{192} = \\\\frac{11}{32}$. Choice A is the probability that a car owner uses the metro, the reverse condition. Choice B is $\\\\frac{66}{400}$, which divides by all residents. Choice D is the probability that a weekly metro user does **not** own a car.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Mashina egalari: $0.55 \\\\times 400 = 220$, ulardan $0.30 \\\\times 220 = 66$ nafari har hafta metrodan foydalanadi. Mashinasi yo‘qlar: $400 - 220 = 180$, ulardan $0.70 \\\\times 180 = 126$ nafari har hafta metrodan foydalanadi. Demak har hafta metrodan $66 + 126 = 192$ nafar foydalanadi va ehtimollik $\\\\frac{66}{192} = \\\\frac{11}{32}$. A javob — mashina egasining metrodan foydalanish ehtimolligi, ya’ni teskari shart. B javob $\\\\frac{66}{400}$ — barcha aholiga bo‘lgan. D javob — har hafta metrodan foydalanuvchining mashinasi **yo‘q** bo‘lish ehtimolligi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"conditional\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"probability_conditional\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"passage\":\"Every participant in a two-week coding workshop was in either Group A or Group B. The table shows the numbers of participants in each group who did and did not complete the final project, where $x$ is a positive integer.\\n\\n| Group | Completed | Did not complete |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Group A | $x$ | 18 |\\n| Group B | 45 | 15 |\",\"stem\":\"If one of the participants who completed the final project is selected at random, the probability that the participant is in Group A is $\\\\frac{3}{8}$. How many participants were in the workshop?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"105\"],\"explanation_en\":\"There are $x + 45$ participants who completed the project, and $x$ of them are in Group A, so $\\\\frac{x}{x + 45} = \\\\frac{3}{8}$. Then $8x = 3x + 135$, so $5x = 135$ and $x = 27$. The total number of participants is $27 + 18 + 45 + 15 = 105$. Answering 27 gives only the value of $x$. Enter 105.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Loyihani yakunlaganlar $x + 45$ nafar, ulardan $x$ nafari A guruhda, demak $\\\\frac{x}{x + 45} = \\\\frac{3}{8}$. U holda $8x = 3x + 135$, ya’ni $5x = 135$ va $x = 27$. Ishtirokchilarning umumiy soni $27 + 18 + 45 + 15 = 105$. 27 deb javob berish faqat $x$ ning qiymatini beradi. Javob: 105.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"two-way table unknown\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"probability_conditional\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A courier company finds that each of its deliveries arrives late with probability 0.1, independently of the other deliveries. A customer orders 3 deliveries. What is the probability that **at least one** of the 3 deliveries arrives late?\",\"option_a\":\"0.001\",\"option_b\":\"0.271\",\"option_c\":\"0.300\",\"option_d\":\"0.729\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"It is easiest to use the complement. The probability that one delivery is on time is $1 - 0.1 = 0.9$, so the probability that all 3 are on time is $0.9^3 = 0.729$. The probability that at least one is late is $1 - 0.729 = 0.271$. Choice A is the probability that **all three** are late ($0.1^3$). Choice C adds $0.1$ three times, which counts the cases with two or three late deliveries more than once. Choice D is the probability that none is late.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"To‘ldiruvchi hodisadan foydalanish eng qulay. Bitta yetkazib berishning o‘z vaqtida bo‘lish ehtimolligi $1 - 0.1 = 0.9$, demak uchalasining ham o‘z vaqtida bo‘lish ehtimolligi $0.9^3 = 0.729$. Kamida bittasining kechikish ehtimolligi $1 - 0.729 = 0.271$. A javob — **uchalasining ham** kechikish ehtimolligi ($0.1^3$). C javob $0.1$ ni uch marta qo‘shgan, bunda ikki yoki uchta kechikish holatlari bir necha marta sanaladi. D javob — hech biri kechikmaslik ehtimolligi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":110,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"complement\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"probability_conditional\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"passage\":\"The table shows the numbers of 10th-grade and 11th-grade students in a school's chess club and art club, where $n$ is a positive integer. No student is in both clubs.\\n\\n| Club | Grade 10 | Grade 11 |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Chess | 12 | 18 |\\n| Art | 20 | $n$ |\",\"stem\":\"The probability that a randomly selected member of the chess club is in grade 11 is equal to the probability that a randomly selected member of the art club is in grade 11. What is the value of $n$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"30\"],\"explanation_en\":\"For the chess club, the probability is $\\\\frac{18}{12 + 18} = \\\\frac{18}{30} = 0.6$. For the art club, it is $\\\\frac{n}{20 + n}$. Setting them equal: $\\\\frac{n}{20 + n} = 0.6$, so $n = 12 + 0.6n$, which gives $0.4n = 12$ and $n = 30$. A common error is to use $\\\\frac{n}{20} = 0.6$ (dividing by grade 10 only), which gives 12. Enter 30.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Shaxmat to‘garagi uchun ehtimollik $\\\\frac{18}{12 + 18} = \\\\frac{18}{30} = 0.6$. San’at to‘garagi uchun u $\\\\frac{n}{20 + n}$. Ularni tenglaymiz: $\\\\frac{n}{20 + n} = 0.6$, demak $n = 12 + 0.6n$, bundan $0.4n = 12$ va $n = 30$. Ko‘p uchraydigan xato — $\\\\frac{n}{20} = 0.6$ deb olish (faqat 10-sinfga bo‘lish), bu 12 ni beradi. Javob: 30.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"two-way table unknown\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"ratios_rates_proportions_units\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A machine fills $240$ bottles of juice every $8$ minutes. At this rate, how many bottles does the machine fill in $1$ hour?\",\"option_a\":\"$30$\",\"option_b\":\"$1{,}800$\",\"option_c\":\"$1{,}920$\",\"option_d\":\"$14{,}400$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The machine fills $\\\\frac{240}{8} = 30$ bottles per minute. One hour is $60$ minutes, so it fills $30 \\\\times 60 = 1{,}800$ bottles.\\n\\nChoice A is the number of bottles per minute, not per hour. Choice C multiplies $240$ by $8$ instead of dividing. Choice D multiplies $240$ by $60$, treating $240$ as the number of bottles per minute.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Mashina bir daqiqada $\\\\frac{240}{8} = 30$ ta shisha to‘ldiradi. Bir soat $60$ daqiqa, demak u $30 \\\\times 60 = 1{,}800$ ta shisha to‘ldiradi.\\n\\nA variant — bir daqiqadagi shishalar soni, bir soatdagi emas. C variant $240$ ni $8$ ga bo‘lish o‘rniga ko‘paytirgan. D variant $240$ ni $60$ ga ko‘paytirgan, go‘yo $240$ ta shisha bir daqiqada to‘ldirilgandek.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"rate-time\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"ratios_rates_proportions_units\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"Two friends share $360{,}000$ soums in the ratio $4 : 5$. How many thousand soums does the friend with the larger share receive?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"200\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The ratio $4 : 5$ splits the money into $4 + 5 = 9$ equal parts. One part is $360{,}000 \\\\div 9 = 40{,}000$ soums, so the larger share is $5 \\\\times 40{,}000 = 200{,}000$ soums, or $200$ thousand soums. Answering $160$ gives the smaller share; dividing $360$ by $5$ ignores the total number of parts.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$4 : 5$ nisbat pulni $4 + 5 = 9$ ta teng qismga bo‘ladi. Bir qism $360{,}000 \\\\div 9 = 40{,}000$ so‘m, demak katta ulush $5 \\\\times 40{,}000 = 200{,}000$ so‘m, ya’ni $200$ ming so‘m. $160$ — bu kichik ulush; $360$ ni $5$ ga bo‘lish esa qismlarning umumiy sonini hisobga olmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"proportional-sharing\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"ratios_rates_proportions_units\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A recipe for one batch of pancakes uses $250$ milliliters of milk. How many batches can be made with $2$ liters of milk? ($1$ liter $= 1{,}000$ milliliters)\",\"option_a\":\"$0.125$\",\"option_b\":\"$0.8$\",\"option_c\":\"$8$\",\"option_d\":\"$500$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Convert liters to milliliters: $2$ liters $= 2{,}000$ milliliters. Then $2{,}000 \\\\div 250 = 8$ batches.\\n\\nChoice A divides in the wrong order ($250 \\\\div 2{,}000$). Choice B uses $1$ liter $= 100$ milliliters ($200 \\\\div 250$). Choice D multiplies $250$ by $2$, which is the milk needed for two batches.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Litrni millilitrga aylantiramiz: $2$ litr $= 2{,}000$ millilitr. U holda $2{,}000 \\\\div 250 = 8$ ta porsiya.\\n\\nA variant teskari tartibda bo‘lgan ($250 \\\\div 2{,}000$). B variant $1$ litr $= 100$ millilitr deb olgan ($200 \\\\div 250$). D variant $250$ ni $2$ ga ko‘paytirgan — bu ikki porsiya uchun kerak bo‘ladigan sut.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"unit-conversion\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"ratios_rates_proportions_units\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"On a map, a distance of $3$ centimeters represents an actual distance of $45$ kilometers. Two towns are $7$ centimeters apart on the map. What is the actual distance, in kilometers, between the two towns?\",\"option_a\":\"$15$\",\"option_b\":\"$49$\",\"option_c\":\"$105$\",\"option_d\":\"$315$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Each centimeter on the map represents $45 \\\\div 3 = 15$ kilometers. So $7$ centimeters represent $7 \\\\times 15 = 105$ kilometers.\\n\\nChoice A is the number of kilometers per centimeter. Choice B adds the difference $7 - 3 = 4$ to $45$ instead of scaling. Choice D multiplies $45$ by $7$ without dividing by $3$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Xaritadagi har bir santimetr $45 \\\\div 3 = 15$ kilometrga to‘g‘ri keladi. Demak, $7$ santimetr $7 \\\\times 15 = 105$ kilometrni bildiradi.\\n\\nA variant — bir santimetrga to‘g‘ri keladigan kilometrlar. B variant masshtablash o‘rniga $45$ ga $7 - 3 = 4$ ni qo‘shgan. D variant $45$ ni $7$ ga ko‘paytirib, $3$ ga bo‘lmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"proportion\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"ratios_rates_proportions_units\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A shop sells rice in four package sizes. Which package has the lowest price per kilogram?\",\"option_a\":\"A $2$-kilogram package for $30{,}000$ soums\",\"option_b\":\"A $3$-kilogram package for $43{,}500$ soums\",\"option_c\":\"A $10$-kilogram package for $148{,}000$ soums\",\"option_d\":\"A $5$-kilogram package for $70{,}000$ soums\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Divide each price by the mass. $2$ kg: $30{,}000 \\\\div 2 = 15{,}000$ soums per kilogram. $3$ kg: $43{,}500 \\\\div 3 = 14{,}500$. $10$ kg: $148{,}000 \\\\div 10 = 14{,}800$. $5$ kg: $70{,}000 \\\\div 5 = 14{,}000$. The lowest price per kilogram is $14{,}000$ soums, for the $5$-kilogram package.\\n\\nChoice A has the lowest total price but the highest price per kilogram. Choice C is the largest package, but a bigger package is not automatically cheaper per kilogram. Choice B costs $500$ soums more per kilogram than choice D.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Har bir narxni massaga bo‘lamiz. $2$ kg: $30{,}000 \\\\div 2 = 15{,}000$ so‘m/kg. $3$ kg: $43{,}500 \\\\div 3 = 14{,}500$. $10$ kg: $148{,}000 \\\\div 10 = 14{,}800$. $5$ kg: $70{,}000 \\\\div 5 = 14{,}000$. Eng arzon narx — kilogrami $14{,}000$ so‘m, ya’ni $5$ kilogrammlik qadoq.\\n\\nA variantning umumiy narxi eng kichik, lekin bir kilogrami eng qimmat. C variant eng katta qadoq, ammo katta qadoq har doim ham kilogrami bo‘yicha arzon bo‘lavermaydi. B variant kilogrami D variantdan $500$ so‘m qimmat.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"unit-rate\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"ratios_rates_proportions_units\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A cyclist rides at a constant speed of $18$ kilometers per hour. How many minutes does it take the cyclist to ride $6$ kilometers?\",\"option_a\":\"$3$\",\"option_b\":\"$20$\",\"option_c\":\"$33$\",\"option_d\":\"$108$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Time $=$ distance $\\\\div$ speed $= \\\\frac{6}{18} = \\\\frac{1}{3}$ hour. One hour is $60$ minutes, so the time is $\\\\frac{1}{3} \\\\times 60 = 20$ minutes.\\n\\nChoice A divides the speed by the distance ($18 \\\\div 6$). Choice C reads $0.33$ hour as $33$ minutes, but hours are not divided into $100$ minutes. Choice D multiplies the speed by the distance.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Vaqt $=$ masofa $\\\\div$ tezlik $= \\\\frac{6}{18} = \\\\frac{1}{3}$ soat. Bir soat $60$ daqiqa, demak vaqt $\\\\frac{1}{3} \\\\times 60 = 20$ daqiqa.\\n\\nA variant tezlikni masofaga bo‘lgan ($18 \\\\div 6$). C variant $0.33$ soatni $33$ daqiqa deb o‘qigan, lekin soat $100$ daqiqaga bo‘linmaydi. D variant tezlikni masofaga ko‘paytirgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"rate-time\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"ratios_rates_proportions_units\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A chess club has $42$ members, and the ratio of boys to girls in the club is $3 : 4$. How many boys must join the club so that the ratio of boys to girls becomes $1 : 1$? (No girls join or leave.)\",\"spr_answers\":[\"6\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The ratio $3 : 4$ splits the $42$ members into $7$ parts of $6$ members each, so there are $3 \\\\times 6 = 18$ boys and $4 \\\\times 6 = 24$ girls. For a $1 : 1$ ratio, the number of boys must equal the number of girls, $24$. So $24 - 18 = 6$ boys must join. Answering $1$ (the difference of the ratio numbers, $4 - 3$) ignores the size of each part.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$3 : 4$ nisbat $42$ a’zoni har birida $6$ kishidan iborat $7$ qismga bo‘ladi, demak $3 \\\\times 6 = 18$ o‘g‘il va $4 \\\\times 6 = 24$ qiz bor. $1 : 1$ nisbat uchun o‘g‘illar soni qizlar soniga, ya’ni $24$ ga teng bo‘lishi kerak. Demak, $24 - 18 = 6$ o‘g‘il qo‘shilishi kerak. $1$ deb javob berish (nisbat sonlarining ayirmasi $4 - 3$) har bir qism kattaligini hisobga olmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"ratio-change\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"ratios_rates_proportions_units\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The floor of a storage room has an area of $12$ square meters. What is the area of the floor in square centimeters? ($1$ meter $= 100$ centimeters)\",\"option_a\":\"$1{,}200$\",\"option_b\":\"$12{,}000$\",\"option_c\":\"$120{,}000$\",\"option_d\":\"$1{,}200{,}000$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"One square meter is a square $100$ cm by $100$ cm, so $1$ m$^2 = 100 \\\\times 100 = 10{,}000$ cm$^2$. Then $12$ m$^2 = 12 \\\\times 10{,}000 = 120{,}000$ cm$^2$.\\n\\nChoice A converts with a factor of $100$, as if the units were not squared. Choice B uses a factor of $1{,}000$. Choice D uses a factor of $100{,}000$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bir kvadrat metr — tomoni $100$ sm bo‘lgan kvadrat, shuning uchun $1$ m$^2 = 100 \\\\times 100 = 10{,}000$ sm$^2$. U holda $12$ m$^2 = 12 \\\\times 10{,}000 = 120{,}000$ sm$^2$.\\n\\nA variant birliklar kvadratda ekanini hisobga olmay, $100$ ga ko‘paytirgan. B variant $1{,}000$ ga ko‘paytirgan. D variant $100{,}000$ ga ko‘paytirgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"squared-units\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"ratios_rates_proportions_units\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A leaking faucet loses water at a constant rate of $0.25$ milliliter per second. At this rate, how many liters of water does the faucet lose in one day? ($1$ liter $= 1{,}000$ milliliters)\",\"spr_answers\":[\"21.6\",\"108/5\"],\"explanation_en\":\"One day has $24 \\\\times 60 \\\\times 60 = 86{,}400$ seconds. The faucet loses $0.25 \\\\times 86{,}400 = 21{,}600$ milliliters, which is $21{,}600 \\\\div 1{,}000 = 21.6$ liters. Forgetting one of the factors of $60$ gives $0.36$ liter, and forgetting to convert to liters gives $21{,}600$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bir sutkada $24 \\\\times 60 \\\\times 60 = 86{,}400$ soniya bor. Jo‘mrakdan $0.25 \\\\times 86{,}400 = 21{,}600$ millilitr suv oqadi, bu $21{,}600 \\\\div 1{,}000 = 21.6$ litr. $60$ ko‘paytuvchilaridan birini unutsak, $0.36$ litr chiqadi; litrga aylantirishni unutsak, $21{,}600$ chiqadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"unit-conversion\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"ratios_rates_proportions_units\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A type of granite has a density of $2.7$ grams per cubic centimeter. What is the mass, in kilograms, of a granite block with a volume of $350$ cubic centimeters? ($1$ kilogram $= 1{,}000$ grams)\",\"option_a\":\"$0.13$\",\"option_b\":\"$0.945$\",\"option_c\":\"$9.45$\",\"option_d\":\"$945$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Mass $=$ density $\\\\times$ volume $= 2.7 \\\\times 350 = 945$ grams. In kilograms, $945 \\\\div 1{,}000 = 0.945$.\\n\\nChoice A divides the volume by the density ($350 \\\\div 2.7 \\\\approx 130$ grams). Choice C divides by $100$ instead of $1{,}000$. Choice D is the mass in grams, not kilograms.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Massa $=$ zichlik $\\\\times$ hajm $= 2.7 \\\\times 350 = 945$ gramm. Kilogrammda $945 \\\\div 1{,}000 = 0.945$.\\n\\nA variant hajmni zichlikka bo‘lgan ($350 \\\\div 2.7 \\\\approx 130$ gramm). C variant $1{,}000$ ga emas, $100$ ga bo‘lgan. D variant — massa grammda, kilogrammda emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"density\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"ratios_rates_proportions_units\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A team of $6$ workers, all working at the same constant rate, can paint a school building in $15$ days. At this rate, how many days would it take a team of $9$ such workers to paint the same building?\",\"option_a\":\"$9$\",\"option_b\":\"$10$\",\"option_c\":\"$12$\",\"option_d\":\"$22.5$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The whole job takes $6 \\\\times 15 = 90$ worker-days. With $9$ workers, it takes $90 \\\\div 9 = 10$ days. More workers finish faster, so the answer must be less than $15$.\\n\\nChoice A uses the number of workers as the answer. Choice C subtracts the $3$ extra workers from $15$ days. Choice D treats the relationship as direct: $15 \\\\times \\\\frac{9}{6}$, which wrongly says more workers need more days.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Butun ish $6 \\\\times 15 = 90$ ishchi-kun talab qiladi. $9$ ishchi bilan u $90 \\\\div 9 = 10$ kunda bajariladi. Ishchilar ko‘paysa, ish tezroq tugaydi, shuning uchun javob $15$ dan kichik bo‘lishi kerak.\\n\\nA variant ishchilar sonini javob deb olgan. C variant $15$ kundan qo‘shimcha $3$ ishchini ayirgan. D variant bog‘lanishni to‘g‘ri proporsional deb olgan: $15 \\\\times \\\\frac{9}{6}$, bu ishchilar ko‘paysa, kunlar ham ko‘payadi degan xato xulosa.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"inverse-proportion\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"ratios_rates_proportions_units\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Suppose that at a certain bank, $1$ US dollar can be exchanged for $12{,}600$ soums and $1$ euro can be exchanged for $13{,}650$ soums. Based on these rates, $390$ US dollars are worth how many euros?\",\"option_a\":\"$30$\",\"option_b\":\"$360$\",\"option_c\":\"$390$\",\"option_d\":\"$422.5$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"First convert dollars to soums: $390 \\\\times 12{,}600 = 4{,}914{,}000$ soums. Then convert soums to euros: $4{,}914{,}000 \\\\div 13{,}650 = 360$ euros. A euro is worth more soums than a dollar, so the number of euros should be less than $390$.\\n\\nChoice A converts only the difference between the two rates ($390 \\\\times 1{,}050 \\\\div 13{,}650$). Choice C assumes a dollar and a euro are worth the same. Choice D uses the rates the wrong way around ($390 \\\\times 13{,}650 \\\\div 12{,}600$).\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Avval dollarni so‘mga aylantiramiz: $390 \\\\times 12{,}600 = 4{,}914{,}000$ so‘m. Keyin so‘mni yevroga aylantiramiz: $4{,}914{,}000 \\\\div 13{,}650 = 360$ yevro. Yevro dollardan qimmatroq, shuning uchun yevrolar soni $390$ dan kam bo‘lishi kerak.\\n\\nA variant faqat ikki kurs orasidagi farqni aylantirgan ($390 \\\\times 1{,}050 \\\\div 13{,}650$). C variant dollar va yevroni teng qiymatli deb olgan. D variant kurslarni teskari ishlatgan ($390 \\\\times 13{,}650 \\\\div 12{,}600$).\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"unit-conversion\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"ratios_rates_proportions_units\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A car accelerates at a constant rate of $2.5$ meters per second squared ($2.5$ m/s$^2$). What is this acceleration in kilometers per hour squared (km/h$^2$)? ($1$ kilometer $= 1{,}000$ meters and $1$ hour $= 3{,}600$ seconds)\",\"spr_answers\":[\"32400\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Convert meters to kilometers: $2.5$ m $= 0.0025$ km. The unit $\\\\text{s}^2$ is in the denominator, and $1$ h$^2 = 3{,}600^2$ s$^2 = 12{,}960{,}000$ s$^2$, so $1$ s$^2 = \\\\frac{1}{12{,}960{,}000}$ h$^2$. Then $2.5 \\\\text{ m/s}^2 = \\\\frac{0.0025 \\\\text{ km}}{\\\\frac{1}{12{,}960{,}000} \\\\text{ h}^2} = 0.0025 \\\\times 12{,}960{,}000 = 32{,}400$ km/h$^2$. Multiplying by $3{,}600$ only once (as for a speed) gives $9$, which is wrong.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Metrni kilometrga aylantiramiz: $2.5$ m $= 0.0025$ km. $\\\\text{s}^2$ maxrajda turibdi va $1$ h$^2 = 3{,}600^2$ s$^2 = 12{,}960{,}000$ s$^2$, demak $1$ s$^2 = \\\\frac{1}{12{,}960{,}000}$ h$^2$. U holda $2.5 \\\\text{ m/s}^2 = \\\\frac{0.0025 \\\\text{ km}}{\\\\frac{1}{12{,}960{,}000} \\\\text{ h}^2} = 0.0025 \\\\times 12{,}960{,}000 = 32{,}400$ km/h$^2$. $3{,}600$ ga faqat bir marta ko‘paytirish (tezlikdagidek) $9$ ni beradi — bu xato.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"squared-units\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"ratios_rates_proportions_units\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A water tank in the shape of a rectangular prism is $120$ centimeters long, $0.8$ meter wide, and $500$ millimeters high. Water flows into the empty tank at a constant rate of $2.5$ liters every $30$ seconds. How many minutes does it take to fill the tank completely? ($1$ liter $= 1{,}000$ cubic centimeters)\",\"option_a\":\"$9.6$\",\"option_b\":\"$48$\",\"option_c\":\"$96$\",\"option_d\":\"$960$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Convert every dimension to centimeters: $120$ cm, $0.8$ m $= 80$ cm, and $500$ mm $= 50$ cm. The volume is $120 \\\\times 80 \\\\times 50 = 480{,}000$ cm$^3 = 480$ liters. The flow rate is $2.5$ liters per $30$ seconds, or $5$ liters per minute. So the time is $480 \\\\div 5 = 96$ minutes.\\n\\nChoice A uses $1$ m$^3 = 100$ liters ($48 \\\\div 5$). Choice B uses a rate of $10$ liters per minute, multiplying by $4$ instead of $2$ when converting the rate. Choice D uses $1$ m$^3 = 10{,}000$ liters; in fact $1$ m$^3 = 1{,}000$ liters.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Barcha o‘lchamlarni santimetrga aylantiramiz: $120$ sm, $0.8$ m $= 80$ sm va $500$ mm $= 50$ sm. Hajm $120 \\\\times 80 \\\\times 50 = 480{,}000$ sm$^3 = 480$ litr. Oqim tezligi $30$ soniyada $2.5$ litr, ya’ni daqiqasiga $5$ litr. Demak, vaqt $480 \\\\div 5 = 96$ daqiqa.\\n\\nA variant $1$ m$^3 = 100$ litr deb olgan ($48 \\\\div 5$). B variant tezlikni aylantirishda $2$ ga emas, $4$ ga ko‘paytirib, daqiqasiga $10$ litr deb olgan. D variant $1$ m$^3 = 10{,}000$ litr deb olgan; aslida $1$ m$^3 = 1{,}000$ litr.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"cubed-units\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"ratios_rates_proportions_units\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A jar contains only red beads and blue beads, and the ratio of red beads to blue beads is $5 : 3$. After $10$ red beads are removed and $6$ blue beads are added, the jar contains equal numbers of red beads and blue beads. How many beads were in the jar originally?\",\"option_a\":\"$16$\",\"option_b\":\"$40$\",\"option_c\":\"$60$\",\"option_d\":\"$64$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Let the original numbers be $5k$ red and $3k$ blue. After the changes: $5k - 10 = 3k + 6$, so $2k = 16$ and $k = 8$. Originally there were $5(8) = 40$ red and $3(8) = 24$ blue beads, a total of $64$. Check: $40 - 10 = 30$ and $24 + 6 = 30$.\\n\\nChoice A is $2k$, not the number of beads. Choice B is the original number of red beads only. Choice C is the total after the changes ($30 + 30$), not the original total.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Dastlabki sonlar $5k$ ta qizil va $3k$ ta ko‘k bo‘lsin. O‘zgarishlardan keyin: $5k - 10 = 3k + 6$, demak $2k = 16$ va $k = 8$. Dastlab $5(8) = 40$ ta qizil va $3(8) = 24$ ta ko‘k munchoq bo‘lgan, jami $64$ ta. Tekshiruv: $40 - 10 = 30$ va $24 + 6 = 30$.\\n\\nA variant — $2k$, munchoqlar soni emas. B variant — faqat dastlabki qizil munchoqlar soni. C variant — o‘zgarishlardan keyingi jami ($30 + 30$), dastlabki emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"ratio-change\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"ratios_rates_proportions_units\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A rectangular room has a floor that is $4.5$ meters by $3.6$ meters, and its walls are $2.7$ meters high. Malika will paint all four walls with two coats of paint. One liter of paint covers $9.6$ square meters for one coat, and the paint is sold only in $2.5$-liter cans. Ignoring doors and windows, what is the minimum number of cans of paint Malika must buy?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"4\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The four walls together form a strip whose length is the perimeter of the floor, $2(4.5 + 3.6) = 16.2$ meters, and whose height is $2.7$ meters, so their area is $16.2 \\\\times 2.7 = 43.74$ m$^2$. Two coats cover $2 \\\\times 43.74 = 87.48$ m$^2$, which needs $87.48 \\\\div 9.6 = 9.1125$ liters. That is $9.1125 \\\\div 2.5 = 3.645$ cans, and since only whole cans are sold, she must buy $4$ cans. Using one coat gives $2$ cans, and using the floor area instead of the walls gives a much smaller amount.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"To‘rtta devor birgalikda uzunligi pol perimetriga, ya’ni $2(4.5 + 3.6) = 16.2$ metrga, balandligi $2.7$ metrga teng bo‘lgan tasmani hosil qiladi, shuning uchun ularning yuzi $16.2 \\\\times 2.7 = 43.74$ m$^2$. Ikki qatlam $2 \\\\times 43.74 = 87.48$ m$^2$ ni qoplaydi va buning uchun $87.48 \\\\div 9.6 = 9.1125$ litr bo‘yoq kerak. Bu $9.1125 \\\\div 2.5 = 3.645$ quti, faqat butun qutilar sotilgani uchun $4$ quti sotib olish kerak. Bir qatlam deb hisoblasak, $2$ quti chiqadi; devorlar o‘rniga pol yuzini olsak, ancha kam miqdor chiqadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"multi-step-rate\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Jupiter is the largest planet in the solar system.\\n- The Great Red Spot is a giant storm in Jupiter's atmosphere.\\n- The storm is wider than the entire planet Earth.\\n- Astronomers have observed the storm for more than 150 years.\\n- Measurements show that the storm has been shrinking over recent decades.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to emphasize the size of the Great Red Spot. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"The Great Red Spot, a storm in Jupiter's atmosphere, is wider than the entire planet Earth.\",\"option_b\":\"Astronomers have been observing the Great Red Spot, a storm on Jupiter, for more than 150 years.\",\"option_c\":\"Jupiter, the planet on which the Great Red Spot is found, is the largest planet in the solar system.\",\"option_d\":\"Measurements show that the Great Red Spot, a storm on Jupiter, has been shrinking over recent decades.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to emphasize how big the Great Red Spot is. Choice A does this by stating that the storm is wider than the entire planet Earth.\\n\\nChoice B emphasizes how long the storm has been observed, not its size. Choice C emphasizes the size of Jupiter, not the size of the storm. Choice D describes a change in the storm's size over time but does not emphasize how large it is.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — Katta Qizil Dog‘ning qanchalik kattaligini ta’kidlash. A buni bajaradi: bo‘ron butun Yer sayyorasidan ham kengroq ekanini aytadi.\\n\\nB bo‘ronning hajmini emas, qancha vaqtdan beri kuzatilayotganini ta’kidlaydi. C bo‘ronning emas, Yupiterning kattaligini ta’kidlaydi. D bo‘ron hajmining vaqt o‘tishi bilan o‘zgarishini tasvirlaydi, lekin uning qanchalik katta ekanini ta’kidlamaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"emphasize-detail\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- The Registan is a public square in the center of Samarkand, Uzbekistan.\\n- Three madrasas (Islamic schools) stand on three sides of the square.\\n- The Ulugh Beg Madrasa was built from 1417 to 1420.\\n- The Sher-Dor Madrasa was built from 1619 to 1636.\\n- The Tilya-Kori Madrasa was built from 1646 to 1660; inside it is a mosque decorated with gold.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to identify the oldest of the three madrasas on the Registan. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"The Tilya-Kori Madrasa, built from 1646 to 1660, contains a mosque decorated with gold.\",\"option_b\":\"The Registan, a public square in Samarkand, is bordered on three sides by madrasas.\",\"option_c\":\"Of the three madrasas on the Registan, the oldest is the Ulugh Beg Madrasa, built from 1417 to 1420.\",\"option_d\":\"The Sher-Dor and Tilya-Kori Madrasas on the Registan were both built in the seventeenth century.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to identify which madrasa is the oldest. Choice C names the Ulugh Beg Madrasa as the oldest of the three and gives its construction dates, 1417 to 1420, which are earlier than those of the other two.\\n\\nChoice A describes the Tilya-Kori Madrasa, which is actually the newest of the three. Choice B describes the square without naming any madrasa. Choice D gives the century of the two newer madrasas but never says which madrasa is the oldest.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — qaysi madrasa eng qadimgi ekanini aniqlash. C uchtasi orasida eng qadimgisi Ulug‘bek madrasasi ekanini aytadi va uning qurilish yillarini — 1417–1420 — keltiradi; bu boshqa ikkalasidan oldinroq.\\n\\nA Tillakori madrasasini tasvirlaydi, u esa aslida uchtasining eng yangisi. B maydonni tasvirlaydi, lekin birorta madrasani nomlamaydi. D ikki yangiroq madrasa qaysi asrda qurilganini aytadi, lekin qaysi biri eng qadimgi ekanini aytmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"identify-detail\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Economist Sardor Karimov studied how shoppers at a large bazaar in Tashkent decide where to buy fruit and vegetables.\\n- He surveyed 400 shoppers over three months.\\n- 68 percent of the shoppers said they usually buy from the same one or two sellers.\\n- Most of these shoppers said they trust those sellers to offer fresh produce at a fair price.\\n- Only 12 percent of the shoppers said they always compare prices at several stalls before buying.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to emphasize that most shoppers in Karimov's study stay loyal to particular sellers. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Karimov surveyed 400 shoppers at a large bazaar in Tashkent over a period of three months.\",\"option_b\":\"Only 12 percent of the shoppers in Karimov's study said they always compare prices at several stalls before buying.\",\"option_c\":\"Karimov wanted to learn how shoppers at a Tashkent bazaar decide where to buy fruit and vegetables.\",\"option_d\":\"In Karimov's survey, 68 percent of shoppers said they usually buy from the same one or two sellers, often because they trust them to offer fresh produce at a fair price.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to emphasize that most shoppers stay loyal to particular sellers. Choice D does this directly: 68 percent—a majority—said they usually buy from the same one or two sellers, and the choice adds their reason.\\n\\nChoice A describes how the study was done, not what it found. Choice B reports how few shoppers always compare prices, but that does not show that most shoppers return to the same sellers. Choice C states the purpose of the study, not its result.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — tadqiqotdagi xaridorlarning aksariyati muayyan sotuvchilarga sodiq qolishini ta’kidlash. D buni to‘g‘ridan-to‘g‘ri bajaradi: 68 foiz — ya’ni ko‘pchilik — odatda bir yoki ikki sotuvchidan xarid qilishini aytgan, variant ularning sababini ham qo‘shadi.\\n\\nA tadqiqot qanday o‘tkazilganini tasvirlaydi, natijasini emas. B narxlarni doim solishtiradigan xaridorlar qanchalik kamligini aytadi, lekin bu ko‘pchilik o‘sha sotuvchilarga qaytishini ko‘rsatmaydi. C tadqiqot maqsadini aytadi, natijasini emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"present-finding\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Honeybees that find a good source of food return to the hive and perform a movement called the waggle dance.\\n- During the dance, the bee runs in a straight line while shaking its body from side to side.\\n- The angle of this run tells other bees the direction of the food relative to the Sun.\\n- The length of time the bee spends waggling tells other bees how far away the food is.\\n- Austrian scientist Karl von Frisch decoded the dance and shared the 1973 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to explain how the waggle dance tells other bees the distance to a food source. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Karl von Frisch, an Austrian scientist, shared the 1973 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine after decoding the honeybee's waggle dance.\",\"option_b\":\"In the waggle dance, the length of time a honeybee spends waggling tells other bees how far away the food is.\",\"option_c\":\"During the waggle dance, a honeybee runs in a straight line while shaking its body from side to side.\",\"option_d\":\"The angle of a honeybee's waggle run tells other bees the direction of the food relative to the Sun.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to explain how the dance communicates distance. Choice B does this: the length of time the bee spends waggling tells other bees how far away the food is.\\n\\nChoice A tells who decoded the dance, not how it shows distance. Choice C describes the movement of the dance but not what it communicates. Choice D explains how the dance shows direction, not distance.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — raqs masofani qanday bildirishini tushuntirish. B buni bajaradi: asalarining tebranishga sarflagan vaqti boshqa asalarilarga ozuqa qanchalik uzoqda ekanini bildiradi.\\n\\nA raqsni kim ochib berganini aytadi, u masofani qanday ko‘rsatishini emas. C raqs harakatini tasvirlaydi, lekin u nimani bildirishini aytmaydi. D raqs masofani emas, yo‘nalishni qanday ko‘rsatishini tushuntiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"explain-detail\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- The Venus flytrap is a plant that catches and digests insects.\\n- In the wild, it grows only in wetlands in North Carolina and South Carolina in the United States.\\n- Each trap is formed by two lobes at the end of a leaf.\\n- Tiny trigger hairs grow on the inner surface of each lobe.\\n- The trap snaps shut when an insect touches the trigger hairs twice within about 20 seconds.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to specify where the Venus flytrap grows in the wild. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"The Venus flytrap catches insects with traps formed by two lobes at the end of a leaf.\",\"option_b\":\"A Venus flytrap's trap snaps shut when its trigger hairs are touched twice within about 20 seconds.\",\"option_c\":\"In the wild, the Venus flytrap grows only in wetlands in North Carolina and South Carolina.\",\"option_d\":\"The Venus flytrap is a plant that catches and digests insects.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to say where the plant grows in the wild. Choice C gives that location: wetlands in North Carolina and South Carolina.\\n\\nChoices A and B describe how the trap is built and how it closes, and choice D says what the plant does. None of them mentions where the plant grows.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — o‘simlik tabiatda qayerda o‘sishini aytish. C aynan shu joyni ko‘rsatadi: Shimoliy va Janubiy Karolinadagi botqoqliklar.\\n\\nA va B tuzoq qanday tuzilgani va qanday yopilishini, D esa o‘simlik nima qilishini tasvirlaydi. Ularning hech biri o‘simlik qayerda o‘sishini aytmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"specify-location\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Suzanis are large decorative textiles embroidered by hand in Central Asia.\\n- The name comes from the Persian word for “needle.”\\n- Suzanis were traditionally made by women as part of a bride's dowry.\\n- Common designs on suzanis include flowers, vines, and sun and moon discs.\\n- Bukhara, Samarkand, Nurata, and Shahrisabz are among the places known for suzani making.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to give examples of designs commonly found on suzanis. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Suzanis are often decorated with designs such as flowers, vines, and sun and moon discs.\",\"option_b\":\"Bukhara, Samarkand, Nurata, and Shahrisabz are among the places known for suzani making.\",\"option_c\":\"The name “suzani” comes from the Persian word for “needle.”\",\"option_d\":\"Suzanis, large embroidered textiles, were traditionally made by women as part of a bride's dowry.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to give examples of designs found on suzanis. Choice A lists three such designs: flowers, vines, and sun and moon discs.\\n\\nChoice B gives examples, but of places where suzanis are made, not of designs. Choice C explains where the name comes from. Choice D explains who traditionally made suzanis and why.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — so‘zanalarda ko‘p uchraydigan naqshlarga misollar keltirish. A uchta shunday naqshni sanab o‘tadi: gullar, novdalar hamda quyosh va oy doiralari.\\n\\nB ham misollar keltiradi, lekin naqshlarga emas, so‘zana tikiladigan joylarga. C nomning kelib chiqishini tushuntiradi. D so‘zanalarni an’anaviy ravishda kim va nima uchun tikkanini tushuntiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"give-examples\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Many limestone caves contain mineral formations called speleothems.\\n- Speleothems form as water drips through a cave and leaves behind tiny amounts of the mineral calcite.\\n- Stalactites are speleothems that hang down from a cave's ceiling.\\n- Stalagmites are speleothems that rise up from a cave's floor.\\n- Both kinds of formations usually grow by only a fraction of a millimeter per year.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to emphasize a difference between stalactites and stalagmites. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Stalactites and stalagmites both form as dripping water leaves behind tiny amounts of calcite.\",\"option_b\":\"Stalactites, which hang from a cave's ceiling, usually grow by only a fraction of a millimeter per year.\",\"option_c\":\"Speleothems, a group of formations that includes stalactites and stalagmites, are found in many limestone caves.\",\"option_d\":\"While stalactites hang down from a cave's ceiling, stalagmites rise up from its floor.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to emphasize a difference between the two formations, so the answer must mention both and show how they differ. Choice D does this: stalactites hang from the ceiling, whereas stalagmites rise from the floor.\\n\\nChoice A mentions both formations but describes a similarity, not a difference. Choice B describes only stalactites. Choice C names both but only says they belong to the same group and are found in caves.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — ikki shakl o‘rtasidagi farqni ta’kidlash, shuning uchun javob ikkalasini ham tilga olib, ular qanday farqlanishini ko‘rsatishi kerak. D buni bajaradi: stalaktitlar shiftdan osilib turadi, stalagmitlar esa poldan yuqoriga o‘sadi.\\n\\nA ikkalasini tilga oladi, lekin farqni emas, o‘xshashlikni tasvirlaydi. B faqat stalaktitlarni tasvirlaydi. C ikkalasini nomlaydi, lekin faqat ular bitta guruhga kirishi va g‘orlarda uchrashini aytadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"contrast\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- The olm is a salamander that lives in underground waters in caves of the Dinaric Alps in southeastern Europe.\\n- The Mexican tetra is a fish; some of its populations live in surface rivers, and others live in caves in northeastern Mexico.\\n- The olm lives in complete darkness and lacks working eyes; its tiny eyes are covered by skin.\\n- Cave-dwelling Mexican tetras also live in complete darkness and have lost their eyes.\\n- River-dwelling Mexican tetras have normal, working eyes.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the olm and cave-dwelling Mexican tetras. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Unlike cave-dwelling Mexican tetras, river-dwelling Mexican tetras have normal, working eyes.\",\"option_b\":\"The olm and cave-dwelling Mexican tetras both live in complete darkness and lack working eyes.\",\"option_c\":\"The olm is a salamander, while the Mexican tetra is a fish.\",\"option_d\":\"The olm lives in caves in southeastern Europe, whereas cave-dwelling Mexican tetras live in caves in northeastern Mexico.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to emphasize a similarity between the olm and cave-dwelling Mexican tetras. Choice B names both animals and states what they share: both live in complete darkness and lack working eyes.\\n\\nChoice A contrasts two groups of Mexican tetras and does not mention the olm. Choices C and D mention both animals but emphasize differences—the kind of animal and the location—rather than a similarity.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — olm va g‘orda yashovchi meksika tetralari o‘rtasidagi o‘xshashlikni ta’kidlash. B ikkala hayvonni nomlaydi va ularning umumiy jihatini aytadi: ikkalasi ham to‘liq qorong‘ilikda yashaydi va ishlaydigan ko‘zlari yo‘q.\\n\\nA meksika tetralarining ikki guruhini solishtiradi va olmni umuman tilga olmaydi. C va D ikkala hayvonni tilga oladi, lekin o‘xshashlikni emas, farqlarni — hayvon turi va yashash joyini — ta’kidlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"similarity\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- An onion's cells contain sulfur-containing compounds and, stored separately, enzymes that can react with them.\\n- When an onion is cut, its cells break open and the enzymes mix with the sulfur compounds.\\n- This reaction produces a chemical called syn-propanethial-S-oxide, which escapes into the air as a gas.\\n- When the gas reaches a person's eyes, it irritates nerves there, and the eyes produce tears to wash it away.\\n- Chilling an onion before cutting it slows the reaction.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to explain why cutting an onion can make a person's eyes water. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Cutting an onion releases a gas that irritates nerves in a person's eyes, which then produce tears to wash the gas away.\",\"option_b\":\"Chilling an onion before cutting it slows the chemical reaction that happens when the onion's cells break open.\",\"option_c\":\"An onion's cells contain sulfur-containing compounds and, stored separately, enzymes that can react with them.\",\"option_d\":\"Syn-propanethial-S-oxide is a chemical that is produced when an onion is cut.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to explain why cutting an onion makes eyes water. Choice A gives the full cause-and-effect chain in brief: cutting releases a gas, the gas irritates nerves in the eyes, and the eyes produce tears.\\n\\nChoice B tells how to slow the reaction, not why tears form. Choice C describes what is inside onion cells but not what happens when they are cut. Choice D names the chemical that is produced but does not connect it to tears.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — piyoz to‘g‘ralganda nima uchun ko‘zdan yosh oqishini tushuntirish. A sabab-oqibat zanjirini qisqa qilib to‘liq beradi: to‘g‘rash gaz chiqaradi, gaz ko‘zdagi nervlarni bezovta qiladi va ko‘zlar yosh chiqaradi.\\n\\nB reaksiyani qanday sekinlashtirishni aytadi, yosh nima uchun paydo bo‘lishini emas. C piyoz hujayralari ichida nima borligini tasvirlaydi, lekin ular kesilganda nima bo‘lishini aytmaydi. D hosil bo‘ladigan kimyoviy moddani nomlaydi, lekin uni ko‘z yoshi bilan bog‘lamaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"explain-cause\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Abdulla Qodiriy (1894–1938) was an Uzbek writer.\\n- His novel O‘tkan kunlar (Bygone Days) was first published in the 1920s.\\n- It is often regarded as the first modern Uzbek novel.\\n- The novel is set in Tashkent and Margilan in the mid-nineteenth century.\\n- Its main character, Otabek, is a young merchant from Tashkent.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to introduce the novel O‘tkan kunlar to an audience unfamiliar with it. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"O‘tkan kunlar's main character is Otabek, a young merchant from Tashkent.\",\"option_b\":\"Abdulla Qodiriy, who lived from 1894 to 1938, was an Uzbek writer.\",\"option_c\":\"O‘tkan kunlar (Bygone Days), a novel by the Uzbek writer Abdulla Qodiriy first published in the 1920s, is often regarded as the first modern Uzbek novel.\",\"option_d\":\"Set in the mid-nineteenth century, O‘tkan kunlar was first published in the 1920s.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"An audience unfamiliar with the novel needs to be told what it is. Choice C does this: it identifies O‘tkan kunlar as a novel, names its author, gives the time it was published, and explains why it is important.\\n\\nChoice A assumes the reader already knows the work and does not even say that it is a novel. Choice B introduces the author, not the novel. Choice D gives dates but does not say what kind of work O‘tkan kunlar is or who wrote it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Asarni bilmaydigan auditoriyaga u nima ekanini aytish kerak. C buni bajaradi: “O‘tkan kunlar” roman ekanini aytadi, muallifini nomlaydi, qachon nashr etilganini va nima uchun muhimligini tushuntiradi.\\n\\nA o‘quvchi asarni allaqachon biladi deb hisoblaydi va hatto uning roman ekanini ham aytmaydi. B romanni emas, muallifni tanishtiradi. D sanalarni beradi, lekin “O‘tkan kunlar” qanday asar ekanini ham, uni kim yozganini ham aytmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"unfamiliar-audience\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Sociologist Gulnora Rahimova wanted to know whether neighbors who share a courtyard garden trust one another more than neighbors who do not.\\n- She interviewed 240 residents of apartment buildings in Samarkand.\\n- Half of the residents lived in buildings with a shared courtyard garden; the other half lived in buildings without one.\\n- Residents with a shared garden were nearly twice as likely to say they would leave a spare key with a neighbor.\\n- Rahimova concluded that shared gardens may help build trust among neighbors.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to describe how Rahimova conducted her study. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Rahimova concluded that shared courtyard gardens may help build trust among neighbors.\",\"option_b\":\"Residents with a shared courtyard garden were nearly twice as likely as other residents to say they would leave a spare key with a neighbor.\",\"option_c\":\"Rahimova wanted to know whether sharing a courtyard garden affects how much neighbors trust one another.\",\"option_d\":\"Rahimova interviewed 240 apartment residents in Samarkand, half of whom lived in buildings with a shared courtyard garden and half in buildings without one.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to describe the study's method—how it was carried out. Choice D does this: it states who was interviewed, how many people took part, and how the two groups were divided.\\n\\nChoice A gives Rahimova's conclusion. Choice B gives a result. Choice C gives the question the study tried to answer. All three are accurate, but none describes how the study was conducted.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — tadqiqot usulini, ya’ni u qanday o‘tkazilganini tasvirlash. D buni bajaradi: kimlar bilan suhbat qilingani, qancha odam qatnashgani va ikki guruh qanday ajratilganini aytadi.\\n\\nA Rahimovaning xulosasini beradi. B natijani beradi. C tadqiqot javob izlagan savolni beradi. Uchalasi ham to‘g‘ri ma’lumot, lekin hech biri tadqiqot qanday o‘tkazilganini tasvirlamaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"methodology\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Before the mid-1400s, books in Europe were usually copied by hand, and copying a single long book could take a scribe many months.\\n- Around 1450, Johannes Gutenberg developed a printing system in Mainz, Germany.\\n- His system used small, reusable metal letters (movable type) that could be arranged into pages, inked, and printed again and again.\\n- His workshop's Bible, completed around 1455, was printed in about 180 copies.\\n- Fewer than 50 copies of the Gutenberg Bible are known to survive today.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to emphasize an advantage of Gutenberg's printing system over copying books by hand. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Fewer than 50 copies of the Gutenberg Bible, which was completed around 1455, are known to survive today.\",\"option_b\":\"Whereas a scribe could spend many months copying a single book by hand, Gutenberg's reusable metal type allowed the same pages to be printed again and again.\",\"option_c\":\"Around 1450, Johannes Gutenberg developed a printing system in Mainz, Germany.\",\"option_d\":\"Before the mid-1400s, books in Europe were usually copied by hand, often by scribes.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to emphasize an advantage of Gutenberg's system over hand copying, so the answer must compare the two. Choice B does this: hand copying a book could take months, while reusable type let the same pages be printed repeatedly.\\n\\nChoice A tells how many copies survive today, which is not an advantage of the system. Choice C tells when and where the system was developed but not what made it better. Choice D describes only hand copying and never mentions Gutenberg's system.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — Gutenberg tizimining qo‘lda ko‘chirishdan afzalligini ta’kidlash, shuning uchun javob ikkalasini solishtirishi kerak. B buni bajaradi: kitobni qo‘lda ko‘chirish oylab vaqt olgan, qayta ishlatiladigan harflar esa bir xil sahifalarni qayta-qayta chop etish imkonini bergan.\\n\\nA bugungi kungacha qancha nusxa saqlanib qolganini aytadi, bu tizimning afzalligi emas. C tizim qachon va qayerda yaratilganini aytadi, lekin uni nima yaxshiroq qilganini aytmaydi. D faqat qo‘lda ko‘chirishni tasvirlaydi va Gutenberg tizimini umuman tilga olmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"emphasize-advantage\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Many desert lizards spend part of the hottest hours of the day perched on top of rocks.\\n- Biologists have traditionally assumed that the lizards do this to warm their bodies in the sun.\\n- Herpetologist Nodira Saidova measured air and surface temperatures in a desert in southern Uzbekistan.\\n- At midday, the air about 30 centimeters above the rocks was up to 8°C cooler than the ground's surface.\\n- Saidova observed that lizards on rocks at midday often raised their bodies off the hot stone.\\n- She concluded that at midday, perching on rocks may help the lizards avoid overheating rather than warm up.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to contrast the traditional explanation for the lizards' midday perching with Saidova's conclusion. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Although biologists have traditionally assumed that desert lizards perch on rocks to warm up, Saidova concluded that at midday the behavior may instead help them avoid overheating.\",\"option_b\":\"Saidova found that at midday, the air above the rocks was up to 8°C cooler than the ground, and she observed that lizards often raised their bodies off the hot stone.\",\"option_c\":\"Biologists have traditionally assumed that desert lizards perch on rocks to warm their bodies, an assumption that Saidova's measurements confirmed.\",\"option_d\":\"Many desert lizards perch on rocks during the hottest hours of the day, a behavior that Saidova studied in a desert in southern Uzbekistan.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal requires two things: the traditional explanation and Saidova's conclusion, presented as a contrast. Choice A gives both—warming up versus avoiding overheating—and links them with “although.”\\n\\nChoice B reports Saidova's evidence but never mentions the traditional explanation or states her conclusion. Choice C mentions the traditional explanation but wrongly claims that Saidova confirmed it; according to the notes, she reached a different conclusion. Choice D describes the behavior and where it was studied but gives neither explanation.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad ikki narsani talab qiladi: an’anaviy tushuntirish va Saidovaning xulosasi, bir-biriga qarama-qarshi qo‘yilgan holda. A ikkalasini ham beradi — isinish va qizib ketishdan saqlanish — hamda ularni “although” bilan bog‘laydi.\\n\\nB Saidovaning dalillarini keltiradi, lekin an’anaviy tushuntirishni ham, uning xulosasini ham aytmaydi. C an’anaviy tushuntirishni tilga oladi, lekin Saidova uni tasdiqlagan deb noto‘g‘ri da’vo qiladi; eslatmalarga ko‘ra, u boshqa xulosaga kelgan. D xatti-harakatni va u qayerda o‘rganilganini tasvirlaydi, lekin hech qanday tushuntirish bermaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"contrast-views\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Loggerhead sea turtles that hatch on beaches in Florida swim thousands of kilometers around the Atlantic Ocean before returning to the coast where they hatched.\\n- Experiments suggest that the turtles detect features of Earth's magnetic field to stay on course.\\n- Homing pigeons can find their way home from places they have never visited; studies indicate that Earth's magnetic field is one of the cues they use.\\n- European robins migrate at night, and experiments show that they orient themselves using Earth's magnetic field.\\n- Some migrating songbirds also use the positions of stars to find their direction.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to make a generalization about how some animals navigate. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Loggerhead sea turtles that hatch in Florida detect features of Earth's magnetic field to stay on course during journeys of thousands of kilometers.\",\"option_b\":\"Animals as different as sea turtles, homing pigeons, and European robins appear to rely, at least in part, on Earth's magnetic field to find their way.\",\"option_c\":\"All migrating animals navigate by detecting Earth's magnetic field rather than by using other cues.\",\"option_d\":\"European robins, which migrate at night, orient themselves using Earth's magnetic field, and some songbirds also use the stars.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"A generalization draws a broad statement from several specific cases. Choice B does this: it groups three very different animals from the notes and states what they have in common—using Earth's magnetic field to navigate—while limiting the claim to “animals as different as” these.\\n\\nChoices A and D each describe specific animals rather than making a general statement. Choice C does generalize, but too far: the notes cover only a few species, and they say that some songbirds also use the stars, so the claim that all migrating animals rely only on the magnetic field is not supported.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Umumlashtirish bir nechta aniq holatlardan keng xulosa chiqaradi. B buni bajaradi: eslatmalardagi bir-biridan juda farq qiladigan uchta hayvonni birlashtiradi va ularning umumiy jihatini — yo‘l topishda Yerning magnit maydonidan foydalanishini — aytadi, shu bilan birga da’voni faqat shu kabi hayvonlar bilan cheklaydi.\\n\\nA va D umumiy fikr aytmasdan, alohida hayvonlarni tasvirlaydi. C umumlashtiradi, lekin haddan tashqari: eslatmalar faqat bir necha tur haqida va ularda ba’zi qo‘shiqchi qushlar yulduzlardan ham foydalanishi aytilgan, shuning uchun barcha ko‘chib yuruvchi hayvonlar faqat magnit maydonga tayanadi degan da’vo asossiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"generalization\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Shashmaqom is a classical music tradition of Central Asia closely associated with the city of Bukhara.\\n- Its name means “six maqoms”; each maqom is a long suite of vocal and instrumental pieces.\\n- Performers include singers and players of instruments such as the tanbur, the dutar, and the doira (a frame drum).\\n- Many of the song texts come from classical poetry in Persian and Uzbek.\\n- Shashmaqom has long been performed in both Uzbekistan and Tajikistan, and UNESCO recognizes it as part of the intangible cultural heritage of both countries.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to introduce Shashmaqom to an audience unfamiliar with it and emphasize that it belongs to the cultural heritage of more than one country. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"UNESCO recognizes Shashmaqom as part of the intangible cultural heritage of both Uzbekistan and Tajikistan.\",\"option_b\":\"Shashmaqom, a classical music tradition associated with Bukhara, is made up of six long suites of vocal and instrumental pieces.\",\"option_c\":\"Shashmaqom, a Central Asian classical music tradition made up of six suites of vocal and instrumental pieces, is recognized by UNESCO as part of the cultural heritage of both Uzbekistan and Tajikistan.\",\"option_d\":\"Performed by singers and by players of the tanbur, dutar, and doira, Shashmaqom draws many of its song texts from poetry in both Persian and Uzbek.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal has two parts: explain what Shashmaqom is for readers who have never heard of it, and emphasize that it belongs to more than one country's heritage. Choice C does both: it explains that Shashmaqom is a classical music tradition made of six suites, and it states that UNESCO recognizes it as heritage of both Uzbekistan and Tajikistan.\\n\\nChoice A covers the shared heritage but never explains what Shashmaqom is. Choice B introduces Shashmaqom but says nothing about more than one country. Choice D mentions two languages, not two countries, and does not clearly explain what Shashmaqom is.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad ikki qismdan iborat: Shashmaqom haqida eshitmagan o‘quvchilarga uning nima ekanini tushuntirish va u bir nechta mamlakatning merosi ekanini ta’kidlash. C ikkalasini ham bajaradi: Shashmaqom oltita turkumdan iborat mumtoz musiqa an’anasi ekanini tushuntiradi va YUNESKO uni O‘zbekiston va Tojikistonning merosi sifatida tan olishini aytadi.\\n\\nA umumiy merosni qamrab oladi, lekin Shashmaqom nima ekanini tushuntirmaydi. B Shashmaqomni tanishtiradi, lekin bir nechta mamlakat haqida hech narsa demaydi. D ikki mamlakatni emas, ikki tilni tilga oladi va Shashmaqom nima ekanini aniq tushuntirmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"audience-two-part\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Astronomer Rustam Xolmatov's team studied a red dwarf star whose mass is about one-fifth that of the Sun.\\n- Red dwarfs are the most common type of star in our galaxy.\\n- By measuring tiny changes in the star's light, the team detected a planet about the size of Saturn orbiting it.\\n- Leading models of planet formation predict that the disks of gas and dust around such small stars contain too little material to build giant planets.\\n- The team plans to study the planet's atmosphere with additional telescopes.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to explain why the team's discovery was surprising. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Red dwarfs, the most common type of star in our galaxy, can have planets about the size of Saturn orbiting them.\",\"option_b\":\"Xolmatov's team detected a Saturn-sized planet orbiting a red dwarf star whose mass is only about one-fifth that of the Sun.\",\"option_c\":\"Because leading models predict that small stars have too little material in their disks to build giant planets, Xolmatov's team plans to study the planet's atmosphere.\",\"option_d\":\"Although leading models predict that small stars lack enough material in their disks to form giant planets, Xolmatov's team found a Saturn-sized planet orbiting a red dwarf only about one-fifth as massive as the Sun.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"A discovery is surprising when it goes against what was expected. Choice D states both the expectation (models predict that small stars cannot form giant planets) and the finding that contradicts it (a Saturn-sized planet around a small red dwarf).\\n\\nChoice B reports the discovery accurately but leaves out the prediction that makes it surprising. Choice A presents the result as an ordinary fact about red dwarfs, hiding the surprise. Choice C mentions the prediction but links it to the team's future plans with an illogical “because” and never states the discovery itself.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Kashfiyot kutilganiga zid bo‘lganda hayratlanarli bo‘ladi. D ham kutilgan natijani (modellarga ko‘ra kichik yulduzlar atrofida ulkan sayyoralar hosil bo‘lmaydi), ham unga zid bo‘lgan topilmani (kichik qizil mitti atrofida Saturn kattaligidagi sayyora) aytadi.\\n\\nB kashfiyotni to‘g‘ri bayon qiladi, lekin uni hayratlanarli qiladigan bashoratni tushirib qoldiradi. A natijani qizil mittilar haqidagi oddiy fakt sifatida ko‘rsatadi va hayratni yashiradi. C bashoratni tilga oladi, lekin uni “because” orqali jamoaning kelajakdagi rejalariga mantiqsiz bog‘laydi va kashfiyotning o‘zini aytmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"explain-surprise\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"right_triangles_trigonometry\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In right triangle $ABC$, angle $B$ is the right angle, $AB = 9$, $BC = 40$, and $AC = 41$. What is the value of $\\\\cos C$?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{9}{41}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{40}{41}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{9}{40}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{40}{9}$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The cosine of an acute angle is the adjacent leg divided by the hypotenuse. For angle $C$, the adjacent leg is $BC = 40$ and the hypotenuse is $AC = 41$, so $\\\\cos C = \\\\frac{40}{41}$. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A is $\\\\sin C$ (opposite over hypotenuse). Choice C is $\\\\tan C$, and choice D is $\\\\tan A$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"O‘tkir burchak kosinusi — yopishgan katetning gipotenuzaga nisbati. $C$ burchak uchun yopishgan katet $BC = 40$, gipotenuza $AC = 41$, demak $\\\\cos C = \\\\frac{40}{41}$. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA — bu $\\\\sin C$ (qarshi katet / gipotenuza). C — bu $\\\\tan C$, D esa $\\\\tan A$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"soh_cah_toa\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"right_triangles_trigonometry\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A straight cable runs from the top of a vertical pole that is 12 meters tall to a point on level ground 35 meters from the base of the pole. What is the length, in meters, of the cable?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"37\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The pole, the ground, and the cable form a right triangle with legs 12 and 35. By the Pythagorean theorem, the cable's length is $\\\\sqrt{12^2 + 35^2} = \\\\sqrt{144 + 1225} = \\\\sqrt{1369} = 37$ meters. If you got 47, you added the legs instead of using the Pythagorean theorem.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ustun, yer va kabel katetlari 12 va 35 bo‘lgan to‘g‘ri burchakli uchburchak hosil qiladi. Pifagor teoremasiga ko‘ra kabel uzunligi $\\\\sqrt{12^2 + 35^2} = \\\\sqrt{144 + 1225} = \\\\sqrt{1369} = 37$ metr. Agar 47 chiqqan bo‘lsa, siz Pifagor teoremasi o‘rniga katetlarni qo‘shgansiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"pythagorean\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"right_triangles_trigonometry\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In a right triangle, $A$ and $B$ are the two acute angles, and $\\\\sin A = 0.28$. What is the value of $\\\\cos B$?\",\"option_a\":\"0.28\",\"option_b\":\"0.72\",\"option_c\":\"0.96\",\"option_d\":\"3.57\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Angles $A$ and $B$ are complementary, so the leg opposite $A$ is the leg adjacent to $B$. Therefore $\\\\cos B = \\\\sin A = 0.28$. Choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoice B is $1 - 0.28$; sine and cosine of complementary angles are equal, they do not add to 1. Choice C is $\\\\cos A = \\\\sqrt{1 - 0.28^2}$. Choice D is about $\\\\frac{1}{0.28}$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$A$ va $B$ burchaklar yig‘indisi $90^\\\\circ$, shuning uchun $A$ qarshisidagi katet $B$ ga yopishgan katetdir. Demak $\\\\cos B = \\\\sin A = 0.28$. To‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\nB — bu $1 - 0.28$; yig‘indisi $90^\\\\circ$ bo‘lgan burchaklarning sinusi va kosinusi teng, ularning yig‘indisi 1 emas. C — bu $\\\\cos A = \\\\sqrt{1 - 0.28^2}$. D — taxminan $\\\\frac{1}{0.28}$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"complementary_angles\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"right_triangles_trigonometry\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In right triangle $JKL$, angle $K$ is the right angle, the measure of angle $J$ is $60^\\\\circ$, and $JK = 5$. What is the length of $\\\\overline{JL}$?\",\"option_a\":\"$5\\\\sqrt{2}$\",\"option_b\":\"$5\\\\sqrt{3}$\",\"option_c\":\"10\",\"option_d\":\"$10\\\\sqrt{3}$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"In a $30^\\\\circ$-$60^\\\\circ$-$90^\\\\circ$ triangle, the hypotenuse is twice the leg opposite the $30^\\\\circ$ angle. Angle $L$ is $30^\\\\circ$, and the leg opposite it is $JK = 5$, so the hypotenuse $JL = 10$. (Also, $\\\\cos 60^\\\\circ = \\\\frac{JK}{JL} = \\\\frac{1}{2}$.) Choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A uses the $45^\\\\circ$-$45^\\\\circ$-$90^\\\\circ$ ratio. Choice B is the length of leg $KL$, which is opposite the $60^\\\\circ$ angle. Choice D multiplies the correct answer by $\\\\sqrt{3}$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$30^\\\\circ$-$60^\\\\circ$-$90^\\\\circ$ uchburchakda gipotenuza $30^\\\\circ$ li burchak qarshisidagi katetdan ikki marta uzun. $L$ burchak $30^\\\\circ$, uning qarshisidagi katet $JK = 5$, demak gipotenuza $JL = 10$. (Shuningdek, $\\\\cos 60^\\\\circ = \\\\frac{JK}{JL} = \\\\frac{1}{2}$.) To‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA javobda $45^\\\\circ$-$45^\\\\circ$-$90^\\\\circ$ nisbati ishlatilgan. B — $60^\\\\circ$ li burchak qarshisidagi $KL$ katet uzunligi. D javobda to‘g‘ri javob $\\\\sqrt{3}$ ga ko‘paytirilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"special_right_triangles\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"right_triangles_trigonometry\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"In the $xy$-plane, what is the distance between the points $(-2, 3)$ and $(6, 9)$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"10\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The horizontal distance is $6 - (-2) = 8$ and the vertical distance is $9 - 3 = 6$. These are the legs of a right triangle, so the distance is $\\\\sqrt{8^2 + 6^2} = \\\\sqrt{100} = 10$. If you got 14, you added the legs; if you got $\\\\sqrt{52}$, you computed $6 - 2 = 4$ instead of $6 - (-2) = 8$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gorizontal masofa $6 - (-2) = 8$, vertikal masofa $9 - 3 = 6$. Bular to‘g‘ri burchakli uchburchak katetlari, demak masofa $\\\\sqrt{8^2 + 6^2} = \\\\sqrt{100} = 10$. Agar 14 chiqqan bo‘lsa, katetlarni qo‘shgansiz; $\\\\sqrt{52}$ chiqqan bo‘lsa, $6 - (-2) = 8$ o‘rniga $6 - 2 = 4$ ni hisoblagansiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"distance_coordinate\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"right_triangles_trigonometry\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A wheelchair ramp rises 0.9 meter over a horizontal distance of 4 meters, and its sloped surface is 4.1 meters long. The sloped surface makes an angle $\\\\theta$ with the level ground. What is the value of $\\\\tan\\\\theta$?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{9}{41}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{9}{40}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{40}{41}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{40}{9}$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The tangent is the opposite leg divided by the adjacent leg. The rise (0.9) is opposite $\\\\theta$ and the horizontal distance (4) is adjacent, so $\\\\tan\\\\theta = \\\\frac{0.9}{4} = \\\\frac{9}{40}$. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A is $\\\\sin\\\\theta = \\\\frac{0.9}{4.1}$, and choice C is $\\\\cos\\\\theta = \\\\frac{4}{4.1}$. Choice D is the reciprocal of $\\\\tan\\\\theta$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tangens — qarshi katetning yopishgan katetga nisbati. Ko‘tarilish (0.9) $\\\\theta$ qarshisida, gorizontal masofa (4) esa unga yopishgan, demak $\\\\tan\\\\theta = \\\\frac{0.9}{4} = \\\\frac{9}{40}$. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA — bu $\\\\sin\\\\theta = \\\\frac{0.9}{4.1}$, C — bu $\\\\cos\\\\theta = \\\\frac{4}{4.1}$. D — $\\\\tan\\\\theta$ ning teskarisi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"tangent_context\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"right_triangles_trigonometry\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In right triangle $ABC$, angle $C$ is the right angle and $\\\\sin A = \\\\frac{20}{29}$. What is the value of $\\\\tan B$?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{20}{21}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{21}{20}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{21}{29}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{29}{21}$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Let $BC = 20$ (opposite $A$) and $AB = 29$. Then $AC = \\\\sqrt{29^2 - 20^2} = \\\\sqrt{441} = 21$. For angle $B$, the opposite leg is $AC = 21$ and the adjacent leg is $BC = 20$, so $\\\\tan B = \\\\frac{21}{20}$. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A is $\\\\tan A$. Choice C is $\\\\sin B$ (equal to $\\\\cos A$). Choice D is the reciprocal of $\\\\sin B$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$BC = 20$ ($A$ qarshisida) va $AB = 29$ bo‘lsin. U holda $AC = \\\\sqrt{29^2 - 20^2} = \\\\sqrt{441} = 21$. $B$ burchak uchun qarshi katet $AC = 21$, yopishgan katet $BC = 20$, demak $\\\\tan B = \\\\frac{21}{20}$. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA — bu $\\\\tan A$. C — bu $\\\\sin B$ ($\\\\cos A$ ga teng). D — $\\\\sin B$ ning teskarisi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"trig_other_angle\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"right_triangles_trigonometry\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A child holds a kite string at ground level. The string is pulled straight, is 80 meters long, and makes an angle of $30^\\\\circ$ with the level ground. How high, in meters, is the kite above the ground?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"40\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The string is the hypotenuse of a right triangle, and the height is the leg opposite the $30^\\\\circ$ angle. So the height is $80 \\\\sin 30^\\\\circ = 80 \\\\cdot \\\\frac{1}{2} = 40$ meters. If you got about 69.3 ($40\\\\sqrt{3}$), you used $\\\\cos 30^\\\\circ$, which gives the horizontal distance.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ip to‘g‘ri burchakli uchburchakning gipotenuzasi, balandlik esa $30^\\\\circ$ li burchak qarshisidagi katet. Demak balandlik $80 \\\\sin 30^\\\\circ = 80 \\\\cdot \\\\frac{1}{2} = 40$ metr. Agar taxminan 69.3 ($40\\\\sqrt{3}$) chiqqan bo‘lsa, siz $\\\\cos 30^\\\\circ$ ni ishlatgansiz — u gorizontal masofani beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"angle_elevation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"right_triangles_trigonometry\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In triangle $ABC$, $AB = 10$, $AC = 16$, and the measure of angle $A$ is $30^\\\\circ$. What is the area of triangle $ABC$?\",\"option_a\":\"40\",\"option_b\":\"$40\\\\sqrt{3}$\",\"option_c\":\"80\",\"option_d\":\"$80\\\\sqrt{3}$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Use $\\\\overline{AC}$ as the base. The height is the perpendicular distance from $B$ to line $AC$, which is $AB \\\\sin 30^\\\\circ = 10 \\\\cdot \\\\frac{1}{2} = 5$. The area is $\\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\cdot 16 \\\\cdot 5 = 40$. Choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoice B uses $\\\\cos 30^\\\\circ$ for the height. Choice C forgets the $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ in the area formula. Choice D makes both errors.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$\\\\overline{AC}$ ni asos deb olamiz. Balandlik — $B$ dan $AC$ to‘g‘ri chiziqqacha perpendikulyar masofa: $AB \\\\sin 30^\\\\circ = 10 \\\\cdot \\\\frac{1}{2} = 5$. Yuza $\\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\cdot 16 \\\\cdot 5 = 40$. To‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\nB javobda balandlik uchun $\\\\cos 30^\\\\circ$ ishlatilgan. C javobda yuza formulasidagi $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ unutilgan. D javobda ikkala xato ham bor.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"area_trig\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"right_triangles_trigonometry\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In right triangle $ABC$, angle $C$ is the right angle, and the measure of angle $A$ is less than the measure of angle $B$. Which of the following must be true?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\sin A > \\\\sin B$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\sin A < \\\\cos A$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\tan A > 1$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\cos B > \\\\cos A$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The acute angles add to $90^\\\\circ$, so $A < B$ means $A < 45^\\\\circ$. Then the leg opposite $A$ ($BC$) is shorter than the leg adjacent to $A$ ($AC$), so $\\\\frac{BC}{AB} < \\\\frac{AC}{AB}$, that is, $\\\\sin A < \\\\cos A$. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A is false: $\\\\sin B = \\\\cos A > \\\\sin A$. Choice C is false: $\\\\tan A = \\\\frac{BC}{AC} < 1$. Choice D is false: $\\\\cos B = \\\\sin A < \\\\cos A$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"O‘tkir burchaklar yig‘indisi $90^\\\\circ$, shuning uchun $A < B$ dan $A < 45^\\\\circ$ kelib chiqadi. U holda $A$ qarshisidagi katet ($BC$) $A$ ga yopishgan katetdan ($AC$) qisqa, ya’ni $\\\\frac{BC}{AB} < \\\\frac{AC}{AB}$, bu $\\\\sin A < \\\\cos A$ demakdir. To‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: $\\\\sin B = \\\\cos A > \\\\sin A$. C noto‘g‘ri: $\\\\tan A = \\\\frac{BC}{AC} < 1$. D noto‘g‘ri: $\\\\cos B = \\\\sin A < \\\\cos A$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"compare_trig_values\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"right_triangles_trigonometry\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"In a right triangle, $\\\\theta$ is one of the acute angles and $\\\\sin\\\\theta = 0.6$. The hypotenuse of the triangle has length 35. What is the perimeter of the triangle?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"84\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The leg opposite $\\\\theta$ is $0.6 \\\\cdot 35 = 21$. The other leg is $\\\\sqrt{35^2 - 21^2} = \\\\sqrt{1225 - 441} = \\\\sqrt{784} = 28$. The perimeter is $21 + 28 + 35 = 84$. If you got 49, you added only the two legs.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$\\\\theta$ qarshisidagi katet $0.6 \\\\cdot 35 = 21$. Ikkinchi katet $\\\\sqrt{35^2 - 21^2} = \\\\sqrt{1225 - 441} = \\\\sqrt{784} = 28$. Perimetr $21 + 28 + 35 = 84$. Agar 49 chiqqan bo‘lsa, siz faqat ikki katetni qo‘shgansiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"sine_side_lengths\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"right_triangles_trigonometry\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"An isosceles triangle has two sides of length 10, and each of its two base angles measures $30^\\\\circ$. What is the length of the base of the triangle?\",\"option_a\":\"10\",\"option_b\":\"$10\\\\sqrt{2}$\",\"option_c\":\"$10\\\\sqrt{3}$\",\"option_d\":\"20\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The altitude from the top vertex bisects the base and forms two $30^\\\\circ$-$60^\\\\circ$-$90^\\\\circ$ triangles with hypotenuse 10. Half of the base is the leg adjacent to the $30^\\\\circ$ angle: $10\\\\cos 30^\\\\circ = 10 \\\\cdot \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2} = 5\\\\sqrt{3}$. The base is $2 \\\\cdot 5\\\\sqrt{3} = 10\\\\sqrt{3}$. Choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A assumes the triangle is equilateral, but its angles are $30^\\\\circ$, $30^\\\\circ$, and $120^\\\\circ$. Choice B uses a $45^\\\\circ$ ratio. Choice D is the sum of the two equal sides, which would not form a triangle.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Uchidan tushirilgan balandlik asosni teng ikkiga bo‘ladi va gipotenuzasi 10 bo‘lgan ikkita $30^\\\\circ$-$60^\\\\circ$-$90^\\\\circ$ uchburchak hosil qiladi. Asosning yarmi $30^\\\\circ$ li burchakka yopishgan katet: $10\\\\cos 30^\\\\circ = 10 \\\\cdot \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2} = 5\\\\sqrt{3}$. Asos $2 \\\\cdot 5\\\\sqrt{3} = 10\\\\sqrt{3}$. To‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA javob uchburchakni teng tomonli deb hisoblaydi, ammo uning burchaklari $30^\\\\circ$, $30^\\\\circ$ va $120^\\\\circ$. B javobda $45^\\\\circ$ nisbati ishlatilgan. D — ikki teng tomon yig‘indisi; bunday uchburchak mavjud bo‘lmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"special_right_triangles\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"right_triangles_trigonometry\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In right triangle $ABC$, angle $C$ is the right angle. Point $D$ lies on $\\\\overline{AB}$ so that $\\\\overline{CD}$ is perpendicular to $\\\\overline{AB}$. If $AC = 15$ and $\\\\sin B = \\\\frac{3}{5}$, what is the length of $\\\\overline{BD}$?\",\"option_a\":\"9\",\"option_b\":\"12\",\"option_c\":\"16\",\"option_d\":\"20\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"In triangle $ABC$, $\\\\sin B = \\\\frac{AC}{AB} = \\\\frac{3}{5}$, so $AB = 25$ and $BC = \\\\sqrt{25^2 - 15^2} = 20$. Triangle $CBD$ is a right triangle with angle $B$, so $\\\\cos B = \\\\frac{BD}{BC}$. Since $\\\\cos B = \\\\frac{BC}{AB} = \\\\frac{4}{5}$, $BD = \\\\frac{4}{5} \\\\cdot 20 = 16$. Choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A is $AD = 25 - 16$. Choice B is the altitude $CD$. Choice D is $BC$, the hypotenuse of triangle $CBD$, not the segment $BD$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$ABC$ uchburchakda $\\\\sin B = \\\\frac{AC}{AB} = \\\\frac{3}{5}$, demak $AB = 25$ va $BC = \\\\sqrt{25^2 - 15^2} = 20$. $CBD$ — $B$ burchakli to‘g‘ri burchakli uchburchak, shuning uchun $\\\\cos B = \\\\frac{BD}{BC}$. $\\\\cos B = \\\\frac{BC}{AB} = \\\\frac{4}{5}$ bo‘lgani uchun $BD = \\\\frac{4}{5} \\\\cdot 20 = 16$. To‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA — bu $AD = 25 - 16$. B — $CD$ balandlik. D — $BC$, ya’ni $CBD$ uchburchakning gipotenuzasi, $BD$ kesma emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"trig_similar_triangles\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"right_triangles_trigonometry\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"In a right triangle, $\\\\theta$ is one of the acute angles and $\\\\tan\\\\theta = 2.4$. What is the value of $\\\\sin\\\\theta + \\\\cos\\\\theta$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"17/13\",\"1.307\",\"1.308\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Write $\\\\tan\\\\theta = 2.4 = \\\\frac{12}{5}$, so the leg opposite $\\\\theta$ can be 12 and the adjacent leg 5. The hypotenuse is $\\\\sqrt{12^2 + 5^2} = 13$. Then $\\\\sin\\\\theta + \\\\cos\\\\theta = \\\\frac{12}{13} + \\\\frac{5}{13} = \\\\frac{17}{13} \\\\approx 1.3077$. Enter 17/13, 1.307, or 1.308.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$\\\\tan\\\\theta = 2.4 = \\\\frac{12}{5}$ deb yozamiz, demak $\\\\theta$ qarshisidagi katet 12, yopishgan katet 5 bo‘lishi mumkin. Gipotenuza $\\\\sqrt{12^2 + 5^2} = 13$. U holda $\\\\sin\\\\theta + \\\\cos\\\\theta = \\\\frac{12}{13} + \\\\frac{5}{13} = \\\\frac{17}{13} \\\\approx 1.3077$. Javobni 17/13, 1.307 yoki 1.308 shaklida yozing.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"tan_to_sin_cos\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"right_triangles_trigonometry\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In triangle $ABC$, $AB = AC$, $BC = 14$, and the area of the triangle is 168. What is the value of $\\\\tan B$?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\frac{7}{24}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{12}{7}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\frac{24}{25}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{24}{7}$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Let $M$ be the midpoint of $\\\\overline{BC}$. In an isosceles triangle, $\\\\overline{AM}$ is the altitude, so $\\\\frac{1}{2}(14)(AM) = 168$ and $AM = 24$. In right triangle $ABM$, $BM = 7$, so $\\\\tan B = \\\\frac{AM}{BM} = \\\\frac{24}{7}$. Choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoice A is the reciprocal, $\\\\frac{BM}{AM}$. Choice B divides the height by the whole base 14. Choice C is $\\\\sin B$, because $AB = \\\\sqrt{24^2 + 7^2} = 25$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$M$ — $\\\\overline{BC}$ ning o‘rtasi bo‘lsin. Teng yonli uchburchakda $\\\\overline{AM}$ balandlik, demak $\\\\frac{1}{2}(14)(AM) = 168$ va $AM = 24$. $ABM$ to‘g‘ri burchakli uchburchakda $BM = 7$, shuning uchun $\\\\tan B = \\\\frac{AM}{BM} = \\\\frac{24}{7}$. To‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\nA — teskari nisbat, $\\\\frac{BM}{AM}$. B javobda balandlik butun asos 14 ga bo‘lingan. C — bu $\\\\sin B$, chunki $AB = \\\\sqrt{24^2 + 7^2} = 25$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"area_trig_isosceles\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"right_triangles_trigonometry\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"In right triangle $PQR$, angle $Q$ is the right angle and $\\\\cos P = 0.8$. The length of $\\\\overline{PR}$ is 9 more than the length of $\\\\overline{QR}$. What is the length of $\\\\overline{PQ}$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"18\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Since $\\\\cos P = \\\\frac{PQ}{PR} = 0.8 = \\\\frac{4}{5}$, the sides are in the ratio $PQ : QR : PR = 4 : 3 : 5$. Let $PQ = 4k$, $QR = 3k$, and $PR = 5k$. Then $PR - QR = 2k = 9$, so $k = 4.5$ and $PQ = 4(4.5) = 18$. If you got 22.5, you found $PR$; if you got 13.5, you found $QR$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$\\\\cos P = \\\\frac{PQ}{PR} = 0.8 = \\\\frac{4}{5}$ bo‘lgani uchun tomonlar nisbati $PQ : QR : PR = 4 : 3 : 5$. $PQ = 4k$, $QR = 3k$, $PR = 5k$ bo‘lsin. U holda $PR - QR = 2k = 9$, demak $k = 4.5$ va $PQ = 4(4.5) = 18$. Agar 22.5 chiqqan bo‘lsa, $PR$ ni; 13.5 chiqqan bo‘lsa, $QR$ ni topgansiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":140,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"trig_ratio_algebra\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"systems_linear_equations\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\begin{aligned} y &= 3x - 2 \\\\\\\\ 2x + y &= 18 \\\\end{aligned}$$\\n\\nWhat is the solution $(x, y)$ to the given system of equations?\",\"option_a\":\"$(3, 7)$\",\"option_b\":\"$(4, 10)$\",\"option_c\":\"$(5, 8)$\",\"option_d\":\"$(10, 4)$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Substitute $y = 3x - 2$ into the second equation: $2x + 3x - 2 = 18$, so $5x = 20$ and $x = 4$. Then $y = 3(4) - 2 = 10$. Check: $2(4) + 10 = 18$.\\n\\nChoice A satisfies only the first equation ($2(3) + 7 = 13$, not $18$). Choice C satisfies only the second equation ($3(5) - 2 = 13$, not $8$). Choice D reverses the coordinates.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$y = 3x - 2$ ni ikkinchi tenglamaga qo‘yamiz: $2x + 3x - 2 = 18$, demak $5x = 20$ va $x = 4$. U holda $y = 3(4) - 2 = 10$. Tekshiruv: $2(4) + 10 = 18$.\\n\\nA variant faqat birinchi tenglamani qanoatlantiradi ($2(3) + 7 = 13$, $18$ emas). C variant faqat ikkinchi tenglamani qanoatlantiradi ($3(5) - 2 = 13$, $8$ emas). D variantda koordinatalar almashtirilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"solve-substitution\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"systems_linear_equations\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\begin{aligned} x + 3y &= 19 \\\\\\\\ x - y &= 3 \\\\end{aligned}$$\\n\\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the given system of equations, what is the value of $x$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"7\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Subtract the second equation from the first: $(x + 3y) - (x - y) = 19 - 3$, so $4y = 16$ and $y = 4$. Then $x = 3 + y = 7$. Check: $7 + 3(4) = 19$. Entering $4$ gives $y$, not $x$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi tenglamadan ikkinchisini ayiramiz: $(x + 3y) - (x - y) = 19 - 3$, demak $4y = 16$ va $y = 4$. U holda $x = 3 + y = 7$. Tekshiruv: $7 + 3(4) = 19$. $4$ deb yozish — bu $y$ ning qiymati, $x$ niki emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"solve-elimination\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"systems_linear_equations\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A theater sold $120$ tickets for a concert. Student tickets cost $8{,}000$ soums each and adult tickets cost $15{,}000$ soums each. The total from ticket sales was $1{,}380{,}000$ soums. If $s$ is the number of student tickets sold and $a$ is the number of adult tickets sold, which system of equations represents this situation?\",\"option_a\":\"$\\\\begin{aligned} s + a &= 120 \\\\\\\\ 15{,}000s + 8{,}000a &= 1{,}380{,}000 \\\\end{aligned}$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\begin{aligned} s + a &= 1{,}380{,}000 \\\\\\\\ 8{,}000s + 15{,}000a &= 120 \\\\end{aligned}$\",\"option_c\":\"$\\\\begin{aligned} 8{,}000s + 15{,}000a &= 120 \\\\\\\\ s - a &= 1{,}380{,}000 \\\\end{aligned}$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\begin{aligned} s + a &= 120 \\\\\\\\ 8{,}000s + 15{,}000a &= 1{,}380{,}000 \\\\end{aligned}$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The number of tickets gives $s + a = 120$. Student tickets bring in $8{,}000s$ soums and adult tickets bring in $15{,}000a$ soums, so $8{,}000s + 15{,}000a = 1{,}380{,}000$.\\n\\nChoice A swaps the prices of the two kinds of tickets. Choices B and C mix up the number of tickets with the amount of money.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Chiptalar soni bo‘yicha $s + a = 120$. Talaba chiptalaridan $8{,}000s$ so‘m, kattalar chiptalaridan $15{,}000a$ so‘m tushadi, shuning uchun $8{,}000s + 15{,}000a = 1{,}380{,}000$.\\n\\nA variantda ikki xil chipta narxlari almashtirilgan. B va C variantlar chiptalar sonini pul miqdori bilan adashtirgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"write-system\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"systems_linear_equations\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\begin{aligned} 2x + 5y &= 26 \\\\\\\\ 5x + 2y &= 9 \\\\end{aligned}$$\\n\\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the given system of equations, what is the value of $x + y$?\",\"option_a\":\"$5$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{17}{3}$\",\"option_c\":\"$7$\",\"option_d\":\"$35$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Add the two equations: $7x + 7y = 35$. Divide by $7$: $x + y = 5$. There is no need to find $x$ and $y$ separately.\\n\\nChoice B is $y - x$, found by subtracting the equations. Choice C is the common coefficient, $7$. Choice D is $7x + 7y$; the division by $7$ was skipped.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikki tenglamani qo‘shamiz: $7x + 7y = 35$. $7$ ga bo‘lamiz: $x + y = 5$. $x$ va $y$ ni alohida topish shart emas.\\n\\nB variant — tenglamalarni ayirib topilgan $y - x$. C variant — umumiy koeffitsiyent $7$. D variant — $7x + 7y$; $7$ ga bo‘lish o‘tkazib yuborilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"combination\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"systems_linear_equations\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\begin{aligned} y &= 2x + 5 \\\\\\\\ 4x - 2y &= 6 \\\\end{aligned}$$\\n\\nHow many solutions does the given system of equations have?\",\"option_a\":\"Zero\",\"option_b\":\"Exactly one\",\"option_c\":\"Exactly two\",\"option_d\":\"Infinitely many\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Solve the second equation for $y$: $-2y = -4x + 6$, so $y = 2x - 3$. Both lines have slope $2$, but their $y$-intercepts are different ($5$ and $-3$). The lines are parallel and never meet, so the system has zero solutions.\\n\\nExactly one solution would need different slopes. Two lines can't meet in exactly two points. Infinitely many solutions would need the same slope and the same $y$-intercept.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkinchi tenglamadan $y$ ni topamiz: $-2y = -4x + 6$, ya’ni $y = 2x - 3$. Ikkala to‘g‘ri chiziqning qiyaligi $2$, lekin $y$ o‘qini kesish nuqtalari har xil ($5$ va $-3$). To‘g‘ri chiziqlar parallel va kesishmaydi, demak sistemaning yechimi yo‘q.\\n\\nBitta yechim uchun qiyaliklar har xil bo‘lishi kerak. Ikki to‘g‘ri chiziq aynan ikki nuqtada kesisha olmaydi. Cheksiz ko‘p yechim uchun qiyalik ham, $y$ o‘qini kesish nuqtasi ham bir xil bo‘lishi kerak.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"number-of-solutions\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"systems_linear_equations\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The sum of two numbers is $64$, and the larger number is $18$ more than the smaller number. What is the larger number?\",\"option_a\":\"$23$\",\"option_b\":\"$41$\",\"option_c\":\"$46$\",\"option_d\":\"$82$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Let the larger number be $x$ and the smaller be $y$: $x + y = 64$ and $x - y = 18$. Adding the equations gives $2x = 82$, so $x = 41$ (and $y = 23$). Check: $41 + 23 = 64$ and $41 - 23 = 18$.\\n\\nChoice A is the smaller number. Choice C is $64 - 18$, which is twice the smaller number. Choice D is $2x$; the division by $2$ was skipped.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Kattaroq son $x$, kichigi $y$ bo‘lsin: $x + y = 64$ va $x - y = 18$. Tenglamalarni qo‘shsak, $2x = 82$, demak $x = 41$ (va $y = 23$). Tekshiruv: $41 + 23 = 64$ va $41 - 23 = 18$.\\n\\nA variant — kichik son. C variant — $64 - 18$, bu kichik sonning ikki baravari. D variant — $2x$; $2$ ga bo‘lish o‘tkazib yuborilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"word-problem\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"systems_linear_equations\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\begin{aligned} 5x + 2y &= 31 \\\\\\\\ 3x + 4y &= 13 \\\\end{aligned}$$\\n\\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the given system of equations, what is the value of $x - y$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"9\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Subtract the second equation from the first: $(5x + 2y) - (3x + 4y) = 31 - 13$, so $2x - 2y = 18$ and $x - y = 9$. (Solving fully gives $x = 7$ and $y = -2$, and $7 - (-2) = 9$.)\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi tenglamadan ikkinchisini ayiramiz: $(5x + 2y) - (3x + 4y) = 31 - 13$, ya’ni $2x - 2y = 18$ va $x - y = 9$. (To‘liq yechsak, $x = 7$ va $y = -2$, $7 - (-2) = 9$.)\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"combination\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"systems_linear_equations\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"The perimeter of a rectangular garden is $58$ meters. The length of the garden is $5$ meters more than twice its width. What is the length, in meters, of the garden?\",\"option_a\":\"$8$\",\"option_b\":\"$16$\",\"option_c\":\"$21$\",\"option_d\":\"$\\\\frac{121}{3}$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Let $L$ be the length and $W$ the width: $2L + 2W = 58$ and $L = 2W + 5$. Substitute: $2(2W + 5) + 2W = 58$, so $6W + 10 = 58$, $W = 8$, and $L = 2(8) + 5 = 21$. Check: $2(21) + 2(8) = 58$.\\n\\nChoice A is the width. Choice B is twice the width, without the extra $5$ meters. Choice D comes from using $L + W = 58$, treating the perimeter as the sum of just one length and one width.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Uzunlik $L$, eni $W$ bo‘lsin: $2L + 2W = 58$ va $L = 2W + 5$. O‘rniga qo‘yamiz: $2(2W + 5) + 2W = 58$, ya’ni $6W + 10 = 58$, $W = 8$ va $L = 2(8) + 5 = 21$. Tekshiruv: $2(21) + 2(8) = 58$.\\n\\nA variant — bog‘ning eni. B variant — enining ikki baravari, qo‘shimcha $5$ metrsiz. D variant perimetrni bitta uzunlik va bitta en yig‘indisi deb, $L + W = 58$ dan foydalangan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"word-problem\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"systems_linear_equations\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A cash box contains only $500$-soum coins and $1{,}000$-soum coins. There are $50$ coins in the box, and their total value is $40{,}500$ soums. How many $1{,}000$-soum coins are in the box?\",\"option_a\":\"$19$\",\"option_b\":\"$31$\",\"option_c\":\"$40$\",\"option_d\":\"$81$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Let $a$ be the number of $500$-soum coins and $b$ the number of $1{,}000$-soum coins: $a + b = 50$ and $500a + 1{,}000b = 40{,}500$. Substitute $a = 50 - b$: $25{,}000 - 500b + 1{,}000b = 40{,}500$, so $500b = 15{,}500$ and $b = 31$. Check: $19$ coins of $500$ soums and $31$ coins of $1{,}000$ soums give $9{,}500 + 31{,}000 = 40{,}500$.\\n\\nChoice A is the number of $500$-soum coins. Choice C is about $40{,}500 \\\\div 1{,}000$, as if all coins were $1{,}000$-soum coins. Choice D is $40{,}500 \\\\div 500$, as if all coins were $500$-soum coins.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$500$ so‘mlik tangalar soni $a$, $1{,}000$ so‘mliklar soni $b$ bo‘lsin: $a + b = 50$ va $500a + 1{,}000b = 40{,}500$. $a = 50 - b$ ni qo‘yamiz: $25{,}000 - 500b + 1{,}000b = 40{,}500$, demak $500b = 15{,}500$ va $b = 31$. Tekshiruv: $19$ ta $500$ so‘mlik va $31$ ta $1{,}000$ so‘mlik tanga $9{,}500 + 31{,}000 = 40{,}500$ so‘m beradi.\\n\\nA variant — $500$ so‘mlik tangalar soni. C variant — taxminan $40{,}500 \\\\div 1{,}000$, go‘yo hamma tangalar $1{,}000$ so‘mlik. D variant — $40{,}500 \\\\div 500$, go‘yo hamma tangalar $500$ so‘mlik.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"word-problem\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"systems_linear_equations\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"At a stationery shop, $4$ notebooks and $3$ pens cost a total of $31{,}000$ soums, and $2$ notebooks and $5$ pens cost a total of $26{,}000$ soums. Each notebook has the same price and each pen has the same price. What is the price, in soums, of one pen?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"3000\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Let $n$ and $p$ be the prices of a notebook and a pen: $4n + 3p = 31{,}000$ and $2n + 5p = 26{,}000$. Multiply the second equation by $2$: $4n + 10p = 52{,}000$. Subtract the first equation: $7p = 21{,}000$, so $p = 3{,}000$. (Then $n = 5{,}500$; check: $2(5{,}500) + 5(3{,}000) = 26{,}000$.) Enter 3000 without a comma.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Daftar narxi $n$, ruchka narxi $p$ bo‘lsin: $4n + 3p = 31{,}000$ va $2n + 5p = 26{,}000$. Ikkinchi tenglamani $2$ ga ko‘paytiramiz: $4n + 10p = 52{,}000$. Undan birinchi tenglamani ayiramiz: $7p = 21{,}000$, demak $p = 3{,}000$. (U holda $n = 5{,}500$; tekshiruv: $2(5{,}500) + 5(3{,}000) = 26{,}000$.) Javobni vergulsiz 3000 deb yozing.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"word-problem\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"systems_linear_equations\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"One equation in a system of two linear equations is $4x - 6y = 10$. Which of the following could be the other equation if the system has infinitely many solutions?\",\"option_a\":\"$2x - 3y = 10$\",\"option_b\":\"$6x - 4y = 10$\",\"option_c\":\"$2x - 3y = 5$\",\"option_d\":\"$4x + 6y = 10$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Dividing $4x - 6y = 10$ by $2$ gives $2x - 3y = 5$, an equivalent equation. Both equations describe the same line, so every point on that line is a solution: infinitely many solutions.\\n\\nChoice A has the same left side as $2x - 3y = 5$ but a different constant, so its line is parallel to the first one: no solution. Choices B and D have different slopes, so each gives exactly one solution.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$4x - 6y = 10$ ni $2$ ga bo‘lsak, $2x - 3y = 5$ hosil bo‘ladi — bu teng kuchli tenglama. Ikkala tenglama bitta to‘g‘ri chiziqni ifodalaydi, shuning uchun shu chiziqdagi har bir nuqta yechim: cheksiz ko‘p yechim.\\n\\nA variantning chap tomoni $2x - 3y = 5$ niki bilan bir xil, lekin ozod hadi boshqa, demak uning chizig‘i birinchisiga parallel: yechim yo‘q. B va D variantlarning qiyaligi boshqacha, shuning uchun har biri aynan bitta yechim beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"infinitely-many\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"systems_linear_equations\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"At a bakery, $3$ cakes and $4$ pies cost a total of $205$ thousand soums, and $5$ cakes and $2$ pies cost a total of $225$ thousand soums. Each cake has the same price and each pie has the same price. How many thousand soums more does one cake cost than one pie?\",\"option_a\":\"$10$\",\"option_b\":\"$20$\",\"option_c\":\"$25$\",\"option_d\":\"$35$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Let $c$ and $p$ be the prices of a cake and a pie, in thousands of soums: $3c + 4p = 205$ and $5c + 2p = 225$. Subtract the first equation from the second: $2c - 2p = 20$, so $c - p = 10$. (Solving fully gives $c = 35$ and $p = 25$.)\\n\\nChoice B is $2c - 2p$; the division by $2$ was skipped. Choice C is the price of one pie. Choice D is the price of one cake.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tort va pirog narxi (ming so‘mda) $c$ va $p$ bo‘lsin: $3c + 4p = 205$ va $5c + 2p = 225$. Ikkinchi tenglamadan birinchisini ayiramiz: $2c - 2p = 20$, demak $c - p = 10$. (To‘liq yechsak, $c = 35$ va $p = 25$.)\\n\\nB variant — $2c - 2p$; $2$ ga bo‘lish o‘tkazib yuborilgan. C variant — bitta pirog narxi. D variant — bitta tort narxi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"combination\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"systems_linear_equations\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\begin{aligned} 3x + 5y &= 7 \\\\\\\\ 5x + 3y &= k \\\\end{aligned}$$\\n\\nIn the given system of equations, $k$ is a constant. If the solution $(x, y)$ to the system satisfies $x + y = 3$, what is the value of $k$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"17\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Add the two equations: $8x + 8y = 7 + k$, so $8(x + y) = 7 + k$. Since $x + y = 3$, $24 = 7 + k$, and $k = 17$. Check: from $3x + 5y = 7$ and $x + y = 3$ we get $x = 4$ and $y = -1$, and $5(4) + 3(-1) = 17$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikki tenglamani qo‘shamiz: $8x + 8y = 7 + k$, ya’ni $8(x + y) = 7 + k$. $x + y = 3$ bo‘lgani uchun $24 = 7 + k$ va $k = 17$. Tekshiruv: $3x + 5y = 7$ va $x + y = 3$ dan $x = 4$, $y = -1$ chiqadi va $5(4) + 3(-1) = 17$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"combination-parameter\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"systems_linear_equations\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"In a math competition with $40$ questions, a contestant earns $2$ points for each correct answer and loses $0.5$ point for each incorrect answer. Unanswered questions earn $0$ points. Ravshan answered $36$ of the questions and finished with a score of $52$ points. How many questions did Ravshan answer incorrectly?\",\"option_a\":\"$4$\",\"option_b\":\"$8$\",\"option_c\":\"$20$\",\"option_d\":\"$28$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Let $c$ be the number of correct answers and $w$ the number of incorrect answers. He answered $36$ questions, so $c + w = 36$, and $2c - 0.5w = 52$. Substitute $c = 36 - w$: $72 - 2w - 0.5w = 52$, so $2.5w = 20$ and $w = 8$. Check: $c = 28$, and $2(28) - 0.5(8) = 56 - 4 = 52$.\\n\\nChoice A is the number of unanswered questions ($40 - 36$). Choice C is the value of $2.5w$; the final division by $2.5$ was skipped. Choice D is the number of correct answers.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"To‘g‘ri javoblar soni $c$, noto‘g‘rilar soni $w$ bo‘lsin. U $36$ ta savolga javob bergan, demak $c + w = 36$ va $2c - 0.5w = 52$. $c = 36 - w$ ni qo‘yamiz: $72 - 2w - 0.5w = 52$, ya’ni $2.5w = 20$ va $w = 8$. Tekshiruv: $c = 28$ va $2(28) - 0.5(8) = 56 - 4 = 52$.\\n\\nA variant — javob berilmagan savollar soni ($40 - 36$). C variant — $2.5w$ ning qiymati; oxirida $2.5$ ga bo‘lish o‘tkazib yuborilgan. D variant — to‘g‘ri javoblar soni.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"word-problem\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"systems_linear_equations\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"$$\\\\begin{aligned} \\\\frac{x}{3} - \\\\frac{y}{2} &= 1 \\\\\\\\ cx - 3y &= 10 \\\\end{aligned}$$\\n\\nIn the given system of equations, $c$ is a constant. For what value of $c$ does the system have no solution?\",\"option_a\":\"$-2$\",\"option_b\":\"$\\\\frac{1}{3}$\",\"option_c\":\"$2$\",\"option_d\":\"$6$\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Multiply the first equation by $6$ to clear the fractions: $2x - 3y = 6$. The second equation is $cx - 3y = 10$. The $y$-coefficients already match, so if $c = 2$ the left sides are identical while the right sides are different ($6 \\\\ne 10$). The lines are parallel and distinct, so there is no solution. For any other value of $c$, the slopes differ and there is exactly one solution.\\n\\nChoice A gets the sign of the $x$-coefficient wrong. Choice B copies the coefficient of $x$ from the first equation without clearing the fractions; the $y$-coefficients would then not match. Choice D is the multiplier $6$, not the resulting coefficient.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Kasrlardan qutulish uchun birinchi tenglamani $6$ ga ko‘paytiramiz: $2x - 3y = 6$. Ikkinchi tenglama $cx - 3y = 10$. $y$ koeffitsiyentlari allaqachon teng, shuning uchun $c = 2$ bo‘lsa, chap tomonlar bir xil, o‘ng tomonlar esa har xil ($6 \\\\ne 10$). To‘g‘ri chiziqlar parallel va ustma-ust tushmaydi, demak yechim yo‘q. $c$ ning boshqa har qanday qiymatida qiyaliklar har xil bo‘lib, aynan bitta yechim bo‘ladi.\\n\\nA variantda $x$ koeffitsiyentining ishorasi xato. B variant kasrlardan qutulmasdan birinchi tenglamadagi $x$ koeffitsiyentini ko‘chirgan; bunda $y$ koeffitsiyentlari mos kelmaydi. D variant — natijaviy koeffitsiyent emas, ko‘paytiruvchi $6$.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"no-solution-parameter\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"systems_linear_equations\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"Akmal deposited a total of $24$ million soums into two savings accounts. One account pays $6\\\\%$ simple interest per year, and the other pays $9\\\\%$ simple interest per year. After one year, the two accounts earned a total of $1.89$ million soums in interest. How many million soums did Akmal deposit into the account that pays $9\\\\%$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"15\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Let $A$ and $B$ be the amounts, in millions of soums, in the $6\\\\%$ and $9\\\\%$ accounts: $A + B = 24$ and $0.06A + 0.09B = 1.89$. Substitute $A = 24 - B$: $1.44 - 0.06B + 0.09B = 1.89$, so $0.03B = 0.45$ and $B = 15$. Check: $0.06(9) + 0.09(15) = 0.54 + 1.35 = 1.89$. Entering $9$ gives the amount in the $6\\\\%$ account.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$6\\\\%$ li va $9\\\\%$ li hisoblardagi summalar (million so‘mda) $A$ va $B$ bo‘lsin: $A + B = 24$ va $0.06A + 0.09B = 1.89$. $A = 24 - B$ ni qo‘yamiz: $1.44 - 0.06B + 0.09B = 1.89$, demak $0.03B = 0.45$ va $B = 15$. Tekshiruv: $0.06(9) + 0.09(15) = 0.54 + 1.35 = 1.89$. $9$ deb yozish — bu $6\\\\%$ li hisobdagi summa.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"word-problem\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In the dry interior of Australia, rain may not fall for many months. The water-holding frog survives these long droughts underground. After a heavy rain, the frog stores extra water in its body and then burrows into the soil. There it sheds several layers of skin, which harden around it into a snug cocoon that slows the loss of moisture. Sealed inside, the frog can wait for months, sometimes far longer, until the next heavy rain softens the ground and it digs its way back to the surface.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"To describe how one animal survives long periods without rain\",\"option_b\":\"To compare the water-holding frog with other desert animals\",\"option_c\":\"To explain why rain is so rare in the interior of Australia\",\"option_d\":\"To argue that the water-holding frog is threatened by climate change\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Nearly every sentence describes the steps the frog takes to get through a drought: storing water, burrowing, forming a cocoon, and waiting for rain. Choice A states this purpose.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because no other animals are mentioned. Choice C is wrong because the text only notes that rain is rare; it never explains why. Choice D is wrong because climate change and threats to the frog are not discussed.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Deyarli har bir gap qurbaqaning qurg‘oqchilikdan qanday omon qolishini bosqichma-bosqich tasvirlaydi: suv to‘plash, yerga ko‘milish, pilla hosil qilish va yomg‘irni kutish. A javob aynan shu maqsadni ifodalaydi.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: boshqa hayvonlar tilga olinmagan. C noto‘g‘ri: matn faqat yomg‘ir kam yog‘ishini qayd etadi, sababini tushuntirmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: iqlim o‘zgarishi yoki qurbaqaga tahdidlar muhokama qilinmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science\",\"main-purpose\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Along the old trade routes of Central Asia, travelers rested at caravanserais, walled inns built around a large open courtyard. These inns were usually spaced about a day's journey apart, so that a caravan setting out at dawn could reach the next one before dark. The single, heavy gate could be locked at night to protect travelers and their goods, and the courtyard held enough space for dozens of camels, while rooms for the merchants lined the surrounding walls.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"To argue that caravanserais were more comfortable than modern hotels\",\"option_b\":\"To explain why trade along Central Asian routes declined\",\"option_c\":\"To tell the story of a particular caravan's journey\",\"option_d\":\"To describe how the design and placement of caravanserais served the needs of travelers\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text explains that caravanserais were spaced a day's journey apart so caravans could arrive before dark, had a lockable gate for safety, and had room for camels and merchants. Each detail links a feature of the inns to a traveler's need, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because modern hotels are never mentioned. Choice B is wrong because the text says nothing about trade declining. Choice C is wrong because the text describes caravanserais in general, not one specific caravan's trip.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda karvonsaroylar karvon qorong‘i tushguncha yetib olishi uchun bir kunlik yo‘l oralig‘ida qurilgani, xavfsizlik uchun qulflanadigan darvozasi va tuyalar hamda savdogarlar uchun joyi borligi tushuntiriladi. Har bir tafsilot binoning biror xususiyatini yo‘lovchilarning ehtiyoji bilan bog‘laydi — D javobda aytilganidek.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: zamonaviy mehmonxonalar tilga olinmagan. B noto‘g‘ri: savdoning pasayishi haqida hech narsa aytilmagan. C noto‘g‘ri: matn muayyan bir karvonning safarini emas, umuman karvonsaroylarni tasvirlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"history\",\"main-purpose\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"When the public library in the city of Nurafshon began staying open until 10 p.m., librarians expected the extra hours to attract mostly students preparing for exams. Instead, the evening visitors turned out to be remarkably varied. __On a typical night, a nurse coming off a late shift might be reading in one corner while a retired engineer helped a group of children with their science projects in another.__ The library's director now describes the evening hours as the busiest and most mixed of the day.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?\",\"option_a\":\"It explains why librarians first decided to extend the library's hours.\",\"option_b\":\"It gives an example of the variety of people who use the library in the evening.\",\"option_c\":\"It presents evidence that most evening visitors are students preparing for exams.\",\"option_d\":\"It describes a problem that the library's director hopes to solve.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The sentence before the underlined one says the evening visitors were “remarkably varied.” The underlined sentence then shows this variety with specific people: a nurse, a retired engineer, and children. It is an example, as choice B says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the sentence does not explain the decision to extend hours. Choice C is wrong because the text says the librarians' expectation about students was wrong, and the sentence mentions no exam students. Choice D is wrong because the sentence describes a positive situation, not a problem.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Belgilangan gapdan oldingi gapda kechki tashrif buyuruvchilar “juda xilma-xil” ekani aytiladi. Belgilangan gap esa bu xilma-xillikni aniq odamlar orqali ko‘rsatadi: hamshira, nafaqadagi muhandis va bolalar. Bu — misol, B javobda aytilganidek.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: gap ish vaqtini uzaytirish qarorini tushuntirmaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: matnga ko‘ra kutubxonachilarning talabalar haqidagi taxmini noto‘g‘ri chiqqan, gapda imtihonga tayyorlanayotgan talabalar tilga olinmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: gap muammoni emas, ijobiy holatni tasvirlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"social-science\",\"underlined-function\",\"example\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from a short story. Zarina is about to perform in her first piano competition.\\n\\nZarina sat in the wings with her hands pressed flat against her knees to keep them still. Out on the stage, the boy before her was finishing his piece, and every note he played seemed to her more perfect than anything she could manage. She went over the opening bars of her own piece in her head, then went over them again, and then could not remember whether the third chord came before or after the long rest.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"To describe the boy who performs before Zarina\",\"option_b\":\"To explain how Zarina chose the piece she will perform\",\"option_c\":\"To show Zarina's nervousness as she waits to perform\",\"option_d\":\"To suggest that Zarina is confident she will win the competition\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Zarina presses her hands against her knees “to keep them still,” thinks the other boy's playing is better than hers, rehearses her opening again and again, and then forgets part of it. These details show her anxiety before performing, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the boy is mentioned only to show how Zarina reacts to him. Choice B is wrong because the text never explains how she chose her piece. Choice D is wrong because she doubts herself; nothing suggests confidence.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Zarina qo‘llari qimirlamasligi uchun ularni tizzalariga bosib turadi, boshqa bolaning chalishi o‘zinikidan yaxshiroq deb o‘ylaydi, asarining boshlanishini qayta-qayta takrorlaydi va oxiri uning bir qismini unutib qo‘yadi. Bu tafsilotlar uning chiqish oldidan hayajonlanayotganini ko‘rsatadi — C javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: bola faqat Zarinaning unga munosabatini ko‘rsatish uchun tilga olinadi. B noto‘g‘ri: matnda u asarni qanday tanlagani aytilmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: u o‘ziga ishonmaydi, ishonch haqida hech qanday ishora yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"literature\",\"main-purpose\",\"character\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Many people believe that a camel's hump is filled with water. In fact, the hump stores fat, which the camel can draw on for energy when food is scarce. Camels survive long stretches without drinking for other reasons. Their bodies lose very little water through sweat and urine, and they can tolerate a degree of dehydration that would kill most other mammals. When they finally reach water, they can drink enormous amounts in just a few minutes.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"It describes how camels find water and then explains how they store it in their humps.\",\"option_b\":\"It compares camels with other mammals and then argues that camels are the best-adapted desert animals.\",\"option_c\":\"It presents a common belief about camels, corrects it, and then gives the actual reasons camels can go long periods without drinking.\",\"option_d\":\"It lists several features of camels and then explains how scientists discovered each one.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text opens with a belief (“Many people believe” the hump holds water), corrects it (“In fact, the hump stores fat”), and then explains the real reasons camels can go without drinking. Choice C describes this structure.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the text denies that camels store water in their humps. Choice B is wrong because other mammals are mentioned only briefly, and the text never claims camels are the best-adapted desert animals. Choice D is wrong because the text never describes how scientists made any discoveries.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn bir qarash bilan boshlanadi (“Ko‘pchilik o‘rkachda suv bor deb hisoblaydi”), so‘ng uni tuzatadi (“Aslida o‘rkachda yog‘ saqlanadi”) va keyin tuyalar uzoq vaqt suv ichmasdan yura olishining haqiqiy sabablarini tushuntiradi. C javob shu tuzilishni tasvirlaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matn tuya o‘rkachida suv saqlashini rad etadi. B noto‘g‘ri: boshqa sutemizuvchilar faqat qisqa tilga olinadi va tuyalar cho‘lga eng yaxshi moslashgan hayvon deb da’vo qilinmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: matnda olimlar biror narsani qanday kashf etgani tasvirlanmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science\",\"overall-structure\",\"misconception\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In Uzbekistan, large embroidered textiles called suzani were traditionally made by the women of a bride's family as part of her dowry. Because a single suzani could take months or even years to complete, the cloth was often cut into several long strips, and each strip was embroidered by a different relative before the pieces were sewn back together. __As a result, the patterns on neighboring strips do not always line up perfectly, and the colors of a single flower may shift slightly from one strip to the next.__\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?\",\"option_a\":\"It describes a visible effect of the way suzani were made.\",\"option_b\":\"It explains why suzani took so long to complete.\",\"option_c\":\"It argues that suzani made by a single person are more valuable.\",\"option_d\":\"It suggests that the women who made suzani lacked skill.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The previous sentence explains that each strip was embroidered by a different relative and then sewn together. The underlined sentence, starting with “As a result,” describes what this produces: patterns that do not line up and colors that shift. It describes an effect of the process, as choice A says.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because the length of the work is explained in the previous sentence, not in the underlined one. Choice C is wrong because the text never compares the value of different suzani. Choice D is wrong because the mismatches come from several people working separately, not from a lack of skill.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Oldingi gapda har bir bo‘lakni boshqa qarindosh tikkani va keyin bo‘laklar birlashtirilgani tushuntiriladi. “As a result” (natijada) bilan boshlanadigan belgilangan gap bu jarayonning oqibatini tasvirlaydi: naqshlar bir-biriga to‘liq mos kelmaydi, ranglar biroz farq qiladi. Demak, gap tayyorlash usulining ko‘zga ko‘rinadigan natijasini tasvirlaydi — A javob.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: ishning uzoq davom etishi belgilangan gapda emas, oldingi gapda tushuntirilgan. C noto‘g‘ri: matnda turli suzanalarning qiymati taqqoslanmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: nomuvofiqliklar mahorat yetishmasligidan emas, bir necha kishi alohida ishlaganidan kelib chiqadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"humanities\",\"underlined-function\",\"cause-effect\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Birds that live in cities often sing at a higher pitch than members of the same species living in forests. Biologist Rustam Qosimov assumed that the birds were simply avoiding the low rumble of traffic, which can drown out deep notes. To test this idea, he recorded birds in quiet city parks far from busy roads. Surprisingly, these birds also sang at high pitches. Qosimov now suspects that hard surfaces such as glass and concrete, which reflect high notes more clearly than low ones, may also shape urban birdsong.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"It describes a difference in birdsong, explains why traffic noise is harmful to birds, and then recommends quieter roads.\",\"option_b\":\"It notes a pattern in birdsong, presents a researcher's explanation and a test of it, and then describes how an unexpected result led him to consider another factor.\",\"option_c\":\"It presents two competing explanations for a pattern in birdsong and then describes an experiment showing that both are wrong.\",\"option_d\":\"It describes how a researcher recorded birdsong and then explains why city birds sing more often than forest birds.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text first notes a pattern (city birds sing at higher pitches), then gives Qosimov's explanation (avoiding traffic noise) and his test of it (recording in quiet parks). The “surprising” result leads him to suspect another factor, reflective surfaces. Choice B follows this order.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the text never recommends quieter roads or discusses harm to birds. Choice C is wrong because the text begins with only one explanation, and the result does not show both explanations are wrong; Qosimov thinks surfaces “may also” play a role. Choice D is wrong because the text is about pitch, not how often birds sing.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn avval bir qonuniyatni qayd etadi (shahar qushlari balandroq ovozda sayraydi), keyin Qosimovning izohini (transport shovqinidan qochish) va uni sinovdan o‘tkazishini (tinch bog‘larda yozib olish) keltiradi. “Kutilmagan” natija uni boshqa omil — aks ettiruvchi sirtlar haqida o‘ylashga undaydi. B javob shu tartibga mos.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matnda tinchroq yo‘llar taklif qilinmaydi va qushlarga zarar muhokama qilinmaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: matn faqat bitta izoh bilan boshlanadi va natija ikkala izoh ham noto‘g‘ri ekanini ko‘rsatmaydi — Qosimov sirtlar “ham” ta’sir qilishi mumkin deb hisoblaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: matn qushlar qanchalik tez-tez sayrashi haqida emas, ovoz balandligi haqida.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science\",\"overall-structure\",\"hypothesis-test\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In 2021, the city of Yangiobod made its buses free to ride. Over the next two years, the number of bus trips rose by 38 percent, and economist Dilshod Ergashev argues that eliminating fares was the main reason. __Ridership did also rise in two nearby cities that kept their fares, though by only about 10 percent, probably because of new housing built along their bus routes.__ Even so, Ergashev notes, Yangiobod's increase was nearly four times as large, a gap that new housing alone cannot easily explain.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?\",\"option_a\":\"It provides the main evidence for Ergashev's claim that free fares increased ridership.\",\"option_b\":\"It explains why Yangiobod decided to stop charging bus fares.\",\"option_c\":\"It suggests that Ergashev's conclusion about Yangiobod is mistaken.\",\"option_d\":\"It acknowledges a fact that might seem to weaken Ergashev's claim before the text explains why that claim still holds.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Ergashev claims that free fares were the main cause of Yangiobod's ridership increase. The underlined sentence admits that ridership rose even in cities that kept fares, which might suggest other causes. The next sentence (“Even so”) explains that Yangiobod's increase was much larger. So the underlined sentence concedes a complicating fact, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because ridership growth in other cities does not support the free-fare explanation; the main evidence is the 38 percent rise and the comparison that follows. Choice B is wrong because the sentence is about other cities, not about Yangiobod's decision. Choice C is wrong because the text goes on to defend Ergashev's conclusion rather than reject it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ergashev Yangiobodda yo‘lovchilar ko‘payishining asosiy sababi bepul yo‘l haqi deb hisoblaydi. Belgilangan gap esa yo‘l haqini saqlab qolgan shaharlarda ham yo‘lovchilar ko‘payganini tan oladi — bu boshqa sabablar ham bor degan fikrga olib kelishi mumkin. Keyingi gap (“Even so” — shunday bo‘lsa-da) Yangiobodda o‘sish ancha katta bo‘lganini tushuntiradi. Demak, belgilangan gap da’voni murakkablashtiruvchi faktni tan oladi — D javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: boshqa shaharlardagi o‘sish bepul yo‘l haqi izohini qo‘llab-quvvatlamaydi; asosiy dalil — 38 foizlik o‘sish va undan keyingi taqqoslash. B noto‘g‘ri: gap Yangiobodning qarori haqida emas, boshqa shaharlar haqida. C noto‘g‘ri: matn keyin Ergashevning xulosasini rad etmaydi, balki himoya qiladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"social-science\",\"underlined-function\",\"concession\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In the 1890s, Mariam Valieva was one of only a handful of women working as professional photographers in the Russian Empire, yet her name rarely appears in histories of the period. Historian Gulnora Saidova argues that this neglect has little to do with the quality of Valieva's work. Most of Valieva's surviving prints, Saidova points out, were stamped with the name of the studio that employed her rather than her own, so for decades archivists simply credited them to the studio's male owner.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"To present a historian's explanation for why a photographer's work has been overlooked\",\"option_b\":\"To argue that Valieva was the most talented photographer of her time\",\"option_c\":\"To describe the techniques Valieva used to produce her photographs\",\"option_d\":\"To criticize archivists for deliberately hiding the work of women photographers\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text notes that Valieva is rarely mentioned in histories and then presents Saidova's explanation: her prints were stamped with the studio's name, so archivists credited them to the studio's owner. Choice A captures this purpose.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because the text says the neglect is not about quality but never claims Valieva was the most talented. Choice C is wrong because no photographic techniques are described. Choice D is wrong because the archivists credited the studio owner “simply” because of the stamp; the text does not say they acted deliberately, and its tone is explanatory rather than critical.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda Valieva tarix kitoblarida kam tilga olinishi qayd etiladi, so‘ng Saidovaning izohi keltiriladi: suratlarga studiya nomi muhrlangani sababli arxivchilar ularni studiya egasiga nisbat bergan. A javob shu maqsadni ifodalaydi.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: matnda e’tibordan chetda qolish sifat bilan bog‘liq emasligi aytiladi, ammo Valieva o‘z davrining eng iste’dodli fotografi deb da’vo qilinmaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: fotosurat texnikalari tasvirlanmagan. D noto‘g‘ri: arxivchilar muhr sababli “shunchaki” studiya egasini ko‘rsatgan; ular buni ataylab qilgani aytilmaydi, matn ohangi esa tanqidiy emas, tushuntiruvchi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"history\",\"main-purpose\",\"wrong-verb-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from a short story. Akmal has returned to his family's farm after several years working in the city.\\n\\nHis father met him at the gate and took his suitcase without a word, as he always had. They walked up the path together past the apricot trees. Akmal noticed that the fence by the well had been mended with new wire, and that the old dog no longer came running. __He wanted to ask about the dog, but his father was already talking about the price of seed, and the moment passed.__ Inside, his mother had set the table for three.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?\",\"option_a\":\"It shows that Akmal has forgotten the details of life on the farm.\",\"option_b\":\"It conveys Akmal's hesitation to raise a painful question and the ease with which the chance to ask it slips away.\",\"option_c\":\"It reveals that Akmal's father is angry that his son left the farm.\",\"option_d\":\"It explains why the fence by the well had to be repaired.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Akmal notices that the old dog “no longer came running,” suggesting the dog may have died. In the underlined sentence he wants to ask about it but does not, and “the moment passed” while his father talks about something ordinary. The sentence conveys his reluctance to raise a painful topic and how easily the chance slips by, as choice B says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Akmal notices small changes on the farm, which shows he remembers it well. Choice C is wrong because the father's silence and talk about seed prices are presented as his usual manner (“as he always had”), not as anger. Choice D is wrong because the sentence is about the dog, not the fence.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Akmal keksa it endi “yugurib chiqmayotgani”ni payqaydi — bu it o‘lgan bo‘lishi mumkinligiga ishora. Belgilangan gapda u bu haqda so‘ramoqchi bo‘ladi, lekin otasi oddiy narsa haqida gapirayotganda “fursat o‘tib ketadi”. Gap Akmalning og‘riqli savolni berishga ikkilanishini va bu imkoniyat qanchalik oson qo‘ldan chiqishini ifodalaydi — B javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Akmal fermadagi kichik o‘zgarishlarni payqaydi, demak uni yaxshi eslaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: otaning jimligi va urug‘ narxi haqidagi gapi uning odatiy xulqi sifatida berilgan (“as he always had”), g‘azab sifatida emas. D noto‘g‘ri: gap to‘siq haqida emas, it haqida.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"literature\",\"underlined-function\",\"character\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Merchant account books from the Black Sea port of Seravan, kept in the 1460s, are mostly long columns of prices and quantities that few historians had studied closely. When historian Anvar Tojiboyev examined the originals rather than printed copies, he noticed that dozens of entries for grain had been scraped away and rewritten, always with higher prices. By comparing these corrections with records of storms and blockades, Tojiboyev concluded that the merchants had revised their accounts during sudden shortages, revealing how quickly grain prices responded to crises at sea.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"It argues that printed copies of historical documents are unreliable and then recommends that historians avoid them.\",\"option_b\":\"It describes a storm that struck a port and then explains how merchants responded to it.\",\"option_c\":\"It explains how account books were kept and then compares the prices of several goods.\",\"option_d\":\"It introduces a type of source that has received little attention, describes a feature one historian noticed in it, and explains what he learned from that feature.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text first introduces the account books, which “few historians had studied closely.” It then describes what Tojiboyev noticed (scraped and rewritten grain prices) and finally explains what he concluded about how prices responded to crises. Choice D matches this sequence.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the text never argues that printed copies are unreliable in general or recommends avoiding them; it only notes that Tojiboyev looked at the originals. Choice B is wrong because no single storm is described; storms are mentioned only as records Tojiboyev compared. Choice C is wrong because the text discusses only grain prices and does not explain how the books were kept.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn avval hisob daftarlarini tanishtiradi — ularni “kam tarixchi sinchiklab o‘rgangan”. Keyin Tojiboyev nimani payqaganini (don narxlari qirib tashlanib, qayta yozilganini) tasvirlaydi va oxirida narxlar inqirozlarga qanday javob bergani haqidagi xulosasini tushuntiradi. D javob shu ketma-ketlikka mos.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matnda bosma nusxalar umuman ishonchsiz deb da’vo qilinmaydi va ulardan voz kechish tavsiya etilmaydi; faqat Tojiboyev asl nusxalarni ko‘rgani aytiladi. B noto‘g‘ri: muayyan bir bo‘ron tasvirlanmagan; bo‘ronlar faqat Tojiboyev taqqoslagan yozuvlar sifatida tilga olinadi. C noto‘g‘ri: matnda faqat don narxlari muhokama qilinadi va daftarlar qanday yuritilgani tushuntirilmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"history\",\"overall-structure\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In regions with distinct seasons, most trees add one growth ring each year, and the width of each ring depends on that year's weather: wet years generally produce wide rings, and dry years narrow ones. Because the pattern of wide and narrow rings over several decades is unlikely to repeat exactly, researchers can match the pattern in an old wooden beam with the overlapping pattern in a living tree or another dated timber. Linking many such overlapping sequences allows them to determine the year in which a beam's tree was cut down.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"To argue that tree rings are the most accurate source of information about past weather\",\"option_b\":\"To describe how weather in wet years differs from weather in dry years\",\"option_c\":\"To explain how patterns in tree rings can be used to date old pieces of wood\",\"option_d\":\"To compare the growth of trees in regions with and without distinct seasons\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text explains that ring widths vary with weather, that ring patterns over decades are distinctive, and that matching overlapping patterns lets researchers date a beam. Every sentence builds toward explaining this dating method, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the text does not compare tree rings with other sources or call them the most accurate. Choice B is wrong because wet and dry years are mentioned only to explain why rings differ in width. Choice D is wrong because regions without distinct seasons are never discussed.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda halqalar kengligi ob-havoga qarab o‘zgarishi, o‘n yilliklar davomidagi halqa naqshi o‘ziga xos bo‘lishi va bir-birini qoplaydigan naqshlarni moslashtirib, to‘sin yoshini aniqlash mumkinligi tushuntiriladi. Har bir gap shu sanalash usulini tushuntirishga xizmat qiladi — C javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matnda daraxt halqalari boshqa manbalar bilan taqqoslanmaydi va eng aniq manba deyilmaydi. B noto‘g‘ri: nam va quruq yillar faqat halqalar kengligi nega farq qilishini tushuntirish uchun tilga olinadi. D noto‘g‘ri: aniq fasllari bo‘lmagan mintaqalar muhokama qilinmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science\",\"main-purpose\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from a short story. Nilufar has returned to the village where she grew up.\\n\\nThe street was narrower than she remembered, and the great plane tree in the square, which had once seemed to hold up the whole sky, was only a tree. The teahouse where her grandfather had argued about politics every evening now sold mobile phone cards. She felt foolish for having expected anything else. Still, when an old woman at the well squinted at her and said, “You're Hamid's granddaughter, aren't you? You have his frown,” Nilufar found that she had to look away for a moment.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"To show that Nilufar is relieved to find that the village has modernized\",\"option_b\":\"To describe the changes in the village in order to criticize its residents for abandoning tradition\",\"option_c\":\"To explain why Nilufar left the village when she was young\",\"option_d\":\"To convey Nilufar's mixed feelings as she finds the village diminished yet discovers that she is still recognized as belonging to it\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first part of the text stresses disappointment: the street is “narrower,” the tree is “only a tree,” the teahouse has changed, and Nilufar feels “foolish.” Then “Still” introduces a turn: an old woman recognizes her as Hamid's granddaughter, and Nilufar is so moved that she must look away. The text conveys both feelings at once, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Nilufar feels foolish and let down by the changes, not relieved. Choice B is wrong because the changes are described from Nilufar's point of view without blaming the residents. Choice C is wrong because the text never says why she left.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnning birinchi qismi hafsalasi pir bo‘lganini ta’kidlaydi: ko‘cha “torroq”, chinor “shunchaki daraxt”, choyxona o‘zgargan, Nilufar esa o‘zini “ahmoqona” his qiladi. Keyin “Still” (shunday bo‘lsa-da) burilishni boshlaydi: keksa ayol uni Hamidning nabirasi deb taniydi va Nilufar shunchalik ta’sirlanadiki, bir lahza ko‘zini olib qochadi. Matn ikkala tuyg‘uni birga ifodalaydi — D javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Nilufar o‘zgarishlardan yengil tortmaydi, aksincha hafsalasi pir bo‘ladi. B noto‘g‘ri: o‘zgarishlar Nilufar nigohi orqali tasvirlanadi, aholi ayblanmaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: matnda uning nega ketgani aytilmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"literature\",\"main-purpose\",\"character\",\"mixed-feelings\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Lichens, partnerships between a fungus and an alga or bacterium, are often called pioneer organisms because they can grow on bare rock where nothing else lives. Over centuries, acids they release help break the rock into the first thin layers of soil. __This is not to say that every patch of bare rock must wait for lichens before plants can take hold: on some lava flows, ecologists have found mosses and even ferns rooted in cracks where windblown dust had collected, with no lichens nearby.__ In most rocky landscapes, though, lichens remain among the first arrivals.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?\",\"option_a\":\"It qualifies the claim that lichens are pioneer organisms by noting cases in which plants became established without them.\",\"option_b\":\"It provides additional evidence that lichens are necessary for soil to form on bare rock.\",\"option_c\":\"It explains how lichens produce the acids that break down rock.\",\"option_d\":\"It rejects the view that lichens contribute to soil formation.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text presents lichens as pioneers that prepare bare rock for later life. The underlined sentence, beginning “This is not to say,” limits that idea: on some lava flows, plants grew without lichens. The final sentence then reaffirms that lichens are usually among the first arrivals. So the underlined sentence qualifies, rather than rejects, the main claim, as choice A says.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because the example shows plants growing without lichens, which goes against the idea that lichens are always necessary. Choice C is wrong because the acids are mentioned in the previous sentence, and the underlined sentence does not explain them. Choice D is too extreme: the sentence notes exceptions, and the text still says lichens help form soil and are usually first.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn lishayniklarni yalang‘och toshni keyingi hayot uchun tayyorlaydigan “kashshof” organizmlar sifatida taqdim etadi. “This is not to say” (bu degani emas) bilan boshlanadigan belgilangan gap bu fikrni cheklaydi: ba’zi lava oqimlarida o‘simliklar lishayniklarsiz o‘sgan. Oxirgi gap esa lishayniklar odatda birinchilar qatorida ekanini yana tasdiqlaydi. Demak, belgilangan gap asosiy da’voni rad etmaydi, balki uni aniqlashtirib, chegaralaydi — A javob.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: misol o‘simliklar lishayniksiz o‘sganini ko‘rsatadi, ya’ni lishayniklar har doim zarur degan fikrga zid. C noto‘g‘ri: kislotalar oldingi gapda tilga olingan, belgilangan gap ularni tushuntirmaydi. D juda keskin: gap faqat istisnolarni qayd etadi, matn esa baribir lishayniklar tuproq hosil bo‘lishiga yordam beradi va odatda birinchi keladi deydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science\",\"underlined-function\",\"qualification\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Scholars have long agreed that the poet Gulruh Sobiri, who wrote in the early nineteenth century, drew heavily on the Persian lyric tradition, borrowing its images of the nightingale and the rose. Literary historian Barno Ahmedova accepts this view but argues that it tells only half the story. In several of Sobiri's poems, Ahmedova observes, these familiar images appear alongside references to local places and events, such as the gardens of Kokand and a specific flood, details that would have meant little to readers outside the region. The borrowed images, she suggests, were reshaped to speak to a particular local audience.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"It presents a researcher's new interpretation of a poet's work and then describes the established view that this interpretation replaced.\",\"option_b\":\"It describes an established view of a poet's influences, presents a researcher who accepts that view but adds to it, and gives the evidence for her addition.\",\"option_c\":\"It describes a disagreement between two scholars and then explains why neither scholar's view is supported by the poems.\",\"option_d\":\"It summarizes a poet's life and then lists the images that appear most often in her work.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text begins with what scholars “have long agreed” on: Sobiri drew on Persian traditions. It then introduces Ahmedova, who “accepts this view” but says it is only “half the story,” and offers evidence (local places and events in the poems) for her addition. Choice B describes this structure in the correct order.\\n\\nChoice A reverses the order and misstates the relationship: the established view comes first, and Ahmedova adds to it rather than replacing it. Choice C is wrong because Ahmedova does not disagree with the earlier scholars, and the text supports her view with evidence from the poems. Choice D is wrong because the text gives no biography of Sobiri and does not simply list images.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn olimlar “uzoq vaqtdan beri kelishgan” fikr bilan boshlanadi: Sobiri fors lirik an’analariga tayangan. Keyin bu qarashni “qabul qiladigan”, ammo u “hikoyaning yarmi, xolos” deydigan Ahmedova tanishtiriladi va uning qo‘shimchasi uchun dalil (she’rlardagi mahalliy joylar va voqealar) keltiriladi. B javob bu tuzilishni to‘g‘ri tartibda tasvirlaydi.\\n\\nA tartibni teskari qiladi va munosabatni noto‘g‘ri beradi: avval eski qarash keladi, Ahmedova esa uni almashtirmaydi, balki to‘ldiradi. C noto‘g‘ri: Ahmedova avvalgi olimlarga qarshi chiqmaydi va matn uning fikrini she’rlardan olingan dalillar bilan quvvatlaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: matnda Sobirining tarjimai holi berilmagan va obrazlar shunchaki sanab o‘tilmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":105,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"humanities\",\"overall-structure\",\"order-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In many countries, people who live near a newly opened public park report feeling happier a year later. Some researchers take this as evidence that green space itself improves well-being. Others point out that new parks are often built in neighborhoods that are already becoming wealthier, and rising incomes could explain the change in mood. Economist Shahnoza Valiyeva notes that the two explanations make different predictions: if green space is responsible, residents who rarely visit the park should feel little change, whereas if income is responsible, park visits should matter less than changes in household earnings.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"To argue that new public parks improve the well-being of nearby residents\",\"option_b\":\"To criticize researchers for ignoring the effects of rising incomes\",\"option_c\":\"To present two possible explanations for a finding and identify a way that evidence could distinguish between them\",\"option_d\":\"To show that people who visit parks often earn more than those who do not\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text describes a finding (residents near new parks feel happier) and two explanations (green space versus rising incomes). Valiyeva then explains how the explanations predict different results, which could be used to tell them apart. The text does not take a side. Choice C states this purpose.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the text presents the green-space view as only one of two explanations and does not endorse it. Choice B uses the wrong verb: the text presents the income explanation as a point some researchers raise, and it does not criticize anyone. Choice D is wrong because the text never compares the incomes of park visitors with those of non-visitors.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda bir natija (yangi bog‘ yonida yashovchilar o‘zini baxtliroq his qiladi) va ikki izoh (yashil hudud yoki daromadning oshishi) tasvirlanadi. Keyin Valiyeva bu izohlar turli natijalarni bashorat qilishini va shu orqali ularni bir-biridan ajratish mumkinligini tushuntiradi. Matn hech qaysi tomonni olmaydi. C javob shu maqsadni ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matn yashil hudud qarashini ikki izohdan biri sifatida beradi va uni qo‘llab-quvvatlamaydi. B noto‘g‘ri fe’l ishlatadi: matn daromad izohini ba’zi tadqiqotchilar ko‘targan fikr sifatida keltiradi, hech kimni tanqid qilmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: matnda bog‘ga boradiganlar va bormaydiganlarning daromadi taqqoslanmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"social-science\",\"main-purpose\",\"wrong-verb-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Most spiders catch their food by spinning sticky webs and waiting for insects to fly into them. ______ jumping spiders do not build webs to trap prey; they stalk an insect, creep close to it, and then leap onto it.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"For instance,\",\"option_b\":\"In contrast,\",\"option_c\":\"Therefore,\",\"option_d\":\"Likewise,\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence describes how most spiders hunt: with sticky webs. The second sentence describes jumping spiders, which hunt in a different way, without webs. “In contrast” signals this difference, so choice B is correct.\\n\\n“For instance” would present jumping spiders as an example of web-building spiders, but they do not build webs. “Therefore” would wrongly suggest that jumping spiders stalk prey because most other spiders use webs. “Likewise” would mean that jumping spiders hunt the same way as other spiders, which the text contradicts.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda aksariyat o‘rgimchaklar qanday ov qilishi — yopishqoq to‘r bilan — tasvirlangan. Ikkinchi gapda esa to‘r to‘qimasdan, boshqacha usulda ov qiladigan sakrovchi o‘rgimchaklar haqida gap boradi. “In contrast” aynan shu farqni bildiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\n“For instance” sakrovchi o‘rgimchaklarni to‘r to‘qiydigan o‘rgimchaklarga misol qilib ko‘rsatadi, holbuki ular to‘r to‘qimaydi. “Therefore” sakrovchi o‘rgimchaklar boshqa o‘rgimchaklar to‘r ishlatgani uchun o‘ljani poylaydi degan noto‘g‘ri sababni bildiradi. “Likewise” ular boshqa o‘rgimchaklar kabi ov qiladi degan ma’noni beradi, bu esa matnga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"contrast\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Most writing systems used today, such as the Latin, Cyrillic, and Greek alphabets, are written from left to right. Some writing systems, however, run in the opposite direction. ______ Arabic and Hebrew are both written from right to left.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Nevertheless,\",\"option_b\":\"Instead,\",\"option_c\":\"As a result,\",\"option_d\":\"For example,\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The second sentence states that some writing systems run in the opposite direction, from right to left. The last sentence names two such systems, Arabic and Hebrew. “For example” introduces specific cases of a general statement, so choice D is correct.\\n\\n“Nevertheless” would signal a contrast, but Arabic and Hebrew agree with the sentence before the blank. “Instead” would suggest that Arabic and Hebrew replace something, which makes no sense here. “As a result” would wrongly suggest that Arabic and Hebrew are written from right to left because some writing systems run that way.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkinchi gapda ba’zi yozuv tizimlari teskari yo‘nalishda — o‘ngdan chapga — yozilishi aytilgan. Oxirgi gap esa shunday ikki tizimni — arab va ivrit yozuvlarini — nomlaydi. “For example” umumiy fikrga aniq misollar keltiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\n“Nevertheless” qarama-qarshilikni bildiradi, lekin arab va ivrit yozuvlari bo‘sh joydan oldingi gapga mos keladi. “Instead” arab va ivrit nimanidir almashtiradi degan ma’no beradi, bu yerda bu mantiqsiz. “As a result” arab va ivrit o‘ngdan chapga yozilishini ba’zi tizimlar shunday yozilgani natijasi qilib ko‘rsatadi, bu noto‘g‘ri.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"example\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"At a workshop near Samarkand, papermakers keep a centuries-old craft alive. They begin by soaking strips of mulberry bark in water and boiling them until the fibers soften. ______ they pound the softened bark into a pulp, which is mixed with water and lifted onto a fine screen to form a sheet.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Next,\",\"option_b\":\"Similarly,\",\"option_c\":\"In contrast,\",\"option_d\":\"For example,\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text describes the steps of making paper in order: first the bark is soaked and boiled, then it is pounded into pulp and formed into a sheet. “Next” signals the following step in a process, so choice A is correct.\\n\\n“Similarly” would suggest that pounding the bark is like boiling it, but it is simply the next step. “In contrast” would signal an opposition between the two steps, but there is none. “For example” would present pounding the bark as an example of soaking and boiling it, which it is not.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda qog‘oz tayyorlash bosqichlari ketma-ket tasvirlangan: avval po‘stloq ivitiladi va qaynatiladi, keyin u maydalanib, bo‘tqaga aylantiriladi va varaq hosil qilinadi. “Next” jarayondagi keyingi bosqichni bildiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\n“Similarly” po‘stloqni maydalash uni qaynatishga o‘xshaydi degan ma’no beradi, holbuki bu shunchaki keyingi bosqich. “In contrast” ikki bosqich bir-biriga qarama-qarshi ekanini bildiradi, lekin bunday qarama-qarshilik yo‘q. “For example” maydalashni ivitish va qaynatishga misol qilib ko‘rsatadi, bu esa noto‘g‘ri.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"sequence\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Unlike most substances, water expands when it freezes, so ice is less dense than liquid water. ______ ice floats, and in winter a layer of ice forms on the surface of a lake while the water beneath it stays liquid.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"However,\",\"option_b\":\"For instance,\",\"option_c\":\"As a result,\",\"option_d\":\"Meanwhile,\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence explains that ice is less dense than liquid water. The second sentence gives the consequence: ice floats and forms a layer on top of a lake. “As a result” signals an effect of what came before, so choice C is correct.\\n\\n“However” would signal a contrast, but floating is exactly what lower density leads to. “For instance” would present the floating ice as an example rather than as a consequence of its lower density. “Meanwhile” would only say that the two facts happen at the same time, missing the cause-and-effect link.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gap muz suyuq suvdan zichligi kamroq ekanini tushuntiradi. Ikkinchi gap esa natijani beradi: muz suv yuzida suzib yuradi va ko‘l ustida qatlam hosil qiladi. “As a result” oldingi fikrning natijasini bildiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\n“However” qarama-qarshilikni bildiradi, lekin suzib yurish aynan zichlik kamligining oqibati. “For instance” suzayotgan muzni zichlik kamligining natijasi emas, misoli qilib ko‘rsatadi. “Meanwhile” faqat ikki holat bir vaqtda sodir bo‘lishini aytadi va sabab-oqibat bog‘lanishini yo‘qotadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"cause-effect\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In a yearlong study at a school in Namangan, researcher Akmal Nazarov found that students who walked to school reported feeling more alert during their first class than students who arrived by car. ______ the students who walked missed fewer days of school because of illness over the course of the year.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Instead,\",\"option_b\":\"In addition,\",\"option_c\":\"Nevertheless,\",\"option_d\":\"For example,\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence gives one benefit that Nazarov found for students who walked: they felt more alert. The second sentence gives another, separate benefit: they missed fewer days because of illness. “In addition” adds a further point in the same direction, so choice B is correct.\\n\\n“Instead” would suggest that the second finding replaces the first. “Nevertheless” would signal a contrast, but both findings favor walking. “For example” would present missing fewer sick days as an example of feeling alert in class, but it is a different finding.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda Nazarov piyoda kelgan o‘quvchilar uchun aniqlagan bitta foyda — ular hushyorroq bo‘lgani — aytilgan. Ikkinchi gapda esa boshqa, alohida foyda: ular kasallik tufayli kamroq dars qoldirgan. “In addition” xuddi shu yo‘nalishdagi yana bir fikrni qo‘shadi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\n“Instead” ikkinchi natija birinchisining o‘rnini bosadi degan ma’no beradi. “Nevertheless” qarama-qarshilikni bildiradi, lekin ikkala natija ham piyoda yurish foydasiga. “For example” kamroq dars qoldirishni darsda hushyor bo‘lishga misol qilib ko‘rsatadi, holbuki bu boshqa natija.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"addition\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Scribes in ancient Mesopotamia wrote by pressing the tip of a reed into tablets of soft, wet clay. When the tablets were baked, whether on purpose or in an accidental fire, the clay hardened like pottery. ______ many of these tablets have survived for thousands of years.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"In contrast,\",\"option_b\":\"Similarly,\",\"option_c\":\"For instance,\",\"option_d\":\"Because of this,\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text explains that baking hardened the clay tablets like pottery. The last sentence gives the result: many tablets have lasted for thousands of years. “Because of this” signals that the survival of the tablets is a consequence of their hardening, so choice D is correct.\\n\\n“In contrast” would signal that the tablets' survival is opposed to their hardening, but hardening helps explain it. “Similarly” would present the last sentence as a parallel fact rather than a result. “For instance” would present the survival of the tablets as an example of baking, which it is not.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda kuydirish natijasida loy taxtachalar sopol kabi qotib qolgani tushuntirilgan. Oxirgi gap natijani beradi: ko‘plab taxtachalar ming yillar davomida saqlanib qolgan. “Because of this” taxtachalarning saqlanib qolishi ularning qotishi oqibati ekanini bildiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\n“In contrast” saqlanib qolish qotishga zid degan ma’no beradi, holbuki qotish buni tushuntiradi. “Similarly” oxirgi gapni natija emas, parallel fakt sifatida ko‘rsatadi. “For instance” taxtachalarning saqlanib qolishini kuydirishga misol qilib ko‘rsatadi, bu esa noto‘g‘ri.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"cause-effect\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Economist Laylo Tursunova studied a new bus line that connected a growing residential district to the city center. She found that the line cut the average commute time of its riders by about 15 minutes. ______ far fewer residents used the line than city planners had projected, largely because its stops were far from the area's biggest employers.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Nevertheless,\",\"option_b\":\"Accordingly,\",\"option_c\":\"Similarly,\",\"option_d\":\"For instance,\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The second sentence reports a clear benefit of the bus line: shorter commutes. The last sentence reports a disappointing fact that holds despite that benefit: few residents used the line. “Nevertheless” signals that something is true in spite of what was just said, so choice A is correct.\\n\\n“Accordingly” would suggest that low ridership follows from the shorter commutes, which is illogical; the text gives a different reason (the location of the stops). “Similarly” would present low ridership as another positive result like the first. “For instance” would present low ridership as an example of shorter commutes.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkinchi gapda avtobus yo‘nalishining aniq foydasi — yo‘lga ketadigan vaqt qisqargani — aytilgan. Oxirgi gapda esa shu foydaga qaramay yuz bergan noxush holat: yo‘nalishdan kam odam foydalangan. “Nevertheless” biror narsa avval aytilgan fikrga qaramay to‘g‘ri ekanini bildiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\n“Accordingly” yo‘lovchilar kamligi yo‘l vaqti qisqargani natijasi degan mantiqsiz ma’no beradi; matnda esa boshqa sabab — bekatlarning joylashuvi — ko‘rsatilgan. “Similarly” kam yo‘lovchini birinchisi kabi ijobiy natija qilib ko‘rsatadi. “For instance” kam yo‘lovchini qisqargan yo‘l vaqtiga misol qilib ko‘rsatadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"concession\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Ornithologists describe the chicks of some bird species, such as ducks and chickens, as precocial: the chicks hatch with open eyes and a coat of downy feathers, and they can walk and leave the nest within hours. ______ precocial chicks are relatively mature and mobile from the moment they hatch.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"By contrast,\",\"option_b\":\"As a result,\",\"option_c\":\"In other words,\",\"option_d\":\"Moreover,\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence defines “precocial” with specific details: open eyes, downy feathers, and the ability to walk within hours. The second sentence sums up the same idea in simpler, general terms. “In other words” introduces a restatement, so choice C is correct.\\n\\n“By contrast” would signal that the second sentence opposes the first, but it agrees with it. “As a result” would treat the summary as a consequence of the definition, but it says the same thing rather than describing an effect. “Moreover” would add new information, but the second sentence only restates what was already said.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda “precocial” atamasi aniq tafsilotlar bilan ta’riflangan: ko‘zlari ochiq, mayin pat bilan qoplangan va bir necha soatda yura oladigan jo‘jalar. Ikkinchi gap xuddi shu fikrni soddaroq, umumiy tarzda xulosalaydi. “In other words” fikrni boshqacha ifodalashni bildiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\n“By contrast” ikkinchi gap birinchisiga zid ekanini bildiradi, holbuki u unga mos. “As a result” xulosani ta’rifning oqibati qilib ko‘rsatadi, lekin u natijani emas, xuddi o‘sha fikrni aytadi. “Moreover” yangi ma’lumot qo‘shadi, ikkinchi gap esa faqat aytilganni qayta ifodalaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"restatement\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In the medieval cities of Central Asia, many writers were at home in more than one literary language. ______ the poet Alisher Navoi (1441–1501) wrote most of his works in Chagatai Turkic but also composed poetry in Persian, which he signed with the pen name Fani.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Nonetheless,\",\"option_b\":\"For instance,\",\"option_c\":\"Subsequently,\",\"option_d\":\"In comparison,\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence makes a general claim: many writers used more than one literary language. The second sentence gives a specific case: Navoi wrote in both Chagatai Turkic and Persian. “For instance” introduces an example of a general claim, so choice B is correct.\\n\\n“Nonetheless” would signal that Navoi's case goes against the claim, but it supports it. “Subsequently” would suggest that Navoi's writing came later in time than the situation described, but it is part of that situation. “In comparison” would set Navoi against the other writers, but he is one of them.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda umumiy fikr aytilgan: ko‘p yozuvchilar bir nechta adabiy tilni bilgan. Ikkinchi gap aniq misol keltiradi: Navoiy ham chig‘atoy turkiysida, ham forsiyda ijod qilgan. “For instance” umumiy fikrga misol keltiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\n“Nonetheless” Navoiy misoli fikrga zid degan ma’no beradi, holbuki u fikrni tasdiqlaydi. “Subsequently” Navoiy ijodi tasvirlangan holatdan keyinroq sodir bo‘lgan degan ma’no beradi, lekin u o‘sha holatning bir qismi. “In comparison” Navoiyni boshqa yozuvchilarga qarama-qarshi qo‘yadi, holbuki u ulardan biri.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"example\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In 1869, chemist Dmitri Mendeleev published a table that arranged the known chemical elements by their properties. Where no known element fit, he left gaps and predicted the properties of elements that had not yet been discovered. ______ chemists discovered gallium in 1875 and germanium in 1886, and both elements closely matched Mendeleev's predictions.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Previously,\",\"option_b\":\"Conversely,\",\"option_c\":\"Likewise,\",\"option_d\":\"Subsequently,\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text first describes Mendeleev's predictions in 1869 and then describes discoveries made in 1875 and 1886, after those predictions. “Subsequently” signals that the second event happened later, so choice D is correct.\\n\\n“Previously” reverses the time order: the discoveries came after 1869, not before. “Conversely” would signal an opposite idea, but the discoveries confirmed Mendeleev's predictions. “Likewise” would present the discoveries as another action similar to Mendeleev's, but they are later events that tested his predictions.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda avval Mendeleyevning 1869-yildagi bashoratlari, keyin esa undan keyin — 1875 va 1886-yillarda — qilingan kashfiyotlar tasvirlangan. “Subsequently” ikkinchi voqea keyinroq sodir bo‘lganini bildiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\n“Previously” vaqt tartibini teskari qiladi: kashfiyotlar 1869-yildan oldin emas, keyin bo‘lgan. “Conversely” qarama-qarshi fikrni bildiradi, lekin kashfiyotlar Mendeleyev bashoratini tasdiqlagan. “Likewise” kashfiyotlarni Mendeleyevnikiga o‘xshash yana bir harakat sifatida ko‘rsatadi, holbuki bular uning bashoratini sinagan keyingi voqealar.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"sequence\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from a short story.\\n\\nDilnoza had practiced the sonata every evening for a month, until her fingers found each note without her having to think about it. She knew the piece as well as she knew her own name. ______ when she stepped onto the stage and saw the rows of faces turned toward her, her hands began to tremble.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Still,\",\"option_b\":\"Thus,\",\"option_c\":\"Likewise,\",\"option_d\":\"In fact,\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first two sentences show that Dilnoza was thoroughly prepared and knew the piece perfectly. The last sentence describes something unexpected given that preparation: her hands trembled. “Still” introduces a fact that holds despite what was just said, so choice A is correct.\\n\\n“Thus” would suggest that her trembling was caused by her careful practice, which is illogical. “Likewise” would present her trembling as similar to her preparation. “In fact” would introduce a stronger version or correction of the previous point, but the trembling contrasts with her confidence rather than strengthening it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Dastlabki ikki gap Dilnoza puxta tayyorlangani va asarni mukammal bilishini ko‘rsatadi. Oxirgi gapda esa bunday tayyorgarlikdan keyin kutilmagan holat tasvirlangan: uning qo‘llari titray boshlagan. “Still” avval aytilganga qaramay yuz bergan holatni bildiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\n“Thus” titrash puxta mashq natijasi degan mantiqsiz ma’no beradi. “Likewise” titrashni tayyorgarlikka o‘xshash holat sifatida ko‘rsatadi. “In fact” oldingi fikrni kuchaytiradi yoki tuzatadi, lekin titrash uning ishonchini kuchaytirmaydi, balki unga zid keladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"concession\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Sea ice is bright and reflects most of the sunlight that strikes it, while the darker open ocean absorbs most of the sunlight that reaches it. When Arctic sea ice melts, more of this dark water is exposed to the Sun. ______ the region absorbs more heat, which warms the water and melts still more ice.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Nonetheless,\",\"option_b\":\"Consequently,\",\"option_c\":\"For example,\",\"option_d\":\"Similarly,\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text explains that dark ocean water absorbs more sunlight than ice and that melting exposes more of this dark water. The last sentence gives the effect: the region absorbs more heat. “Consequently” signals a result, so choice B is correct.\\n\\n“Nonetheless” would signal that absorbing more heat is unexpected given what came before, but it is exactly what the text predicts. “For example” would present the extra heat as an example of exposed water rather than a result of it. “Similarly” would present the last sentence as a parallel fact, missing the cause-and-effect link.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda qorong‘i okean suvi muzdan ko‘ra ko‘proq quyosh nurini yutishi va muz erishi bilan bunday suv ko‘proq ochilishi tushuntirilgan. Oxirgi gap natijani beradi: mintaqa ko‘proq issiqlik yutadi. “Consequently” natijani bildiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\n“Nonetheless” ko‘proq issiqlik yutilishi oldingi fikrga qaraganda kutilmagan degan ma’no beradi, holbuki matn aynan shuni bashorat qiladi. “For example” qo‘shimcha issiqlikni ochilgan suvning natijasi emas, misoli qilib ko‘rsatadi. “Similarly” oxirgi gapni parallel fakt sifatida ko‘rsatadi va sabab-oqibat bog‘lanishini yo‘qotadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"cause-effect\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Visitors to the city's historical archive often assume that the faded manuscripts displayed in the glass cases of its main hall are the archive's oldest and most valuable documents. ______ most of the manuscripts on display are careful modern copies; the originals, which would be damaged by constant exposure to light, are kept in a dark, climate-controlled vault beneath the building.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Indeed,\",\"option_b\":\"Granted,\",\"option_c\":\"Actually,\",\"option_d\":\"Accordingly,\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence describes a common assumption: the manuscripts on display are the archive's oldest documents. The second sentence shows that this assumption is mistaken: the displayed manuscripts are mostly copies. “Actually” introduces a fact that corrects a mistaken belief, so choice C is correct.\\n\\n“Indeed” would confirm or strengthen the visitors' assumption, but the sentence contradicts it. “Granted” would concede a point before a counterargument, but no counterargument follows; the sentence is itself the correction. “Accordingly” would suggest that the copies are on display because visitors make this assumption, which is illogical.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda keng tarqalgan taxmin tasvirlangan: ko‘rgazmadagi qo‘lyozmalar arxivning eng qadimgi hujjatlari. Ikkinchi gap bu taxmin noto‘g‘ri ekanini ko‘rsatadi: ko‘rgazmadagi qo‘lyozmalarning aksariyati nusxa. “Actually” noto‘g‘ri tasavvurni tuzatuvchi faktni kiritadi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\n“Indeed” tashrif buyuruvchilar taxminini tasdiqlaydi yoki kuchaytiradi, holbuki gap unga zid. “Granted” qarshi dalildan oldin biror fikrni tan olishni bildiradi, lekin keyin hech qanday qarshi dalil kelmaydi — gapning o‘zi tuzatish. “Accordingly” nusxalar tashrif buyuruvchilar shunday o‘ylagani uchun ko‘rgazmaga qo‘yilgan degan mantiqsiz ma’no beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"correction-actually\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Historian Bobur Aliyev argues that the yearly trade fair established in a small Silk Road town in the fourteenth century transformed the local economy. ______ the town's surviving tax records are incomplete, covering only four of the fair's first twenty years. But those four years show customs revenue nearly tripling, a pattern Aliyev finds hard to explain in any other way.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Indeed,\",\"option_b\":\"Therefore,\",\"option_c\":\"Fittingly,\",\"option_d\":\"Granted,\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"After stating Aliyev's claim, the text admits a weakness in his evidence: the tax records are incomplete. The next sentence, beginning with “But,” answers that weakness by pointing to what the records do show. “Granted” concedes a point that seems to work against an argument before the argument is defended, so choice D is correct.\\n\\n“Indeed” would present the incomplete records as strong support for Aliyev's claim, but they are a limitation. “Therefore” would suggest that the records are incomplete because of Aliyev's argument, which is illogical. “Fittingly” would describe the incomplete records as an appropriate or neatly matching detail, but they are a problem the text concedes.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Aliyevning da’vosi aytilgandan so‘ng, matn uning dalillaridagi zaif tomonni tan oladi: soliq yozuvlari to‘liq emas. “But” bilan boshlanadigan keyingi gap esa yozuvlar aslida nimani ko‘rsatishini aytib, bu zaiflikka javob beradi. “Granted” dalilni himoya qilishdan oldin unga qarshi ko‘ringan fikrni tan olishni bildiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\n“Indeed” to‘liq bo‘lmagan yozuvlarni Aliyev da’vosini kuchli qo‘llab-quvvatlovchi dalil qilib ko‘rsatadi, holbuki bu kamchilik. “Therefore” yozuvlar Aliyevning fikri tufayli to‘liq emas degan mantiqsiz ma’no beradi. “Fittingly” to‘liq bo‘lmagan yozuvlarni munosib yoki mos tafsilot sifatida ko‘rsatadi, lekin matn ularni muammo sifatida tan oladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"concession-granted\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"For four decades, engineer Feruza Qodirova designed irrigation systems that helped farmers in Uzbekistan's dry regions use less water, most notably a low-cost drip system that delivers water directly to plant roots. When a regional agricultural research center opened a new laboratory building, it named the building after her. ______ the garden in the building's courtyard is watered entirely by a version of her drip system.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Fittingly,\",\"option_b\":\"Nevertheless,\",\"option_c\":\"Specifically,\",\"option_d\":\"Previously,\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text explains that the building is named after Qodirova, who is known for her water-saving drip system. The last sentence adds that the building's own garden is watered by her system, a detail that suits the honor given to her. “Fittingly” introduces a detail that is appropriate in light of what came before, so choice A is correct.\\n\\n“Nevertheless” would signal that using her system goes against what was said, but it matches it. “Specifically” would introduce a more precise version of the previous statement, but the garden is not a detail of how the building was named. “Previously” would place the watering of the garden before the building was named, which the text does not indicate.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda bino suvni tejaydigan tomchilatib sug‘orish tizimi bilan tanilgan Qodirova sharafiga nomlangani aytilgan. Oxirgi gap binoning o‘z bog‘i ham uning tizimi bilan sug‘orilishini qo‘shimcha qiladi — bu unga ko‘rsatilgan hurmatga juda mos tafsilot. “Fittingly” avvalgi fikrga munosib, mos keladigan tafsilotni kiritadi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\n“Nevertheless” uning tizimidan foydalanish aytilganlarga zid degan ma’no beradi, holbuki u ularga mos. “Specifically” oldingi fikrning aniqroq ifodasini kiritadi, lekin bog‘ bino qanday nomlangani haqidagi tafsilot emas. “Previously” bog‘ni sug‘orishni bino nomlanishidan oldinga qo‘yadi, matnda esa bunday ishora yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"fittingly\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"A district committee was asked to evaluate a new reading program that had been tested in three schools. In its report, the committee described the program's early results as “promising but preliminary” and stopped well short of endorsing it. ______ it recommended that the district wait for at least two more years of data before offering the program in any additional schools.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Likewise,\",\"option_b\":\"Even so,\",\"option_c\":\"Indeed,\",\"option_d\":\"Granted,\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The second sentence says that the committee was cautious and did not endorse the program. The last sentence reinforces that point with stronger evidence of caution: the committee recommended waiting at least two more years. “Indeed” introduces a statement that confirms and intensifies the previous one, so choice C is correct.\\n\\n“Likewise” would suggest that a different person or group did something similar, but the same committee is acting. “Even so” would signal that the recommendation is surprising given the committee's caution, but it is exactly what that caution would lead to. “Granted” would concede a point that weakens the previous claim, but the recommendation strengthens it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkinchi gapda qo‘mita ehtiyotkor bo‘lgani va dasturni ma’qullamagani aytilgan. Oxirgi gap bu fikrni ehtiyotkorlikning yanada kuchliroq dalili bilan mustahkamlaydi: qo‘mita kamida yana ikki yil kutishni tavsiya qilgan. “Indeed” oldingi fikrni tasdiqlaydigan va kuchaytiradigan gapni kiritadi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\n“Likewise” boshqa shaxs yoki guruh shunga o‘xshash ish qilgan degan ma’no beradi, holbuki gap o‘sha qo‘mita haqida. “Even so” tavsiya qo‘mitaning ehtiyotkorligiga qaraganda kutilmagan degan ma’no beradi, holbuki u aynan shu ehtiyotkorlikning natijasi. “Granted” oldingi fikrni zaiflashtiruvchi narsani tan olishni bildiradi, tavsiya esa uni kuchaytiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"emphasis-indeed\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"two_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"On each of 18 summer days, a juice stand in Bukhara recorded the day's high temperature $x$, in degrees Celsius, and the number of cups of juice sold $y$. The line of best fit for the data is $y = 4.5x - 62$. Which of the following is the best interpretation of 4.5 in this context?\",\"option_a\":\"For each increase of 1 degree Celsius in the high temperature, the predicted number of cups sold increases by 4.5.\",\"option_b\":\"When the high temperature is 0 degrees Celsius, the predicted number of cups sold is 4.5.\",\"option_c\":\"For each increase of 4.5 degrees Celsius in the high temperature, the predicted number of cups sold increases by 1.\",\"option_d\":\"For each additional cup sold, the predicted high temperature increases by 4.5 degrees Celsius.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"In $y = mx + b$, the slope $m$ is the predicted change in $y$ when $x$ increases by 1. Here $x$ is the temperature and $y$ is the number of cups, so 4.5 means 4.5 more predicted cups for each 1°C increase. Choice B describes the $y$-intercept, which is $-62$, not 4.5. Choice C turns the rate upside down. Choice D switches the roles of $x$ and $y$: the model predicts cups from temperature, not temperature from cups.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$y = mx + b$ da $m$ qiyalik — $x$ 1 ga ortganda $y$ ning bashorat qilingan o‘zgarishi. Bu yerda $x$ — harorat, $y$ — stakanlar soni, demak 4.5 harorat har 1°C ga ko‘tarilganda bashorat qilingan stakanlar soni 4.5 taga ortishini bildiradi. B javob $y$-kesishmani tasvirlaydi, u esa 4.5 emas, $-62$. C javob nisbatni teskari qilgan. D javob $x$ va $y$ ning rolini almashtirgan: model haroratdan stakanlar sonini bashorat qiladi, aksincha emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"slope interpretation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"two_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A typing teacher recorded the number of hours $x$ that each of 25 students practiced per week and each student's typing speed $y$, in words per minute. The line of best fit for the data is $y = 1.8x + 23$. Based on the line of best fit, what is the predicted typing speed, in words per minute, of a student who practices 15 hours per week?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"50\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Substitute $x = 15$: $y = 1.8(15) + 23 = 27 + 23 = 50$ words per minute. Answering 27 forgets to add the intercept 23. Enter 50.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$x = 15$ ni qo‘yamiz: $y = 1.8(15) + 23 = 27 + 23 = 50$ so‘z/daqiqa. 27 deb javob berish 23 kesishmani qo‘shishni unutishdir. Javob: 50.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"prediction\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"two_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"passage\":\"A geography student recorded the elevation and the mean July temperature of 6 weather stations in a mountain region.\\n\\n| Elevation (meters) | Mean July temperature (°C) |\\n| ---: | ---: |\\n| 400 | 29.1 |\\n| 900 | 26.0 |\\n| 1,400 | 23.2 |\\n| 1,900 | 20.1 |\\n| 2,400 | 17.0 |\\n| 2,900 | 13.9 |\",\"stem\":\"Which of the following best describes the association between elevation and mean July temperature for the weather stations in the table?\",\"option_a\":\"A strong positive linear association\",\"option_b\":\"A strong negative linear association\",\"option_c\":\"A negative association that is clearly nonlinear\",\"option_d\":\"No association\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"As the elevation increases by 500 meters each time, the temperature decreases by about the same amount each time (about 3°C), so the points would lie close to a line that slopes downward. That is a strong negative linear association. Choice A would require the temperature to rise with elevation. Choice C is wrong because the decreases are nearly constant, which is a linear pattern, not a curved one. Choice D ignores the clear, steady decrease.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Balandlik har safar 500 metrga ortganda harorat har safar taxminan bir xil miqdorga (taxminan 3°C ga) kamayadi, demak nuqtalar pastga qiya to‘g‘ri chiziqqa yaqin joylashadi. Bu kuchli manfiy chiziqli bog‘liqlikdir. A javob uchun harorat balandlik bilan birga oshishi kerak edi. C javob noto‘g‘ri, chunki kamayishlar deyarli o‘zgarmas — bu egri emas, chiziqli qonuniyat. D javob aniq va bir tekis kamayishni e’tiborsiz qoldiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"association\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"two_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"For 12 students, a scatterplot shows the number of hours $x$ that each student studied for a quiz and the student's quiz score $y$. The line of best fit for the data is $y = 2.5x + 61$. One of the students studied for 8 hours and scored 85 on the quiz. What is the residual for this student, defined as the actual score minus the score predicted by the line of best fit?\",\"option_a\":\"$-4$\",\"option_b\":\"$4$\",\"option_c\":\"$81$\",\"option_d\":\"$85$\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The predicted score is $2.5(8) + 61 = 81$. The residual is actual minus predicted: $85 - 81 = 4$. Choice A subtracts in the wrong order (predicted minus actual). Choice C is the predicted score and choice D is the actual score, not the difference between them.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bashorat qilingan ball $2.5(8) + 61 = 81$. Qoldiq (residual) — haqiqiy qiymat minus bashorat: $85 - 81 = 4$. A javob ayirishni teskari tartibda bajargan (bashorat minus haqiqiy). C javob bashorat qilingan ball, D javob esa haqiqiy ball — ularning ayirmasi emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"residual\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"two_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"For 20 cotton fields, an agronomist recorded the amount of irrigation water $x$, in thousands of cubic meters per hectare, and the cotton yield $y$, in centners per hectare. The line of best fit for the data is $y = 3.2x + 14$. According to the line of best fit, what amount of irrigation water, in thousands of cubic meters per hectare, corresponds to a predicted yield of 46 centners per hectare?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"10\"],\"explanation_en\":\"Set $y = 46$: $46 = 3.2x + 14$, so $3.2x = 32$ and $x = 10$. Substituting 46 for $x$ instead of $y$ (which gives 161.2) answers a different question. Enter 10.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$y = 46$ deb olamiz: $46 = 3.2x + 14$, demak $3.2x = 32$ va $x = 10$. 46 ni $y$ o‘rniga emas, $x$ o‘rniga qo‘yish (161.2 chiqadi) boshqa savolga javob beradi. Javob: 10.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"prediction\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"two_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A car dealer made a scatterplot of the age $x$, in years, and the price $y$, in millions of soums, of 40 used cars of the same model. The line of best fit for the data is $y = -6.5x + 172$. Which of the following is the best interpretation of 172 in this context?\",\"option_a\":\"The predicted price of a car of this model decreases by 172 million soums each year.\",\"option_b\":\"The predicted age of a car of this model that costs 0 soums is 172 years.\",\"option_c\":\"The predicted price of a car of this model that is 0 years old is 172 million soums.\",\"option_d\":\"The mean price of the 40 used cars is 172 million soums.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"In $y = mx + b$, the constant $b$ is the predicted value of $y$ when $x = 0$. Here $x = 0$ means a car that is 0 years old, so 172 is the predicted price, in millions of soums, of a new car of this model. Choice A describes a slope, but the slope is $-6.5$. Choice B confuses the intercept with the $x$-value where $y = 0$ (about 26.5 years). Choice D is not given by the model: a line of best fit does not tell the mean of the prices directly.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$y = mx + b$ da $b$ o‘zgarmas — $x = 0$ bo‘lgandagi $y$ ning bashorat qilingan qiymati. Bu yerda $x = 0$ — 0 yoshli mashina, demak 172 — bu modeldagi yangi mashinaning million so‘mdagi bashorat qilingan narxi. A javob qiyalikni tasvirlaydi, lekin qiyalik $-6.5$. B javob kesishmani $y = 0$ bo‘ladigan $x$ qiymati (taxminan 26.5 yil) bilan adashtirgan. D javobni model bermaydi: eng mos chiziq narxlarning o‘rtachasini bevosita ko‘rsatmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"intercept interpretation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"two_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"passage\":\"| Age (years) | Height (meters) |\\n| ---: | ---: |\\n| 1 | 1.9 |\\n| 2 | 2.6 |\\n| 3 | 4.4 |\\n| 4 | 5.1 |\\n| 5 | 6.5 |\\n| 6 | 7.9 |\",\"stem\":\"A forester measured the age $x$, in years, and the height $y$, in meters, of 6 young poplar trees, as shown in the table. The line of best fit for the data is $y = 1.2x + 0.5$. For how many of the 6 trees is the actual height greater than the height predicted by the line of best fit?\",\"option_a\":\"2\",\"option_b\":\"3\",\"option_c\":\"4\",\"option_d\":\"6\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The predicted heights for ages 1 through 6 are 1.7, 2.9, 4.1, 5.3, 6.5, and 7.7 meters. The actual heights are greater for the trees of ages 1 (1.9), 3 (4.4), and 6 (7.9), so the answer is 3. The trees of ages 2 and 4 are below the line, and the 5-year-old tree (6.5 meters) lies exactly on the line, so it is not above it. Choice C counts that tree as above the line. Choice A misses one of the trees above the line, and choice D assumes that every point is above the line.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"1 dan 6 gacha yoshlar uchun bashorat qilingan balandliklar: 1.7, 2.9, 4.1, 5.3, 6.5 va 7.7 metr. Haqiqiy balandlik 1 yoshli (1.9), 3 yoshli (4.4) va 6 yoshli (7.9) daraxtlarda kattaroq, demak javob 3. 2 va 4 yoshli daraxtlar chiziqdan pastda, 5 yoshli daraxt (6.5 metr) esa aynan chiziq ustida yotadi, shuning uchun u chiziqdan yuqorida emas. C javob shu daraxtni ham yuqoridagilarga qo‘shgan. A javob yuqoridagi daraxtlardan birini o‘tkazib yuborgan, D javob esa barcha nuqtalar chiziqdan yuqori deb hisoblagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"residual\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"two_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"passage\":\"| $t$ (days) | Area (square centimeters) |\\n| ---: | ---: |\\n| 0 | 40 |\\n| 1 | 61 |\\n| 2 | 89 |\\n| 3 | 136 |\\n| 4 | 201 |\",\"stem\":\"A biologist measured the area $y$, in square centimeters, covered by a colony of algae in a tank $t$ days after the start of an experiment, as shown in the table. Which of the following equations best models the data?\",\"option_a\":\"$y = 20t + 40$\",\"option_b\":\"$y = 40(0.5)^t$\",\"option_c\":\"$y = 1.5(40)^t$\",\"option_d\":\"$y = 40(1.5)^t$\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Each day the area is multiplied by about the same factor: $\\\\frac{61}{40} \\\\approx 1.53$, $\\\\frac{89}{61} \\\\approx 1.46$, $\\\\frac{136}{89} \\\\approx 1.53$, $\\\\frac{201}{136} \\\\approx 1.48$. A constant growth factor of about 1.5 with a starting value of 40 gives $y = 40(1.5)^t$, which predicts 40, 60, 90, 135, and 202.5. Choice A is linear: it fits the first two days but predicts only 120 on day 4, because the increases in the data keep getting larger. Choice B has a factor of 0.5, so it models a decrease. Choice C switches the base and the coefficient; it predicts 2,400 on day 2.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Har kuni maydon taxminan bir xil songa ko‘paytiriladi: $\\\\frac{61}{40} \\\\approx 1.53$, $\\\\frac{89}{61} \\\\approx 1.46$, $\\\\frac{136}{89} \\\\approx 1.53$, $\\\\frac{201}{136} \\\\approx 1.48$. Boshlang‘ich qiymati 40 va o‘zgarmas o‘sish koeffitsiyenti taxminan 1.5 bo‘lsa, $y = 40(1.5)^t$ hosil bo‘ladi; u 40, 60, 90, 135 va 202.5 ni beradi. A javob chiziqli: birinchi ikki kunga mos keladi, lekin 4-kun uchun atigi 120 ni beradi, chunki ma’lumotlardagi o‘sishlar tobora kattalashib boradi. B javobda koeffitsiyent 0.5, ya’ni u kamayishni ifodalaydi. C javob asos va koeffitsiyentni almashtirgan; u 2-kun uchun 2,400 ni beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"choose model\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"two_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A scatterplot shows the arm span $x$ and the height $y$, both in centimeters, of 30 adults. The line of best fit for the data is $y = 0.95x + 9$. One of the adults has an arm span of 170 centimeters and a height of 168 centimeters. What is the residual, in centimeters, for this adult? (The residual is the actual height minus the height predicted by the line of best fit.)\",\"spr_answers\":[\"-2.5\",\"-5/2\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The predicted height is $0.95(170) + 9 = 161.5 + 9 = 170.5$ centimeters. The residual is $168 - 170.5 = -2.5$. The residual is negative because the actual point lies below the line. Answering 2.5 subtracts in the wrong order. Enter -2.5 or -5/2.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bashorat qilingan bo‘y $0.95(170) + 9 = 161.5 + 9 = 170.5$ santimetr. Qoldiq $168 - 170.5 = -2.5$. Qoldiq manfiy, chunki haqiqiy nuqta chiziqdan pastda yotadi. 2.5 deb javob berish ayirishni teskari tartibda bajarishdir. Javob: -2.5 yoki -5/2.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"residual\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"two_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A researcher studied the number of cafés in two towns, Town A and Town B. For each town, the number of cafés $y$ is modeled by a line of best fit, where $x$ is the number of years since 2018.\\\\n\\\\nTown A: $y = 1.4x + 20$\\\\n\\\\nTown B: $y = 2.1x + 12$\\\\n\\\\nWhich of the following statements is true according to the lines of best fit?\",\"option_a\":\"In 2018, Town A had a greater predicted number of cafés than Town B did, and the predicted number of cafés increases faster in Town A.\",\"option_b\":\"In 2018, Town A had a greater predicted number of cafés than Town B did, and the predicted number of cafés increases faster in Town B.\",\"option_c\":\"In 2018, Town B had a greater predicted number of cafés than Town A did, and the predicted number of cafés increases faster in Town B.\",\"option_d\":\"In 2018, Town B had a greater predicted number of cafés than Town A did, and the predicted number of cafés increases faster in Town A.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"In 2018, $x = 0$, so the predictions are the $y$-intercepts: 20 for Town A and 12 for Town B, so Town A started higher. The slopes are the predicted increases per year: 1.4 cafés for Town A and 2.1 cafés for Town B, so Town B's predicted number increases faster. Choices A and D get the growth rates backward, and choices C and D get the starting values backward.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"2018-yilda $x = 0$, demak bashoratlar $y$-kesishmalar: A shahar uchun 20, B shahar uchun 12 — A shahar balandroq boshlagan. Qiyaliklar — yiliga bashorat qilingan o‘sish: A shahar uchun 1.4 ta, B shahar uchun 2.1 ta kafe, demak B shahardagi bashorat tezroq o‘sadi. A va D javoblar o‘sish sur’atlarini, C va D javoblar esa boshlang‘ich qiymatlarni teskari olgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"compare lines\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"two_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"A scatterplot shows the number of members $y$ of a chess club in Andijan and the number of years $x$ since the club opened. The line of best fit for the data passes through the points $(2, 31)$ and $(10, 55)$. According to the line of best fit, what is the predicted number of members 7 years after the club opened?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"46\"],\"explanation_en\":\"The slope of the line is $\\\\frac{55 - 31}{10 - 2} = \\\\frac{24}{8} = 3$. Using the point $(2, 31)$: $31 = 3(2) + b$, so $b = 25$ and the line is $y = 3x + 25$. When $x = 7$, $y = 3(7) + 25 = 46$. Enter 46.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Chiziqning qiyaligi $\\\\frac{55 - 31}{10 - 2} = \\\\frac{24}{8} = 3$. $(2, 31)$ nuqtadan: $31 = 3(2) + b$, demak $b = 25$ va chiziq $y = 3x + 25$. $x = 7$ bo‘lganda $y = 3(7) + 25 = 46$. Javob: 46.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"prediction\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"two_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"A scatterplot shows the value $y$, in thousands of soums, of a certain model of washing machine and the number of years $x$ since it was bought. An exponential model that fits the data is $y = 4{,}800(0.92)^x$. Which of the following is the best interpretation of 0.92 in this context?\",\"option_a\":\"Each year, the predicted value of the washing machine decreases by 8% of its value the year before.\",\"option_b\":\"Each year, the predicted value of the washing machine decreases by 92% of its value the year before.\",\"option_c\":\"Each year, the predicted value of the washing machine decreases by 0.92 thousand soums.\",\"option_d\":\"Each year, the predicted value of the washing machine increases by 92%.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"In the model $y = a(b)^x$, each increase of 1 in $x$ multiplies the predicted value by $b$. Multiplying by 0.92 keeps 92% of the value, which is a decrease of $100\\\\% - 92\\\\% = 8\\\\%$ each year. Choice B confuses the percent kept with the percent lost; a 92% decrease would leave only 8% of the value. Choice C describes a linear model, which loses the same amount each year. Choice D is wrong because a factor less than 1 means the value decreases.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"$y = a(b)^x$ modelda $x$ 1 ga ortganda bashorat qilingan qiymat $b$ ga ko‘paytiriladi. 0.92 ga ko‘paytirish qiymatning 92% ini saqlab qoladi, ya’ni har yili $100\\\\% - 92\\\\% = 8\\\\%$ ga kamayish. B javob saqlanib qolgan foizni yo‘qotilgan foiz bilan adashtirgan; 92% ga kamaysa, qiymatning faqat 8% i qolardi. C javob chiziqli modelni tasvirlaydi, unda har yili bir xil miqdor yo‘qotiladi. D javob noto‘g‘ri, chunki 1 dan kichik koeffitsiyent qiymat kamayishini bildiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"exponential model\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"two_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"passage\":\"| Speed (km/h) | Fuel consumption (liters per 100 km) |\\n| ---: | ---: |\\n| 30 | 7.9 |\\n| 50 | 6.1 |\\n| 70 | 5.5 |\\n| 90 | 6.1 |\\n| 110 | 7.9 |\",\"stem\":\"An engineer tested a car at 5 different constant speeds $x$, in kilometers per hour, and recorded the fuel consumption $y$, in liters per 100 kilometers, as shown in the table. A scatterplot of the data suggests a quadratic relationship. Which of the following equations best models the data?\",\"option_a\":\"$y = 0.0015x^2 - 0.21x + 12.85$\",\"option_b\":\"$y = 0.0015x^2 + 0.21x + 12.85$\",\"option_c\":\"$y = -0.0015x^2 + 0.21x - 1.85$\",\"option_d\":\"$y = 0.0015x^2 - 0.21x + 5.5$\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The data are symmetric about $x = 70$, where the least value, 5.5, occurs, so the model is a parabola that opens upward with vertex $(70, 5.5)$: $y = a(x - 70)^2 + 5.5$. Using $(30, 7.9)$: $7.9 = a(1{,}600) + 5.5$, so $a = 0.0015$. Expanding: $0.0015(x^2 - 140x + 4{,}900) + 5.5 = 0.0015x^2 - 0.21x + 12.85$. Choice B has its vertex at $x = -70$. Choice C has vertex $(70, 5.5)$ but opens downward, so 5.5 would be the greatest value, not the least. Choice D uses 5.5 as the constant term, but the constant term is the value at $x = 0$, not at the vertex; it predicts $-1.85$ at $x = 70$.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ma’lumotlar $x = 70$ ga nisbatan simmetrik va eng kichik qiymat 5.5 shu yerda, demak model — uchi $(70, 5.5)$ bo‘lgan, shoxlari yuqoriga qaragan parabola: $y = a(x - 70)^2 + 5.5$. $(30, 7.9)$ dan: $7.9 = a(1{,}600) + 5.5$, demak $a = 0.0015$. Qavslarni ochamiz: $0.0015(x^2 - 140x + 4{,}900) + 5.5 = 0.0015x^2 - 0.21x + 12.85$. B javobning uchi $x = -70$ da. C javobning uchi $(70, 5.5)$, lekin shoxlari pastga qaragan, ya’ni 5.5 eng kichik emas, eng katta qiymat bo‘lardi. D javob 5.5 ni ozod had sifatida olgan, lekin ozod had uchidagi emas, $x = 0$ dagi qiymat; u $x = 70$ da $-1.85$ ni beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":130,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"quadratic model\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"two_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"spr\",\"stem\":\"The line of best fit for a data set is $y = 2.4x + k$, where $k$ is a constant. The data set includes the point $(15, 40)$, and the residual for this point is $-3$. (The residual is the actual $y$-value minus the $y$-value predicted by the line of best fit.) What is the value of $k$?\",\"spr_answers\":[\"7\"],\"explanation_en\":\"A residual of $-3$ means the actual value is 3 less than the predicted value, so the predicted value at $x = 15$ is $40 + 3 = 43$. Then $2.4(15) + k = 43$, so $36 + k = 43$ and $k = 7$. Using the wrong sign for the residual gives a predicted value of 37 and $k = 1$. Enter 7.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qoldiq $-3$ bo‘lishi haqiqiy qiymat bashoratdan 3 ga kichik ekanini bildiradi, demak $x = 15$ dagi bashorat $40 + 3 = 43$. U holda $2.4(15) + k = 43$, ya’ni $36 + k = 43$ va $k = 7$. Qoldiq ishorasini noto‘g‘ri olsak, bashorat 37 va $k = 1$ chiqadi. Javob: 7.\",\"estimated_seconds\":110,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"residual parameter\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"two_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"Two online shops opened in the same month. For each shop, the number of orders $y$ per month is modeled by a line of best fit, where $x$ is the number of months since the shops opened.\\\\n\\\\nShop A: $y = 3.5x + 120$\\\\n\\\\nShop B: $y = 5x + 84$\\\\n\\\\nAccording to the models, what is the least whole number of months $x$ for which Shop B's predicted number of orders is greater than Shop A's?\",\"option_a\":\"23\",\"option_b\":\"24\",\"option_c\":\"25\",\"option_d\":\"34\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Shop B's prediction is greater when $5x + 84 > 3.5x + 120$, that is, $1.5x > 36$, or $x > 24$. At $x = 24$ the two predictions are equal (204 orders each), so the least whole number of months for which Shop B's prediction is greater is 25. Choice B is where the predictions are equal, not where B is greater. Choice A is a month when Shop A's prediction is still greater (200.5 versus 199). Choice D is where Shop B's prediction is greater by 15, which is more than the question asks.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"B do‘konning bashorati $5x + 84 > 3.5x + 120$ bo‘lganda kattaroq, ya’ni $1.5x > 36$ yoki $x > 24$. $x = 24$ da ikki bashorat teng (har biri 204 ta buyurtma), demak B do‘kon bashorati katta bo‘ladigan eng kichik butun oy soni 25. B javob — bashoratlar teng bo‘ladigan oy, B kattaroq bo‘ladigan oy emas. A javobda A do‘konning bashorati hali ham katta (200.5 ga qarshi 199). D javob — B do‘kon bashorati 15 ga katta bo‘ladigan oy, bu savolda so‘ralganidan ortiq.\",\"estimated_seconds\":110,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"compare lines\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"two_variable_data\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stem\":\"For 50 apartments in a city, a scatterplot shows the floor area $x$, in square meters, and the monthly heating cost $y$, in hundreds of thousands of soums, in January. The line of best fit for the data is $y = 0.045x + 1.2$. Which of the following is the best interpretation of the line of best fit in this context?\",\"option_a\":\"For each increase of 20 square meters in floor area, the predicted monthly heating cost increases by 90,000 soums.\",\"option_b\":\"For each increase of 20 square meters in floor area, the predicted monthly heating cost increases by 900,000 soums.\",\"option_c\":\"For each increase of 1 square meter in floor area, the predicted monthly heating cost increases by 45,000 soums.\",\"option_d\":\"The predicted monthly heating cost of an apartment with a floor area of 20 square meters is 90,000 soums.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The slope 0.045 means the predicted cost increases by 0.045 hundred thousand soums, or 4,500 soums, per square meter. For 20 square meters, the increase is $20(0.045) = 0.9$ hundred thousand soums, which is 90,000 soums. Choice B treats 0.9 as millions of soums. Choice C treats 0.045 as millions of soums; the actual increase per square meter is 4,500 soums. Choice D ignores the intercept: the predicted cost for 20 square meters is $0.045(20) + 1.2 = 2.1$ hundred thousand, or 210,000 soums.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"0.045 qiyalik har bir kvadrat metrga bashorat qilingan xarajat 0.045 yuz ming so‘mga, ya’ni 4,500 so‘mga ortishini bildiradi. 20 kvadrat metr uchun o‘sish $20(0.045) = 0.9$ yuz ming so‘m, ya’ni 90,000 so‘m. B javob 0.9 ni million so‘m deb olgan. C javob 0.045 ni million so‘m deb olgan; aslida har kvadrat metrga o‘sish 4,500 so‘m. D javob kesishmani hisobga olmagan: 20 kvadrat metr uchun bashorat $0.045(20) + 1.2 = 2.1$ yuz ming, ya’ni 210,000 so‘m.\",\"estimated_seconds\":120,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"slope interpretation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Octopuses can change both the color and the texture of their skin in less than a second. This ability allows them to ______ almost perfectly with the rocks, sand, and coral around them. Marine biologist Nodira Usmonova notes that experienced divers often swim within a meter of a resting octopus without noticing it at all.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"blend\",\"option_b\":\"compete\",\"option_c\":\"collide\",\"option_d\":\"interfere\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text says divers swim right past octopuses “without noticing” them, so the color changes must let octopuses merge visually with their surroundings. “Blend” (to mix in so as to be hard to tell apart) fits.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because nothing suggests the octopus is competing with rocks or coral. Choice C is wrong because “collide” means to crash into something, which has nothing to do with camouflage. Choice D is wrong because the octopus is not getting in the way of its surroundings; it is becoming hard to see against them.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda g‘avvoslar sakkizoyoqlar yonidan ularni “sezmasdan” o‘tib ketishi aytiladi. Demak, rang o‘zgarishi sakkizoyoqqa atrofidagi narsalar bilan qo‘shilib, ko‘rinmay qolishga imkon beradi. “Blend” (qo‘shilib ketmoq, ajratib bo‘lmas darajada aralashmoq) so‘zi mos keladi.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: sakkizoyoq tosh yoki marjonlar bilan raqobatlashayotgani haqida gap yo‘q. C noto‘g‘ri: “collide” (to‘qnashmoq) niqoblanishga aloqasi yo‘q. D noto‘g‘ri: sakkizoyoq atrofiga xalaqit bermaydi, aksincha ular fonida ko‘rinmay qoladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science\",\"context-clue\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Before the railway reached it in 1902, the town of Qoraqum was a small and ______ settlement, founded only a few decades earlier. Travelers rarely stopped there, and few people outside the region even knew its name. Within twenty years of the railway's arrival, however, it had become one of the busiest trading centers in the province.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"crowded\",\"option_b\":\"prosperous\",\"option_c\":\"obscure\",\"option_d\":\"ancient\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text says travelers rarely stopped in the town and few outsiders “even knew its name,” so the town was little known. “Obscure” (not well known) fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because a town that was small and rarely visited was not crowded. Choice B is wrong because the town became a busy trading center only after the railway arrived; before that nothing suggests wealth. Choice D is wrong because the town had been “founded only a few decades earlier,” so it was not ancient.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda sayohatchilar bu shaharchada kamdan-kam to‘xtagani va mintaqadan tashqarida uning “nomini ham” kam odam bilgani aytiladi. Demak, shaharcha deyarli noma’lum bo‘lgan. “Obscure” (kam tanilgan, noma’lum) so‘zi mos keladi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: kichik va kam qatnaladigan shaharcha gavjum bo‘lmagan. B noto‘g‘ri: shaharcha faqat temir yo‘l kelgandan keyin gavjum savdo markaziga aylangan; undan oldin boylik haqida hech narsa aytilmagan. D noto‘g‘ri: shaharcha “bor-yo‘g‘i bir necha o‘n yil oldin” tashkil topgan, demak u qadimiy emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"history\",\"context-clue\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In a survey of 1,200 bus riders, economist Farid Rahimov found that most respondents were ______ to pay a slightly higher fare in exchange for faster service: nearly 70 percent said they would accept a fare increase if it cut their daily travel time by ten minutes.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"reluctant\",\"option_b\":\"unable\",\"option_c\":\"forbidden\",\"option_d\":\"willing\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The colon introduces evidence: nearly 70 percent said they “would accept” a higher fare. So most riders were ready to pay more. “Willing” fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because “reluctant” (unwilling) is the opposite of what the survey found. Choice B is wrong because the survey asked what riders would accept, not whether they could afford it. Choice C is wrong because nothing in the text says anyone was prohibited from paying.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikki nuqtadan keyin dalil keltiriladi: qariyb 70 foiz respondent yo‘l haqi oshishini “qabul qilishini” aytgan. Demak, ko‘pchilik ko‘proq to‘lashga tayyor. “Willing” (rozi, tayyor) so‘zi mos keladi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: “reluctant” (istamaydigan) so‘rov natijasiga zid. B noto‘g‘ri: so‘rovda yo‘lovchilar nimani qabul qilishi so‘ralgan, puli yetish-yetmasligi emas. D noto‘g‘ri: matnda hech kimga to‘lash taqiqlangani aytilmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"social-science\",\"context-clue\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from a short story.\\n\\nMalika had expected the old house to feel gloomy after standing empty for so long. But the morning sun poured through every window, lighting up the yellow walls and the painted ceilings, and the rooms seemed almost ______. Before long she found herself humming as she carried her boxes up the stairs.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"hostile\",\"option_b\":\"cheerful\",\"option_c\":\"cramped\",\"option_d\":\"silent\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"“But” signals a contrast with Malika's expectation that the house would be “gloomy.” Sunlight fills the rooms, and she begins humming, so the rooms seem bright and happy. “Cheerful” fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because “hostile” (unfriendly) is negative, while the details are pleasant. Choice C is wrong because nothing describes the rooms as small or tight. Choice D is wrong because silence is not the opposite of gloominess, and the sunny details do not point to quiet.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“But” (lekin) Malikaning uy “g‘amgin” bo‘ladi degan kutilmasiga qarama-qarshilikni bildiradi. Xonalar quyosh nuriga to‘la, Malika esa xirgoyi qila boshlaydi — demak xonalar yorug‘ va quvnoq ko‘rinadi. “Cheerful” (quvnoq, xushchaqchaq) so‘zi mos keladi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: “hostile” (dushmanona) salbiy so‘z, tafsilotlar esa yoqimli. C noto‘g‘ri: xonalar tor ekani haqida hech narsa aytilmagan. D noto‘g‘ri: jimjitlik g‘amginlikning teskarisi emas va quyoshli tafsilotlar sukunatga ishora qilmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"literature\",\"contrast-signal\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Many desert plants sprout, flower, and die within a few weeks after a rare rainstorm. The shrub studied by botanist Aziz Karimov follows a very different strategy: it is remarkably ______. By counting the growth rings in its woody stems, Karimov estimated that some individuals in his study area are more than 300 years old.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"fast-growing\",\"option_b\":\"rare\",\"option_c\":\"long-lived\",\"option_d\":\"poisonous\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The shrub follows a “different strategy” from plants that die within weeks, and some individuals are more than 300 years old. So the shrub lives a very long time. “Long-lived” fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the text gives no information about growth speed; the evidence concerns age. Choice B is wrong because nothing says the shrub is uncommon, and its age does not show rarity. Choice D is wrong because toxicity is never mentioned.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Buta bir necha haftada nobud bo‘ladigan o‘simliklardan “boshqacha strategiya”ga ega, ba’zi tuplari esa 300 yildan oshiq yashagan. Demak, buta juda uzoq yashaydi. “Long-lived” (uzoq umr ko‘radigan) so‘zi mos keladi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matnda o‘sish tezligi haqida ma’lumot yo‘q, dalil yosh haqida. B noto‘g‘ri: butaning kam uchrashi aytilmagan, yoshi esa kamyobligini ko‘rsatmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: zaharlilik umuman tilga olinmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science\",\"contrast-signal\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Historians once assumed that most merchants on the medieval Silk Road traveled only short distances, carrying goods between neighboring towns before handing them on to other traders. A recently studied collection of merchants' letters, however, seems to ______ this assumption: the letters show that some traders regularly made journeys of several thousand kilometers.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"confirm\",\"option_b\":\"challenge\",\"option_c\":\"repeat\",\"option_d\":\"ignore\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"“However” signals that the letters go against the earlier assumption of short journeys, and the colon gives evidence of very long journeys. So the letters call the assumption into question. “Challenge” fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the evidence contradicts the assumption rather than supporting it. Choice C is wrong because the letters say something different from the assumption, not the same thing. Choice D is wrong because the letters directly address how far merchants traveled, so they do not ignore the assumption.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“However” (biroq) xatlar avvalgi “qisqa safarlar” haqidagi taxminga zid ekanini bildiradi, ikki nuqtadan keyin esa juda uzoq safarlar haqida dalil keltiriladi. Demak, xatlar bu taxminni shubha ostiga qo‘yadi. “Challenge” (e’tiroz bildirmoq, shubha ostiga olmoq) so‘zi mos keladi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: dalil taxminni tasdiqlamaydi, aksincha unga zid. C noto‘g‘ri: xatlar taxmindan boshqacha narsani ko‘rsatadi, uni takrorlamaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: xatlar savdogarlar qancha uzoqqa borganiga bevosita taalluqli, ya’ni taxminni e’tiborsiz qoldirmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"history\",\"contrast-signal\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Ecologist Sevara Tursunova predicted that adding nitrogen fertilizer to her test plots would boost the growth of the wild grasses planted there. Instead, the fertilizer appeared to ______ their growth: grasses in fertilized plots were on average 15 percent shorter than those in unfertilized plots, most likely because fast-growing weeds thrived on the extra nitrogen and crowded the grasses out.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"inhibit\",\"option_b\":\"stimulate\",\"option_c\":\"measure\",\"option_d\":\"predict\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"“Instead” signals that the result was the opposite of the prediction that fertilizer would “boost” growth, and the grasses in fertilized plots were shorter. So the fertilizer held back their growth. “Inhibit” (to restrain or slow) fits.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because “stimulate” means the same as “boost,” the outcome that did not happen. Choice C is wrong because a fertilizer cannot measure growth; researchers do that. Choice D is wrong because a fertilizer does not predict anything, and the sentence describes an effect on the grasses.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“Instead” (aksincha) natija o‘g‘it o‘sishni “kuchaytiradi” degan bashoratga teskari bo‘lganini bildiradi: o‘g‘itlangan maydonlardagi o‘tlar pastroq bo‘lib chiqqan. Demak, o‘g‘it o‘sishni to‘xtatib qo‘ygan. “Inhibit” (sekinlashtirmoq, to‘sqinlik qilmoq) so‘zi mos keladi.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: “stimulate” (rag‘batlantirmoq) aynan “boost” ma’nosini beradi — bu esa ro‘y bermagan natija. C noto‘g‘ri: o‘g‘it o‘sishni o‘lchay olmaydi, buni tadqiqotchilar qiladi. D noto‘g‘ri: o‘g‘it hech narsani bashorat qilmaydi; gap uning o‘tlarga ta’siri haqida.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science\",\"wrong-charge-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Sociologist Javlon Rashidov argues that the rapid spread of community gardens in Central Asian cities over the past decade cannot be traced to any one cause. Instead, he contends, it reflects a ______ of several developments, including rising food prices, growing public interest in healthy eating, and city residents' desire for shared green spaces.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"dispute\",\"option_b\":\"scarcity\",\"option_c\":\"hierarchy\",\"option_d\":\"confluence\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Rashidov says the trend has no single cause and lists several developments that together produced it. A “confluence” is a coming together of several things, which fits “a ______ of several developments.”\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the developments are not in conflict with one another; they combine. Choice B is wrong because the text lists several developments, so there is no shortage of them. Choice C is wrong because the text does not rank the causes; it presents them as joint contributors.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Rashidov bu tendensiyaning yagona sababi yo‘qligini aytib, uni birgalikda yuzaga keltirgan bir nechta omilni sanab o‘tadi. “Confluence” — bir nechta narsaning bir joyga kelib qo‘shilishi; bu “a ______ of several developments” iborasiga mos keladi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: omillar bir-biri bilan nizolashmaydi, aksincha birlashadi. B noto‘g‘ri: matnda bir nechta omil sanalgan, ya’ni ularning tanqisligi yo‘q. C noto‘g‘ri: matn sabablarni darajalarga ajratmaydi, ularni teng hissa qo‘shuvchilar sifatida ko‘rsatadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"social-science\",\"latinate\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from a short story.\\n\\nBahrom was no orator. He stammered, lost his place in his notes, and twice apologized to the audience for taking up their time. Yet his plain account of the night the river rose, and of the neighbors he had pulled from the water, carried the meeting, and when the vote was finally taken, nearly every hand in the hall went up in favor of rebuilding the dam.\",\"stem\":\"As used in the text, what does the word “carried” most nearly mean?\",\"option_a\":\"transported\",\"option_b\":\"lifted\",\"option_c\":\"won over\",\"option_d\":\"extended\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Although Bahrom was a poor speaker, his account led “nearly every hand in the hall” to vote for rebuilding the dam. His speech persuaded the people at the meeting, so “carried” most nearly means “won over.”\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Bahrom did not physically move the meeting from one place to another. Choice B is wrong because nothing was raised up by his account; the hands went up because people were persuaded. Choice D is wrong because the account did not make the meeting last longer; it convinced the audience.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bahrom yaxshi notiq bo‘lmasa-da, uning hikoyasi “zaldagi deyarli barcha qo‘llar” to‘g‘onni qayta qurish uchun ko‘tarilishiga olib keldi. Uning nutqi yig‘ilishdagilarni ishontirdi, shuning uchun “carried” bu yerda “won over” (o‘z tomoniga og‘dirdi, ishontirdi) ma’nosini beradi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Bahrom yig‘ilishni bir joydan boshqa joyga ko‘chirmagan. B noto‘g‘ri: hikoya hech narsani yuqoriga ko‘tarmagan; qo‘llar odamlar ishonch hosil qilgani uchun ko‘tarildi. D noto‘g‘ri: hikoya yig‘ilishni cho‘zmagan, tinglovchilarni ishontirgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"literature\",\"most-nearly-means\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Because the fossil record of the earliest birds is so ______, paleontologists must often reconstruct whole branches of the bird family tree from a few partial skeletons, sometimes from a single incomplete wing or a handful of scattered bones.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"fragmentary\",\"option_b\":\"abundant\",\"option_c\":\"controversial\",\"option_d\":\"recent\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Paleontologists must work from “a few partial skeletons” and even “a single incomplete wing,” which shows that the record is incomplete and broken into pieces. “Fragmentary” (made up of fragments; incomplete) fits.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because an abundant record would not force scientists to rely on a few partial skeletons. Choice C is wrong because a disputed record would not explain why so few bones are available. Choice D is wrong because the age of the record has nothing to do with scientists having so little material.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Paleontologlar “bir nechta to‘liq bo‘lmagan skelet” va hatto “bitta yarim-yorti qanot” bilan ishlashga majbur — bu qoldiqlar to‘liq emas, parcha-parcha ekanini ko‘rsatadi. “Fragmentary” (parchalardan iborat, to‘liq bo‘lmagan) so‘zi mos keladi.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: qoldiqlar mo‘l bo‘lsa, olimlar bir nechta parcha bilan cheklanib qolmas edi. C noto‘g‘ri: qoldiqlarning bahsli bo‘lishi suyaklar nega bunchalik kamligini tushuntirmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: qoldiqlarning yoshi materialning ozligiga aloqador emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science\",\"latinate\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Historian Kamola Yusupova cautions against treating the travel journal of seventeenth-century merchant Ilyos Barlos as a ______ account of the routes he followed. Barlos, she notes, wrote many entries years after the journeys they describe, and several of the dates he gives conflict with customs records kept at the same ports.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"candid\",\"option_b\":\"anecdotal\",\"option_c\":\"voluminous\",\"option_d\":\"dependable\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Yusupova points out two problems: entries were written years later, and dates conflict with customs records. Both reasons show that the journal cannot be fully trusted. She therefore cautions against treating it as a “dependable” (reliable) account.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the problems she lists concern accuracy, not whether Barlos was frank or honest about his feelings. Choice B is wrong because a journal based on personal stories is anecdotal, and Yusupova's reasons are not about that. Choice C is wrong because the length of the journal is never discussed.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Yusupova ikki muammoni ko‘rsatadi: yozuvlar safardan yillar o‘tib yozilgan va sanalar bojxona hujjatlariga zid. Ikkalasi ham kundalikka to‘liq ishonib bo‘lmasligini bildiradi. Shuning uchun u uni “dependable” (ishonchli) manba deb hisoblamaslikni maslahat beradi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: u sanagan muammolar aniqlik haqida, Barlosning samimiyligi haqida emas. B noto‘g‘ri: “anecdotal” (shaxsiy hikoyalarga asoslangan) degan ma’no Yusupovaning dalillariga mos kelmaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: kundalikning hajmi umuman muhokama qilinmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"history\",\"context-clue\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"When an earthquake occurs, its seismic waves reach different monitoring stations at slightly different times. By comparing these arrival times across several stations, seismologists can now ______ the point where the earthquake began to within a few hundred meters, often in less than a minute.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"anticipate\",\"option_b\":\"pinpoint\",\"option_c\":\"obscure\",\"option_d\":\"dramatize\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text describes locating an earthquake's origin “to within a few hundred meters,” which means identifying it very precisely. “Pinpoint” (to locate exactly) fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the method uses waves that have already arrived, so it locates an earthquake after it begins rather than anticipating (expecting) it. Choice C is wrong because the method reveals the location; it does not hide it. Choice D is wrong because nothing suggests exaggerating or making the location seem more exciting.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda zilzila boshlangan nuqtani “bir necha yuz metr aniqlikda” topish tasvirlangan, ya’ni joy juda aniq belgilanadi. “Pinpoint” (aniq belgilamoq) so‘zi mos keladi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: usul allaqachon yetib kelgan to‘lqinlardan foydalanadi, ya’ni zilzilani oldindan kutmaydi (“anticipate”), balki boshlangandan keyin uning joyini aniqlaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: usul joyni yashirmaydi, aksincha ochib beradi. D noto‘g‘ri: joyni bo‘rttirib ko‘rsatish haqida gap yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science\",\"precision\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Early naturalists described the nest building of potter wasps as almost mechanical, assuming that each wasp simply ran through a fixed series of steps. Yet when entomologist Rano Abdullayeva damaged half-built nests, the wasps did not start the series over from the beginning; they repaired only the damaged section and then altered the shape of the next cells to compensate. Such behavior suggests that the wasps' building is far less ______ than early accounts implied.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"adaptive\",\"option_b\":\"intricate\",\"option_c\":\"invariable\",\"option_d\":\"laborious\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Early naturalists thought the wasps followed “a fixed series of steps.” Abdullayeva found that the wasps changed what they did in response to damage. So the building is less fixed or unchanging than the early accounts said. “Invariable” (never changing) fits.\\n\\nChoice A is the reverse trap: the wasps' repairs show that the behavior is more adaptive, not less. Choice B is wrong because the early accounts called the behavior “mechanical,” not complex, and the new evidence does not show it is simpler. Choice D is wrong because the text does not discuss how much effort the building requires.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ilk tabiatshunoslar arilar “qat’iy bir qator bosqichlar”ga amal qiladi deb o‘ylashgan. Abdullayeva esa arilar shikastga qarab xatti-harakatini o‘zgartirishini aniqladi. Demak, uya qurish avvalgi ta’riflardagidan ancha kamroq qat’iy, o‘zgarmas. “Invariable” (o‘zgarmas) so‘zi mos keladi.\\n\\nA — teskari tuzoq: tuzatishlar xatti-harakat kamroq emas, balki ko‘proq moslashuvchan (“adaptive”) ekanini ko‘rsatadi. B noto‘g‘ri: ilk ta’riflarda xatti-harakat murakkab emas, “mexanik” deyilgan va yangi dalil uni soddaroq deb ko‘rsatmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: matnda qurilish qancha mehnat talab qilishi muhokama qilinmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science\",\"distant-clue\",\"latinate\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Critics long dismissed the late canvases of painter Ozod Mirzayev as ______, pointing to their thin layers of paint and loose, rapid brushwork as signs that the aging artist no longer took much trouble over his work. A recent technical study, however, found detailed preparatory drawings beneath nearly every one of these canvases, suggesting that their apparent spontaneity was carefully planned.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"meticulous\",\"option_b\":\"perfunctory\",\"option_c\":\"ponderous\",\"option_d\":\"derivative\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The critics saw the late paintings as signs that the artist “no longer took much trouble over his work.” “Perfunctory” means done with little care or effort, merely to get it done, which matches the critics' dismissal.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because “meticulous” (extremely careful) describes what the technical study revealed, not what the critics claimed; it reverses the charge. Choice C is wrong because “ponderous” means heavy and slow, the opposite of “loose, rapid brushwork.” Choice D is wrong because “derivative” means copied from others, and the critics' complaint is about lack of effort, not lack of originality.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tanqidchilar kechki rasmlarni rassom “endi ishiga unchalik e’tibor bermay qo‘ygani”ning belgisi deb bilishgan. “Perfunctory” — shunchaki qutulish uchun, yuzaki va kam e’tibor bilan qilingan degani; bu tanqidchilarning fikriga to‘liq mos keladi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: “meticulous” (o‘ta sinchkov) texnik tadqiqot aniqlagan narsani ifodalaydi, tanqidchilarning da’vosini emas — ma’no teskari. C noto‘g‘ri: “ponderous” (og‘ir, sust) “erkin, tez mo‘yqalam harakatlari”ga zid. D noto‘g‘ri: “derivative” (boshqalardan ko‘chirilgan) originallik yo‘qligini bildiradi, tanqidchilar esa mehnat yetishmasligidan shikoyat qilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"humanities\",\"latinate\",\"wrong-charge-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Anthropologist Laylo Hamidova spent a year studying vendors in a large bazaar, where sellers of the same goods almost never undercut one another's prices. No vendor she interviewed could say when the practice began or who had first proposed it, and none had ever discussed it openly with competitors. Yet violations were rare and were swiftly punished, usually by the offender's neighbors refusing to trade with him. The arrangement, in other words, is ______ but firmly enforced.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"tacit\",\"option_b\":\"provisional\",\"option_c\":\"contentious\",\"option_d\":\"codified\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The vendors' agreement was never proposed by anyone known and never “discussed … openly,” yet everyone follows it. “Tacit” means understood without being stated, which fits. The word “but” contrasts this unspoken quality with the firm enforcement.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because nothing suggests the arrangement is temporary; no one remembers when it began. Choice C is wrong because a contentious arrangement would be disputed, yet the text says no one discusses it and violations are rare. Choice D is the reverse trap: “codified” means written down as formal rules, the opposite of an agreement that was never stated.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Sotuvchilarning kelishuvini kim taklif qilgani noma’lum va u hech qachon “ochiq muhokama qilinmagan”, lekin hamma unga amal qiladi. “Tacit” — aytilmasa ham tushuniladigan, so‘zsiz degani; bu mos keladi. “But” (lekin) shu so‘zsizlikni qattiq nazorat bilan qarama-qarshi qo‘yadi.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: kelishuv vaqtinchalik (“provisional”) ekaniga hech qanday ishora yo‘q; uning qachon boshlanganini hech kim eslamaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: “contentious” (bahsli) kelishuv tortishuvlarga sabab bo‘lardi, matnda esa uni hech kim muhokama qilmaydi va buzilishlar kam. D — teskari tuzoq: “codified” (rasmiy qoidalar sifatida yozib qo‘yilgan) hech qachon aytilmagan kelishuvning aksi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"social-science\",\"latinate\",\"distant-clue\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"For decades, astronomers assumed that the bright spot near the south pole of an icy moon orbiting a distant planet was a lasting surface feature, perhaps a field of fresh frost. But images collected since 1990 by planetary scientist Timur Ganiyev's team tell a different story. Rather than a constant presence, the spot is ______: it has faded from view and then reappeared at least four times, each time remaining visible for a year or more.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"ephemeral\",\"option_b\":\"immutable\",\"option_c\":\"incipient\",\"option_d\":\"intermittent\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"“Rather than a constant presence” and the colon's explanation (the spot fades and reappears “at least four times”) show that the spot comes and goes repeatedly. “Intermittent” (stopping and starting at intervals) fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because “ephemeral” means lasting only a very short time; it says nothing about returning, and each appearance lasts “a year or more.” Choice B is wrong because “immutable” (unchanging) fits the old view of a lasting feature, which the new images contradict. Choice C is wrong because “incipient” means just beginning to appear, but the spot has been observed repeatedly over decades.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“Rather than a constant presence” (doimiy mavjud bo‘lish o‘rniga) iborasi va ikki nuqtadan keyingi izoh (dog‘ “kamida to‘rt marta” yo‘qolib, yana paydo bo‘lgan) dog‘ vaqti-vaqti bilan paydo bo‘lib, yo‘qolib turishini ko‘rsatadi. “Intermittent” (uzilib-uzilib, oraliqlar bilan takrorlanadigan) so‘zi mos keladi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: “ephemeral” juda qisqa muddat davom etadigan degani va qayta paydo bo‘lishni bildirmaydi; bundan tashqari, har safar dog‘ “bir yil yoki undan ko‘proq” ko‘rinib turgan. B noto‘g‘ri: “immutable” (o‘zgarmas) yangi tasvirlar rad etgan eski “doimiy belgi” qarashiga mos. C noto‘g‘ri: “incipient” endigina paydo bo‘layotgan degani, dog‘ esa o‘n yillar davomida qayta-qayta kuzatilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v4\",\"science\",\"latinate\",\"near-synonym-trap\"]}]" }
